/* OCR GCSE Mathematics (J560) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Sonnet 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'OCR-J560', KITS: {"number-operations":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"number-operations","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Number operations and integers open the OCR specification because every later room leans on it: a wrong sign in a ratio calculation, a missed bracket in a formula, or a mis-ordered power and root all trace back to the four rules, priority of operations and inverse operations taught here. Papers 2 and 5 are non-calculator, so a written method that shows each step is what earns the marks, not a bare answer, and Papers 1, 3, 4 and 6 still expect a calculator display to be checked against an estimate built by hand. Prime factorisation and HCF/LCM are the tools behind simplifying fractions, ratio and algebra questions set anywhere else on the paper, so a gap here costs marks well beyond this room.","idea":[{"h":"The four rules with positive and negative whole numbers","t":"Add, subtract, multiply and divide positive and negative whole numbers using a written method rather than a calculator, since this is tested without one. Subtracting a negative number is the same as adding it, and adding a negative number is the same as subtracting it: a − (−b) = a + b and a + (−b) = a − b. When multiplying or dividing, two signs the same give a positive answer and two signs that differ give a negative answer. Work through a calculation with several signed numbers one operation at a time, keeping track of the sign at each step rather than trying to do it in one jump.","code":"1.01a"},{"h":"Odd, even, prime, factor, multiple and place value","t":"A factor (or divisor) of a number divides into it exactly, with no remainder; a multiple of a number is in its times table. A common factor divides exactly into two or more numbers, and a common multiple appears in the times tables of two or more numbers. A prime number has exactly two factors, itself and 1, so 1 is not prime. A square number is a whole number multiplied by itself and a cube number is a whole number multiplied by itself twice more (n × n × n); a square root and a cube root undo those. Place value is the value a digit carries from its position in a number, and it is what keeps a calculation's size under control.","code":"1.02a"},{"h":"Prime factorisation and index form","t":"Identify the prime numbers less than 20 (2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19), and express any whole number as a product of its prime factors by dividing repeatedly by the smallest prime that fits. Every whole number above 1 breaks into primes in exactly one way, so the answer is always the same however the divisions are carried out. Write the result in power (index) notation to keep it short, for example 600 = 2³ × 3 × 5², rather than writing every prime factor out separately.","code":"1.02b"},{"h":"HCF and LCM of two whole numbers","t":"The highest common factor (HCF) of two numbers is the greatest number that divides exactly into both, and the lowest common multiple (LCM) is the smallest number that both divide into exactly. Both can be found by listing every factor or multiple of each number and comparing the lists. Once each number is written as a product of prime factors, the HCF is the product of the lowest power of each prime the two factorisations share, and the LCM is the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either factorisation — this is faster than listing once the numbers are large.","code":"1.02c"},{"h":"Priority of operations","t":"Calculations involving brackets, the four rules and powers, roots and reciprocals are not carried out in the order they are written. Work out anything inside brackets first, then powers, roots and reciprocals, then multiplication and division from left to right, then addition and subtraction from left to right. A question that asks for 'one calculation, with brackets where they are needed' is testing whether the bracket is placed correctly as much as the arithmetic that follows it, so the bracket itself often carries a mark.","code":"1.03a"},{"h":"Inverse operations","t":"Addition and subtraction are inverse operations, as are multiplication and division, and powers and roots: each one undoes the other. This is used to check a calculation (dividing an answer by one factor should return the other) and to simplify one (25 × 12 = 50 × 6 = 100 × 3 = 300, since doubling one factor and halving the other leaves the product unchanged). It also solves 'I'm thinking of a number' or 'missing digit' problems: undo each operation in reverse order, starting with the last one applied, to work back to the starting number.","code":"1.04a"}],"examples":[{"title":"Working out a calculation with brackets and a power","setup":"Work out 3 × (7 − 2)² + 4, showing the order in which the operations are carried out.","steps":["The bracket is worked out first: 7 − 2 = 5, so the calculation becomes 3 × 5² + 4.","The power comes next, before any multiplying or adding: 5² = 25, giving 3 × 25 + 4.","Multiplication comes before addition: 3 × 25 = 75.","The addition is left until last: 75 + 4 = 79."],"cues":["Which part of the calculation is dealt with before anything else?","Once the bracket is gone, what kind of term is 5²?","Between the multiplication and the addition that are left, which comes first?","What is left once the multiplication has been carried out?"]},{"title":"Four rules with directed numbers in context","setup":"A diver is 18 m below sea level. She rises 7 m, then dives a further 12 m. Work out her final position, giving your answer as a number of metres below sea level.","steps":["Below sea level is written as negative, so her starting position is −18.","Rising is an increase, so add 7: −18 + 7 = −11.","Diving further down is a decrease, so subtract 12: −11 − 12 = −23.","A position of −23 means 23 m below sea level, so that is her final position."],"cues":["How is 'below sea level' written as a signed number?","Does rising add to or take away from the current position?","Does diving further down add to or take away from the current position?","What does a negative position of −23 mean in words?"]},{"title":"Writing a number as a product of prime factors","setup":"Express 432 as a product of its prime factors, giving your answer in index form.","steps":["432 is even, so divide by 2: 432 = 2 × 216.","216 is also even, so divide by 2 again: 216 = 2 × 108, giving 432 = 2 × 2 × 108.","Keep dividing by 2 while it still fits: 108 = 2 × 54 and 54 = 2 × 27, so 432 = 2⁴ × 27.","27 = 3 × 3 × 3 = 3³, the next prime, so 432 = 2⁴ × 3³."],"cues":["Which small prime divides into an even number straight away?","Does the same prime divide into the result again?","How can you tell when a prime no longer divides in exactly?","What is 27 as a power of the next prime after 2?"]},{"title":"Finding the HCF and LCM from two prime factorisations","setup":"90 = 2 × 3² × 5 and 168 = 2³ × 3 × 7. Use these factorisations to find the highest common factor and the lowest common multiple of 90 and 168.","steps":["For the HCF, take the lower power of each prime that appears in both lists: 2¹ (not 2³) and 3¹ (not 3²); 5 and 7 each appear in only one list, so are left out.","Multiply those lower powers together: HCF = 2 × 3 = 6.","For the LCM, take the higher power of every prime that appears in either list: 2³, 3², 5¹ and 7¹.","Multiply those together: LCM = 8 × 9 × 5 × 7 = 2520."],"cues":["For a factor shared by both numbers, which power of 2 can both supply?","What do the chosen powers of 2 and 3 multiply to give?","For a multiple of both numbers, which power of each prime is needed?","What do the four chosen powers multiply to give?"]},{"title":"Using inverse operations to find a missing number","setup":"A number is doubled and then 9 is added. The result is 31. Use inverse operations to work out the original number, and check your answer.","steps":["The last operation applied was 'add 9', so undo it first: 31 − 9 = 22.","Before the 9 was added, the number had been doubled, so undo the doubling by halving: 22 ÷ 2 = 11.","Check by working forwards from 11: double it to get 22, then add 9 to get 31, which matches, so the original number is 11."],"cues":["Which operation was applied last, and how is it undone?","What was done to the number before the 9 was added, and how is that undone?","How can working forwards from the answer confirm it is correct?"]},{"title":"Reading off primes, factors and squares from a list","setup":"From the list 15, 16, 17, 21, 25, 27, write down (a) the prime numbers, (b) the numbers that are factors of 42, (c) the square numbers.","steps":["Check each number for exactly two factors, itself and 1: 17 has exactly two factors, so 17 is prime; none of the rest are.","A factor of 42 divides into it exactly: 42 ÷ 21 = 2 is exact, but 42 does not divide exactly by any of the others, so 21 is the only factor of 42 in the list.","A square number is a whole number multiplied by itself: 4 × 4 = 16, and separately 5 × 5 = 25, so both 16 and 25 are square numbers.","Combining all three parts, four numbers from the list satisfy at least one property: 17 (prime), 21 (factor of 42), and 16 and 25 (square numbers)."],"cues":["How many factors does a prime number have?","What must be true for a number to divide exactly into 42?","Which two numbers multiplied by themselves appear in the list?","Once the three parts are combined, how many different numbers from the list satisfy at least one of the properties?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Work out −5 × (−3).","a":"15"},{"q":"Is 1 a prime number?","a":"No"},{"q":"Write 20 as a product of prime factors.","a":"2² × 5"},{"q":"What is the HCF of 12 and 18?","a":"6"},{"q":"Which operation is carried out first in 8 − 2 × 3?","a":"The multiplication, 2 × 3"},{"q":"What is the inverse operation of multiplying by 5?","a":"Dividing by 5"}]},"room":{"facts":["Priority of operations: brackets first, then powers, roots and reciprocals, then multiplication and division left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","a − (−b) = a + b and a + (−b) = a − b; a negative multiplied or divided by a negative gives a positive, and a positive multiplied or divided by a negative gives a negative.","A prime number has exactly two factors, itself and 1 (so 1 is not prime); a factor divides exactly into a number and a multiple is in that number's times table.","Every whole number above 1 is a product of prime factors in exactly one way.","HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime the two factorisations share.","LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either factorisation.","Addition/subtraction, multiplication/division and powers/roots are inverse operation pairs; undoing the last operation first, in reverse order, checks a calculation or solves a missing-number problem.","OCR GCSE (9–1) Mathematics J560 is assessed by three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes: J560/01–03 (Foundation, grades 5–1) and J560/04–06 (Higher, grades 9–4). Paper 2 and Paper 5 are non-calculator; Papers 1, 3, 4 and 6 allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out (a) −8 + 15 − (−6) (b) 7 × (−4) ÷ 2","a":"(a) 13   (b) −14","sol":"(a) Subtracting a negative adds: −8 + 15 − (−6) = −8 + 15 + 6 = 13 (B1). (b) Multiply first: 7 × (−4) = −28, then divide: −28 ÷ 2 = −14 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two signs written side by side can always be replaced by a single sign.","Subtracting a negative is the same as adding, and in part (b) work left to right through the multiplication and division.","The first line of (a) rewrites −(−6) as +6, and of (b) is 7 × (−4)."],"codes":["1.01a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. State whether 51 is odd, even or prime. Then list the factors of 40.","a":"51 is odd and not prime; the factors of 40 are 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 40","sol":"51 = 3 × 17, so it has more than two factors: 51 is odd, but it is not prime (B1). The factors of 40 are found in pairs: 1 × 40, 2 × 20, 4 × 10, 5 × 8, giving 1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 20, 40 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A number's last digit shows whether it is odd or even, but that alone does not decide whether it is prime.","Try dividing 51 by small primes to see whether it splits; list the factors of 40 in pairs that multiply to 40.","The first line for 51 tests whether 3 divides it exactly, and for 40 is the pair 1 × 40."],"codes":["1.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 5 + 2 × 3²","a":"23","sol":"Work out the power first: 3² = 9, so the calculation becomes 5 + 2 × 9 (M1). Multiplication before addition: 2 × 9 = 18, so 5 + 18 = 23 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["These operations are not carried out in the order they are written.","Deal with the power first, then the multiplication, and leave the addition until last.","The first line rewrites the calculation with 3² replaced by its value."],"codes":["1.03a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A conference centre has 150 chairs to arrange into equal rows, with none left over. Write 150 as a product of its prime factors, giving your answer in index form.","a":"2 × 3 × 5²","sol":"150 is even, so divide by 2: 150 = 2 × 75 (M1). 75 = 3 × 25 = 3 × 5 × 5, so 150 = 2 × 3 × 5² (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Every whole number above 1 breaks into primes in exactly one way.","Divide by the smallest prime that goes in, then keep dividing the result, gathering any repeats into an index.","The first line is 150 = 2 × 75."],"codes":["1.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Use an inverse operation to check whether 17 × 6 = 102 is correct.","a":"It is correct","sol":"Division is the inverse of multiplication, so check by dividing: 102 ÷ 6 = 17, which matches the number it was multiplied by, so 17 × 6 = 102 is correct (B1).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A multiplication can be checked without repeating the same multiplication.","Divide the answer by one of the two numbers multiplied and see what comes back.","The first line is 102 ÷ 6."],"codes":["1.04a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out −45 ÷ (−9) + (−3) × 6","a":"−13","sol":"Division and multiplication are carried out before addition: −45 ÷ (−9) = 5 (M1) and (−3) × 6 = −18 (M1). Adding these gives 5 + (−18) = −13 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two operations here outrank the third, so are dealt with first.","Work out the division and the multiplication separately before combining the results with addition.","The first line is −45 ÷ (−9) alongside (−3) × 6."],"codes":["1.01a","1.03a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A safe's combination code is created from the prime factorisation of 686. Write 686 as a product of its prime factors, giving your answer in index form.","a":"2 × 7³","sol":"686 is even, so divide by 2: 686 = 2 × 343 (M1). 343 = 7 × 7 × 7 = 7³, so 686 = 2 × 7³ (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The same repeated division works whatever the size of the number.","Divide by 2 first, then check whether the remaining factor is itself a power of a single prime.","The first line is 686 = 2 × 343."],"codes":["1.02b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Find the HCF and LCM of 18 and 24 by listing their factors and multiples.","a":"HCF = 6, LCM = 72","sol":"List the factors of each number: factors of 18 are 1, 2, 3, 6, 9, 18 and factors of 24 are 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24 (M1). The highest number in both lists is 6, so HCF = 6 (A1). List multiples of each number: 18, 36, 54, 72, … and 24, 48, 72, … (M1). The lowest number in both lists is 72, so LCM = 72 (A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The HCF and the LCM come from comparing two separate lists.","List every factor of each number for the HCF, and the first few multiples of each for the LCM, then compare the lists.","The first lines are the factors of 18 and of 24, listed in full."],"codes":["1.02c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) (a) Work out 223 − 98 by first adding 2 to each number. (b) Check 25 × 12 by rewriting it as a product with a doubled and a halved factor.","a":"(a) 125   (b) 300","sol":"(a) Add 2 to both numbers so the subtraction becomes easy: 223 − 98 = 225 − 100 = 125 (M1A1). (b) Halving one factor and doubling the other leaves the product unchanged: 25 × 12 = 50 × 6 = 300 (M1A1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Both parts use a shortcut that leaves the result of the calculation unchanged.","In (a) make the second number a round hundred by adjusting both numbers equally; in (b) halve one factor and double the other.","The first line of (a) is 225 − 100, and of (b) is 50 × 6."],"codes":["1.04a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) (a) Write down the smallest prime factor of 91. (b) Write down a common multiple of 6 and 8 that is less than 30. (c) Work out 12 − 3².","a":"(a) 7   (b) 24   (c) 3","sol":"(a) 91 = 7 × 13, so its smallest prime factor is 7 (B1). (b) The multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, 24, … and of 8 are 8, 16, 24, …, and 24 is common to both and less than 30 (B1). (c) Powers are worked out before subtraction: 3² = 9, so 12 − 9 = 3 (B1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each part uses a different one of the ideas in this room.","Try dividing 91 by small primes; list multiples of 6 and of 8 side by side; and deal with the power before the subtraction.","The first line of (a) tests division by small primes in turn, and of (c) is 12 − 9."],"codes":["1.02a","1.02b","1.03a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A market stall packs 84 oranges and 126 apples into identical bags, each bag holding the same number of oranges and the same number of apples, with none left over. (a) What is the greatest number of bags that can be made? (b) How many oranges and how many apples are in each bag?","a":"(a) 42 bags   (b) 2 oranges and 3 apples in each bag","sol":"Write each total as a product of primes: 84 = 2² × 3 × 7 and 126 = 2 × 3² × 7 (M1). The greatest number of bags is the HCF, taking the lowest power of each shared prime: 2 × 3 × 7 = 42 (M1A1). Each bag then holds 84 ÷ 42 = 2 oranges (A1) and 126 ÷ 42 = 3 apples (A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The bags are identical and nothing is left over, so the number of bags divides both totals exactly.","The greatest such number is the highest common factor, found from the prime factorisations of both totals.","The first lines are 84 and 126 written as products of prime factors."],"codes":["1.02b","1.02c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A plumber charges a call-out fee of £15 plus 3 times her hourly rate, £x. For one job the total charge is £54. Use inverse operations to find her hourly rate, £x.","a":"£13","sol":"The call-out fee is added last, so undo it first: 54 − 15 = 39 (M1). The rate was tripled, so undo this by dividing: 39 ÷ 3 = 13 (M1). Check by working forwards: 3 × 13 + 15 = 39 + 15 = 54, which matches, so the hourly rate is £13 (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Working backwards through what was done undoes it, one step at a time.","Undo the operation applied last first, using its inverse, then undo the operation before that.","The first line is 54 − 15."],"codes":["1.04a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. n = 2³ × 3² × 5 and m = 2⁴ × 3 × 5². (a) Write down the values of n and m. (b) Find the HCF and LCM of n and m, and verify that HCF × LCM = n × m. (c) Work out LCM − HCF, and state whether the result is odd or even.","a":"(a) n = 360, m = 1200   (b) HCF = 120, LCM = 3600, and 120 × 3600 = 432000 = 360 × 1200   (c) 3480, which is even","sol":"n = 8 × 9 × 5 = 360 and m = 16 × 3 × 25 = 1200 (B1). For the HCF take the lowest power of each shared prime: 2³, 3¹ and 5¹, giving HCF = 8 × 3 × 5 = 120 (M1A1). For the LCM take the highest power of each prime: 2⁴, 3² and 5², giving LCM = 16 × 9 × 25 = 3600 (M1A1). Multiplying the HCF and LCM gives 120 × 3600 = 432000, and multiplying n and m gives 360 × 1200 = 432000, so HCF × LCM = n × m is confirmed (B1). LCM − HCF = 3600 − 120 = 3480, which ends in 0, so it is even (B1).","m":7,"d":3,"hints":["The factorisations can be multiplied out directly, and compared prime by prime for the HCF and LCM.","Take the lower power of each prime for the HCF and the higher power for the LCM, then multiply each pair of results together to check they match.","The first line multiplies out 2³ × 3² × 5 and 2⁴ × 3 × 5²."],"codes":["1.02a","1.02b","1.02c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A pub quiz gives 5 points for a correct answer and deducts 3 points for a wrong one. A team answers 12 questions correctly and 4 incorrectly, then loses a further 6 points as a penalty. Write a single calculation, using brackets where needed, for the team's final score, and work it out.","a":"12 × 5 + 4 × (−3) − 6 = 42","sol":"One correct calculation is 12 × 5 + 4 × (−3) − 6 (M1). Priority of operations takes the multiplications first: 12 × 5 = 60 and 4 × (−3) = −12 (M1). Working left to right through what remains: 60 + (−12) = 48, then 48 − 6 = 42 (A1A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A wrong answer is a negative number of points, and the penalty is taken away separately.","Set up one calculation for the correct answers, the wrong answers and the penalty together, then apply priority of operations to work it out.","The first line is 12 × 5 + 4 × (−3) − 6."],"codes":["1.01a","1.03a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Work out −7 − (−10).","a":"3"},{"q":"Write 45 as a product of its prime factors.","a":"3² × 5"},{"q":"Find the LCM of 8 and 12.","a":"24"},{"q":"Work out 6 + 4 × (9 − 6).","a":"18"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Odd and even numbers","back":"An even number divides exactly by 2; an odd number does not. Every whole number is one or the other, and they alternate: …, 3, 4, 5, 6, ….","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Prime number","back":"A whole number greater than 1 with exactly two factors, itself and 1. The primes less than 20 are 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19. 1 is not prime, and 2 is the only even prime.","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Factor and multiple","back":"A factor (or divisor) of a number divides into it exactly, with no remainder. A multiple of a number is in its times table: the multiples of 6 are 6, 12, 18, 24, ….","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Common factor and common multiple","back":"A common factor divides exactly into two or more numbers; a common multiple appears in the times tables of two or more numbers.","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Square number and cube number","back":"A square number is a whole number multiplied by itself: 6 × 6 = 36. A cube number is multiplied by itself twice more (n × n × n): 4 × 4 × 4 = 64.","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Square root and cube root","back":"The square root undoes squaring: √49 = 7 because 7² = 49. The cube root undoes cubing: the cube root of 125 is 5 because 5³ = 125.","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Place value","back":"The value a digit carries because of its position in a number: in 4523, the 5 stands for 500. It is what keeps very large and very small numbers under control.","code":"1.02a","open":false},{"front":"Writing a number as a product of prime factors","back":"Divide repeatedly by the smallest prime that fits, then write any repeated prime in index (power) form: 90 = 2 × 3 × 3 × 5 = 2 × 3² × 5.","code":"1.02b","open":false},{"front":"Unique factorisation","back":"Every whole number greater than 1 is a product of prime factors in exactly one way — however the repeated division is carried out, the same set of primes is reached.","code":"1.02b","open":false},{"front":"HCF from two prime factorisations","back":"The highest common factor is the product of the lowest power of each prime the two factorisations share; a prime in only one factorisation is left out.","code":"1.02c","open":false},{"front":"LCM from two prime factorisations","back":"The lowest common multiple is the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either factorisation.","code":"1.02c","open":false},{"front":"Priority of operations","back":"Brackets first, then powers, roots and reciprocals, then multiplication and division left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right.","code":"1.03a","open":false},{"front":"Subtracting and adding negative numbers","back":"a − (−b) = a + b, and a + (−b) = a − b. Same signs multiply or divide to a positive; different signs give a negative.","code":"1.01a","open":false},{"front":"Inverse operation pairs","back":"Addition and subtraction are inverse operations, as are multiplication and division, and powers and roots: each one exactly undoes the other.","code":"1.04a","open":false},{"front":"Give one way to use an inverse operation to check a calculation","back":"Divide the answer by one factor to see if the other factor comes back; add the number subtracted back to the answer to see if the original number comes back; square a square-rooted answer to see if the original number comes back.","code":"1.04a","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["None of this room's content is listed among the formulae to know in 6.02d, and none of it is a result the Foundation tier formulae sheet prints (that sheet gives only the area and circumference of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, right-angled trigonometry, the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, compound interest and the addition rule for probability) — check the current insert, but nothing below is given to you on the day.","Remember: priority of operations — brackets first, then powers, roots and reciprocals, then multiplication and division left to right, then addition and subtraction left to right (1.03a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: a − (−b) = a + b and a + (−b) = a − b; a negative multiplied or divided by a negative gives a positive, and a positive multiplied or divided by a negative gives a negative (1.01a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: every whole number greater than 1 is a product of prime factors in exactly one way (1.02b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: HCF = the product of the lowest power of each prime the two factorisations share (1.02c). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: LCM = the product of the highest power of every prime that appears in either factorisation (1.02c). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: addition and subtraction, multiplication and division, and powers and roots are inverse operation pairs; undo the last operation applied first, in reverse order, to check a calculation or solve a missing-number problem (1.04a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: the meanings of odd, even, prime, factor, multiple, common factor, common multiple, square, cube, root and place value (1.02a). Not on the formulae sheet, and not given to you — these are definitions to know."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:39:00.018405Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key in the bank, the check list and the exit ticket re-solves correctly and every sol tally adds to its m, so the mathematics is sound; before publishing, add OCR's paper facts to room.facts, mark question 1 as a calculator question, and rewrite the two third hints that hand over the answer."}}},"fractions-decimals-percentages":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"fractions-decimals-percentages","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are three ways of writing the same numbers, and every OCR Foundation paper depends on moving between them: Number carries a quarter of the marks, and this room's ideas also sit underneath ratio, proportion, money, statistics and probability questions that are never labelled 'number' at all. Paper 2 is non-calculator, so the written method for converting, ordering and finding a fraction or a percentage of an amount is what earns the method marks; Papers 1 and 3 expect the equivalent calculator method, keyed in as a single multiplier, with the display read back correctly in context. Knowing that 0.375 = 3/8, that 'of' always means multiply and that a percentage change is a single multiplier makes a large share of every Foundation paper routine rather than a struggle.","idea":[{"h":"Equivalent fractions and mixed numbers","t":"Two fractions are equivalent if multiplying or dividing the top and bottom by the same number turns one into the other, so 2/6 and 1/3 name the same amount; always cancel to the version with the smallest possible numbers, using the highest common factor. A mixed number such as 2 1/2 combines a whole number with a fraction; to write it as an improper (top-heavy) fraction, multiply the whole number by the denominator and add the numerator, keeping the same denominator: 2 1/2 = (2×2+1)/2 = 5/2. Exam questions often ask for an answer 'in its simplest form' or 'as a mixed number', and a mark is lost for leaving a fraction that still cancels.","code":"2.01a"},{"h":"Calculations with fractions","t":"Add and subtract fractions by rewriting them over a common denominator first — never add the tops and the bottoms separately. Multiply fractions by cancelling common factors, then multiplying the numerators and the denominators; divide by a fraction by multiplying by its reciprocal (turn the second fraction upside down). Every mixed number is converted to an improper fraction before any of these four operations begins, and the usual rules for negatives apply throughout, so −3 × 4/5 = −12/5. On Paper 2 (non-calculator) these written methods are exactly what the method marks reward, so the middle lines must be shown.","code":"2.01b"},{"h":"Fractions of a quantity, and one quantity as a fraction of another","t":"'Of' means multiply, so a/b of x is worked out as (x ÷ b) × a: find one part by dividing by the denominator, then scale up by multiplying by the numerator. To express one quantity as a fraction of another, write the first quantity over the second and simplify: £6 out of £15 is 6/15 = 2/5. Foundation tier also expects fractions greater than 1, so a quantity that has grown to five quarters of its start value is described with the fraction 5/4 directly, without first converting it to a mixed number unless the question asks for one.","code":"2.01c"},{"h":"Decimals and fractions","t":"A simple fraction converts to a terminating decimal by dividing the numerator by the denominator, or by scaling the denominator to 10, 100 or 1000 when that is possible, all without a calculator: 1/6 needs long division and gives 0.1666…, written with a recurring dot over the repeating digit as 0.16̇. Going the other way, a terminating decimal is written over the matching power of 10 — one zero for each decimal place — and then cancelled, so 0.4 = 4/10 = 2/5. Place value controls both directions, so know which column each digit sits in before converting.","code":"2.02a"},{"h":"Addition, subtraction and multiplication of decimals","t":"Add and subtract decimals by lining up the decimal points so digits of the same place value sit in the same column, including negative decimals, where the usual sign rules apply. Multiply decimals by ignoring the decimal points, multiplying the digits as whole numbers, then counting how many decimal places the two original numbers have between them and placing the point that many digits from the right of the answer: 0.3 × 0.04 has three decimal places between them, so 3 × 4 = 12 becomes 0.012. These are Paper 2 skills, tested without a calculator, so the working must be set out in full.","code":"2.02b"},{"h":"Division of decimals","t":"Dividing a decimal by a whole number uses the same written method as dividing whole numbers, keeping the decimal point in the same column throughout: 0.24 ÷ 6 = 0.04. Dividing by a decimal is turned into dividing by a whole number first: multiply both the number being divided and the divisor by the same power of 10 (10, 100, …) until the divisor is a whole number, then divide as before — 0.3 ÷ 0.6 becomes 3 ÷ 6 = 0.5. Doing this without a calculator, on Paper 2, is a Foundation-tier requirement, so the scaling step itself must be shown as working.","code":"2.02c"},{"h":"Percentage conversions","t":"Fractions, decimals and percentages are three ways of writing the same value, and questions move freely between all three: a decimal becomes a percentage by multiplying by 100 (0.25 = 25%), a percentage becomes a decimal by dividing by 100 (25% = 0.25), and a fraction converts to a percentage via a decimal, or by scaling to a denominator of 100. Values greater than 1 are allowed: 1 1/2 = 1.5 = 150%. The safest route between any two of the three forms nearly always runs through the decimal, so convert to a decimal first whenever the direct route is not obvious.","code":"2.03a"},{"h":"Percentage calculations","t":"A percentage is a number of parts per hundred, so p% of a quantity x is (p ÷ 100) × x — turn the percentage into a decimal multiplier and multiply. To express one quantity as a percentage of another, divide the first by the second and multiply by 100: £12 out of £40 is (12 ÷ 40) × 100 = 30%. Without a calculator, build awkward percentages from 10%, 5% and 1% (find 10% by dividing by 10, then halve, quarter or add pieces as needed); with a calculator, the single multiplier is quicker and less prone to error.","code":"2.03b"},{"h":"Percentage change","t":"A percentage increase multiplies the original amount by 1 + p/100 and a percentage decrease multiplies it by 1 − p/100, so a 10% rise uses 1.1 and a 50% discount uses 0.5; this single-multiplier method also covers simple interest, where the interest each year is calculated on the original amount only, never on a previous year's total. To find an original value after a known percentage change (a reverse percentage), divide the new amount by the multiplier rather than trying to undo the percentage by adding or subtracting it: a price of £10 after a 50% discount had an original price of 10 ÷ 0.5 = £20.","code":"2.03c"},{"h":"Ordinality — ordering fractions, decimals and percentages","t":"To order a mixture of integers, fractions, decimals and percentages, convert every value into one common form — decimals are usually quickest — compare them there, and then write the final list using the forms the question gave, because the marks are for the order of the original values, not for the decimals used to compare them. Negative values are smaller than every positive value, and of two negatives the one further from zero is the smaller. This idea underlies almost every other room: money, probability and statistics questions all rely on being able to order mixed forms correctly.","code":"2.04a"},{"h":"Symbols: <, >, ≤, ≥, =, ≠","t":"< means 'is less than', > means 'is greater than', ≤ means 'is less than or equal to', ≥ means 'is greater than or equal to', = means 'is equal to' and ≠ means 'is not equal to'; in every case the symbol's narrow end points at the smaller value. To decide which symbol fits between two numbers written in different forms, convert them to the same form first (usually decimals), compare, and then write the symbol pointing the correct way — writing the comparison back to front is a common and costly error.","code":"2.04b"}],"examples":[{"title":"Adding a mixed number and a fraction","setup":"Add 1 3/4 and 2/3, giving your answer as a mixed number in its simplest form.","steps":["Write 1 3/4 as an improper fraction: 1 3/4 = 7/4.","Find a common denominator for 7/4 and 2/3: the lowest common denominator is 12, so 7/4 = 21/12 and 2/3 = 8/12.","Add the numerators over that common denominator: 21/12 + 8/12 = 29/12.","Write 29/12 as a mixed number: 29/12 = 2 5/12."],"cues":["What must a mixed number become before it can be added to another fraction?","What is the smallest denominator that both 4 and 3 divide into?","Once both fractions have the same denominator, which parts of them get added?","How many whole 12ths make one whole, and what is left over?"]},{"title":"Dividing by a decimal without a calculator","setup":"(non-calculator) Work out 4.35 ÷ 0.5, showing your method.","steps":["Multiply both numbers by 10 so the divisor becomes a whole number: 4.35 × 10 = 43.5 and 0.5 × 10 = 5.","Divide the whole-number part first: 43 ÷ 5 = 8 remainder 3.","Bring the remainder together with the .5 to make 3.5, then divide: 3.5 ÷ 5 = 0.7, so 43.5 ÷ 5 = 8.7."],"cues":["What kind of number should the divisor become before dividing by hand?","What is 43 divided by 5, and what is left over?","What do you get when you divide the leftover 3.5 by 5?"]},{"title":"A test score as a fraction, a decimal and a percentage","setup":"A test is out of 40 marks. Amir scores 34 marks. Write Amir's score as a fraction of the total in its simplest form, then as a decimal, then as a percentage.","steps":["Write the score as a fraction of the total and simplify by dividing top and bottom by 2: 34/40 = 17/20.","Change 17/20 to a decimal by scaling the denominator to 100: 17/20 = 85/100 = 0.85.","Change the decimal to a percentage by multiplying by 100: 0.85 × 100 = 85%."],"cues":["What single number can both 34 and 40 be divided by?","What must 20 be multiplied by to reach 100, and what happens to the 17?","What operation turns a decimal into a percentage?"]},{"title":"The multiplier for a percentage decrease, and reversing it","setup":"A washing machine's price is reduced by 12% in a sale to £246.40. Work out the original price of the washing machine.","steps":["A reduction of 12% leaves 100% − 12% = 88% of the price, so the multiplier is 0.88.","The original price multiplied by 0.88 gives the sale price, so original × 0.88 = 246.40.","Divide the sale price by 0.88 to undo the multiplier: 246.40 ÷ 0.88 = £280."],"cues":["What percentage of the original price is left after a 12% reduction?","Which multiplication links the unknown original price to the £246.40 sale price?","Which operation undoes a multiplication?"]},{"title":"Ordering numbers given in different forms","setup":"Put these four numbers in order, smallest first, writing each number in the form it was given in: 7/9, 0.7, 68%, 5/6.","steps":["Change 7/9 to a decimal by dividing: 7 ÷ 9 = 0.777… (recurring 7).","Change 5/6 to a decimal by dividing: 5 ÷ 6 = 0.8333… (recurring 3).","Change 68% to a decimal by dividing by 100: 68 ÷ 100 = 0.68.","Compare the four decimals: 0.68 < 0.7 < 0.777… < 0.8333…","Write the answer back in the forms given, smallest first: 68%, 0.7, 7/9, 5/6."],"cues":["Which single form should every number be changed into before comparing?","What is 5/6 as a decimal?","What does dividing a percentage by 100 give?","Which of the four decimals is the smallest?","Which original form does each decimal belong to?"]},{"title":"One quantity as a fraction of another, applied to a new amount","setup":"In a survey, 45 out of 60 people preferred tea to coffee. Write the number who preferred tea as a fraction of the total, in its simplest form, then find how many of a group of 108 people would be expected to prefer tea, using that fraction.","steps":["Divide the top and bottom of 45/60 by 15, their highest common factor: 45/60 = 3/4.","Apply the fraction as an operator to the new group: 3/4 of 108 = 108 ÷ 4 × 3.","One quarter of 108 is 108 ÷ 4 = 27.","Three quarters is 3 × 27 = 81, so 81 of the 108 people would be expected to prefer tea."],"cues":["What is the highest common factor of 45 and 60?","How does a fraction act on a different amount?","What is one quarter of 108?","How many quarters are needed, and what do they total?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 0.55 as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"11/20"},{"q":"Work out 2/3 of 90.","a":"60"},{"q":"Write down the multiplier for a 12% increase.","a":"1.12"},{"q":"Put these in order, smallest first: 0.4, 3/8, 45%.","a":"3/8, 0.4, 45%"}]},"room":{"facts":["Mixed number ↔ improper fraction: multiply the whole number by the denominator and add the numerator, keeping the denominator; reverse it by dividing the numerator by the denominator for the whole number part, writing the remainder over the denominator.","Add or subtract fractions over a common denominator; multiply by cancelling then multiplying tops and bottoms; divide by multiplying by the reciprocal. Convert any mixed number to an improper fraction first.","A fraction or a percentage acting on a quantity is a multiplication: a/b of x = (x ÷ b) × a, and p% of x = (p ÷ 100) × x.","One quantity as a fraction (or percentage) of another: divide the first quantity by the second, then simplify (or multiply by 100 for a percentage).","A terminating decimal becomes a fraction over a power of 10, one zero per decimal place, then cancels: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8. A fraction becomes a decimal by dividing the numerator by the denominator; a non-terminating result is written with a recurring dot, e.g. 1/6 = 0.16̇.","Add or subtract decimals by lining up the decimal points; multiply decimals as whole numbers, then place the decimal point so the answer has as many decimal places as the two numbers had between them.","To divide by a decimal, multiply both numbers by the same power of 10 until the divisor is a whole number, then divide as normal.","Fraction → decimal → percentage: divide top by bottom, then ×100 for a percentage; percentage → decimal → fraction: ÷100, then write over 100 and cancel. Values above 100% and below 1 are all allowed, e.g. 1 1/2 = 150%.","A percentage increase of p% multiplies by 1 + p/100; a decrease of p% multiplies by 1 − p/100. A reverse percentage divides the new amount by that multiplier to find the original amount.","To order numbers given in mixed forms, convert them all into one form (usually decimals), compare, then write the answer back in the original forms; negatives are smaller than positives, and the more negative value is the smaller.","The comparison symbols: < less than, > greater than, ≤ less than or equal to, ≥ greater than or equal to, = equal to, ≠ not equal to; the narrow end of < or > points at the smaller value.","OCR GCSE (9-1) Mathematics J560 has three papers per tier: Foundation is J560/01, /02 and /03 (grades 5-1); Higher is J560/04, /05 and /06 (grades 9-4). Each paper is 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator, the rest allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 4 2/3 as an improper fraction.","a":"14/3","sol":"Multiply the whole number by the denominator and add the numerator, keeping the same denominator: 4 2/3 = (4 × 3 + 2)/3 = 14/3. B1 for 14/3 (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A mixed number is a whole number plus a fraction.","Multiply the whole number by the denominator, then add the numerator.","The first line is 4 × 3 + 2, all over the denominator 3."],"codes":["2.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 7/8 as a decimal.","a":"0.875","sol":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875. B1 for 0.875 (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A fraction converts to a decimal using one of the four operations.","Divide the top of the fraction by the bottom.","The first line is 7 ÷ 8, carried out as a division."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Write 0.6 as a percentage.","a":"60%","sol":"A decimal becomes a percentage by multiplying by 100: 0.6 × 100 = 60%. B1 for 60% (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage is out of 100.","Multiply the decimal by 100.","The first line is 0.6 × 100."],"codes":["2.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the correct symbol, < or >, between 2/5 and 0.35.","a":"2/5 > 0.35","sol":"Change the fraction to a decimal: 2/5 = 0.4. Since 0.4 is greater than 0.35, 2/5 > 0.35. B1 for the correct symbol used the correct way round (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The two values must be in the same form before they can be compared.","Change the fraction into a decimal, then compare the two decimals.","The first line is 2/5 written as a decimal."],"codes":["2.04b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Work out 20% of 85.","a":"17","sol":"20% as a decimal multiplier is 20 ÷ 100 = 0.2. M1 for 0.2 × 85 (oe, for example 85 ÷ 100 × 20). 0.2 × 85 = 17. A1 for 17 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A percentage is a number of parts per hundred.","Turn the percentage into a decimal multiplier and apply it to the amount.","The first line is 20 ÷ 100 = the multiplier — then multiply 85 by it."],"codes":["2.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 3/4 + 5/6, giving your answer as a mixed number in its simplest form.","a":"1 7/12","sol":"Rewrite both fractions over their lowest common denominator, 12: 3/4 = 9/12 and 5/6 = 10/12. M1 for both fractions correctly over 12. Add the numerators: 9/12 + 10/12 = 19/12. M1 for 19/12. Write as a mixed number: 19/12 = 1 7/12, which cannot be simplified further. A1 for 1 7/12 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Fractions can only be added once they are counted in pieces of the same size.","Find the lowest common denominator of 4 and 6, rewrite both fractions over it, then add the numerators.","The first line rewrites 3/4 and 5/6 over a denominator of 12."],"codes":["2.01b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Express £15 as a percentage of £60.","a":"25%","sol":"Divide the first quantity by the second: 15 ÷ 60 = 0.25. M1 for 15 ÷ 60. Multiply by 100 to write as a percentage: 0.25 × 100 = 25%. A1 for 25% (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["One quantity as a percentage of another starts with a division.","Divide the first amount by the second, then multiply the result by 100.","The first line is 15 ÷ 60."],"codes":["2.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 6.4 + 3.85, then multiply your answer by 0.3. Give your final answer as a decimal.","a":"3.075","sol":"Lining up the decimal points, 6.4 + 3.85 = 10.25. B1 for 10.25. Multiply this by 0.3: ignoring the points, 1025 × 3 = 3075 (M1). The two factors have three decimal places between them, so 10.25 × 0.3 = 3.075. A1 for 3.075 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is an addition of decimals followed by a multiplication, each done without a calculator.","Add first, then multiply that result by 0.3.","The first line is 6.4 + 3.85 set out in columns."],"codes":["2.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 0.72 ÷ 6, then divide your answer by 0.6. Give your final answer as a decimal.","a":"0.2","sol":"Dividing a decimal by a whole number keeps the decimal point in the same column: 0.72 ÷ 6 = 0.12. B1 for 0.12. Divide this by 0.6: multiply both numbers by 10 so the divisor is a whole number, 1.2 ÷ 6 (M1). 1.2 ÷ 6 = 0.2. A1 for 0.2 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This is a division by a whole number followed by a division by a decimal, each done without a calculator.","Divide by the whole number first; for the second division, scale both numbers by 10 so the divisor is whole.","The first line is 0.72 ÷ 6, then divide that result by 0.6."],"codes":["2.02c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Increase £64 by 15%.","a":"£73.60","sol":"An increase of 15% gives the multiplier 1 + 15/100 = 1.15. M1 for 1.15 × 64 (oe, for example 0.15 × 64 = 9.60 followed by addition). 1.15 × 64 = 73.6. A1 for £73.60 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["An increase keeps all of the original amount and adds more on top.","Use a single multiplier greater than 1, or find the extra amount and add it on.","The first line is 1 + 15/100 = the multiplier — then multiply £64 by it."],"codes":["2.03c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Put these numbers in order, smallest first: 4/5, 0.77, 82%, 3/4.","a":"3/4, 0.77, 4/5, 82%","sol":"Change every value to a decimal: 4/5 = 0.8, 0.77 stays as 0.77, 82% = 0.82 and 3/4 = 0.75. M1 for all four values correctly changed to decimals. Comparing, 0.75 < 0.77 < 0.8 < 0.82. M1 for the values correctly ordered. Writing the answer back in the forms given: 3/4, 0.77, 4/5, 82%. A1 for the correct order in the original forms (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Numbers in different forms cannot be compared directly.","Convert every value to a decimal, then compare them, then swap back into the forms the question gave.","The first line changes 4/5, 82% and 3/4 into decimals, leaving 0.77 as it is."],"codes":["2.04a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Farah says that 5/8 is bigger than 0.65. Use division to change 5/8 to a decimal, and decide whether Farah is correct, giving a reason.","a":"No, Farah is not correct","sol":"Divide the numerator by the denominator: 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625. M1 for 0.625 seen. Comparing, 0.625 is less than 0.65 (M1). Since 5/8 = 0.625 < 0.65, Farah is not correct. A1 for the correct conclusion with a comparison shown (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Farah's claim can be checked by putting both numbers in the same form.","Divide 5 by 8, and compare the decimal you get with 0.65.","The first line is 5 ÷ 8 carried out as a division."],"codes":["2.02a","2.04b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. After a 15% pay rise, Marcus's salary is £26,450 per year. Work out his salary before the rise.","a":"£23,000","sol":"A rise of 15% gives the multiplier 1 + 15/100 = 1.15. M1 for 1.15 identified as the multiplier. The salary before the rise multiplied by 1.15 gives £26,450, so divide to reverse it: 26450 ÷ 1.15 (M1). 26450 ÷ 1.15 = 23000. A1 for £23,000 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The final salary is the original salary after a single multiplication.","Work out the multiplier for the rise, then divide the final salary by it to reverse the change.","The first line is 100% + 15% = … %, written as a decimal."],"codes":["2.03c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A tank holds 4 1/2 litres of paint. Yuki uses 2/3 of the paint on a first coat, then uses 1/4 of what remains on touch-ups. Work out how much paint is left, giving your answer as a mixed number of litres, in its simplest form.","a":"1 1/8 litres","sol":"Write the total as an improper fraction: 4 1/2 = 9/2. M1 for 9/2. The first coat uses 2/3 of 9/2 = 2/3 × 9/2 = 3 litres. M1 for 3 litres used. The remaining paint is 9/2 − 3 = 3/2 litres. Touch-ups use 1/4 of 3/2 = 3/8 litres. M1 for 3/8 litres used on touch-ups. Paint left is 3/2 − 3/8 = 12/8 − 3/8 = 9/8 = 1 1/8 litres. A1 for 1 1/8 litres (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each fraction acts on whatever paint is left at that point, not on the original amount every time.","Work out how much the first coat uses and subtract it, then work out how much the touch-ups use from what remains.","The first line is 4 1/2 rewritten as the improper fraction 9/2."],"codes":["2.01b","2.01c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a school of 800 students, 3/8 study French. Of these students, 24% also study German. Work out how many students study both French and German.","a":"72","sol":"Find the number who study French: 3/8 of 800 = 800 ÷ 8 × 3 = 300. M1 for 300. Find 24% of that group: 0.24 × 300 (M1). 0.24 × 300 = 72. A1 for 72 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The percentage in this question acts on the French group, not on the whole school.","Find how many students study French first, then take the percentage of that number.","The first line is 800 ÷ 8 — that gives one eighth of the school."],"codes":["2.01c","2.03b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 3 1/4 as an improper fraction.","a":"13/4"},{"q":"Write 9/20 as a decimal.","a":"0.45"},{"q":"Write down the multiplier that decreases an amount by 8%.","a":"0.92"},{"q":"Write down the correct symbol, < or >, between 7/20 and 0.4.","a":"7/20 < 0.4"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Mixed number to improper fraction","back":"Multiply the whole number by the denominator and add the numerator, keeping the denominator: 2 1/2 = (2×2+1)/2 = 5/2.","code":"2.01a","open":false},{"front":"Adding and subtracting fractions","back":"Rewrite both fractions over a common denominator first, then add or subtract the numerators. Never add the tops and bottoms separately.","code":"2.01b","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying and dividing fractions","back":"To multiply, cancel then multiply the tops and the bottoms. To divide, multiply by the reciprocal of the second fraction (turn it upside down).","code":"2.01b","open":false},{"front":"A fraction as an operator","back":"'Of' means multiply: a/b of x = (x ÷ b) × a. For example, 3/4 of 96 = 96 ÷ 4 × 3 = 72.","code":"2.01c","open":false},{"front":"One quantity as a fraction of another","back":"Write the first quantity over the second and simplify: £6 out of £15 is 6/15 = 2/5.","code":"2.01c","open":false},{"front":"Terminating decimal to fraction, and back again","back":"Put the decimal digits over a power of 10, one zero per decimal place, then cancel: 0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8. Reverse it by dividing top by bottom.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Fraction to decimal by division","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator. If it does not terminate, write the repeating digit(s) with a dot over them: 1/6 = 0.16̇.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Adding, subtracting and multiplying decimals by hand","back":"Line up the decimal points to add or subtract. To multiply, ignore the points, multiply as whole numbers, then count the total decimal places back in.","code":"2.02b","open":false},{"front":"Give two ways to divide 0.72 by 0.06 without a calculator","back":"Multiply both numbers by 100 so the divisor is whole, then divide, since 72 ÷ 6 = 12; write it as the fraction 0.72/0.06 and cancel the same way; multiply both by 10 repeatedly until the divisor is whole.","code":"2.02c","open":true},{"front":"Converting between fraction, decimal and percentage","back":"Fraction to decimal: divide top by bottom. Decimal to percentage: ×100. Percentage to decimal: ÷100. Values above 100% are allowed, e.g. 1 1/2 = 150%.","code":"2.03a","open":false},{"front":"Percentage of an amount, and one amount as a percentage of another","back":"p% of x = (p ÷ 100) × x. To express one amount as a percentage of another, divide the first by the second and multiply by 100.","code":"2.03b","open":false},{"front":"The multiplier for a percentage increase or decrease","back":"Increase of p%: multiplier 1 + p/100. Decrease of p%: multiplier 1 − p/100. A 6% rise uses 1.06; an 18% fall uses 0.82.","code":"2.03c","open":false},{"front":"Reverse percentage: finding the original value","back":"Divide the new amount by the percentage-change multiplier, not by the percentage itself: original = new amount ÷ multiplier.","code":"2.03c","open":false},{"front":"Ordering numbers written in different forms","back":"Convert every value into one form, usually decimals, compare them there, then write the answer back in the forms the question used.","code":"2.04a","open":false},{"front":"The six comparison symbols =, ≠, <, >, ≤, ≥","back":"= equal to; ≠ not equal to; < less than; > greater than; ≤ less than or equal to; ≥ greater than or equal to. The symbol always points at the smaller value.","code":"2.04b","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Mixed number ↔ improper fraction (2.01a): multiply the whole number by the denominator and add the numerator. Recall: not in 6.02d and not printed on the Foundation tier formulae sheet.","Adding, subtracting, multiplying and dividing fractions (2.01b): common denominator to add or subtract; cancel then multiply tops and bottoms; multiply by the reciprocal to divide. Recall: not in 6.02d and not on the formulae sheet.","A fraction or a percentage as an operator (2.01c, 2.03b): a/b of x = (x ÷ b) × a; p% of x = (p ÷ 100) × x. Recall: not in 6.02d and not on the formulae sheet.","One quantity as a fraction or a percentage of another (2.01c, 2.03b): divide the first quantity by the second, then simplify or multiply by 100. Recall: not on the formulae sheet.","Terminating decimal ↔ fraction (2.02a): decimal digits over 10ⁿ, n being the number of decimal places, then cancel; or divide numerator by denominator the other way. Recall: not on the formulae sheet.","Adding, subtracting and multiplying decimals by hand (2.02b): line up the decimal points to add or subtract; multiply as whole numbers and place the point by counting total decimal places. Recall: not on the formulae sheet.","Dividing by a decimal (2.02c): multiply both the number being divided and the divisor by the same power of 10 until the divisor is a whole number, then divide. Recall: not on the formulae sheet.","Percentage change as a multiplier, and reversing it (2.03c): increase × (1 + p/100), decrease × (1 − p/100); original = new amount ÷ multiplier. Recall: not on the formulae sheet, and not the same result as compound interest, Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ — the Foundation tier sheet does print that, but it belongs to the ratio and growth room, not this one.","Ordering mixed forms, and the six comparison symbols <, >, ≤, ≥, =, ≠ (2.04a, 2.04b): convert to one form before comparing; the symbol points at the smaller value. Recall: not on the formulae sheet.","What governs on the day: 6.02d names only the circle, Pythagoras and trigonometry formulae as facts Foundation learners must know from memory; none of this room's conversions, operators or ordering methods appear there, and none of them appear on OCR's Foundation tier formulae sheet (issued alongside every paper since 2022) either, so every result above must be recalled and written from memory, never read off the insert."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:45:56.489Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged after the fixes: every one of the 15 questions, 6 worked examples, 4 check and 4 exit answers was re-solved with node and all are correct, including the reworded mixed-number asks (Q5 1 7/12, Q13 1 1/8 litres) and the rewritten Q7 3.075, Q8 0.2, Q10 3/4 < 0.77 < 4/5 < 82%, Q11 5/8 = 0.625 so Farah is wrong, Q12 26450 ÷ 1.15 = £23,000; mark tallies all add to m, difficulties are 5/7/3 with Q11 correctly demoted to d=2, 8 non-calculator questions are hand-workable, the paper facts (J560/01-06, 100 marks, 1 h 30, Papers 2 and 5 non-calculator, grades 5-1 and 9-4) are now in room.facts, example 2 step 2 does real division work and example 5 cue 2 no longer pre-empts the comparison, and the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md - publishable, with only the exit-ticket overlap worth tidying."}}},"fractions-decimals-percentages-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"fractions-decimals-percentages-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"OCR's Higher papers expect a student to move between a fraction and a recurring decimal in both directions and to know why some fractions terminate and others recur, since Paper 5 is non-calculator and asks for the exact fraction in lowest terms while Papers 4 and 6 expect a rounded calculator display to be read back correctly with dots. The short algebra of multiplying by a power of 10 and subtracting is a method mark the examiner looks for, so a bare answer scores nothing. The specification's own cross-reference also asks a Higher student to see that the common-denominator rule used for numeric fractions is the same rule that combines algebraic fractions, which the Algebraic fractions room develops further; both ideas feed directly into exact working throughout Number, Algebra and Ratio.","idea":[{"h":"Fractions with algebraic denominators","code":"2.01b","t":"Adding or subtracting numeric fractions always starts by writing both over a common denominator, and exactly the same idea carries over once letters appear in the denominator. To add 1/a + 1/b, write each fraction over the denominator ab: 1/a becomes b/(ab) and 1/b becomes a/(ab), so the sum is (a + b)/ab. When both denominators already share a common factor, such as 1/(2n) and 1/(3n), the lowest common denominator is smaller than the plain product — here it is 6n, not 6n² — so look for a shared factor before multiplying blindly. This room also covers denominators that are simple linear expressions with no common factor, such as (n − 1) and (n + 1): their product (n − 1)(n + 1), which expands to n² − 1, is the common denominator in exactly the same way. Denominators that need factorising first, such as an already-expanded quadratic, are developed fully in Algebraic fractions."},{"h":"Turning a recurring decimal into a fraction","code":"2.02a","t":"Call the number x and write out enough digits to see the repeat. Count the digits in the repeating block: one digit needs a multiplier of 10, two digits 100, three digits 1000 — in general 10ⁿ for a block of n digits. Multiplying by that power of 10 shifts the decimal along by exactly one block, so the two numbers have identical tails and subtracting clears the recurrence completely. That subtraction line is where the method mark falls. Divide by the whole number that is left and cancel to lowest terms. When the recurrence starts late, as in 0.16̇, first multiply by 10 to step past the digits that do not repeat, then multiply again to shift one block, and subtract those two multiples rather than the original. The same argument gives 0.9̇ = 1: 10x − x = 9, so 9x = 9 and x = 1 exactly."},{"h":"Turning a fraction into a recurring decimal","code":"2.02a","t":"Divide the numerator by the denominator by short division and watch the remainders. As soon as a remainder you have already had comes back, the digits from that point repeat for ever, so you can stop and write the block. Mark the repeat with a dot on the first and the last digit of the block: 0.2̇7̇ means 27 repeating and 0.41̇6̇ would mean 16 repeating after the 4. Whether a fraction recurs at all is settled by its denominator in lowest terms: if the only prime factors are 2 and 5 the decimal terminates, because the fraction can be scaled to a power of 10; any other prime factor, such as 3, 7 or 11, makes it recur. A block can never be longer than one less than the denominator, which is why sevenths repeat in blocks of six. On a calculator the display is rounded, so read the pattern from it and then write the dots yourself. "}],"examples":[{"title":"A two-digit block: 0.4̇5̇ as a fraction","setup":"The recurring decimal 0.4̇5̇ has the two digits 45 repeating for ever. Write it as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Let x = 0.454545…","The repeating block is two digits long, so multiply both sides by 100: 100x = 45.454545…","Subtract the original value to clear the recurrence: 100x − x = 45.454545… − 0.454545…, so 99x = 45","Divide both sides by 99: x = 45/99","Cancel using the common factor 9: x = 5/11"],"cues":["What has to be done with the decimal itself before any multiplying can begin?","Which power of 10 shifts the decimal along by exactly one block?","What happens to the endless tail when the two lines are subtracted?","What must both sides be divided by now?","What is the highest common factor of 45 and 99?"]},{"title":"Back the other way: 5/11 as a decimal","setup":"Write the fraction 5/11 as a decimal. Use short division, and give your answer with dots showing the digits that repeat.","steps":["Set out 5 ÷ 11: eleven does not go into five, so the answer begins 0. and the remainder is 5","Carry the remainder to make 50: 50 ÷ 11 = 4 remainder 6, so the first decimal digit is 4","Carry again to make 60: 60 ÷ 11 = 5 remainder 5, so the next decimal digit is 5","The remainder 5 has come back, so the pair of digits repeats for ever: 0.454545…","Put a dot on the first and last digit of the block: 0.4̇5̇"],"cues":["How many times does 11 go into 5?","What is the next number to divide by 11?","What is the remainder this time?","Why can the division be stopped at this point?","Which two digits carry the dots?"]},{"title":"A recurrence that starts late: 0.13̇","setup":"In the recurring decimal 0.13̇ only the 3 repeats. Write it as a fraction in its lowest terms.","steps":["Let x = 0.13333…","Multiply by 10 to step past the digit that does not repeat: 10x = 1.3333…","Multiply by 10 once more to shift one block along: 100x = 13.333…","Subtract these two lines, not the original: 100x − 10x = 13.333… − 1.3333…, so 90x = 12","Divide and cancel using the common factor 6: x = 12/90 = 2/15"],"cues":["This is another decimal to be turned into a fraction — what is done with it before any multiplying?","What does multiplying by 10 do to the position of the repeating digit?","Why is a second multiplication needed here?","Which two lines have exactly the same endless tail?","What is the highest common factor of 12 and 90?"]},{"title":"Which of these decimals will recur?","setup":"Three fractions are given: 7/8, 9/40 and 7/12. Decide for each one whether it terminates or recurs, then write each one as a decimal.","steps":["Factorise each denominator: 8 = 2³, 40 = 2³ × 5 and 12 = 2² × 3","Only 2s and 5s appear in 8 and in 40, so those two terminate; 12 has a prime factor of 3, so that one recurs","Divide for the terminating ones: 7 ÷ 8 = 0.875 and 9 ÷ 40 = 0.225","Divide for the last one: 7 ÷ 12 = 0.58333…, where only the 3 repeats, so 7/12 = 0.583̇"],"cues":["What are the prime factors of each denominator?","Which prime factors allow a fraction to be scaled to a power of 10?","What two divisions turn the terminating fractions into their decimals?","Which fraction is still to be turned into a decimal, and what notation will show its repeat?"]},{"title":"A recurring decimal used as an operator","setup":"A rope is 55 m long. A piece measuring 0.7̇2̇ of the whole rope is cut off, where the digits 72 repeat for ever. Work out the exact length of the piece cut off, in metres.","steps":["Let x = 0.727272… and multiply by 100 to shift one block: 100x = 72.727272…","Subtract: 100x − x gives 99x = 72, so x = 72/99","Cancel using the common factor 9: x = 8/11, so the piece is 8/11 of the 55 m rope","Take that fraction of the length: 8/11 × 55 = 8 × 5 = 40 m"],"cues":["How long is the repeating block, and which multiplier does it need?","What whole number is left on the right after subtracting?","What is the highest common factor of 72 and 99?","What does 55 ÷ 11 come to?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A recurring decimal has a three-digit repeating block. Which power of 10 do you multiply by?","a":"1000"},{"q":"Write 0.8̇1̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"9/11 (99x = 81, so x = 81/99 = 9/11)"},{"q":"Does 9/22 terminate or recur? Give the reason.","a":"It recurs, because 22 = 2 × 11 has the prime factor 11, which is neither 2 nor 5."},{"q":"Write 5/12 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.416̇ (5 ÷ 12 = 0.41666…)"}]},"room":{"facts":["To change a recurring decimal into a fraction, let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the repeating block, subtract the original, then divide and cancel.","A block of one digit gives ninths, two digits gives ninety-ninths and three digits gives 999ths: 0.ȧ = a/9, 0.ȧḃ = ab/99, 0.ȧbċ = abc/999.","When the recurrence starts late, multiply by 10 first to step past the digits that do not repeat, then by 10 again for each digit of the block, and subtract those two multiples: for 0.16̇, 100x − 10x = 15, so x = 15/90 = 1/6.","A fraction in its lowest terms gives a terminating decimal exactly when the only prime factors of its denominator are 2 and 5; any other prime factor makes the decimal recur.","Dot notation: a dot goes on the first and the last digit of the repeating block, so 0.2̇7̇ = 0.272727… and 0.41̇6̇ = 0.416161616…","0.9̇ = 1 exactly, because 10x − x = 9 gives 9x = 9.","The repeating block of 1/n can never be longer than n − 1 digits, which is why sevenths repeat in blocks of six: 1/7 = 0.1̇42857̇.","The common-denominator rule for numeric fractions carries over unchanged to fractions with algebraic denominators: 1/a + 1/b = (a + b)/ab, and denominators that share a factor combine to a smaller common denominator, as 1/(2n) + 1/(3n) = 5/(6n).","OCR's Higher tier of Mathematics J560 is assessed by three papers, J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 5 is the non-calculator paper; Higher tier grades run from 9 down to 4."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Add 3/p + 1/q, giving your answer as a single fraction.","a":"(3q + p)/(pq)","sol":"Write both fractions over the common denominator pq: 3/p = 3q/(pq) and 1/q = p/(pq). M1 for 3q/(pq) and p/(pq) (oe, for example stating the common denominator pq). Adding the numerators over that common denominator gives 3q/(pq) + p/(pq) = (3q + p)/(pq), which will not simplify further since p and q are unknown letters. A1 for (3q + p)/(pq) (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is added the same way as any two fractions — think about what both denominators need to become before they can be combined.","Multiply each fraction, top and bottom, by the letter missing from its own denominator, so both fractions share one denominator.","The first line is: 3/p = 3q/(pq) and 1/q = p/(pq)."],"codes":["2.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Which is larger, 0.3̇4̇ or 1/3? Show working to justify your answer.","a":"0.3̇4̇ is larger","sol":"Let x = 0.343434… . The block is two digits long, so 100x = 34.343434… , and subtracting gives 100x − x = 99x = 34, so x = 34/99. M1 for x = 34/99 (oe). Writing 1/3 with the same denominator gives 1/3 = 33/99. Since 34/99 > 33/99, 0.3̇4̇ is larger than 1/3. A1 for the correct comparison and conclusion (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Turning the recurring decimal into a fraction is the first step before any comparison can be made.","Once 0.3̇4̇ is written as a fraction over 99, rewrite 1/3 with the same denominator to compare directly.","The first line is: let x = 0.343434…, then write down 100x."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 0.2̇59̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"7/27","sol":"The repeating block 259 is three digits long, so multiply by 1000: let x = 0.259259…, then 1000x = 259.259259… M1 for 1000x = 259.259259… and the subtraction 1000x − x. Subtracting clears the tail: 1000x − x = 999x = 259, so x = 259/999. Dividing top and bottom by their common factor 37 gives x = 7/27. A1 for 7/27 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Give the decimal a name and work out how many digits are in the repeating block.","Multiply by the power of 10 that shifts the decimal along by exactly one block, then subtract the original.","The first line is: let x = 0.259259…, then write down 1000x."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 7/20 is equal to a terminating decimal by writing it with a denominator that is a power of 10.","a":"0.35","sol":"20 = 4 × 5, so multiplying numerator and denominator by 5 gives a denominator of 100: 7/20 = 35/100. M1 for 35/100 (oe, for example recognising 20 × 5 = 100). Writing this as a decimal gives 0.35, which terminates because the denominator became an exact power of 10. A1 for 0.35 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A denominator that is a factor of a power of 10 can always be scaled up to become one.","Work out what 20 must be multiplied by to reach 100, then multiply the numerator by the same amount.","The first line is: 20 × 5 = 100, so 7/20 = 35/100."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify 1/(4n) + 1/(6n) as a single fraction in its simplest form.","a":"5/(12n)","sol":"The two denominators 4n and 6n share the factor 2n, so the lowest common denominator is 12n rather than 24n². M1 for identifying the common denominator 12n (oe). Writing each fraction over 12n: 1/(4n) = 3/(12n) and 1/(6n) = 2/(12n). M1 for 3/(12n) and 2/(12n) (oe). Adding the numerators gives 3/(12n) + 2/(12n) = 5/(12n), which has no common factor left to cancel. A1 for 5/(12n) (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both denominators are multiples of the same letter, so the lowest common denominator is smaller than their plain product.","Find the lowest common multiple of 4n and 6n, then rewrite each fraction with that denominator before adding the numerators.","The first line is: the common denominator is 12n, so 1/(4n) = 3/(12n) and 1/(6n) = 2/(12n)."],"codes":["2.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify 1/(n − 1) + n/(n + 1) as a single fraction in its simplest form.","a":"(n² + 1)/(n² − 1)","sol":"The two denominators share no common factor, so the lowest common denominator is their product (n − 1)(n + 1). M1 for identifying the common denominator (n − 1)(n + 1) (oe, for example n² − 1). Writing each fraction over that denominator: 1/(n − 1) = (n + 1)/((n − 1)(n + 1)) and n/(n + 1) = n(n − 1)/((n − 1)(n + 1)). M1 for both numerators rewritten correctly (oe). Expanding the second numerator: n(n − 1) = n² − n. M1 for n² − n (oe). Adding the numerators gives (n + 1) + (n² − n) = n² + 1, so the sum is (n² + 1)/(n² − 1), which will not cancel since n² + 1 and n² − 1 share no common factor. A1 for (n² + 1)/(n² − 1) (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two denominators share no letter or number in common, so think about what their lowest common denominator must be.","Multiply each fraction, top and bottom, by whichever bracket its own denominator is missing, then expand the numerator that has brackets in it.","The first line is: the common denominator is (n − 1)(n + 1), so 1/(n − 1) = (n + 1)/((n − 1)(n + 1)) and n/(n + 1) = n(n − 1)/((n − 1)(n + 1))."],"codes":["2.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write 7/30 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.23̇","sol":"Divide 7 by 30 by short division: 70 ÷ 30 = 2 remainder 10, then 100 ÷ 30 = 3 remainder 10 — the remainder 10 has come back straight away, so only the 3 repeats. M1 for 7 ÷ 30 = 0.2333… (oe, the division shown). A dot is needed only on the repeating digit: 0.23̇. A1 for 0.23̇ (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Divide the numerator by the denominator and keep track of the remainders.","The first digit after the point need not repeat — check whether the remainder that produces it ever comes back.","The first line is 7 ÷ 30 by short division, continued to at least two decimal places."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 0.9̇ = 1.","a":"9x = 9, so x = 1","sol":"Let x = 0.9999… . The block is one digit, so 10x = 9.9999… . M1 for 10x = 9.9999… and the subtraction 10x − x. The endless tails are identical, so they cancel: 10x − x = 9.9999… − 0.9999… gives 9x = 9. Dividing by 9, x = 1, so 0.9̇ = 1 exactly. A1 for a complete argument ending in x = 1 (total 2 marks). The result must be reached by working, since the answer is printed in the question.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Treat it like any other recurring decimal and give it a name, even though the answer is already printed.","Multiply by 10 and subtract the original, then read off what is left on each side.","The first line is: let x = 0.9999…, then write down 10x."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A charity spends 4/15 of its budget on outreach and 3/15 on admin. Is the total fraction spent on outreach and admin combined greater than or less than 0.4̇? Show working to justify your answer.","a":"Greater than 0.4̇ (7/15 = 21/45 > 20/45 = 4/9 = 0.4̇)","sol":"Adding the fractions first: 4/15 + 3/15 = 7/15. M1 for 7/15 (oe). Converting 0.4̇ to a fraction: let x = 0.4444…, so 10x − x = 9x = 4, giving x = 4/9. M1 for 4/9 (oe). Writing both fractions over a common denominator of 45: 7/15 = 21/45 and 4/9 = 20/45. Since 21/45 > 20/45, the total fraction spent is greater than 0.4̇. A1 for the correct comparison and conclusion (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Add the two fractions of the budget together, and separately turn 0.4̇ into a fraction.","Once both amounts are fractions, rewrite them with the same denominator to compare directly.","The first line is 4/15 + 3/15 = 7/15 — now let x = 0.4444… and find 10x − x."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out 0.4̇ + 0.3̇1̇, giving your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"25/33","sol":"For the first: let x = 0.4444…, so 10x − x = 9x = 4 and 0.4̇ = 4/9. M1 for 4/9. For the second: let y = 0.313131…, so 100y − y = 99y = 31 and 0.3̇1̇ = 31/99. M1 for 31/99. A common denominator of 99 gives 4/9 = 44/99, so the sum is 44/99 + 31/99 = 75/99. M1 for 44/99 + 31/99 (oe). Dividing the numerator and denominator by 3 gives 25/33. A1 for 25/33 (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Recurring decimals cannot be added reliably in decimal form — the tails go on for ever.","Turn each decimal into a fraction by its own multiply-and-subtract, then add the fractions in the usual way.","The first line is: let x = 0.4444… and let y = 0.313131…, then write down 10x and 100y."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Show that 0.5̇4̇ ÷ 0.1̇8̇ = 3.","a":"6/11 ÷ 2/11 = 3","sol":"First decimal: let x = 0.545454…, so 100x − x = 99x = 54 and x = 54/99 = 6/11. M1 for 54/99 or 6/11. Second decimal: let y = 0.181818…, so 100y − y = 99y = 18 and y = 18/99 = 2/11. M1 for 18/99 or 2/11. Dividing means multiplying by the reciprocal: 6/11 ÷ 2/11 = 6/11 × 11/2. M1 for the inversion. This equals 66/22 = 3, as required. A1 for a complete argument reaching 3 (total 4 marks). Since the answer is printed, every line must be shown.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Division of two endless decimals is only safe once both are exact fractions.","Convert each decimal separately, then divide the fractions by multiplying by the reciprocal of the second.","The first line is: let x = 0.545454… and let y = 0.181818…, then write down 100x and 100y."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In a box, the number of red counters and the number of blue counters are in the ratio 0.3̇ : 0.5̇. Write this ratio in its simplest form, using whole numbers.","a":"3 : 5","sol":"Each part is converted first: 9x = 3 gives 0.3̇ = 3/9 = 1/3, and 9y = 5 gives 0.5̇ = 5/9. M1 for 1/3 and 5/9 (oe). Written over a common denominator of 9 the ratio is 3/9 : 5/9. M1 for 3/9 : 5/9 (oe, for example multiplying both parts of the ratio by 9). Multiplying both parts by 9 clears the fractions and leaves 3 : 5, which has no common factor. A1 for 3 : 5 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A ratio is only simplified once both parts are whole numbers, so deal with the decimals first.","Change each recurring decimal into a fraction, put both over the same denominator, then scale the ratio up until both parts are whole.","The first line is: let x = 0.3333… and let y = 0.5555…, then write down 10x and 10y."],"codes":["2.02a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A storage tank is completely full of water. A tap is opened and 0.6̇ of the water drains away. If 26 litres of water remain in the tank, work out how many litres the tank held when full.","a":"78 litres","sol":"Let x = 0.6666… ; multiplying by 10 and subtracting gives 10x − x = 9x = 6, so x = 6/9 = 2/3 of the water drained away. M1 for 2/3 (oe). The fraction remaining is 1 − 2/3 = 1/3. M1 for 1/3 (oe). If 1/3 of the full volume is 26 litres, the full volume is 26 × 3. M1 for 26 × 3 (oe). This gives 78 litres. A1 for 78 litres (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work out the exact fraction of the water that drained away first.","The 26 litres that remain are the fraction of the tank that was NOT drained — find that fraction before using it.","The first line is: let x = 0.6666…, then write down 10x to find the drained fraction as 2/3."],"codes":["2.02a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 0.8̇ as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"8/9"},{"q":"What is the lowest common denominator of 1/m and 1/n?","a":"mn"},{"q":"Write 4/33 as a recurring decimal, using dot notation.","a":"0.1̇2̇"},{"q":"What is the longest possible number of digits in the repeating block of 1/13?","a":"12 (13 − 1)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Recurring decimal","back":"A decimal that never stops, in which a block of one or more digits repeats for ever, such as 0.272727… . Every recurring decimal is equal to an exact fraction.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Dot notation for a repeating block","back":"A dot sits on the first and the last digit of the block: 0.2̇7̇ = 0.272727…, 0.4̇ = 0.4444… and 0.41̇6̇ = 0.4161616…","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Method for changing a recurring decimal into a fraction","back":"Let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the block, subtract the original to clear the tail, divide, then cancel.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"The reason subtracting clears the recurrence","back":"Multiplying by 10ⁿ shifts the decimal along by exactly one block, so both lines have identical endless tails and subtracting removes the recurrence completely.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"The fraction for a one-digit repeating block","back":"0.ȧ = a/9, so 0.7̇ = 7/9 and 0.3̇ = 3/9 = 1/3.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"The fraction for a two-digit repeating block","back":"0.ȧḃ = ab/99, so 0.2̇4̇ = 24/99 = 8/33. For a three-digit block the denominator is 999.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Converting a decimal whose repeat starts late","back":"Multiply by 10 for each digit before the block, then again by 10ⁿ for the block, and subtract those two multiples: for 0.16̇, 100x − 10x = 15, so x = 1/6.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"The value of 0.9̇","back":"Exactly 1. Let x = 0.9999… ; then 10x − x = 9, so 9x = 9 and x = 1.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Changing a fraction into a decimal","back":"Divide the numerator by the denominator by short division, watching the remainders; the digits repeat from the point where a remainder comes back.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Test for a terminating decimal","back":"In lowest terms, a fraction terminates exactly when the only prime factors of its denominator are 2 and 5, because only then can it be scaled to a power of 10.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Test for a recurring decimal","back":"In lowest terms, any prime factor of the denominator other than 2 or 5 — such as 3, 7 or 11 — makes the decimal recur. So 7/12 recurs and 7/8 does not.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Longest possible repeating block of 1/n","back":"At most n − 1 digits, since only n − 1 different non-zero remainders are available. Sevenths take the full six digits: 1/7 = 0.1̇42857̇.","code":"2.02a","open":false},{"front":"Adding fractions with a variable denominator","back":"Use a common denominator exactly as with numeric fractions: 1/a + 1/b = b/(ab) + a/(ab) = (a + b)/ab.","code":"2.01b","open":false},{"front":"Adding fractions with binomial denominators","back":"Denominators like (n − 1) and (n + 1) with no shared factor combine by multiplying the brackets: their product (n − 1)(n + 1) = n² − 1 is the common denominator, exactly as with numeric fractions.","code":"2.01b","open":false},{"front":"Lowest common denominator when both denominators share a letter","back":"Look for a shared factor before multiplying the denominators together: 2n and 3n have lowest common denominator 6n, not 6n².","code":"2.01b","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Recurring decimal to fraction: let x be the decimal, multiply by 10ⁿ where n is the number of digits in the repeating block, subtract the original, divide and cancel — for 0.4̇5̇, 99x = 45 so x = 5/11. Recall: this is a method, not a printed formula; 6.02d does not list it and it is not on the Higher tier formulae sheet.","One-, two- and three-digit blocks: 0.ȧ = a/9, 0.ȧḃ = ab/99, 0.ȧbċ = abc/999. Recall: not in 6.02d and not on the Higher tier formulae sheet.","A repeat that starts late: multiply by 10 once for each digit before the block, then by 10ⁿ for the block, and subtract those two multiples — for 0.16̇, 100x − 10x = 15 gives x = 1/6. Recall.","0.9̇ = 1. Recall: a result derived from 10x − x = 9, not a formula anyone prints.","Fraction to decimal: numerator ÷ denominator by short division; the digits repeat from the point where a remainder returns. Recall.","Terminating or recurring: in lowest terms, the decimal terminates exactly when the denominator's only prime factors are 2 and 5; any other prime factor makes it recur. Recall.","Recurring and terminating decimals are exactly the rational numbers: every fraction gives a decimal that terminates or recurs, and every terminating or recurring decimal can be written as a fraction. Recall: 6.02d lists no formula for this room and the Higher tier formulae sheet prints nothing for it, so every item on this sheet must be recalled or derived on the day.","Dot notation: one dot marks a single repeating digit, as 0.83̇ = 0.8333…, and a pair of dots marks the first and the last digit of a repeating block, as 0.1̇23̇ = 0.123123… . Recall: notation is never printed on a formulae sheet.","Arithmetic with recurring decimals: change each decimal to a fraction first, then add, subtract, multiply or divide the fractions — 0.2̇ + 0.1̇5̇ = 2/9 + 15/99 = 22/99 + 15/99 = 37/99. Recall.","Block length: dividing by n can leave only the remainders 1 to n − 1, so the repeating part of 1/n is never longer than n − 1 digits — 1/7 = 0.1̇42857̇ reaches that limit with six. Recall.","Adding fractions with algebraic denominators: write each fraction over a common denominator exactly as with numbers — 1/a + 1/b = (a + b)/ab, and brackets with no shared factor, such as (n − 1) and (n + 1), combine the same way by multiplying the brackets. Recall: this common-denominator rule is not a printed formula; only a denominator that needs factorising first is developed in Algebraic fractions, not on any formulae sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:10:26.462Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit: I re-solved all 13 questions, 5 worked examples, 4 check and 4 exit answers independently (node for the arithmetic, sympy for 3/p + 1/q, 1/(4n) + 1/(6n) and 1/(n - 1) + n/(n + 1)) and every key is correct, including the new 7/27 = 0.2̇59̇, 7/30 = 0.23̇, 7/15 = 0.46̇, 34/99 > 1/3, 7/20 = 35/100 = 0.35, (n² + 1)/(n² - 1) and the reverse operator 26 ÷ 1/3 = 78 litres; every sol tally adds to m, difficulties split 5/3/5 with no d=1 above 2 marks, 11 of the 13 questions are marked (non-calculator) and all are hand-workable, room.facts now carries OCR’s Higher paper numbers and the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md exactly (nothing in this room is on the Higher sheet, every item marked “Recall”) - publishable once lesson.idea[0] stops disowning the (n - 1) question the kit now asks."}}},"indices-surds":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"indices-surds","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Indices and surds sit underneath almost every other room in this course: the same three laws that simplify 3.01c power expressions are what make algebraic fractions, growth and expressions in mensuration work, and standard form is how OCR sets very large and very small measurements in science-flavoured contexts on every paper. Papers 2 and 5 are non-calculator, so a written method for negative indices, root arithmetic and standard form calculations earns the method marks even when the final value is small; on Papers 1, 3, 4 and 6 the calculator's own index display has to be read and written out properly, never copied straight off the screen. 'Exactly' turns up across the whole specification, and the fraction and π rules taught here are what keep an answer exact instead of a rounded approximation that loses the accuracy mark.","idea":[{"h":"Index notation and negative indices","t":"A power aⁿ is a written as a base a multiplied by itself n times, so 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 is written 2⁴. A negative index does not mean a negative number: it means a reciprocal, so a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ, and 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25. Reading index notation both ways — building it from repeated multiplication, and unpicking a negative index into a fraction — is tested as a 'write down' question, where the answer alone carries the mark, so accuracy in the notation itself matters.","code":"3.01a"},{"h":"Calculating powers and roots, and recognising powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5","t":"A power is worked out by repeated multiplication and a root undoes it: √49 = 7 because 7² = 49, and ∛125 = 5 because 5³ = 125. You are expected to recognise the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5 on sight, so 27 can be read straight away as 3³ and 64 as either 2⁶, 4³ or 8². On a non-calculator paper a root has to be calculated exactly rather than estimated, so a number is tested against the powers you recognise until one of them fits, such as spotting that 169 is 13² before writing √169 = 13.","code":"3.01b"},{"h":"The laws of indices","t":"Three laws govern powers of the same base: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, and (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ. They apply just as much to a letter as to a number, so p⁷ × p⁴ = p¹¹ and (r³)⁴ = r¹² by exactly the same reasoning as 3⁵ × 3⁻² = 3³. On a non-calculator paper the line that names which law is being used, and adds or multiplies the indices correctly, is where the method mark sits — a bare final power with no working shown can lose it.","code":"3.01c"},{"h":"Standard form: interpreting, ordering and converting","t":"Standard form writes a number as A × 10ⁿ, with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; n is positive for a number of 10 or more and negative for a number smaller than 1. To convert a large ordinary number, count how many places the decimal point moves to land between 1 and 10, and that count is n; for a small number the point moves the other way and n is negative, so 0.00045 = 4.5 × 10⁻⁴. Two numbers already in standard form are compared by their index first — the one with the larger index is the larger number, whatever its front number is — and only by the front number once the indices are equal.","code":"3.02a"},{"h":"Calculating with numbers in standard form","t":"On a calculator paper, a display such as 4.5⁻⁰⁴ has to be read as 4.5 × 10⁻⁴ and written out that way, never copied straight from the screen. To multiply or divide numbers in standard form, the front numbers and the powers of ten are handled separately — multiply or divide the front numbers, and add or subtract the indices of ten — then the front number is adjusted back into the range 1 ≤ A < 10 if it has drifted out of it. Foundation tier learners also do this without a calculator, and to add or subtract two standard form numbers they must first be rewritten with the same power of ten before the front numbers are combined.","code":"3.02b"},{"h":"Exact calculations with fractions and multiples of π","t":"The instruction 'exactly' rules out a rounded decimal: a fraction answer is left in its lowest terms, and a circle answer is left as a multiple of π, such as 9π cm² rather than 28.3 cm². Fractions are added and subtracted over a common denominator, multiplied by cancelling then multiplying tops and bottoms, and divided by multiplying by the reciprocal, all without a calculator. Multiplying a multiple of π by a fraction only touches the number in front of the π, so 2/3 × 9π = 6π, with the π carried through untouched at every step.","code":"3.03a"}],"examples":[{"title":"Writing a repeated product as a power, and a negative index as a fraction","setup":"A number is written as the product 6 × 6 × 6 × 6 × 6. Write this product as a power of 6, and then use that same base to write 6⁻¹ as a single fraction.","steps":["Five equal factors of 6 multiplied together give the index form 6⁵.","A negative index turns the base into a reciprocal, so 6⁻¹ means 1 over 6 to the power 1.","Written as a single fraction, 6⁻¹ = 1/6.","Collecting both results: the product is 6⁵, and 6⁻¹ written as a single fraction is 1/6."],"cues":["How many times does 6 appear as a factor in the product?","What does a negative index tell you to do with the base?","What single fraction does 1 over 6¹ simplify to?","What are the two results now that both parts are worked out?"]},{"title":"Working out powers, square roots and cube roots","setup":"Work out (a) 12³, (b) √196, (c) ∛216, and (d) √225.","steps":["12³ means 12 multiplied by itself three times: 12 × 12 × 12 = 1728.","√196 asks which number squares to give 196, and 14 × 14 = 196, so √196 = 14.","∛216 asks which number cubes to give 216, and 6 × 6 × 6 = 216, so ∛216 = 6.","√225 asks which number squares to give 225, and 15 × 15 = 225, so √225 = 15.","Collecting all four results together: 12³ = 1728, √196 = 14, ∛216 = 6 and √225 = 15."],"cues":["What does raising 12 to the power 3 mean?","Which number multiplied by itself gives 196?","Which number multiplied by itself three times gives 216?","Which recognised square gives 225?","What are the four separate results now that every part is worked out?"]},{"title":"Simplifying algebraic powers with the three laws of indices","setup":"Simplify each of the following, giving each answer as a single power: (a) p⁷ × p⁴, (b) q¹² ÷ q⁵, (c) (r³)⁴.","steps":["Multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices: p⁷ × p⁴ = p⁷⁺⁴ = p¹¹.","Dividing powers of the same base subtracts the indices: q¹² ÷ q⁵ = q¹²⁻⁵ = q⁷.","Raising a power to a power multiplies the indices: (r³)⁴ = r³ˣ⁴ = r¹²."],"cues":["What happens to the indices when two powers of the same base are multiplied?","What happens to the indices when one power is divided by another?","What happens to the indices when a power is itself raised to a power?"]},{"title":"Converting between an ordinary number and standard form","setup":"Convert 68 000 to standard form, and convert 5.02 × 10⁻⁴ to an ordinary number.","steps":["68 000 has to be written as a number between 1 and 10 multiplied by a power of 10: moving the point four places gives 6.8.","That is four places, so 68 000 = 6.8 × 10⁴.","For 5.02 × 10⁻⁴, a negative index moves the digits down the place-value columns the same number of places as the index shows: four this time.","Moving the point four places gives 5.02 × 10⁻⁴ = 0.000502."],"cues":["What single digit must stand before the decimal point in standard form?","What power of 10 matches a shift of four places, giving 68 000 in full standard form?","Which way do the digits move for a negative index?","So what ordinary number does 5.02 × 10⁻⁴ give?"]},{"title":"Multiplying numbers in standard form without a calculator","setup":"Without a calculator, work out (6 × 10⁶) × (3 × 10⁻²), giving the answer in standard form.","steps":["Multiply the front numbers separately from the powers of ten: 6 × 3 = 18.","Multiply the powers of ten by adding the indices: 10⁶ × 10⁻² = 10⁶⁺⁽⁻²⁾ = 10⁴.","That gives 18 × 10⁴, and since the front number must be between 1 and 10 it is adjusted to 1.8 × 10⁵."],"cues":["Which two parts of the calculation can be worked out separately?","What happens to the indices of ten when two powers of ten are multiplied?","Is the front number already between 1 and 10, or does it still need adjusting?"]},{"title":"An exact fraction sum and an exact circle in terms of π","setup":"Work out exactly (a) 1/3 + 1/5, giving the answer as a single fraction, and (b) the circumference and the area of a circle of radius 6 cm, giving each as a multiple of π.","steps":["The lowest common denominator of 3 and 5 is 15, so 1/3 = 5/15 and 1/5 = 3/15.","Adding gives 5/15 + 3/15 = 8/15, which cannot be cancelled any further.","The circumference is 2πr = 2 × π × 6 = 12π cm.","The area is πr² = π × 6² = 36π cm².","Collecting all three results: 1/3 + 1/5 = 8/15, the circumference is 12π cm and the area is 36π cm²."],"cues":["What must the two fractions be rewritten over before they can be added?","Does the resulting fraction cancel any further?","Which circle formula uses the radius once, not squared?","Which circle formula uses the radius squared?","What are the three results now that every part is worked out?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 8⁻¹ as a fraction.","a":"1/8"},{"q":"Simplify m⁵ × m³.","a":"m⁸"},{"q":"Write 730 000 in standard form.","a":"7.3 × 10⁵"},{"q":"Work out 1/4 + 1/6, giving your answer as a single fraction.","a":"5/12"},{"q":"Give the area of a circle of radius 2 cm as an exact multiple of π.","a":"4π cm²"},{"q":"Which is larger, 4.6 × 10⁵ or 5.1 × 10⁴?","a":"4.6 × 10⁵"}]},"room":{"facts":["The three laws of indices: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ; (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ.","A negative index means a reciprocal: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ.","A root is calculated exactly by testing against the powers you recognise: √169 = 13 because 13² = 169, and ∛216 = 6 because 6³ = 216.","The powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5 up to 2⁸ = 256, 3⁵ = 243, 4⁴ = 256 and 5⁴ = 625 are recognised on sight.","Standard form is A × 10ⁿ, with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer; n is negative for a number smaller than 1.","To multiply or divide numbers in standard form: multiply or divide the front numbers, add or subtract the indices of 10, then adjust the front number back into range.","'Exactly' means a fraction left in its lowest terms or an answer left as a multiple of π, never a rounded decimal.","OCR GCSE (9–1) Mathematics J560 is assessed by three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes: J560/01–03 (Foundation, grades 5–1) and J560/04–06 (Higher, grades 9–4). Paper 2 and Paper 5 are non-calculator; Papers 1, 3, 4 and 6 allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) (a) Write 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 in index form. (b) Write 8⁻² as a fraction.","a":"(a) 3⁵   (b) 1/64","sol":"Five equal factors of 3 multiplied together give the index form 3⁵ (B1). A negative index means a reciprocal, so 8⁻² = 1/8² = 1/64 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One part builds index notation from a repeated product; the other turns a negative index into a fraction.","Count how many times 3 is multiplied together for part (a); turn the base and index into a reciprocal for part (b).","Part (a) starts by counting the five factors of 3; part (b) starts from 1 over 8²."],"codes":["3.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) (a) Work out 9³. (b) Work out the cube root of 64.","a":"(a) 729   (b) 4","sol":"9 × 9 × 9 = 729 (B1). The cube root asks which number multiplied by itself three times gives 64, and 4 × 4 × 4 = 64, so the cube root is 4 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One part is a power, the other is a root that undoes a power.","For (a) multiply the base by itself three times; for (b) find the number that cubes to give 64.","Part (a) starts from 9 × 9; part (b) starts by testing 4 × 4 × 4."],"codes":["3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify (a) t⁶ × t², (b) w¹⁰ ÷ w⁴.","a":"(a) t⁸   (b) w⁶","sol":"Multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices: t⁶ × t² = t⁶⁺² = t⁸ (B1). Dividing powers of the same base subtracts the indices: w¹⁰ ÷ w⁴ = w¹⁰⁻⁴ = w⁶ (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each part is one of the three laws of indices.","One operation on the indices matches multiplying the powers, a different one matches dividing them.","Part (a) starts from t⁶⁺²; part (b) starts from w¹⁰⁻⁴."],"codes":["3.01c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A country's population is 2 630 000. (a) Write this population in standard form. (b) A bacterium has mass 3.8 × 10⁻⁹ g. Write this mass as an ordinary number, in grams.","a":"(a) 2.63 × 10⁶   (b) 0.0000000038 g","sol":"Moving the point six places gives a front number between 1 and 10, so 2 630 000 = 2.63 × 10⁶ (B1). A negative index moves the digits down the place-value columns by that many places, so 3.8 × 10⁻⁹ = 0.0000000038 g (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts convert between an ordinary number and A × 10ⁿ.","Count how many places the decimal point has to move for part (a); move it the same number of places, in the direction the negative index gives, for part (b).","Part (a) starts by counting how many places the point moves from 2.63; part (b) starts by counting nine places from 3.8."],"codes":["3.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A tank is 2/7 full of water. A delivery adds another 1/3 of a full tank. What fraction of the tank is now full, giving the answer as a single fraction?","a":"13/21","sol":"The lowest common denominator of 7 and 3 is 21, so 2/7 = 6/21 and 1/3 = 7/21 (M1). Adding gives 6/21 + 7/21 = 13/21, which cannot be cancelled further (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Fractions can only be added once they are counted in pieces of the same size.","Find the lowest common denominator of 7 and 3 and rewrite both fractions over it.","The first line rewrites 2/7 and 1/3 over the denominator 21."],"codes":["3.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out (a) 3⁻² as a fraction, (b) (2³)².","a":"(a) 1/9   (b) 64","sol":"A negative index means a reciprocal, so 3⁻² = 1/3² = 1/9 (B1). Raising a power to a power multiplies the indices, so (2³)² = 2³ˣ² = 2⁶ (M1) = 64 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One part is a negative index, the other is a power raised to a power.","Turn the negative index into a reciprocal for (a); multiply the two indices together for (b), then work out the resulting power.","Part (a) starts from 1 over 3²; part (b) starts from 2³ˣ²."],"codes":["3.01a","3.01c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write (a) 243 as a power of 3, (b) 125 as a power of 5.","a":"(a) 3⁵   (b) 5³","sol":"3 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 = 243, so 243 = 3⁵ (B1). 5 × 5 × 5 = 125, so 125 = 5³ (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each of these is one of the powers of 3 or 5 you are expected to recognise on sight.","Test repeated multiplication by the stated base until it reaches the target number, and count how many times the base was used.","The first lines are 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 × 3 and 5 × 5 × 5."],"codes":["3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A courier delivers 8 × 10³ parcels in a year. Each parcel weighs 5 × 10⁻² tonnes. Without a calculator, work out the total weight of all the parcels, in tonnes, giving your answer in standard form.","a":"4 × 10² tonnes","sol":"Multiply the front numbers: 8 × 5 = 40 (M1). Multiply the powers of ten by adding the indices: 10³ × 10⁻² = 10³⁺⁽⁻²⁾ = 10¹ (M1). That gives 40 × 10¹, and since the front number must lie between 1 and 10 it is adjusted to 4 × 10² tonnes (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The front numbers and the powers of ten can be dealt with separately.","Multiply the front numbers, add the indices of ten, then check whether the front number still lies between 1 and 10 and adjust the index if it does not.","The first line is 8 × 5 alongside 10³ × 10⁻²."],"codes":["3.02b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. (a) Use a calculator to work out (5.4 × 10⁷) ÷ (2.4 × 10⁻³), giving your answer in standard form. (b) A different calculation shows the display 8.1⁻⁰³. Write this display as an ordinary number.","a":"(a) 2.25 × 10¹⁰   (b) 0.0081","sol":"Divide the front numbers: 5.4 ÷ 2.4 = 2.25 (M1). Divide the powers of ten by subtracting the indices: 10⁷ ÷ 10⁻³ = 10⁷⁻⁽⁻³⁾ = 10¹⁰ (M1). Putting them together gives 2.25 × 10¹⁰, already in standard form (A1). The raised digits on a calculator display are the index of ten, so 8.1⁻⁰³ means 8.1 × 10⁻³, which written out is 0.0081 (B1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Part (a) is a division in standard form; part (b) is about reading, not calculating.","For (a), divide the front numbers and subtract the indices, taking care with the negative index; for (b), the raised digits on the display are the power of ten.","Part (a) starts from 5.4 ÷ 2.4 alongside 10⁷ ÷ 10⁻³; part (b) starts by reading 8.1⁻⁰³ as 8.1 × 10⁻³."],"codes":["3.02a","3.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circular table has diameter 20 cm. Give (a) its circumference and (b) its area exactly, as multiples of π.","a":"(a) 20π cm   (b) 100π cm²","sol":"Diameter 20 cm means the radius is 20 ÷ 2 = 10 cm (B1). Circumference = 2πr = 2 × π × 10 = 20π cm (M1, A1). Area = πr² = π × 10² = 100π cm² (B1).","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Leaving an answer 'exactly' means the π is never multiplied out.","Find the radius from the diameter first, then substitute it into the circumference formula and the area formula, stopping once the π is in place.","The first line finds the radius from the diameter; then substitute that radius into 2 × π × r for (a) and π × r² for (b)."],"codes":["3.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out exactly (a) 3/4 × 2/9, (b) 5/6 ÷ 1/3, giving each answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"(a) 1/6   (b) 5/2 (or 2 1/2)","sol":"Multiplying tops and bottoms: 3/4 × 2/9 = 6/36, which cancels to 1/6 (B1). Dividing by a fraction means multiplying by its reciprocal: 5/6 ÷ 1/3 = 5/6 × 3/1 = 15/6 (M1), which cancels to 5/2 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One part is a product of fractions, the other a division.","Multiply tops and bottoms and cancel for (a); turn the division into a multiplication by the reciprocal for (b).","Part (a) starts from 3 × 2 over 4 × 9; part (b) starts from 5/6 × 3/1."],"codes":["3.03a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Light travels at 3 × 10⁸ metres per second. The Sun is 1.5 × 10¹¹ metres from Earth. (a) Work out how long light takes to travel from the Sun to Earth, in seconds, giving your answer in standard form. (b) A nearby star is 100 times further from Earth than the Sun is. Work out the star's distance from Earth in standard form.","a":"(a) 5 × 10² s   (b) 1.5 × 10¹³ m","sol":"Time = distance ÷ speed, so divide the front numbers: 1.5 ÷ 3 = 0.5 (M1). Divide the powers of ten: 10¹¹ ÷ 10⁸ = 10³ (M1). That gives 0.5 × 10³, adjusted to 5 × 10² s since the front number must be between 1 and 10 (A1). Multiplying by 100 = 10² adds 2 to the index without changing the front number: 1.5 × 10¹¹ × 10² = 1.5 × 10¹³ m (M1, A1).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Part (a) links speed, distance and time; part (b) is a straight multiplication by a power of ten.","For (a), divide distance by speed, working on the front numbers and the powers of ten separately; for (b), multiplying by 100 is the same as multiplying by 10².","Part (a) starts from 1.5 ÷ 3 alongside 10¹¹ ÷ 10⁸; part (b) starts from 1.5 × 10¹¹ × 10²."],"codes":["3.02a","3.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A rectangle has length 2² cm and width 2⁻⁵ cm. (a) Work out its area, giving the answer first as a power of 2 and then as a fraction. (b) The width is then doubled while the length stays the same. Work out the new area, again as a power of 2.","a":"(a) 2⁻³ = 1/8 cm²   (b) 2⁻² cm²","sol":"Area = length × width = 2² × 2⁻⁵ = 2²⁺⁽⁻⁵⁾ = 2⁻³ (M1), which as a fraction is 1/2³ = 1/8 cm² (A1). Doubling the width multiplies it by 2¹, giving a new width of 2⁻⁵⁺¹ = 2⁻⁴ (M1); the new area is 2² × 2⁻⁴ = 2⁻² cm² (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Area is length times width, so this combines the index laws with negative indices.","Add the indices to multiply the powers in (a); for (b), doubling is multiplying by 2¹, so add 1 to the width's index before multiplying by the length again.","Part (a) starts from 2²⁺⁽⁻⁵⁾; part (b) starts from the new width 2⁻⁵⁺¹."],"codes":["3.01a","3.01c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circular pond of radius 4 m sits inside a square garden of side 10 m. Give the exact area of the garden not covered by the pond, as an expression in terms of π.","a":"(100 − 16π) m²","sol":"The area of the square garden is 10 × 10 = 100 m² (B1). The area of the circular pond is πr² = π × 4² = 16π m² (M1). The area not covered is the square's area minus the pond's area, 100 − 16π (M1), left exactly in terms of π rather than evaluated as a decimal (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The area not covered is what is left of the square once the circle is taken away.","Work out the square's area and the circle's area separately, using the circle area formula, then subtract; leave the π in place rather than multiplying it out.","The first lines are side × side for the square, and π × radius² for the pond."],"codes":["3.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify (2x³)⁴ ÷ (4x²)², giving your answer in its simplest form.","a":"x⁸","sol":"Raise each bracket to its power first: (2x³)⁴ = 2⁴x¹² = 16x¹² (M1), and (4x²)² = 4²x⁴ = 16x⁴ (M1). Dividing the coefficients gives 16 ÷ 16 = 1 (M1), and dividing the powers of x gives x¹² ÷ x⁴ = x¹²⁻⁴ = x⁸, so the expression simplifies to x⁸ (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each bracket has to be expanded with the laws of indices before anything can be divided.","Raise both the number and the letter inside each bracket to the outer power, then divide the two results using the same laws.","The first lines are 2⁴x¹² for the top bracket and 4²x⁴ for the bottom bracket."],"codes":["3.01c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 7⁻² as a fraction.","a":"1/49"},{"q":"Simplify k¹⁰ ÷ k⁴.","a":"k⁶"},{"q":"Write 0.000315 in standard form.","a":"3.15 × 10⁻⁴"},{"q":"Work out exactly 2/3 − 1/5, giving your answer as a single fraction.","a":"7/15"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Index notation","back":"aⁿ means a multiplied by itself n times: the base a raised to the index n. For example 2⁴ = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2.","code":"3.01a","open":false},{"front":"Negative index","back":"a⁻ⁿ means the reciprocal of aⁿ: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ. For example 5⁻² = 1/5² = 1/25.","code":"3.01a","open":false},{"front":"Powers of 2 to recognise","back":"2¹=2, 2²=4, 2³=8, 2⁴=16, 2⁵=32, 2⁶=64, 2⁷=128, 2⁸=256.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"Powers of 3, 4 and 5 to recognise","back":"3¹=3, 3²=9, 3³=27, 3⁴=81, 3⁵=243; 4¹=4, 4²=16, 4³=64, 4⁴=256; 5¹=5, 5²=25, 5³=125, 5⁴=625.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"Square root and cube root","back":"√a is the number that squares to give a; ∛a is the number that cubes to give a. √49=7 because 7²=49; ∛125=5 because 5³=125.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"Calculating an exact root","back":"Test the number against the powers you recognise until one fits: √169 = 13 because 13² = 169; ∛216 = 6 because 6³ = 216.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"First law of indices","back":"aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ: multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices.","code":"3.01c","open":false},{"front":"Second law of indices","back":"aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ: dividing powers of the same base subtracts the indices.","code":"3.01c","open":false},{"front":"Third law of indices","back":"(aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ: raising a power to a power multiplies the indices.","code":"3.01c","open":false},{"front":"Standard form","back":"A × 10ⁿ, where 1 ≤ A < 10 and n is an integer; n is positive for a number of 10 or more and negative for a number smaller than 1.","code":"3.02a","open":false},{"front":"Reading a calculator's standard form display","back":"The raised digits on the display are the index of 10: a display of 4.5⁻⁰⁴ means 4.5 × 10⁻⁴, written out that way and never copied as it appears.","code":"3.02b","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying or dividing numbers in standard form","back":"Multiply or divide the front numbers, and add or subtract the indices of 10; then adjust the front number back into the range 1 ≤ A < 10.","code":"3.02b","open":false},{"front":"Adding or subtracting numbers in standard form","back":"Rewrite both numbers with the same power of 10 first, then add or subtract the front numbers; adjust the front number back into range afterwards.","code":"3.02b","open":false},{"front":"Calculating exactly","back":"Leave the answer as a fraction in lowest terms or as a multiple of π, never as a rounded decimal: the area of a circle of radius 3 cm is 9π cm², not 28.3 cm².","code":"3.03a","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying a multiple of π by a fraction","back":"Only the number in front of π changes: 2/3 × 9π = 6π, with the π carried through untouched.","code":"3.03a","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Given to you: circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd, where r is the radius and d the diameter. The Foundation tier formulae sheet prints it, and it is what an exact circumference such as 20π cm is written straight from.","Given to you: area of a circle = πr², where r is the radius. The Foundation tier formulae sheet prints this too, and it is what an exact area such as 100π cm² is built from.","Those two circle results are the only things this room uses that the Foundation tier formulae sheet prints. Everything below must be recalled.","Remember: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ (3.01c). Not on the Foundation tier formulae sheet.","Remember: aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ (3.01c). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ (3.01c). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ (3.01a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: a root with an exact value is found by testing against the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5 you recognise (3.01b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: the powers of 2, 3, 4 and 5 up to 2⁸, 3⁵, 4⁴ and 5⁴ (3.01b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: standard form is A × 10ⁿ with 1 ≤ A < 10 and n an integer (3.02a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to multiply or divide numbers in standard form, multiply or divide the front numbers and add or subtract the indices of 10, then adjust the front number back into range (3.02b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to add or subtract numbers in standard form, first rewrite them with the same power of 10 (3.02b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to add or subtract fractions, write them over a common denominator first (3.03a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to multiply fractions, cancel then multiply the tops and bottoms; to divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal (3.03a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: 'exactly' means leaving the answer as a fraction in lowest terms or as a multiple of π, never as a rounded decimal (3.03a). Not on the formulae sheet.","For reference, the rest of the Foundation tier formulae sheet — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, compound interest, and the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another. The sheet is reissued every series, so check the insert at the front of your paper before relying on this list."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:01:41.308Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Fifth pass: room.questions[0] is now internally consistent — the stem's five factors of 3, the key 3⁵ and the sol line 'Five equal factors of 3 multiplied together give the index form 3⁵ (B1)' agree, and hints 2 and 3 count factors of 3. With that, every answer key in the kit is correct and single-valued in the form asked, every sol's mark codes add to its m, 12 of the 15 questions are non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, difficulties run 6/5/4 with no d:1 above 2 marks, the 15 cards are all closed fronts within the word limits, the check and exit tickets are correct and do not duplicate each other, codes stay within 3.01a-3.03a, no Higher-only technique survives anywhere (the surd-combination law is gone from every field), and the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md — the two circle results printed on the 2026 Foundation sheet, everything else marked must-be-recalled, and the closing list of the rest of the sheet accurate. Publish it; the one remaining note about √225 can be picked up at the next revision."}}},"indices-surds-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"indices-surds-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room is the third of three visits to indices and surds: the Foundation room next door covers the laws of indices, standard form and simplifying a numeric root, and the Higher tier expects all of that as prior knowledge before adding four extra techniques worth grade 7 to 9 marks. Fractional indices let an exam question hide a root and a power inside one small number, so 8^(2/3) has to be unpacked before it can be worked out; estimating a power or root is tested as a written method in its own right, with the marks in the trapping inequality rather than the final digit. Paper 5 is non-calculator, so every surd manipulation here — simplifying, multiplying, rationalising a denominator — has to be done by hand, and 'exactly' rules out a rounded decimal completely. These four ideas also feed forward into the rest of the Higher paper: an exact triangle area, a circle's exact radius or a quadratic solution routinely comes out as a surd, so a wrong simplification here costs marks in a later room too.","idea":[{"h":"Fractional indices as roots and combinations of powers and roots","code":"3.01a","t":"A fractional index is a different way of writing a root: x^(1/n) means the positive nth root of x, so x^(1/2) = √x and x^(1/3) = ∛x. When the numerator is not 1, x^(m/n) means the nth root of x raised to the power m, x^(m/n) = (ⁿ√x)^m, and it can equally be read as the mth power of x rooted afterwards — both give the same value, but taking the root first keeps the numbers small. A negative fractional index still means a reciprocal on top of all this, so x^(−m/n) = 1/x^(m/n), and with a fraction as the base it is quicker to turn the fraction upside down first, since (a/b)^(−n) = (b/a)^n. Every index law from the Foundation room still holds once the indices are fractions or negative."},{"h":"Calculating fractional powers, and estimating powers and roots","code":"3.01b","t":"Once a fractional index is read as a root and a power, it can be calculated exactly without a calculator: 16^(−3/4) means take the reciprocal, then the fourth root of 16, then cube it — 16^(−3/4) = 1/(∜16)³ = 1/8. The root is always taken first so the power is applied to a small whole number rather than a large one. Separately, 'estimate' a power or root means trap it between two values you already know rather than reach for a decimal: for a root, find the square or cube numbers either side, for example 49 < 51 < 64 gives 7 < √51 < 8, and judge which one it is nearer to; for a power of a decimal, round the base to a whole number first and say whether that rounding makes the estimate too big or too small."},{"h":"Exact calculations with surds","code":"3.03a","t":"A surd is a root left unevaluated so the answer stays exact rather than being rounded to a decimal; an instruction to work something out 'exactly' on a non-calculator paper is a signal to keep any root in surd form all the way through. Squaring a surd removes it completely, (√a)² = a, which is what turns two surd side lengths in Pythagoras' theorem into a whole-number square straight away. Multiplying two surd lengths for an area or a volume works the same way as multiplying any two roots, a√b × c√d = ac√(bd), and the result is then simplified as any surd would be. The habit throughout is to keep the root symbol in every line of working and only remove it where the mathematics genuinely allows it, never by rounding."},{"h":"Simplifying surds and rationalising denominators","code":"3.03b","t":"A surd is simplified by taking out the largest square factor of the number under the root, so that the b left in a√b has no square factor of its own: √12 = √4 × √3 = 2√3. Two surds combine under one root sign exactly as multiplication and division do, √a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a ÷ b), and a root multiplied by itself removes the root, √a × √a = a. A denominator is never left with a surd in it: for a single surd, multiply top and bottom by that surd, so 1/√3 = √3/3; for a denominator of the form a ± √b or √b ± a, multiply top and bottom instead by its conjugate, because (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b and (√b + a)(√b − a) = b − a² both remove the root completely, so 1/(√3 + 1) = (√3 − 1)/((√3+1)(√3−1)) = (√3 − 1)/2."}],"examples":[{"title":"Reading a fractional index as a root and a power","setup":"8^(2/3) is written with a fractional index. Write it as a root of 8 raised to a power, and work out its exact value without a calculator.","steps":["The denominator of the fractional index, 3, gives the root to take, and the numerator, 2, gives the power, so 8^(2/3) = (∛8)².","The cube root of 8 is 2, since 2 × 2 × 2 = 8.","So 8^(2/3) = 2², and 2² = 4."],"cues":["Which part of the fraction 2/3 tells you which root to take, and which part tells you the power?","What number cubes to give 8?","What is that number squared?"]},{"title":"Working out a negative fractional power exactly","setup":"Without a calculator, work out the exact value of 16^(−3/4).","steps":["The negative sign means the reciprocal, so 16^(−3/4) = 1/16^(3/4).","The denominator 4 of the index gives the fourth root, and the numerator 3 the power, so 16^(3/4) = (∜16)³.","The fourth root of 16 is 2, since 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16, so 16^(3/4) = 2³ = 8.","Therefore 16^(−3/4) = 1/8."],"cues":["What does the minus sign in front of the index tell you to do first?","Which part of the remaining fraction gives the root and which gives the power?","What number, raised to the fourth power, gives 16, and what is that number cubed?","Putting the reciprocal back, what single fraction is 16^(−3/4)?"]},{"title":"Estimating a square root between consecutive whole numbers","setup":"Without a calculator, estimate √51 to the nearest whole number. Show the square numbers you use to trap it.","steps":["The square numbers either side of 51 are 7 × 7 = 49 and 8 × 8 = 64.","Since 49 < 51 < 64, taking square roots gives 7 < √51 < 8.","51 is only 2 above 49 but 13 below 64, so √51 is much closer to 7.","To the nearest whole number, √51 ≈ 7."],"cues":["What are the two square numbers that sit either side of 51?","What inequality do those two square numbers give for √51?","Is 51 closer to the lower square number or the upper one?","So what is √51 to the nearest whole number?"]},{"title":"The exact area of a right-angled triangle with surd sides","setup":"A right-angled triangle has two perpendicular sides of length √2 cm and √8 cm. Work out its exact area, giving the answer in its simplest form.","steps":["The two perpendicular sides act as the base and the height, so area = ½ × √2 × √8.","Two roots multiplied together sit under one root sign, so √2 × √8 = √16.","√16 = 4, so the area is ½ × 4 = 2 cm²."],"cues":["Which two given lengths can be used directly as the base and the height?","What single root do the two separate roots combine into?","What is the value of that combined root, and so what is the area?"]},{"title":"Simplifying a surd and rationalising a single-surd denominator","setup":"Without a calculator: (a) simplify √12 fully, giving the answer in the form a√b, and (b) rationalise the denominator of 1/√3, giving the answer as a fraction.","steps":["The largest square factor of 12 is 4, so √12 = √4 × √3.","√4 = 2, so √12 = 2√3.","To rationalise 1/√3, multiply top and bottom by √3: 1/√3 = √3/(√3 × √3).","√3 × √3 = 3, so 1/√3 = √3/3."],"cues":["What is the largest square number that divides exactly into 12?","What does the square root of that factor simplify to?","What can the top and bottom of 1/√3 both be multiplied by to clear the root?","What does the denominator become once that multiplication is carried out?"]},{"title":"Rationalising a denominator that is a sum with a surd","setup":"Without a calculator, rationalise the denominator of 1/(√3 + 1), giving your answer in its simplest form.","steps":["Multiplying (√3 + 1) by its conjugate, √3 − 1, removes the surd since (√b + a)(√b − a) = b − a² for this order, so multiply top and bottom by √3 − 1.","1/(√3 + 1) = (√3 − 1)/((√3 + 1)(√3 − 1)).","Expanding the bottom: (√3 + 1)(√3 − 1) = (√3 × √3) − (√3 × 1) + (1 × √3) − (1 × 1) = 3 − 1.","So the denominator is 2, and 1/(√3 + 1) = (√3 − 1)/2."],"cues":["What expression, multiplied by √3 + 1, gives a whole-number denominator?","What must happen to the numerator if the denominator is multiplied by that expression?","What four terms appear when the two brackets on the bottom are expanded, and which two cancel?","What single whole number is the denominator left as, and so what is the full answer?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 16^(1/2) as a root and evaluate it.","a":"4"},{"q":"Work out the exact value of 27^(−1/3).","a":"1/3"},{"q":"Simplify √32 fully, giving the answer in the form a√b.","a":"4√2"},{"q":"Between which two consecutive whole numbers does √40 lie?","a":"6 and 7"}]},"room":{"facts":["x^(1/n) = ⁿ√x, and x^(m/n) = (ⁿ√x)^m: take the root first, then raise to the power.","A negative fractional index means a reciprocal: x^(−m/n) = 1/x^(m/n); for a fraction base, (a/b)^(−n) = (b/a)^n.","To estimate a root, trap it between the two nearest square or cube numbers: 49 < 51 < 64 gives 7 < √51 < 8.","√a × √b = √(ab); √a ÷ √b = √(a ÷ b); √a × √a = a.","Simplify a surd by taking out the largest square factor, so a√b is left with no square factor under the root.","Rationalise a single-surd denominator by multiplying top and bottom by that surd: 1/√a = √a/a.","Rationalise a denominator of the form a ± √b by multiplying top and bottom by its conjugate a ∓ √b, since (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b.","The square numbers to 15² = 225 and the cubes 2³ = 8, 3³ = 27, 4³ = 64, 5³ = 125, 6³ = 216, 7³ = 343, which trap an estimated root.","OCR's Higher tier is assessed by three papers, J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 5 (J560/05) is non-calculator; the Higher tier grades awarded are 9 to 4."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) (a) Write 36^(1/2) as a square root and evaluate it. (b) Write 64^(1/3) as a cube root and evaluate it.","a":"(a) 6   (b) 4","sol":"An index of 1/2 is the positive square root, so 36^(1/2) = √36 = 6 (B1). An index of 1/3 is the cube root, so 64^(1/3) = ∛64 = 4 (B1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both parts turn a fractional index straight into a root.","The denominator of each fractional index tells you which root to take.","Part (a) starts from √36; part (b) starts from ∛64."],"codes":["3.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Between which two consecutive whole numbers does √95 lie?","a":"9 and 10","sol":"The square numbers either side of 95 are 9² = 81 and 10² = 100 (M1). Since 81 < 95 < 100, taking square roots gives 9 < √95 < 10, so the two whole numbers are 9 and 10 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A square root can only be placed once you know the square numbers around it.","Find the square number just below 95 and the square number just above it.","The first line traps 95 between two consecutive square numbers, without yet naming the whole numbers they give."],"codes":["3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the exact value of 9^(3/2).","a":"27","sol":"The denominator 2 of the index gives the square root, so 9^(3/2) = (√9)³ (M1). √9 = 3, so 9^(3/2) = 3³ = 27 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Take the root the denominator of the index asks for before raising to the power.","Find the square root of 9 first, then cube the result.","The first line rewrites the expression as (√9)³."],"codes":["3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify √20 fully, giving the answer in the form a√b.","a":"2√5","sol":"The largest square factor of 20 is 4, so √20 = √4 × √5 (M1). √4 = 2, so √20 = 2√5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only a square factor can be taken out from under a root sign.","List the factor pairs of 20 and pick the pair whose first number is a square number.","The first line splits 20 into 4 × 5 under separate roots."],"codes":["3.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out the exact value of 32^(−4/5), giving your answer as a fraction.","a":"1/16","sol":"The negative sign means the reciprocal, so 32^(−4/5) = 1/32^(4/5) (M1). The denominator 5 of the index gives the fifth root, and the fifth root of 32 is 2, so 32^(4/5) = 2⁴ = 16 (M1). Therefore 32^(−4/5) = 1/16 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["There are two things happening in this index: a minus sign and a fraction.","Deal with the reciprocal first, then take the fifth root of 32 before raising it to the power 4.","The first line rewrites the expression as 1 over 32^(4/5)."],"codes":["3.01a","3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Estimate ∛200 to the nearest whole number. You must show the cube numbers you use.","a":"6","sol":"The cube numbers either side of 200 are 5³ = 125 and 6³ = 216 (M1). Since 125 < 200 < 216, ∛200 lies between 5 and 6 (M1). 200 is 75 above 125 but only 16 below 216, so ∛200 is much closer to 6, giving ∛200 ≈ 6 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A cube root is placed by knowing the cube numbers either side of it.","Find the cube number just below 200 and the cube number just above it, then judge which is closer.","The first line writes down 5³ and 6³ either side of 200."],"codes":["3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Rationalise the denominator of 10/√5, simplifying your answer fully.","a":"2√5","sol":"Multiply numerator and denominator by √5: 10/√5 = (10 × √5)/(√5 × √5) (M1). √5 × √5 = 5, so this is 10√5/5 (A1). Dividing 10 by 5 gives 2√5 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An answer is not acceptable while a root is still on the bottom of the fraction.","Multiply the top and the bottom by the surd that appears in the denominator, then cancel the whole numbers.","The first line multiplies numerator and denominator by √5."],"codes":["3.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully: √8 × √6, giving your answer in the form a√b.","a":"4√3","sol":"Two roots multiplied together sit under one root sign, so √8 × √6 = √48 (M1). The largest square factor of 48 is 16, so √48 = √16 × √3 = 4√3 (A1).","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate roots multiplied together can be combined under a single root sign.","Combine the two roots first, then look for a square factor in the number left under the root.","The first line writes the product as √48."],"codes":["3.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A rectangle has length √18 cm and width √2 cm. (a) Work out the exact area of the rectangle. (b) Work out the exact length of the diagonal of the rectangle.","a":"(a) 6 cm²   (b) 2√5 cm","sol":"For the area, the two given sides multiply directly under one root sign, so area = √18 × √2 = √36 = 6 cm² (M1, A1). For the diagonal, Pythagoras' theorem gives d² = (√18)² + (√2)² = 18 + 2 = 20 (M1, A1), and the largest square factor of 20 is 4, so d = √20 = √4 × √5 = 2√5 cm (A1).","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["The area comes from multiplying the two given surd lengths; the diagonal comes from Pythagoras' theorem on those same two lengths.","Combine the two surds under one root sign for the area; square each surd separately for the diagonal, then add and simplify.","The first line for (a) is √18 × √2; for (b) it is (√18)² + (√2)²."],"codes":["3.03a","3.03b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Solve for n: 4^(3/2) × 2ⁿ = 2⁸.","a":"n = 5","sol":"Write 4 as a power of 2: 4 = 2², so 4^(3/2) = (2²)^(3/2) (M1). Multiplying the indices gives (2²)^(3/2) = 2³ = 8 (M1). Multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices, so 2³ × 2ⁿ = 2³⁺ⁿ (M1); setting 3 + n = 8 gives n = 5 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Both bases in this equation are powers of the same smaller number, 2.","Rewrite 4 as a power of 2, apply the fractional index, then use the rule for multiplying powers of the same base.","The first line replaces 4 with 2² before the fractional index is applied."],"codes":["3.01a","3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A cuboid has a square base of side √8 cm and height √2 cm. Work out its exact volume, giving your answer in the form a√b cm³.","a":"8√2 cm³","sol":"The area of the square base is (√8)² = 8 cm² (M1). The volume is the base area times the height, so volume = 8 × √2 = 8√2 cm³ (M1, A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Squaring the surd side length of the base removes the root before the height is brought in.","Find the area of the square base first, then multiply by the height, keeping the remaining root in place.","The first line is (√8)² for the base area."],"codes":["3.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The area of a circle is exactly 45π cm². Work out the exact radius of the circle, giving your answer in the form a√b cm.","a":"3√5 cm","sol":"The area of a circle is πr², so πr² = 45π; dividing both sides by π gives r² = 45 (M1). Therefore r = √45 (M1), and the largest square factor of 45 is 9, so √45 = √9 × √5 = 3√5 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The area formula links the given exact area to the square of the radius.","Set the area formula equal to 45π, cancel the π, then square root and simplify the surd you get.","The first line sets πr² equal to 45π."],"codes":["3.03a","3.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A cube has volume 300 cm³. By trapping ∛300 between two consecutive whole numbers, decide whether the side length is closer to 6 cm or to 7 cm.","a":"Closer to 7 cm","sol":"The cube numbers either side of 300 are 6³ = 216 and 7³ = 343 (M1). Since 216 < 300 < 343, the side length lies between 6 cm and 7 cm (M1). 300 is 84 above 216 but only 43 below 343, so the side length is closer to 7 cm (A1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The side length of the cube is a cube root that can be trapped between two whole numbers.","Find the cube numbers either side of 300, then compare how far 300 is from each one.","The first line writes down 6³ and 7³ either side of 300."],"codes":["3.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify fully: 1/(2 + √3) + 1/(2 − √3), giving your answer as an integer.","a":"4","sol":"Write both fractions over the common denominator (2 + √3)(2 − √3), which is the product of a pair of conjugates (M1). Expanding, (2 + √3)(2 − √3) = 4 − 3 = 1 (M1). The numerator is (2 − √3) + (2 + √3) = 4 (M1), so the sum is 4/1 = 4 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two denominators are a conjugate pair, so combining the fractions clears every surd.","Write both fractions over the product of the two denominators, expand that product, then add the numerators.","The first line rewrites the sum over the single denominator (2 + √3)(2 − √3)."],"codes":["3.03b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 100^(1/2) as a root and evaluate it.","a":"10"},{"q":"Work out the exact value of 8^(−2/3).","a":"1/4"},{"q":"Simplify √72 fully, giving the answer in the form a√b.","a":"6√2"},{"q":"Rationalise the denominator of 4/√2, simplifying your answer fully.","a":"2√2"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The meaning of x^(1/n)","back":"The positive nth root of x: x^(1/2) = √x and x^(1/3) = ∛x. For example 25^(1/2) = √25 = 5.","code":"3.01a","open":false},{"front":"The meaning of x^(m/n)","back":"The nth root of x, raised to the power m: x^(m/n) = (ⁿ√x)^m. Take the root first to keep the numbers small, so 27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 9.","code":"3.01a","open":false},{"front":"A negative fractional index","back":"x^(−m/n) = 1/x^(m/n), the reciprocal of the positive version. For a fraction base, (a/b)^(−n) = (b/a)^n, so it is quicker to flip the fraction first.","code":"3.01a","open":false},{"front":"Method for calculating a fractional power","back":"Take the root the denominator of the index asks for, then raise the result to the power on the numerator: 16^(3/4) = (∜16)³ = 2³ = 8.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a square root","back":"Trap the number between the two nearest square numbers, then take roots; the root is nearer the closer square. 49 < 51 < 64 gives 7 < √51 < 8, nearer 7.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a cube root","back":"Trap the number between the two nearest cube numbers, then take cube roots: 125 < 200 < 216 gives 5 < ∛200 < 6.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a power of a decimal","back":"Round the base to a convenient whole number and raise that instead: 3.1³ ≈ 3³ = 27. Rounding the base down gives an underestimate.","code":"3.01b","open":false},{"front":"'Exactly' in a surd calculation","back":"The answer must stay as a surd or a whole number reached by the surd rules, never a rounded decimal; a root is only removed where squaring or a surd rule genuinely allows it.","code":"3.03a","open":false},{"front":"Squaring a surd","back":"(√a)² = a: squaring removes the root completely. This is what turns two surd side lengths in Pythagoras' theorem into whole numbers.","code":"3.03a","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying two surd lengths for an area or volume","back":"a√b × c√d = ac√(bd): multiply the whole-number parts together and the numbers under the roots together, then simplify the result.","code":"3.03a","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying a surd","back":"Take out the largest square factor: √48 = √16 × √3 = 4√3. The form a√b is fully simplified only when b has no square factor left.","code":"3.03b","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying and dividing surds","back":"√a × √b = √(ab) and √a ÷ √b = √(a ÷ b); also √a × √a = a. For example √3 × √6 = √18 = 3√2.","code":"3.03b","open":false},{"front":"Rationalising a single-surd denominator","back":"Multiply numerator and denominator by that surd, then cancel: 1/√3 = √3/(√3 × √3) = √3/3.","code":"3.03b","open":false},{"front":"The conjugate of a + √b","back":"a − √b. (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b; if the surd comes first, (√b + a)(√b − a) = b − a² instead. Either way the result is a whole number, clearing a surd denominator.","code":"3.03b","open":false},{"front":"Give one way of checking that a surd has been fully simplified","back":"check the number left under the root has no square factor greater than 1; list the factor pairs of the number under the root and check none of them is a square number; try dividing the number under the root by 4, 9, 16, 25, ... and check none divides it exactly","code":"3.03b","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Given to you: area of a circle = πr². The Higher tier formulae sheet prints it, and in this room it is used in reverse, dividing an exact area by π to find r² before the radius is simplified as a surd. Note that the specification's own Appendix (6.02d, written before the sheet existed) also lists area of a circle among the results learners 'should know'; the later sheet governs what is given on the day.","Given to you: Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c² in a right-angled triangle where c is the hypotenuse. The Higher tier formulae sheet prints it, and here it is the route from two surd side lengths to an exact third side, since squaring each root removes it. The Appendix places it among the results learners 'should know'; the sheet governs.","So, of everything the Higher tier formulae sheet prints, this room uses only those two — area of a circle and Pythagoras' theorem. Everything below must be recalled; none of it is on either tier's formulae sheet. The formulae sheet itself is an exam insert, issued since 2022 under an Ofqual requirement and printed outside the specification's own formulae list at 6.02d.","Remember: x^(1/n) = ⁿ√x and x^(m/n) = (ⁿ√x)^m — take the root the denominator of the index gives, then raise to the power the numerator gives (3.01a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: x^(−m/n) = 1/x^(m/n), and for a fraction base (a/b)^(−n) = (b/a)^n (3.01a). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: to estimate a root, trap it between the nearest square or cube numbers either side, then judge which it is closer to (3.01b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: √a × √b = √(ab), √a ÷ √b = √(a ÷ b) and √a × √a = a (3.03a, 3.03b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: simplify a surd by taking out its largest square factor, so a√b is left with no square factor under the root (3.03b). Not on the formulae sheet.","Remember: rationalise a single-surd denominator by multiplying top and bottom by that surd, or a denominator a ± √b by its conjugate a ∓ √b, using (a + √b)(a − √b) = a² − b (3.03b). Not on the formulae sheet.","For reference, the rest of the Higher tier formulae sheet — none of it needed in this room — is the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, circumference of a circle, the quadratic formula, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the angle between them, compound interest, the addition rule for probability and the multiplication rule for probability using a conditional probability. The sheet is reissued every series, so check the insert at the front of your paper before relying on this list."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:58:32.983822Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.83,"notes":"Mathematically clean - all fourteen answer keys, every sol mark tally (each adds exactly to m), all six worked examples and both quick-check sets re-solve correctly, and the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md item for item; the faults are editorial (no OCR exam facts, a bank question and hint that give themselves away, and a triangle question that repeats a worked example), so fix those before publishing."}}},"approximation-estimation":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"approximation-estimation","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Non-calculator papers J560/02 and J560/05 open with questions that reward exactly this room: rounding a number cleanly, estimating a calculation by hand before you trust it, and writing down the band a rounded measurement really sits in. The method mark for an estimate question is awarded for the rounded values you write, not for a lucky final figure, so skipping that step loses marks even when the arithmetic is right. Error-interval and limits-of-accuracy questions test something different again: whether you understand that a rounded number is not the truth, only the truth's nearest label, and Higher tier pushes that further by asking you to carry two such bands through a whole calculation to find its most extreme possible results.","idea":[{"h":"Rounding to decimal places, significant figures and a sensible degree of accuracy","t":"Rounding to a number of decimal places counts digits after the point; rounding to a number of significant figures counts digits starting at the first non-zero digit, so 0.07253 to 3 significant figures is 0.0725 even though two of those three digits are zeros held for place value. Whole numbers round the same way to the nearest ten, hundred, thousand and so on. In every case, look at the digit immediately after the one you are keeping: 5 or more rounds the kept digit up, anything less leaves it alone. Foundation tier also expects you to choose the accuracy a context needs when a question does not name one — money to the nearest penny, people to a whole number, a measurement to a sensible place value — rather than copying every digit a calculator display shows.","code":"4.01a"},{"h":"Estimating a calculation without a calculator","t":"To estimate, round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, write those rounded values down, and work out the simpler calculation that results — the written rounded values, not the final figure, are what a mark scheme credits. Use ≈ for 'is approximately equal to'. Foundation tier extends this to more complex calculations, including ones with a root: rounding first and then taking the root of a much simpler number, such as √(3 ÷ (0.05 × 0.6)) = √100 = 10, gives a quick check on what a calculator's more exact answer ought to look like. An estimate is also how you catch a keying slip: if a calculator display is ten times or a tenth of your estimate, a digit or a decimal point has gone astray.","code":"4.01b"},{"h":"Error intervals, limits of accuracy, and (Higher tier) bounds of a calculation","t":"A rounded or truncated number is a label for a whole band of possible true values, and an error interval states that band in inequality notation. Rounded to the nearest u, a value v could have been anything from v − u/2 up to, but never reaching, v + u/2: v − u/2 ≤ x < v + u/2. Truncated (digits after a place simply cut off, not rounded) the value written is already the smallest the truth could be: v ≤ x < v + 1 in the last place kept. Foundation tier also applies this to a real context — comparing the error intervals of two separately rounded measurements to decide whether one is certain to fit inside, or stay under, the other. (Higher tier additionally: when a quantity is calculated from rounded measurements, its own upper and lower bounds come from choosing, for each measurement, the end of its interval that pushes the result the way you want — sums and products push the same way as their inputs, so the largest answer uses every largest input; a difference or a quotient reverses the second quantity, because subtracting or dividing by less leaves more. Higher tier also distinguishes a continuous measurement's bounds from a discrete count's: 200 cars to the nearest hundred gives 150 ≤ n < 250 by the ordinary rounding rule, but because a number of cars must be a whole number the true range is 150 ≤ n ≤ 249 — the upper bound of 250 itself can never be reached either way, but for a discrete quantity the true largest possible value is one whole unit below it.)","code":"4.01c"}],"examples":[{"title":"Rounding to the nearest hundred and to significant figures","setup":"Round 3846 to the nearest hundred, and round 6.482 to 2 significant figures.","steps":["3846 to the nearest hundred: the hundreds digit is the one that will change, so look at the tens digit straight after it, which is 4.","4 is less than 5, so the hundreds digit stays the same and everything after it becomes zero: 3846 rounds to 3800.","6.482 to 2 significant figures: the first two significant digits are 6 and 4, so look at the digit straight after them, which is 8.","8 is 5 or more, so the second significant digit rounds up: 6.482 rounds to 6.5."],"cues":["Which digit decides whether the hundreds column changes?","Does that digit round the hundreds column up or leave it alone?","Which two digits are the first two significant figures of 6.482, and which digit decides whether they change?","Does that digit round the second significant figure up or leave it alone?"]},{"title":"Estimating the cost of a weighed item","setup":"Estimate, to one significant figure, the cost of 2.8 kg of potatoes at 68p per kg, without a calculator.","steps":["Round each number to 1 significant figure: 2.8 kg ≈ 3 kg, and 68p per kg ≈ 70p per kg.","Cost = mass × price per kilogram, so the estimate is 3 × 70.","3 × 70 = 210, so the estimated cost is 210p.","210p is £2.10, so the potatoes cost approximately £2.10."],"cues":["What does each of the two numbers round to, to 1 significant figure?","Which calculation turns a mass and a price per kilogram into a total cost?","What does that multiplication come to?","How is that amount written in pounds and pence?"]},{"title":"Estimating a calculation that includes a square root","setup":"Estimate the value of √(2.9 ÷ (0.051 × 0.62)) by rounding each number to 1 significant figure before you take the root.","steps":["Round each number to 1 significant figure: 2.9 ≈ 3, 0.051 ≈ 0.05, 0.62 ≈ 0.6.","Work out the bracket first: 0.05 × 0.6 = 0.03.","Divide: 3 ÷ 0.03 = 100.","Take the square root of the simplified value: √100 = 10, so the estimate is 10."],"cues":["What does each of the three numbers round to, to 1 significant figure?","Which part of the expression has to be worked out before the division?","What does the rounded division come to?","What is the square root of that result?"]},{"title":"The error interval of a value rounded to 1 decimal place","setup":"A length x = 2.1 cm is rounded to 1 decimal place. Write down the error interval for x using inequality notation.","steps":["The rounding unit is the last decimal place kept, 0.1 cm, so the true length lies within half of that either side of 2.1: half of 0.1 is 0.05.","Smallest possible value = 2.1 − 0.05 = 2.05.","Largest possible value = 2.1 + 0.05 = 2.15, but a length of exactly 2.15 would itself round to 2.2, not 2.1.","So the error interval is 2.05 ≤ x < 2.15."],"cues":["What is half of the rounding unit here?","Which limit do you reach by subtracting that half?","Which limit do you reach by adding it, and is that limit ever actually reached?","How do the two limits combine into one inequality?"]},{"title":"Applying limits of accuracy to a fitting problem","setup":"A window recess is 92 cm wide, correct to the nearest centimetre. A blind is exactly 91.6 cm wide. Ren says the blind is certain to fit into the recess. Is Ren correct? Show working to support your answer.","steps":["The recess width is rounded to the nearest centimetre, so it lies within half a centimetre of 92 cm: half of 1 is 0.5.","The narrowest the recess could actually be is 92 − 0.5 = 91.5 cm.","The blind is 91.6 cm wide, which is wider than the recess's narrowest possible width of 91.5 cm.","So the recess could be too narrow for the blind, and Ren is not correct to say it is certain to fit."],"cues":["How far either side of 92 cm could the true width of the recess lie?","What is the least the recess could actually measure?","How does the blind's width compare with that least possible width?","What does that comparison tell you about whether the fit is certain?"]},{"title":"(Higher tier) The upper bound of a calculated area","setup":"A rectangle has length 8.4 cm and width 5.2 cm, each correct to 1 decimal place. Calculate the upper bound of the area of the rectangle.","steps":["Half of the rounding unit, 0.1 cm, is 0.05 cm, so the length lies in 8.35 ≤ l < 8.45 and the width lies in 5.15 ≤ w < 5.25.","The upper bounds of the two measurements are 8.45 cm and 5.25 cm.","A product of two positive lengths is greatest when both factors are at their greatest, so the upper bound of the area is 8.45 × 5.25.","8.45 × 5.25 = 44.3625, so the upper bound of the area is 44.3625 cm²."],"cues":["What are the error intervals for the length and the width?","Which end of each interval is its upper bound?","To make the area as large as possible, which bound of each measurement do you multiply?","What does that multiplication come to?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Round 0.03187 to 2 significant figures.","a":"0.032"},{"q":"Work out an estimate for 4.1 × 78.6 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"4 × 80 = 320"},{"q":"A mass is 15 g, correct to the nearest gram. Write down the error interval for the mass, m.","a":"14.5 ≤ m < 15.5"},{"q":"(Higher tier) a = 9.0 and b = 3.0, each correct to 1 decimal place. Work out the upper bound of a ÷ b.","a":"About 3.07 (9.05 ÷ 2.95)"}]},"room":{"facts":["Decimal places count digits after the point; significant figures count from the first non-zero digit. Look at the next digit after the one you are keeping: 5 or more rounds up, otherwise the kept digit stays.","To estimate, round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, write the rounded values down, and work out the simpler calculation. Use ≈ for 'is approximately equal to'.","Rounded to the nearest u: value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2. Truncated at a given place: value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept. The lower limit is included, the upper limit is not.","(Higher tier) Combining bounds: UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b) and LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b); UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b) and LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b); for positive a and b, UB(ab) = UB(a) × UB(b) and LB(ab) = LB(a) × LB(b); UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b) and LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","(Higher tier) A continuous measurement's error interval keeps the strict < at its upper limit; a discrete count's true range stops one whole unit below that limit instead, because no whole number can sit strictly inside the last gap — 200 cars to the nearest hundred gives 150 ≤ n < 250 by the rounding rule, but 150 ≤ n ≤ 249 for the actual possible counts.","OCR GCSE (9-1) Mathematics J560 has three papers per tier: Foundation is J560/01, /02 and /03 (grades 5-1); Higher is J560/04, /05 and /06 (grades 9-4). Each paper is 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator, the rest allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 129.6 to the nearest 10.","a":"130","sol":"The tens digit is what will change, so look at the units digit, 6. Since 6 is 5 or more, the tens digit rounds up: 129.6 → 130 (B1). (1 mark: B1)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Rounding to the nearest 10 means every answer ends in a zero.","Decide which digit of 129.6 decides whether the tens column goes up or stays.","Look at the units digit of 129.6 and compare it with 5."],"codes":["4.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 0.07253 to 3 significant figures.","a":"0.0725","sol":"Significant figures start at the first non-zero digit, so the first three are 7, 2 and 5. The next digit is 3, which is less than 5, so the third significant figure stays the same: 0.0725 (B1). (1 mark: B1)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The zeros right after the decimal point are not significant figures themselves.","Find the first non-zero digit, then count three significant digits from there.","Keep those three digits and look at the digit immediately after them to decide whether the third one changes."],"codes":["4.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 4.5678 to 2 decimal places.","a":"4.57","sol":"Two decimal places keeps the digits 5 and 6 after the point. The next digit is 7, which is 5 or more, so the second decimal place rounds up: 4.5678 → 4.57 (B1). (1 mark: B1)","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Decimal places are counted after the decimal point.","Keep two digits after the point, then look at the digit immediately after them.","Compare that third decimal digit of 4.5678 with 5."],"codes":["4.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out an estimate for 5.9 × 21.4. You must write down the rounded values you use.","a":"6 × 20 = 120","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 5.9 ≈ 6 and 21.4 ≈ 20, giving 6 × 20 (M1). 6 × 20 = 120, so the estimate is 120 (A1). (2 marks: M1 A1)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An estimate replaces each number with a simpler one of about the same size.","Round both numbers to one significant figure before multiplying.","Write the two rounded values side by side as a multiplication."],"codes":["4.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the error interval for a value y = 36, correct to the nearest whole number.","a":"35.5 ≤ y < 36.5","sol":"The rounding unit is 1, so the true value lies within half a unit either side of 36: half of 1 is 0.5 (B1). Lower limit = 36 − 0.5 = 35.5 and upper limit = 36 + 0.5 = 36.5, giving 35.5 ≤ y < 36.5 (B1). (2 marks: B1 B1)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A value rounded to the nearest whole number is not necessarily exactly that number.","Find half of the rounding unit and use it either side of 36.","Write 36 minus that half as the lower limit and 36 plus it as the upper limit."],"codes":["4.01c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out an estimate for (58.3 + 19.7) × 4.9. You must write down the rounded values you use.","a":"(60 + 20) × 5 = 400","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 58.3 ≈ 60, 19.7 ≈ 20 and 4.9 ≈ 5 (M1). The bracket is worked out first: 60 + 20 = 80, so the estimate becomes 80 × 5 (M1). 80 × 5 = 400, so the estimate is 400 (A1). (3 marks: M1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An estimate replaces every number in the calculation with a one-significant-figure version.","Round all three numbers first, work the bracket out, then multiply.","Write the two rounded values inside the bracket as an addition, then multiply by the rounded third value."],"codes":["4.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Round 428.65 (a) to 1 decimal place (b) to 3 significant figures.","a":"(a) 428.7 (b) 429","sol":"(a) One decimal place keeps the digit 6; the next digit is 5, so it rounds up: 428.65 → 428.7 (B1). (b) The first three significant digits are 4, 2 and 8; the next digit is 6, which is 5 or more, so the third significant figure rounds up and the digits after it become zeros: 429 (B1). (2 marks: B1 B1)","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The two parts round the same number to two different kinds of accuracy.","For (a) look at the digit after the first decimal place; for (b) count three digits from the first non-zero digit.","Compare the deciding digit with 5 in each part before writing the rounded value."],"codes":["4.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A stadium's attendance is given as 48 700, correct to the nearest 100. Write down the error interval for the attendance, n.","a":"48 650 ≤ n < 48 750","sol":"The rounding unit is 100, so the true attendance lies within half of that of 48 700: half of 100 is 50 (B1). Lower limit = 48 700 − 50 = 48 650 and upper limit = 48 700 + 50 = 48 750, giving 48 650 ≤ n < 48 750 (B1). (2 marks: B1 B1)","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A number rounded to the nearest hundred could be any of a whole band of exact attendances.","Find half of the rounding unit and use it either side of the stated attendance.","Write 48 700 minus that half as the lower limit and 48 700 plus it as the upper limit."],"codes":["4.01c"]},{"q":"A parking bay is 240 cm wide, correct to the nearest 10 cm. A car is 236 cm wide, correct to the nearest centimetre. Nasreen says the car is certain to fit in the bay. Is Nasreen correct? Show working to support your answer. A calculator may be used.","a":"No — the bay could be as narrow as 235 cm, and the car could be as wide as just under 236.5 cm","sol":"The bay's width is within half of 10 cm, that is 5 cm, of 240 cm, so the narrowest it could be is 240 − 5 = 235 cm (B1). The car's width is within half of 1 cm of 236 cm, so the widest it could be is just under 236 + 0.5 = 236.5 cm (B1). Since 236.5 cm is more than the bay's narrowest possible width of 235 cm, the bay might not be wide enough, so Nasreen is not correct to say the fit is certain (A1). (3 marks: B1 B1 A1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Neither the bay nor the car is guaranteed to measure exactly the number written down.","Find the narrowest the bay could be and the widest the car could be, then compare them.","Write the bay's width minus half of 10 cm as your first line."],"codes":["4.01c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Work out an estimate for 3.9 × 41.6 ÷ 12.8. You must write down the rounded values you use.","a":"4 × 40 ÷ 10 = 16","sol":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 3.9 ≈ 4, 41.6 ≈ 40 and 12.8 ≈ 10 (M1). The multiplication is worked out first: 4 × 40 = 160, so the estimate becomes 160 ÷ 10 (M1). 160 ÷ 10 = 16, so the estimate is 16 (A1). (3 marks: M1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An estimate swaps every awkward number for a one-significant-figure version of about the same size.","Round all three numbers, multiply the first two rounded values, then divide.","Write the two rounded values being multiplied as your first line."],"codes":["4.01b"]},{"q":"Tickets for a concert cost £18.75 each, and 3187 tickets are sold. (a) Estimate the total money taken, by rounding each number to 1 significant figure. (b) The actual total taken is £59 756.25. Use your estimate to say whether this total is sensible. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 20 × 3000 = £60 000 (b) Yes — £59 756.25 is close to the £60 000 estimate","sol":"(a) Round each number to 1 significant figure: £18.75 ≈ £20 and 3187 ≈ 3000 (M1). Estimate = 20 × 3000 (M1) = £60 000 (A1). (b) £59 756.25 is close to the estimate of £60 000, so the actual total is sensible (B1). (4 marks: M1 M1 A1 B1)","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The estimate uses one-significant-figure versions of the price and the number of tickets.","Round the price and the number of tickets, multiply them, then compare with the actual total.","Write the rounded price multiplied by the rounded number of tickets as your first line of part (a)."],"codes":["4.01b"]},{"q":"A recipe needs exactly 3.4 kg of flour. A bag of flour is labelled as containing 3.4 kg, correct to the nearest 0.1 kg. Explain, using the error interval for the bag's actual contents, why the recipe might not be able to be made using the contents of just one bag. A calculator may be used.","a":"The bag could contain as little as 3.35 kg, which is less than the 3.4 kg the recipe needs","sol":"The rounding unit is 0.1 kg, so half of it is 0.05 kg, and the bag's actual mass could be as low as 3.4 − 0.05 = 3.35 kg (B1). Since the recipe needs exactly 3.4 kg but the bag might only contain 3.35 kg, which is less than that (M1), the recipe might not be able to be made using just one bag (A1). (3 marks: B1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The label on the bag is not a guarantee of the exact mass inside.","Find the smallest amount the bag could actually contain, then compare it with the amount needed.","Write 3.4 minus half of 0.1 as your first line."],"codes":["4.01c"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A plank of wood is 215 cm long, correct to the nearest centimetre. A piece 68 cm long, also correct to the nearest centimetre, is cut from it. Calculate the lower bound of the length of the remaining plank. A calculator may be used.","a":"146 cm","sol":"The plank and the piece cut off are each rounded to the nearest cm, so half of 1 cm is 0.5 cm, giving 214.5 ≤ L < 215.5 and 67.5 ≤ C < 68.5 (B1). A difference is smallest when the number subtracted is at its largest, so the lower bound of the remaining length uses the lower bound of L and the upper bound of C: 214.5 − 68.5 (M1). 214.5 − 68.5 = 146, so the lower bound of the remaining plank's length is 146 cm (A1). (3 marks: B1 M1 A1)","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Each rounded length has its own band of possible true values.","Making a difference as small as possible means starting as small as possible and subtracting as large as possible.","Write the lower bound of the original length minus the upper bound of the piece cut off as your first line."],"codes":["4.01c"]},{"q":"(Higher tier) A charity collects tins of food. The number of tins collected is 450, correct to the nearest 50. (a) Write down the error interval for the number of tins, t, using the usual rounding rule. (b) Since t must be a whole number, write down the true range of possible values of t, using ≤ at both ends. (c) Explain why the upper limit changes between (a) and (b). A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 425 ≤ t < 475 (b) 425 ≤ t ≤ 474 (c) t is a whole number, so it cannot equal 475, and the largest whole number below 475 is 474","sol":"(a) Half of the rounding unit, 50, is 25, so 450 − 25 = 425 and 450 + 25 = 475, giving 425 ≤ t < 475 (B1). (b) A number of tins is discrete, so t can equal 425 but the largest whole number it can actually equal is 474, giving 425 ≤ t ≤ 474 (B1). (c) 475 itself is never a possible number of tins under either interval, but for a continuous quantity values can sit arbitrarily close to 475 without reaching it, while for a discrete quantity there is no whole number between 474 and 475, so the true upper limit is 474 rather than an unreachable 475 (B1). (3 marks: B1 B1 B1)","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["A number of tins cannot be a fraction, unlike a length or a mass.","Apply the usual rounding rule first, then think about which whole numbers actually lie inside that band.","Write 450 minus and plus half of 50 for part (a), then decide the largest whole number below the upper limit for part (b)."],"codes":["4.01c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Round 7.6284 to 3 decimal places.","a":"7.628"},{"q":"Work out an estimate for 39.4 × 2.1 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","a":"40 × 2 = 80"},{"q":"A distance is 64 km, correct to the nearest kilometre. Write down the error interval for the distance, d.","a":"63.5 ≤ d < 64.5"},{"q":"(Higher tier) a = 20 and b = 4, each correct to the nearest whole number. Work out the upper bound of a ÷ b.","a":"5.857… (20.5 ÷ 3.5)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Rounding to a given number of decimal places","back":"Keep that many digits after the point. If the digit straight after them is 5 or more, add 1 to the last digit kept; otherwise leave it as it is.","code":"4.01a","open":false},{"front":"Rounding to a given number of significant figures","back":"Counting starts at the first non-zero digit; every digit after it counts, including zeros. 0.07253 to 3 significant figures is 0.0725.","code":"4.01a","open":false},{"front":"Rounding a whole number to the nearest ten, hundred, thousand, etc.","back":"Look at the digit immediately to the right of the place value you are rounding to; 5 or more rounds up, otherwise the digit stays and everything after it becomes zero.","code":"4.01a","open":false},{"front":"Choosing an appropriate degree of accuracy","back":"Round an answer to match what the context needs — money to the nearest penny, people to a whole number, a measurement to a sensible place value — rather than copying every calculator digit.","code":"4.01a","open":false},{"front":"How to estimate the answer to a calculation","back":"Round every number in the calculation to 1 significant figure, write the rounded values down, then work out the simpler calculation. Use ≈ for 'is approximately equal to'.","code":"4.01b","open":false},{"front":"Estimating a calculation that includes a root","back":"Round every number under the root sign, and any number it is combined with, to 1 significant figure first, then take the root of the simplified value.","code":"4.01b","open":false},{"front":"Using an estimate to check a calculator display","back":"If a displayed answer is about ten times, or a tenth of, your estimate, a digit or a decimal point has probably been keyed in wrongly.","code":"4.01b","open":false},{"front":"Error interval for a value rounded to the nearest u","back":"value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2. The lower limit is a possible value; the upper limit is not, because it would itself round to the next value up.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"Error interval after truncation","back":"Truncating chops the extra digits off instead of rounding, so the stated value is the smallest possible: value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"Applying limits of accuracy to a fitting problem","back":"Write the error interval for each rounded measurement, then compare the one limit that decides the outcome — the smallest the space could be, against the largest the object could be, or the reverse.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"(Higher tier) Bounds of a sum or a difference","back":"UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b), LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b). UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b), LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b): subtracting less leaves more.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"(Higher tier) Bounds of a product or a quotient, for positive a and b","back":"UB(ab) = UB(a) × UB(b), LB(ab) = LB(a) × LB(b). UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b), LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b): dividing by less gives more.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"(Higher tier) Bounds of a discrete quantity","back":"Rounding still gives value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2, but a whole-number count cannot sit between whole numbers, so its true range is value − u/2 ≤ x ≤ value + u/2 − 1.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"The upper limit of an error interval is never reached","back":"A value exactly on the upper bound would itself round up to the next figure, so it belongs to the next interval, not this one: the top of an error interval is always strict.","code":"4.01c","open":false},{"front":"Rounding only the final answer of a multi-step calculation","back":"Rounding part-way through a calculation pushes the final answer away from the true value; keep full accuracy through the working and round only the final answer.","code":"4.01a","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Recall: rounding to a given number of decimal places or significant figures — the deciding digit is the one straight after the last one kept.","Recall: to estimate a calculation, round every number to 1 significant figure and work out the simplified calculation; use ≈.","Recall: rounded to the nearest u, value − u/2 ≤ x < value + u/2.","Recall: truncated at a given place, value ≤ x < value + 1 in the last place kept.","Recall (Higher tier): UB(a + b) = UB(a) + UB(b), LB(a + b) = LB(a) + LB(b), UB(a − b) = UB(a) − LB(b), LB(a − b) = LB(a) − UB(b).","Recall (Higher tier): for positive a and b, UB(ab) = UB(a) × UB(b), LB(ab) = LB(a) × LB(b), UB(a ÷ b) = UB(a) ÷ LB(b), LB(a ÷ b) = LB(a) ÷ UB(b).","Recall (Higher tier): a discrete quantity's true range stops one whole unit below its rounding upper limit, unlike a continuous measurement's.","Not on the formulae sheet: OCR's Foundation and Higher tier formulae sheets (June and November 2026 series) print area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine/cosine/tangent ratios, compound interest, and the probability addition rule — the Higher sheet adds the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, and the probability multiplication rule. None of those results belongs to rounding, estimation, error intervals or bounds, so every rule in this room's facts must be recalled from memory, on both tiers and on every paper. The sheet is reissued for each series, so read the one printed with your own paper."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:43:59.868Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.8,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark tally and worked example re-solved and correct (14 questions, 6 examples, 4 check and 4 exit items); the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md exactly and the Higher-tier segment is correctly limited to two labelled questions — fix the missing OCR paper facts and the duplicated exit-ticket item before publishing."}}},"ratio-proportion":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"ratio-proportion","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Ratio and proportion is the thread that ties together fractions, percentages and algebra, and it is the mathematics behind everyday decisions: sharing money fairly, scaling a recipe, converting currency on holiday, or working out how much a savings account or a loan will be worth after several years. A ratio question is rarely a single step — it usually asks you to simplify, share, compare and interpret in the same problem, so the biggest source of lost marks is skipping the ordering step (getting units the same, or deciding which quantity is the subject) rather than the arithmetic itself. Recognising whether a relationship is direct or inverse, and writing it as an equation before substituting, is the habit this room is built around.","idea":[{"h":"Simplifying a ratio, including the form 1 : n","t":"A ratio a : b compares two quantities of the same kind. To simplify one, first write both parts in the same unit, then divide every part by their highest common factor: 50 cm : 1.5 m becomes 50 : 150 once both are in centimetres, and dividing by 50 gives 1 : 3. A ratio can also be written in the form 1 : n by dividing both parts by the first number, which is useful for comparing scales or rates directly. Converting the units before simplifying is the step a mark scheme looks for, so it is always worth writing down even when it feels obvious.","code":"5.01a"},{"h":"Dividing a quantity into two or more parts in a given ratio","t":"To split a quantity in the ratio a : b (or a : b : c for three or more parts), add the parts to find the total number of parts, divide the quantity by that total to find the value of one part, then multiply by each number in the ratio. Sharing £60 in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5 gives 10 parts, one part worth £6, so the shares are £12, £18 and £30 — and checking that they add back to £60 is the quickest way to catch a mistake. The reverse also matters: given one share and the ratio, you can work backwards to find the whole quantity or another share.","code":"5.01b"},{"h":"Writing each part of a ratio as a fraction of the whole","t":"A ratio a : b splits a whole into a + b equal parts, so the first quantity is the fraction a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). Splitting £9 in the ratio 2 : 1 gives shares of 2/3 × £9 and 1/3 × £9, that is £6 and £3. This is different from writing the ratio itself: the ratio 2 : 1 compares the two shares to each other, while the fractions 2/3 and 1/3 compare each share to the total. Both descriptions come from the same total number of parts, a + b.","code":"5.01c"},{"h":"Solving ratio and proportion problems, including recipes and the link to linear functions","t":"Many ratio problems are solved with the unitary method: find the value of one unit of whatever is being shared or scaled, then multiply up or down to the amount wanted — a recipe for 6 people scales to 4 people by finding the amount per person first. A ratio a : b also defines a straight-line relationship between the two quantities: the multiplier from the first quantity to the second is always b/a, so plotting the second quantity against the first gives a line through the origin with gradient b/a, the linear function y = (b/a)x.","code":"5.01d"},{"h":"Direct proportion, including the formal relationship y = kx","t":"Two quantities are in direct proportion when their ratio stays the same: double one and the other doubles. Informally this is solved with the unitary method or by scaling a known pair of values, which is exactly how currency conversion works — if £1 buys $1.28, £20 buys 20 × $1.28. Formally, y directly proportional to x is written y = kx for a constant k: substitute a known pair of values to find k, write the full equation, then use it to find any other value. y₁/x₁ = y₂/x₂ says the same thing using equal ratios.","code":"5.02a"},{"h":"Inverse proportion, including the formal relationship y = k/x","t":"Two quantities are in inverse proportion when their product stays the same: double one and the other halves. This is common in rate problems — if a job takes 6 workers 10 days, doubling the workers to 12 halves the time to 5 days, because the total worker-days stays at 60. Formally, y inversely proportional to x is written y = k/x for a constant k, equivalently xy = k: substitute a known pair to find k, write the equation, then substitute again. Deciding direct or inverse first is the mark that everything else depends on.","code":"5.02b"},{"h":"Simple interest and growth or decay by repeated percentage change","t":"Simple interest pays the same amount each period, always a percentage of the original sum: interest = P × r/100 × n, for principal P, rate r% and n periods. Growth and decay by a fixed percentage each period instead multiply the current amount, so a 3% yearly rise is × 1.03 each year and a 12% yearly fall is × 0.88 each year — this compounding is what makes compound interest grow faster than simple interest over time. The rate can also change from one period to the next, as with a car that depreciates by different percentages in successive years: apply each year's multiplier in turn.","code":"5.03a"}],"examples":[{"title":"Simplifying a ratio given in mixed units","setup":"Write 50 cm : 1.5 m as a ratio in its simplest form.","steps":["Convert 1.5 m into centimetres: 1.5 × 100 = 150 cm.","Write the ratio with both parts in the same unit: 50 : 150.","Divide both parts by their highest common factor, 50: 50 ÷ 50 : 150 ÷ 50 = 1 : 3."],"cues":["What unit should both lengths be changed into before they can be compared?","Once both lengths are in centimetres, how do you write the ratio?","What is the largest number that divides exactly into both 50 and 150?"]},{"title":"Dividing an amount in a ratio of three parts","setup":"A prize fund of £60 is divided between three winners in the ratio 2 : 3 : 5. Work out each winner's share.","steps":["Add the parts of the ratio: 2 + 3 + 5 = 10.","Divide £60 by the total number of parts: £60 ÷ 10 = £6 for one part.","Multiply £6 by each number in the ratio: 2 × £6 = £12, 3 × £6 = £18 and 5 × £6 = £30, which check back to £12 + £18 + £30 = £60. The three winners receive £12, £18 and £30."],"cues":["How many equal parts is the £60 being split into altogether?","How much money does one part of the ratio represent?","How do you turn the value of one part into each of the three shares, and what should they add up to?"]},{"title":"Sharing an amount and writing each share as a fraction","setup":"£9 is split between two people in the ratio 2 : 1. Work out each person's share, and write each share as a fraction of the £9.","steps":["Add the parts of the ratio: 2 + 1 = 3.","Each part is worth £9 ÷ 3 = £3.","The two shares are 2 × £3 = £6 and 1 × £3 = £3.","As fractions of the whole, the shares are 2/3 and 1/3."],"cues":["How many equal parts does the ratio split the £9 into?","How much is one part of the ratio worth?","What are the two shares in pounds?","How is a share written as a fraction of the total, using the numbers in the ratio?"]},{"title":"Direct proportion with an unknown constant","setup":"The cost, C pounds, of ribbon is directly proportional to its length, L metres. When L = 4, C = 7. Work out the cost of 10 metres of ribbon. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Direct proportion means C = kL for some constant k.","Substitute the known pair of values: 7 = k × 4, so k = 7 ÷ 4 = 1.75.","Write the full equation: C = 1.75L.","Substitute L = 10: C = 1.75 × 10 = £17.50."],"cues":["What equation form does direct proportion always take?","How do you find k once you know one matching pair of values?","What does the equation look like once k is known?","What value of L do you substitute to answer the question?"]},{"title":"Inverse proportion with an unknown constant","setup":"A train covers a fixed distance. Its time, T hours, is inversely proportional to its speed, S km/h. When S = 80, T = 3. Work out the time taken when the speed is 60 km/h. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Inverse proportion means T = k/S for some constant k.","Substitute the known pair of values: k = S × T = 80 × 3 = 240.","Write the full equation, T = 240/S, ready to use it at the speed of 60 km/h asked about.","Substitute S = 60: T = 240 ÷ 60 = 4 hours."],"cues":["What equation form links two quantities in inverse proportion?","Which two known values multiply together to give k?","What does the equation look like once k is known?","What value of S do you substitute to answer the question?"]},{"title":"Compound interest on a savings account","setup":"£2000 is invested in a savings account paying 3% compound interest per year. Work out the value of the investment after 4 years, to the nearest penny. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Each year the amount is multiplied by 1 + 3/100 = 1.03.","Over 4 years, that multiplier is applied 4 times: value = 2000 × 1.03⁴.","1.03⁴ = 1.12550881.","2000 × 1.12550881 = 2251.01762, so to the nearest penny the value is £2251.02."],"cues":["What is the yearly multiplier for a 3% increase?","How many times is that multiplier applied over 4 years?","What is the value of the multiplier raised to the power 4?","How should the money answer be rounded?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write 18 : 24 in its simplest form.","a":"3 : 4"},{"q":"In the ratio 2 : 5, what fraction of the whole is the first part?","a":"2/7"},{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. If x doubles, what happens to y?","a":"y also doubles"},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x. If x doubles, what happens to y?","a":"y halves"},{"q":"What multiplier gives a 6% decrease?","a":"0.94"}]},"room":{"facts":["From a ratio a : b: first share = a/(a + b) of the whole, second share = b/(a + b) of the whole.","To divide a quantity in the ratio a : b: total parts = a + b, value of one part = quantity ÷ total parts, then multiply by each number in the ratio.","Direct proportion: y = kx (a straight line through the origin). Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k.","Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, for principal P, rate r% per period and n periods.","Repeated percentage growth or decay: a rise of r% each period is × (1 + r/100) each period; a fall of r% each period is × (1 − r/100) each period.","OCR GCSE (9–1) Mathematics J560 is assessed by three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes: J560/01–03 (Foundation, grades 5–1) and J560/04–06 (Higher, grades 9–4). Paper 2 and Paper 5 are non-calculator; Papers 1, 3, 4 and 6 allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"Write the ratio 36 : 24 in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"3 : 2","sol":"The highest common factor of 36 and 24 is 12. 36 ÷ 12 = 3 and 24 ÷ 12 = 2, so the ratio is 3 : 2. B1 for 3 : 2.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Both numbers in a ratio can be divided by the same amount without changing what it compares.","Look for the largest number that goes exactly into both 36 and 24.","Write 36 ÷ n : 24 ÷ n, where n is that common factor."],"codes":["5.01a"]},{"q":"Write 8 : 20 in the form 1 : n. (non-calculator)","a":"1 : 2.5","sol":"To write a ratio in the form 1 : n, divide both parts by the first number. 8 ÷ 8 = 1 and 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5. B1 for 1 : 2.5.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The form 1 : n has a 1 as the first number.","Whatever you divide the first number by, you must divide the second number by too.","Divide both 8 and 20 by 8."],"codes":["5.01a"]},{"q":"Divide £40 in the ratio 3 : 5. (non-calculator)","a":"£15 and £25","sol":"There are 3 + 5 = 8 parts altogether. M1 for £40 ÷ 8 = £5, the value of one part. Then 3 × £5 = £15 and 5 × £5 = £25, and £15 + £25 = £40 as a check. A1 for £15 and £25.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two numbers in the ratio count parts, not pounds.","Find how much one part is worth before working out either share.","£40 ÷ (3 + 5)"],"codes":["5.01b"]},{"q":"In the ratio 3 : 7, what fraction of the whole is the second part? (non-calculator)","a":"7/10","sol":"The whole is split into 3 + 7 = 10 equal parts, and the second part is 7 of those 10 parts, so it is 7/10 of the whole. B1 for 7/10.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio splits a whole into a number of equal parts.","Add the two numbers in the ratio to find the total number of parts.","Put the second number in the ratio over the total number of parts."],"codes":["5.01c"]},{"q":"A map has a scale of 1 cm : 5 km. A distance between two towns on the map measures 3 cm. Work out the real distance between the towns, in km. (non-calculator)","a":"15 km","sol":"The scale means every 1 cm on the map represents 5 km in real life. M1 for multiplying the map distance by the scale factor: 3 × 5. 3 × 5 = 15. A1 for 15 km.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The scale gives the number of real kilometres that one map centimetre stands for.","Multiply the map distance by how many kilometres one centimetre represents.","3 × 5"],"codes":["5.01d"]},{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. y = 15 when x = 3. Find the value of y when x = 7. Calculator may be used.","a":"35","sol":"Direct proportion means y = kx. M1 for k = 15 ÷ 3 = 5. So y = 5x: M1 for 5 × 7. y = 35. A1 for 35.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["In direct proportion the two quantities always divide to give the same constant.","Write the relationship as an equation with a constant in it, then find that constant.","y = kx, so k = 15 ÷ 3"],"codes":["5.02a"]},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x. y = 10 when x = 4. Find the value of y when x = 8. Calculator may be used.","a":"5","sol":"Inverse proportion means xy = k. M1 for k = 4 × 10 = 40. So y = 40/x: M1 for 40 ÷ 8. y = 5. A1 for 5.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["In inverse proportion the product of the two quantities stays the same.","Use the given pair of values to find that constant product.","k = 4 × 10, then y = k ÷ 8"],"codes":["5.02b"]},{"q":"A recipe for 5 people uses 250 g of rice. Work out the amount of rice needed for 8 people. (non-calculator)","a":"400 g","sol":"The unitary method finds the amount needed for one person first, then scales up to 8 people. M1 for a complete method: 250 ÷ 5 × 8. 250 ÷ 5 × 8 = 400. A1 for 400 g.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The unitary method finds the amount for one person first.","Divide the amount of rice by the number of people it is currently feeding.","250 ÷ 5, then multiply by 8"],"codes":["5.01d"]},{"q":"Calculate the simple interest earned when £600 is invested at 4% per year for 3 years. Calculator may be used.","a":"£72","sol":"Simple interest = P × r/100 × n. M1 for a complete method: 600 × 4/100 × 3. 600 × 4/100 × 3 = 72. A1 for £72.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Simple interest pays the same amount of interest every year.","Work out one year's interest as a percentage of the original amount, then scale up.","600 × 4/100, then multiply by 3"],"codes":["5.03a"]},{"q":"The exchange rate is £1 = €1.15. Convert £40 into euros. Calculator may be used.","a":"€46","sol":"Pounds and euros are in direct proportion. M1 for multiplying by the exchange rate: 40 × 1.15. 40 × 1.15 = 46. A1 for €46.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The number of euros is directly proportional to the number of pounds.","Multiply the amount in pounds by however many euros one pound is worth.","40 × 1.15"],"codes":["5.02a"]},{"q":"A car is bought for £20 000. Its value depreciates by 10% in the first year, 15% in the second year and 20% in the third year. Work out its value at the end of the three years, to the nearest pound. Calculator may be used.","a":"£12 240","sol":"The three multipliers are 0.90, 0.85 and 0.80. M1 for identifying all three multipliers. Value = 20 000 × 0.90 × 0.85 × 0.80: M1 for a complete method multiplying by all three in turn. 0.90 × 0.85 × 0.80 = 0.612: A1 for the combined multiplier 0.612. 20 000 × 0.612 = 12 240: A1 for £12 240.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each year's fall is a percentage of that year's value, not of the original £20 000.","Turn each year's percentage fall into a multiplier, then apply the three multipliers in turn.","20 000 × 0.90 × 0.85 × 0.80"],"codes":["5.03a"]},{"q":"It takes 8 taps 15 minutes to fill a tank, all the taps running at the same constant rate. Work out how long it would take 5 taps, working at the same rate, to fill the same tank. Calculator may be used.","a":"24 minutes","sol":"The number of taps and the time are inversely proportional, so their product stays constant. M1 for 8 × 15 = 120 (the total tap-minutes needed). M1 for dividing by the new number of taps: 120 ÷ 5. 120 ÷ 5 = 24. A1 for 24 minutes.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Fewer taps take longer, so this is not a direct proportion.","Find the total amount of tap-time the tank needs, which stays the same how ever many taps are used.","8 × 15, then divide by 5"],"codes":["5.02b"]},{"q":"Squash A is orange concentrate and water in the ratio 1 : 4. Squash B is orange concentrate and water in the ratio 2 : 9. Which squash has the greater proportion of orange concentrate? You must show your working. Calculator may be used.","a":"Squash A","sol":"Write each as the fraction of the drink that is concentrate: M1 for 1/5 for A and 2/11 for B. A common denominator is 55: M1 for 1/5 = 11/55 and 2/11 = 10/55. A1 for both correct fractions (or decimals 0.2 and 0.1818...). 11/55 is greater than 10/55, so Squash A has the greater proportion of concentrate. A1 for Squash A with the comparison shown.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two ratios compare concentrate with water, so they cannot be compared directly.","Write the amount of concentrate as a fraction of the whole drink for each squash.","Squash A is 1/5 concentrate"],"codes":["5.01a","5.01c"]},{"q":"£360 is shared between Priya, Sam and Tom in the ratio 2 : 3 : 4. Priya then invests her share for 2 years at 5% per year simple interest. Work out the total amount of interest Priya earns. Calculator may be used.","a":"£8","sol":"There are 2 + 3 + 4 = 9 parts, so M1 for 360 ÷ 9 = 40, the value of one part. Priya's share is 2 × 40 = 80: A1 for £80. Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, so M1 for 80 × 5/100 × 2. 80 × 5/100 × 2 = 8. A1 for £8.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two separate ideas are joined here: a share in a ratio, then simple interest on that share.","Work out Priya's share in pounds before you touch the interest.","360 ÷ (2 + 3 + 4), then multiply by 2 for Priya's share"],"codes":["5.01b","5.03a"]},{"q":"Sam invests £500. After 4 years, simple interest has added £80 to the investment. Work out the annual rate of simple interest. Calculator may be used.","a":"4%","sol":"Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, so 80 = 500 × r/100 × 4. M1 for setting up this equation (or for finding one year's interest, 80 ÷ 4 = 20). 500 × 4/100 = 20 is one year's interest at 1%, and 80 ÷ 4 = 20 is the actual yearly interest, so M1 for matching these. r = 20 ÷ 500 × 100 = 4. A1 for 4%.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Simple interest adds the same amount of interest every year.","Work out how much interest was earned in one year before finding the rate.","£80 ÷ 4 = £20 per year, so r/100 = 20 ÷ 500"],"codes":["5.03a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Simplify the ratio 28 : 35.","a":"4 : 5"},{"q":"Divide £50 in the ratio 1 : 4.","a":"£10 and £40"},{"q":"Work out the simple interest on £800 at 5% per year for 2 years.","a":"£80"},{"q":"y is directly proportional to x. When x = 6, y = 18. Find y when x = 2.","a":"6"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Ratio in its simplest form","back":"Both parts in the same unit, then divide every part by their highest common factor: 50 cm : 1.5 m = 50 : 150 = 1 : 3.","code":"5.01a","open":false},{"front":"Ratio in the form 1 : n","back":"Divide both parts of the ratio by the first number, so the first number becomes 1. 8 : 20 becomes 1 : 2.5.","code":"5.01a","open":false},{"front":"Dividing a quantity in a given ratio","back":"Add the parts to get the total number of parts, divide the quantity by that total to find one part, then multiply by each number in the ratio.","code":"5.01b","open":false},{"front":"Dividing a quantity in a ratio of three or more parts","back":"The same method as for two parts: add all the parts for the total, find the value of one part, then multiply by each number. The shares must add back to the whole.","code":"5.01b","open":false},{"front":"Fraction of the whole from a ratio a : b","back":"The first quantity is a/(a + b) of the whole and the second is b/(a + b). The ratio 2 : 1 gives fractions 2/3 and 1/3.","code":"5.01c","open":false},{"front":"The unitary method","back":"Find the value of one unit (one item, one person, one metre) first, then multiply to find the value of any other number of units.","code":"5.01d","open":false},{"front":"Ratio and the linear function it defines","back":"A ratio a : b gives a constant multiplier b/a from the first quantity to the second, so plotting the second against the first gives a line through the origin with gradient b/a.","code":"5.01d","open":false},{"front":"Direct proportion","back":"y = kx for a constant k: the ratio y : x is always the same, doubling x doubles y, and the graph is a straight line through the origin.","code":"5.02a","open":false},{"front":"An example of two directly proportional quantities","back":"cost and quantity bought at a fixed price; distance and time at constant speed; ingredients and number of servings in a recipe","code":"5.02a","open":true},{"front":"Inverse proportion","back":"y = k/x for a constant k, equivalently xy = k: doubling x halves y, and the graph is a curve falling towards the axes.","code":"5.02b","open":false},{"front":"Simple interest formula","back":"Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, for principal P, rate r% per period and n periods. The same amount of interest is added every period.","code":"5.03a","open":false},{"front":"Compound interest formula","back":"Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, where P is the amount invested, r the percentage rate per period and n the number of periods.","code":"5.03a","open":false},{"front":"Growth and decay by repeated percentage change","back":"A rise of r% each period is × (1 + r/100) each period; a fall of r% each period is × (1 − r/100) each period, applied once per period in turn.","code":"5.03a","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ — compound interest or repeated percentage growth, where P is the starting amount, r the percentage rate per period and n the number of periods. Printed on the sheet: OCR's Foundation tier formulae sheet (for June and November 2026) prints 'compound interest (the total accrued)', so this is given to you in the exam. It is not in the specification's own list of formulae you must know (6.02d only lists circle, Pythagoras and trigonometry formulae for this tier), but know what the letters mean and be ready to use it.","Simple interest = P × r/100 × n, for principal P, rate r% per period and n periods. Not printed on the Foundation formulae sheet and not in 6.02d: recall it, and do not confuse it with the compound interest formula above — simple interest adds the same amount every period.","From a ratio a : b: first share = a/(a + b) of the whole, second share = b/(a + b) of the whole. Not printed on the sheet and not in 6.02d: recall it.","Direct proportion: y = kx. Inverse proportion: y = k/x, equivalently xy = k. Not printed on the sheet and not in 6.02d: recall these, and write the equation down before substituting — that step earns the method mark.","To divide a quantity in the ratio a : b: total parts = a + b, value of one part = quantity ÷ total parts, then multiply by each number in the ratio. Not a formula the sheet prints, but the line of working the mark scheme expects to see.","The specification's own rule (2b) is that no formula sheet is provided and only 6.02d's list is required knowledge; everything else needed is given in the assessment. Since 2022 Ofqual has required every GCSE Mathematics paper to carry a formulae sheet regardless, and OCR's Foundation insert for 2026 also prints the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios and the addition rule for probability — none of which belongs to this room. Of this room's results, only compound interest is on that sheet; check the sheet at the front of your own paper, as it is reissued each series."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:49:07.414Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-judged from the current kit JSON: all 15 answer keys, 6 worked examples, 5 check questions and 4 exit-ticket answers re-solved in node and every one is correct, and all 15 sol mark tallies now add to m exactly (3, 4, 7, 8 and 10 all verified). The new map-scale question 4 (1 cm : 5 km, 3 cm → 15 km) is correct, non-calculator and hand-workable, sits within 5.01d and reuses no example context; room.facts now carries OCR paper structure (J560/01–03 and /04–06, 100 marks, 1 hour 30 minutes, Papers 2 and 5 non-calculator, grades 5–1 and 9–4); example 2 states its ask and ends on the answer; the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md. Publishable — only the slightly overloaded final step of example 2 keeps it off a clean 1.0."}}},"ratio-proportion-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"ratio-proportion-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Ratio and proportion move from arithmetic to algebra once a relationship involves a power or a root: OCR's Higher tier paper asks you to formulate the equation y = kxⁿ or y = k/xⁿ from a sentence, find the constant of proportionality from one pair of values, and then use the completed equation forwards or backwards — that first 'equation with k in it' line is where the method mark sits. Growth and decay is the same idea of repeated multiplication written as a formula, A = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ, reversed to find a missing rate, a missing time or a missing starting value, and sometimes applied one step at a time as an iterative process. Both threads are Higher-tier-only additions, building on the plain proportion and percentage-change work of the Foundation room, and they carry AO2 marks for constructing the model and AO3 marks for solving the multi-step problem in context.","idea":[{"h":"Formulating and solving direct proportion to a power or root","t":"Turn the words into an equation with a constant. 'y is directly proportional to x²' is y = kx²; 'to the cube of x' is y = kx³; 'to the square root of x' is y = k√x. The method never changes: write the equation with k in it first, substitute the one pair of values the question gives to find k, write the completed equation, then substitute the new value — that first line is where the method mark sits, so write it down even when the answer can be seen. Working backwards is examined just as often: given y, rearrange the completed equation to find x, remembering to take the matching root or power at the end. On a graph, y = kxⁿ is a curve through the origin that gets steeper as x grows; y = k√x is a curve through the origin that flattens out.","code":"5.02a"},{"h":"Formulating and solving inverse proportion to a power or root","t":"'y is inversely proportional to x²' means y is proportional to 1/x², so y = k/x², and 'inversely proportional to the square root of x' is y = k/√x. Because yxⁿ (or y√x) stays constant, a table of values can be tested for this kind of proportion by checking that the product is the same for every pair, which proves the relationship rather than assumes it. The same method as for direct proportion applies: write the equation with k in it, substitute the given pair to find k, write the completed equation, then substitute the new value or rearrange to find x. On a graph, y = k/xⁿ is a curve that falls towards the x-axis as x grows and rises steeply as x approaches zero, never touching either axis.","code":"5.02b"},{"h":"Growth and decay expressed as a formula, including iterative processes","t":"A quantity that changes by the same percentage every period follows a repeated multiplication that can be written as a single formula: A = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ for growth (for example compound interest) or A = P(1 − r/100)ⁿ for decay (for example depreciation), where P is the starting value, r the percentage rate per period and n the number of periods. This is an iterative process seen as a whole: each period applies the rule xₙ₊₁ = xₙ × multiplier to the previous value. The formula also runs backwards — given the overall change over n periods, the multiplier for one period is the nth root of the overall multiplier, which converts back into a percentage rate. Not every iteration is a single multiplier: a rule such as xₙ₊₁ = a·xₙ + b, where part is lost and a fixed amount is also added or removed each period, must be applied one line at a time from the starting value, and every intermediate value carries a method mark.","code":"5.03a"}],"examples":[{"title":"Energy stored in a stretched spring","setup":"The energy E joules stored in a stretched spring is directly proportional to the square of its extension x centimetres. When x = 2 cm, E = 12 J. Work out E when x = 5 cm. (non-calculator)","steps":["Directly proportional to the square of x means E = kx², where k is the constant of proportionality.","Substitute the pair given, x = 2 and E = 12: 12 = k × 2² = 4k, so k = 12 ÷ 4 = 3.","The completed equation is therefore E = 3x².","Now substitute the new extension: E = 3 × 5² = 3 × 25 = 75 J."],"cues":["What power of x does 'the square of x' put into the equation?","Which pair of values can be put into that equation to pin down the constant?","With the constant known, what connects E and x now?","What replaces x to answer the question asked?"]},{"title":"Current inversely proportional to a square root","setup":"The current I amps in a circuit is inversely proportional to the square root of the resistance R ohms. When R = 4, I = 6. Work out I when R = 9. (non-calculator)","steps":["Inversely proportional to the square root of R means I = k/√R, where k is the constant of proportionality.","Substitute the pair given, R = 4 and I = 6: 6 = k/√4 = k/2, so k = 6 × 2 = 12.","The completed equation is I = 12/√R.","Substitute R = 9 into the completed equation: I = 12/√9.","√9 = 3, so I = 12/3 = 4 A."],"cues":["What does 'inversely proportional to the square root of R' do to R in the equation?","Which pair of values can be put into that equation to pin down the constant?","Now that k is known, what single equation connects I and R?","What replaces R in that equation to answer the question asked?","What is the square root of 9, and what does dividing 12 by it give?"]},{"title":"A growing colony written as a formula","setup":"A colony of insects grows by 15% every year. There are 200 insects at the start. Write a formula for the number of insects P after n years, and use it to work out the number of insects after 4 years, to the nearest whole insect. A calculator may be used.","steps":["A 15% increase every year multiplies the previous year's number by 1 + 15/100 = 1.15, the same multiplier applied again each year.","After n years the multiplier has been applied n times, so the formula is P = 200 × 1.15ⁿ.","Substitute n = 4: P = 200 × 1.15⁴.","1.15⁴ = 1.74900625, so P = 200 × 1.74900625 = 349.80125, which rounds to 350 insects."],"cues":["What single multiplier does a 15% yearly increase apply?","How many times is that multiplier applied after n years, and what formula does that give?","What replaces n to answer for 4 years?","How do you turn the calculator value into a whole number of insects?"]},{"title":"Gas pressure inversely proportional to a square, reversed","setup":"The pressure P of a fixed mass of gas is inversely proportional to the square of its volume V. When V = 2, P = 20. Work out V when P = 5. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Inversely proportional to the square of V means P = k/V², where k is the constant of proportionality.","Substitute the pair given, V = 2 and P = 20: 20 = k/2² = k/4, so k = 20 × 4 = 80.","The completed equation is P = 80/V².","Substitute P = 5 into the completed equation: 5 = 80/V², so V² = 80 ÷ 5 = 16.","Taking the positive square root, V = 4."],"cues":["What power of V does 'the square of V' put into the equation?","Which pair of values can be put into that equation to pin down the constant?","Now that k is known, what single equation connects P and V?","What does substituting P = 5 give for V²?","What operation undoes a square, and which root makes sense for a volume?"]},{"title":"A water tank: evaporation and a weekly top-up","setup":"A water tank is topped up every week. At the end of each week 10% of the water in the tank evaporates, and then 50 litres of fresh water is added. Let wₙ be the amount of water in the tank at the end of the nth week, starting from w₀ = 300 litres before any evaporation or top-up. Work out w₂, the amount at the end of the second week. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Losing 10% leaves 90% of the water, so each week multiplies by 0.9 and then adds the 50 litres that are topped up: wₙ₊₁ = 0.9wₙ + 50.","Apply the rule once, starting from w₀ = 300: w₁ = 0.9 × 300 + 50 = 270 + 50 = 320.","Apply the same rule to that output: w₂ = 0.9 × 320 + 50 = 288 + 50 = 338 litres."],"cues":["What single multiplier is left once a tenth of the water has evaporated, and what rule combines that with the top-up?","Which value goes into the rule first?","What is the input to the rule the second time it is used?"]},{"title":"A balloon's volume proportional to the cube of its radius, reversed","setup":"The volume V cubic centimetres of a spherical balloon is directly proportional to the cube of its radius r centimetres. When r = 3, V = 108. Work out r when V = 32. (non-calculator)","steps":["Directly proportional to the cube of r means V = kr³, where k is the constant of proportionality.","Substitute the pair given, r = 3 and V = 108: 108 = k × 3³ = 27k, so k = 108 ÷ 27 = 4.","The completed equation is V = 4r³.","Substitute V = 32 into the completed equation: 32 = 4r³, so r³ = 32 ÷ 4 = 8.","Taking the cube root, r = 2 cm."],"cues":["What power of r does 'the cube of r' put into the equation?","Which pair of values can be put into that equation to pin down the constant?","Now that k is known, what single equation connects V and r?","What does substituting V = 32 give for r³?","What operation undoes a cube?"]}],"check":[{"q":"P is directly proportional to the square of t. When t = 4, P = 48. Write down the equation connecting P and t.","a":"P = 3t² (since k = 48 ÷ 16 = 3)"},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to the square root of x. When x = 9, y = 12. Work out y when x = 4.","a":"18 (k = 12 × 3 = 36, so y = 36/√x = 36 ÷ 2)"},{"q":"A quantity of 250 grows by 4% each year. Work out its value after 2 years.","a":"270.4 (250 × 1.04²)"},{"q":"A quantity is multiplied by 1.44 over 2 equal yearly steps, the same percentage rate each year. Work out the percentage increase per year.","a":"20% (√1.44 = 1.2)"}]},"room":{"facts":["Direct proportion to a power or root: if y is directly proportional to xⁿ (or to √x), then y = kxⁿ (or y = k√x). Substitute the one pair of values given to find k, then use the completed equation for any other value.","Inverse proportion to a power or root: if y is inversely proportional to xⁿ (or to √x), then y = k/xⁿ (or y = k/√x), so yxⁿ (or y√x) stays constant — a constant product is the test for this kind of proportion.","Growth and decay as a formula: a quantity changing by the same percentage every period follows A = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ for growth or A = P(1 − r/100)ⁿ for decay, where P is the starting value, r the percentage rate per period and n the number of periods.","Reversing growth or decay: if the overall multiplier over n equal periods is M, the multiplier for one period is the nth root of M, ⁿ√M, which can be turned back into a percentage rate.","An iterative growth or decay rule need not be a single multiplier: a recurrence such as xₙ₊₁ = a·xₙ + b, where part is lost and a fixed amount added each period, is applied one step at a time from the starting value, each output becoming the next input.","OCR sets three exam papers per tier: J560/01–03 for Foundation and J560/04–06 for Higher, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator; grades awarded run 5–1 on Foundation tier and 9–4 on Higher tier."],"questions":[{"q":"y is directly proportional to x². When x = 3, y = 27. Work out an equation connecting y and x. (non-calculator)","a":"y = 3x²","sol":"Directly proportional to the square of x means y = kx². Substituting the pair given, 27 = k × 3² = 9k. M1 for 27 = k × 3² (or k = 27 ÷ 9). So k = 3 and the equation is y = 3x². A1 for y = 3x².","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Direct proportion to a power means y is a fixed multiple of that power of x, not of x itself.","Write the relationship with a constant in front of x², then use the pair of values given to find that constant.","The first line is y = kx², with the given pair substituted into it."],"codes":["5.02a"]},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to the cube of x. When x = 2, y = 3. Work out y when x = 1. (non-calculator)","a":"24","sol":"Inversely proportional to the cube of x means y = k/x³. Substituting x = 2 and y = 3 gives 3 = k/2³ = k/8, so k = 3 × 8 = 24. M1 for k = 24. When x = 1, y = 24/1³ = 24. A1 for 24.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Under this kind of inverse proportion it is yx³ that never changes, not yx.","Find the constant from the pair of values you are given, then use it with the new value of x.","The first line is y = k/x³, with the known pair substituted to find k."],"codes":["5.02b"]},{"q":"A quantity is 300 at the start and increases by 10% each year. Work out its value after 2 years. (non-calculator)","a":"363","sol":"A 10% increase multiplies by 1.1 each year, the same multiplier applied twice, so the value after 2 years is 300 × 1.1². M1 for 300 × 1.1² (or the chain 330, 363 seen). 1.1² = 1.21, and 300 × 1.21 = 363. A1 for 363.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The same percentage increase applies to the value at the start of each year.","Turn the 10% increase into a multiplier and apply it once for each of the two years.","The first line is 300 multiplied by the yearly multiplier squared."],"codes":["5.03a"]},{"q":"T is directly proportional to the square root of x. When x = 49, T = 21. Work out an equation connecting T and x. A calculator may be used.","a":"T = 3√x","sol":"Directly proportional to the square root of x means T = k√x. Substituting x = 49 and T = 21 gives 21 = k × √49 = 7k. M1 for 21 = k × √49 (or k = 21 ÷ 7). So k = 3 and the equation is T = 3√x. A1 for T = 3√x.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["It is the square root of x, not x itself, that T is a fixed multiple of.","Write the relationship with a constant in front of √x, then substitute the pair of values given.","The first line is T = k√x, with the given pair substituted into it."],"codes":["5.02a"]},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to √x. When x = 16, y = 10. Work out y when x = 4. A calculator may be used.","a":"20","sol":"Inversely proportional to √x means y = k/√x. Substituting x = 16 and y = 10 gives 10 = k/√16 = k/4, so k = 10 × 4 = 40. M1 for k = 40. The completed equation is y = 40/√x. A1 for y = 40/√x (oe). When x = 4, y = 40/√4 = 40/2 = 20. A1 for 20.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["It is the square root of x, not x itself, that y is inversely proportional to.","Find the constant from the pair of values you are given, then write the completed equation before using the new value of x.","The first line is y = k/√x, with the known pair substituted to find k."],"codes":["5.02b"]},{"q":"A quantity is 500 and decreases by 8% each year. Work out its value after 2 years, to the nearest whole number. A calculator may be used.","a":"423","sol":"An 8% decrease multiplies by 1 − 0.08 = 0.92 each year. B1 for the multiplier 0.92. After 2 years the value is 500 × 0.92². M1 for 500 × 0.92². 0.92² = 0.8464, and 500 × 0.8464 = 423.2, which rounds to 423. A1 for 423.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The same percentage decrease applies to the value at the start of each year, not to the original value each time.","Turn the 8% decrease into a multiplier below 1, then apply it once for each year.","The first line is 500 multiplied by the yearly multiplier squared."],"codes":["5.03a"]},{"q":"The mass M grams of a cube-shaped crystal is directly proportional to the cube of its side length L cm. When L = 2, M = 40. Work out L when M = 135. (non-calculator)","a":"3","sol":"Directly proportional to the cube of L means M = kL³. Substituting L = 2 and M = 40 gives 40 = k × 2³ = 8k, so k = 5. M1 for k = 5. The completed equation is M = 5L³; substituting M = 135 gives 135 = 5L³, so L³ = 27. M1 for L³ = 27. Taking the cube root, L = 3. A1 for L = 3.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["It is the cube of L, not L itself, that M is a fixed multiple of.","Find the constant first, then rearrange the completed equation to get at L.","The first line is M = kL³, with the pair of values given substituted to find the constant."],"codes":["5.02a"]},{"q":"F is inversely proportional to the square of d. When d = 3, F = 40. Work out d when F = 10. (non-calculator)","a":"6","sol":"Inversely proportional to the square of d means F = k/d². Substituting d = 3 and F = 40 gives 40 = k/3² = k/9, so k = 360. M1 for 40 = k/9 (k = 360). A1 for k = 360 (or F = 360/d²). Putting F = 10: 10 = 360/d², so d² = 360 ÷ 10 = 36. M1 for d² = 36. Taking the positive root, d = 6. A1 for 6.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["F is not inversely proportional to d itself, but to the square of d.","Find the constant from the pair of values you are given, then put the new value of F into the completed equation and unpick it to reach d.","The first line is F = k/d², with the pair of values given substituted to find the constant."],"codes":["5.02b"]},{"q":"A quantity grows from 800 to 968 over 2 years, increasing by the same percentage each year. Work out the annual percentage increase. A calculator may be used.","a":"10%","sol":"The same yearly multiplier is applied twice, so (multiplier)² = 968/800 = 1.21. M1 for 968/800 = 1.21. Taking the square root, the yearly multiplier is √1.21 = 1.1. M1 for √1.21 = 1.1. A multiplier of 1.1 is 1 + 10/100, so the annual increase is 10%. A1 for 10%.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two years apply the same yearly multiplier twice.","Divide the final value by the starting value to get the overall multiplier, then undo the squaring.","The first line is 968 divided by 800."],"codes":["5.03a"]},{"q":"y is directly proportional to the square of x. The value of x is increased by 50%. Work out the percentage change in y. (non-calculator)","a":"An increase of 125%","sol":"Write y = kx². Increasing x by 50% replaces x by 1.5x. M1 for using 1.5x in place of x. The new value of y is k(1.5x)² = 2.25kx² = 2.25y. M1 for the multiplier 2.25. Since 2.25 = 1 + 1.25, y is multiplied by 2.25, an increase of 125%. A1 for an increase of 125%.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["No values are given, so work with the equation itself rather than with numbers.","Replace x by the increased value in the equation and see what multiplies y.","The first line is y = kx² with x replaced by the multiplier for a 50% increase times x."],"codes":["5.02a"]},{"q":"The time T hours for a team of workers to build a wall is inversely proportional to the square of the number of workers n. 4 workers take 9 hours. Work out how long 8 workers would take, giving your answer in hours and minutes. A calculator may be used.","a":"2 hours 15 minutes","sol":"Inversely proportional to the square of n means T = k/n². Substituting n = 4 and T = 9 gives 9 = k/16, so k = 144. M1 for k = 144. The completed equation is T = 144/n². A1 for T = 144/n² (oe). When n = 8, T = 144/64 = 2.25. M1 for 144/64 = 2.25. 2.25 hours is 2 hours and 0.25 × 60 = 15 minutes. A1 for 2 hours 15 minutes.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["More workers means less time, and it is the square of the number of workers that matters here.","Find the constant from the pair given, then use the completed equation with the new number of workers.","The first line is T = k/n², with the pair of values given substituted to find the constant."],"codes":["5.02b"]},{"q":"Town A has 5000 residents and grows by 6% each year. Town B has 7000 residents and shrinks by 4% each year. Work out the number of complete years after which Town A first has more residents than Town B. A calculator may be used.","a":"4 years","sol":"Each town's population is its own iterative process, so after n years the populations are 5000 × 1.06ⁿ and 7000 × 0.96ⁿ. M1 for 5000 × 1.06ⁿ. M1 for 7000 × 0.96ⁿ. Testing whole numbers of years: after 3 years, 5000 × 1.06³ ≈ 5955.08 and 7000 × 0.96³ ≈ 6193.15, so Town A is still smaller; after 4 years, 5000 × 1.06⁴ ≈ 6312.38 and 7000 × 0.96⁴ ≈ 5945.43, so Town A is now larger. M1 for both pairs of values compared. The first time is after 4 years. A1 for 4 years.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each population changes by the same percentage every year, so each is its own repeated multiplication.","Write each population after n years as its starting value times its own yearly multiplier raised to the power n, then try whole-number values of n until the order swaps.","The first line is each starting population multiplied by its own yearly multiplier raised to the power of the number of years."],"codes":["5.03a"]},{"q":"A chemical sample loses 15% of its mass every hour, and then 5 mg is added by an automatic dispenser. Let mₙ be the mass in mg after n hours, starting from m₀ = 40 mg before the first hour. Work out m₂, the mass after 2 hours. A calculator may be used.","a":"38.15 mg","sol":"Losing 15% leaves 85%, so the rule for each hour is mₙ₊₁ = 0.85mₙ + 5. B1 for the rule mₙ₊₁ = 0.85mₙ + 5. Applying it once from m₀ = 40: m₁ = 0.85 × 40 + 5 = 34 + 5 = 39. M1 for 39. Applying the same rule again: m₂ = 0.85 × 39 + 5 = 33.15 + 5 = 38.15. A1 for 38.15 mg.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Two things happen in the same order every hour, so the rule must be applied one hour at a time.","Write the rule as a multiplier for the loss followed by the fixed amount added, then apply it from the starting mass.","The first line is the mass at the end of one hour: 0.85 times the starting mass, plus 5."],"codes":["5.03a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"y is directly proportional to x³. When x = 2, y = 24. Work out y when x = 3.","a":"81"},{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x². When x = 3, y = 4. Work out y when x = 6.","a":"1"},{"q":"A quantity of 800 grows by 5% each year. Work out its value after 2 years.","a":"882"},{"q":"A quantity of 1000 decays by 10% each year. Work out its value after 3 years.","a":"729"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Direct proportion to a power, y ∝ xⁿ","back":"y = kxⁿ. Doubling x multiplies y by 2ⁿ (by 4 if n = 2, by 8 if n = 3). Find k by substituting the one pair of values given, then write the completed equation.","code":"5.02a","open":false},{"front":"Direct proportion to a root, y ∝ √x","back":"y = k√x. Multiplying x by 4 doubles y. To find x from y, divide by k to get √x, then square.","code":"5.02a","open":false},{"front":"Constant of proportionality, k","back":"The fixed number in y = kxⁿ, y = k√x, y = k/xⁿ or y = k/√x. Substitute the one pair of values the question gives, solve for k, then write the completed equation before using it again.","code":"5.02a","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = kxⁿ for a positive whole number n","back":"A curve through the origin that gets steeper as x increases: y = kx² has a parabola shape, y = kx³ an S-shaped curve through the origin.","code":"5.02a","open":false},{"front":"Inverse proportion to a power, y ∝ 1/xⁿ","back":"y = k/xⁿ, so yxⁿ = k stays constant. Doubling x divides y by 2ⁿ (by 4 if n = 2, by 8 if n = 3).","code":"5.02b","open":false},{"front":"Inverse proportion to a root, y ∝ 1/√x","back":"y = k/√x, so y√x = k stays constant. Multiplying x by 4 halves y.","code":"5.02b","open":false},{"front":"Testing a table for proportion to a power","back":"If y = kxⁿ then y/xⁿ is the same for every pair in the table; if y = k/xⁿ then yxⁿ is the same for every pair. Showing this proves the proportionality rather than assumes it.","code":"5.02b","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = k/xⁿ","back":"A curve that falls towards the x-axis as x grows and rises steeply as x approaches zero; it never touches either axis.","code":"5.02b","open":false},{"front":"Growth formula","back":"A = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ for a quantity growing by r% every period, where P is the starting value and n the number of periods. This is the compound interest formula.","code":"5.03a","open":false},{"front":"Decay formula","back":"A = P(1 − r/100)ⁿ for a quantity falling by r% every period, where P is the starting value and n the number of periods; the same structure as growth, with a multiplier below 1.","code":"5.03a","open":false},{"front":"Finding a rate from an overall multiplier","back":"If a quantity is multiplied by M over n equal periods, one period's multiplier is the nth root of M, ⁿ√M; writing that as 1 ± r/100 gives the percentage rate.","code":"5.03a","open":false},{"front":"Iterative growth or decay rule","back":"A rule applied period after period, each output becoming the next input, for example xₙ₊₁ = a·xₙ + b when part is lost and a fixed amount is added each time; work through it one step at a time.","code":"5.03a","open":false},{"front":"Give one example of a real quantity that is inversely proportional to a power of another","back":"The intensity of light and the square of the distance from the source; the time to empty a tank and the square of the pipe's radius; the gravitational force between two objects and the square of the distance between them.","code":"5.02b","open":true},{"front":"Give one example of a real quantity that grows or decays by a fixed percentage each period","back":"Money in a savings account with compound interest; the value of a car depreciating each year; a population of bacteria or insects growing at a constant rate.","code":"5.03a","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Total accrued = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ — compound interest, where P is the principal, r the rate per period and n the number of periods. This one is printed on the day: OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet (the insert for exams from 2026) lists 'compound interest (the total accrued)', so it is given to you. It is not on 6.02d's list of formulae you must know either, but still know what each letter means and be ready to write it as the same multiplier applied n times (5.03a).","Growth and decay as a general formula: A = P(1 + r/100)ⁿ for growth, A = P(1 − r/100)ⁿ for decay. Only the growth (compound interest) wording is on OCR's Higher tier sheet; the decay form, with the minus sign, is not listed there and not in 6.02d: recall and derive it from the same multiplier idea (5.03a).","Direct proportion to a power or root: y = kxⁿ (for example y = kx² or y = kx³) or y = k√x. Not in 6.02d's list of formulae to know and not on OCR's formulae sheet: build the equation from the wording of the proportionality statement, then substitute the one pair of values given to find k (5.02a).","Inverse proportion to a power or root: y = k/xⁿ (for example y = k/x² or y = k/x³) or y = k/√x, equivalently yxⁿ = k or y√x = k. Not in 6.02d and not on the sheet: recall the pattern and find k the same way (5.02b).","Reversing an iterative growth or decay process: if a quantity is multiplied by M over n equal periods, one period's multiplier is the nth root of M, ⁿ√M, giving the percentage rate r = 100(ⁿ√M − 1). Not in 6.02d and not on the sheet: recall it (5.03a).","An iterative rule need not be a single multiplier: xₙ₊₁ = a·xₙ + b, applied one step at a time from a starting value, also counts as a growth or decay process for this room. Not on the sheet: show every intermediate value, since those lines carry the method marks (5.03a).","What OCR's Higher tier sheet prints overall, from section 4 of the course appendix (the insert for exams from 2026): the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference of a circle, the area of a circle, the quadratic formula, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, compound interest (the total accrued), the addition rule for probability and the multiplication rule using a conditional probability. Of this room's results only compound interest is on it, so every 'recall it' above stands; the inserts are reissued each series, so check the sheet at the front of your own paper."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:02:29.481Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark tally, worked example, check question and exit-ticket answer re-solved and correct; six non-calculator questions are marked and hand-workable, difficulty splits 4/5/4 across 13 questions, and the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md exactly (compound interest printed on the Higher sheet, everything else recalled) — fix the facts list, the example-6/question-6 overlap and the two \"Write down\" stems and it is ready to publish."}}},"algebra":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"algebra","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room gathers OCR's whole Algebra content area: the notation and manipulation skills that carry marks in every other room, the standard formulae you must recall or that the exam sheet gives you, equations, inequalities, functions and sequences. Algebra runs through all three Foundation papers (J560/01-03) as both AO1 routine technique and AO3 problems set in mensuration, ratio, science and everyday contexts, so a mark lost here is rarely lost only here. The standard here is higher than it looks: an answer is expected in its simplest form even when the question does not say so, a coefficient is written as a fraction and not a decimal, both roots of a quadratic must be given, and a solution set is written as an inequality, never as an equals sign. On the non-calculator papers (J560/02) the written method is what earns the marks, so every line of working matters as much as the final answer.","idea":[{"code":"6.01a","h":"Vocabulary: expression, equation, formula, identity, inequality, term, factor","t":"The specification expects this vocabulary to be used correctly, not just recognised. An expression, such as 5y − 2, has no equals sign: it can be simplified or evaluated but never solved. An equation, such as 5y − 2 = 13, is true only for particular values. A formula, such as v = u + at, is a rule with one letter, its subject, worked out from the others. An identity is true for every value of the letter and is written with ≡, as in 2(x + 3) ≡ 2x + 6. An inequality compares two expressions with <, ≤, > or ≥. A term is a part of an expression separated by + or −; a factor divides it exactly. To show two expressions are equivalent, expand every bracket and collect every like term on one side until it is exactly the other side, then write a sentence saying so: testing one value only shows the two sides agree there, not everywhere."},{"code":"6.01b","h":"Collecting like terms","t":"Only terms with exactly the same letters raised to exactly the same powers can be joined: 5a and −2a are like terms, but 5a and 5a² are not. Each term carries the sign written in front of it, so in 7m − 4k + 2m + 9k the terms 7m and 2m collect to 9m and −4k and 9k collect to 5k, giving 9m + 5k. This is the most basic algebra mark on the paper and is usually worth one mark for each letter collected correctly; a common way to lose it is dropping the sign in front of a term when it is moved."},{"code":"6.01c","h":"Simplifying algebraic products and quotients","t":"Repeated multiplication of the same letter is written as a power: a × a × a = a³. The laws of indices let you simplify without expanding: multiplying powers of the same letter adds the indices, xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; dividing subtracts them, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; raising a power to a power multiplies them, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; and any letter to the power 0 is 1. Coefficients are multiplied or divided separately from the letters, so 5c⁴ × 3c² = 15c⁶ and 12d⁷ ÷ 4d³ = 3d⁴. These same laws return throughout Higher content, so getting the pattern secure now pays off later."},{"code":"6.01d","h":"Multiplying out brackets, including two binomials","t":"A single term in front of a bracket multiplies every term inside it, signs included: 2(a + 3b) = 2a + 6b. When two brackets are multiplied, every term in the first multiplies every term in the second, giving four products to collect: (x − 1)(x − 2) = x² − 2x − x + 2 = x² − 3x + 2. The method mark is usually for at least three of the four products correct; the accuracy mark is for the fully collected answer. A rectangle with sides given as expressions is a common context: its area is the product of two brackets, and its perimeter is the sum of four expressions, doubled and collected."},{"code":"6.01e","h":"Factorising, including quadratics","t":"Taking out a common factor means dividing every term by the highest common factor and writing it outside a bracket: 18a²b − 12ab² = 6ab(3a − 2b). 'Factorise fully' means nothing may be left that every term inside the bracket still shares. A quadratic x² + bx + c factorises as (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b, so x² − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2); the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a), and results such as x² − 3 = (x − √3)(x + √3) follow the same pattern. Always check a factorisation by expanding it back out."},{"code":"6.02a","h":"Formulating expressions and formulae from a real-world context","t":"Many marks in this topic are for the translation from words into algebra, not for the algebra itself. Start by saying what the letter stands for and its unit — let m be the number of miles, let h be the number of hours — because an unexplained letter can lose a mark. Then build the formula from the situation: a fixed charge plus an amount for each unit gives a formula such as C = 50d + 0.1m for car hire at £50 per day plus 10p per mile; a perimeter is the sum of the sides, doubled where sides repeat. Once written, the formula is used exactly like any other: substitute to find a cost, or solve to find how many units a fixed budget allows."},{"code":"6.02b","h":"Substituting values into formulae and expressions","t":"To find a value, replace every letter by the number it stands for and then follow the usual order of operations: brackets, then powers, then × and ÷, then + and −. Write the substitution line down before evaluating anything, because that line carries the method mark, and on the non-calculator paper an answer with no working can lose it. A negative value must be written in brackets as it is substituted, since (−4)² = 16 while −4² means −(4²) = −16. Foundation tier substitution also covers powers, roots and algebraic fractions together, such as v = √(u² + 2as), and 'unfamiliar formulae will be given in the question', so the marks are for using the formula correctly, not for recalling it."},{"code":"6.02c","h":"Changing the subject of a formula","t":"A formula's subject is the letter standing alone on one side. To make a different letter the subject, undo the operations applied to it, in reverse order, doing the same to both sides each time, until it stands alone and appears only once: to make d the subject of c = πd, divide both sides by π. Foundation tier also covers a subject that appears twice, or that is raised to a power or written as a reciprocal. When the wanted letter appears twice, collect its terms on one side of the equation and factorise it out before dividing, as when making t the subject of 2ty = t + 1: subtract t from both sides to get 2ty − t = 1, factorise to t(2y − 1) = 1, then divide by (2y − 1)."},{"code":"6.02d","h":"Standard formulae to recall: circles, Pythagoras and trigonometry","t":"The specification's own rule is that the assessment carries no formula sheet and that every formula a learner must know is listed under 6.02d: the circumference of a circle, 2πr = πd, and its area, πr². Foundation tier also names Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c², and the trigonometry formulae sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent. Since 2022, though, OCR has also issued a formulae sheet with every paper, and — this room's Formulae sheet extra says exactly which — the current Foundation tier insert prints every one of these results, so on the day they are given, not recalled; 6.02d still means you must know how and when to use each one."},{"code":"6.02e","h":"Using the kinematics formulae","t":"Three formulae connect the motion of an object moving with constant acceleration: v = u + at, s = ut + ½at², and v² = u² + 2as, where a is the constant acceleration, u the initial velocity, v the final velocity, s the displacement from the position when t = 0, and t the time taken. The specification says knowledge of what each letter stands for will not be required, so each formula and an explanation of its letters are always printed with the question that needs it — the marks are entirely for substituting correctly and working the arithmetic out, exactly as for any other given formula."},{"code":"6.03a","h":"Setting up and solving linear equations","t":"Solving a linear equation means peeling the operations off the unknown in reverse order, doing the same to both sides each time: to undo + 5 subtract 5, to undo × 7 divide by 7. A bracket is usually expanded first. When the unknown appears on both sides, collect the x terms on the side with the larger coefficient — so that no negative coefficient appears — and collect the numbers on the other side. Foundation tier also expects an equation to be set up from a mathematical or non-mathematical context and the solution interpreted back into that context, for example finding the distance at which two taxi firms charge the same amount. Always check by substituting the value back into the original equation."},{"code":"6.03b","h":"Solving quadratic equations by factorising","t":"A quadratic equation with the coefficient of x² equal to 1 is solved from the form x² + bx + c = 0, with zero on one side. Factorise the left-hand side into two brackets, then use the zero product rule: a product of two numbers is zero only when one of them is zero, so (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x + p = 0 or x + q = 0, and both roots, x = −p and x = −q, must be written down. A common context sets x as a length: an x cm by (x + 3) cm rectangle with area 40 cm² gives x² + 3x − 40 = 0, and any negative root is then rejected because a length cannot be negative."},{"code":"6.03c","h":"Setting up and solving simultaneous linear equations","t":"A single linear equation in x and y has many solutions; a pair of them usually has exactly one, and the answer is a pair of values that satisfies both. Make the coefficients of one letter equal in size, multiplying one or both equations if needed, then add the two equations when those terms have opposite signs and subtract when they have the same sign; that leaves one equation in one unknown to solve, after which the value found is substituted back to give the other letter. Always check the pair in whichever equation was not used for the substitution, and write the answer as x = … and y = …, never as a single number."},{"code":"6.03d","h":"Approximate solutions from a graph","t":"A linear equation such as 3x − 1 = 5 can be solved approximately by drawing y = 3x − 1 and y = 5 on the same axes: the solution is the x-coordinate where they cross. Foundation tier extends this to quadratic equations, where the roots are read from where the curve y = x² + bx + c crosses the x-axis, and to a pair of linear simultaneous equations, where each equation is a straight line and the solution is the point where the two lines cross, read as a coordinate pair. A graphical answer is only as accurate as the scale allows, so it is read off, not calculated exactly."},{"code":"6.04a","h":"Solving inequalities and showing the solution set","t":"The symbols <, ≤, > and ≥ compare two expressions, and a linear inequality is solved by exactly the same balancing steps as an equation, with one extra rule: multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses the inequality sign, so from −2x < −6 comes x > 3. The answer is a solution set, written as an inequality such as x ≤ 6, never as x = 6. On a number line a strict inequality, < or >, is drawn with an open (unfilled) circle because that endpoint is excluded, and ≤ or ≥ with a closed (filled) circle because it is included, with an arrow or shading showing which values are in the set; a double inequality such as 1 < 3x − 5 ≤ 10 is solved the same way, across both sides at once."},{"code":"6.05a","h":"Expressions as functions: inputs and outputs","t":"An expression such as 2x + 3 can be read as a function machine: an input goes in, it is multiplied by 2, then 3 is added, and one output comes out, written x → ×2 → +3 → y or as the mapping y = 2x + 3. Substituting an input gives the output. Running the machine backwards — undoing each operation in the reverse order, exactly the inverse-operation thinking used to change the subject of a formula — takes a given output back to the input that produced it. Each input has exactly one output, which is what makes the expression a function."},{"code":"6.06a","h":"Generating a sequence, and deducing the nth term","t":"A term-to-term rule builds a sequence from the term before it, so the first term must be given as well as the rule: 'start at 4 and add 3' gives 4, 7, 10, 13, …. A position-to-term rule, the nth term, works straight from the position: substituting n = 1, 2, 3, … into n² + 2n gives 3, 8, 15, …, and the 50th term needs only n = 50. For a linear sequence such as 40, 37, 34, 31, … the common difference, −3, is the coefficient of n; comparing the sequence with the multiples of −3 gives the constant, so the nth term is 43 − 3n. Diagram patterns of tiles or sticks are examined the same way: count each pattern, write the counts as a sequence, then find its rule."},{"code":"6.06b","h":"Recognising special sequences","t":"Some sequences must be known on sight. The triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … add one more each time; the square numbers 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … are n²; the cube numbers 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, … are n³. A simple arithmetic progression has a constant common difference. Foundation tier also expects a Fibonacci-type sequence, where each term is the sum of the two before it, so two starting terms are needed, as in 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, …; a simple geometric progression, where each term is the one before multiplied by a fixed common ratio r, a rational number greater than 0, as in 3, 6, 12, 24, … with r = 2; and a quadratic sequence, whose first differences themselves go up by a constant amount, so it is continued by continuing those differences."}],"examples":[{"title":"Showing two expressions are equivalent","setup":"Show that (x + 1)² + 2 is equivalent to x² + 2x + 3 for every value of x, and say what kind of statement this makes it.","steps":["Expand the bracket: (x + 1)² = (x + 1)(x + 1) = x² + x + x + 1 = x² + 2x + 1","Add the 2 that follows the bracket: x² + 2x + 1 + 2 = x² + 2x + 3","Compare this with the right-hand side, x² + 2x + 3: the two sides are identical","Because the two sides are equal for every value of x, the statement is an identity, and may be written (x + 1)² + 2 ≡ x² + 2x + 3"],"cues":["How many terms does expanding a squared bracket produce before they are collected?","What still has to be combined with the expanded bracket?","What has to be true of the two sides for the statement to be shown?","What kind of statement is one that is true for every value of the letter?"]},{"title":"Factorising a quadratic expression","setup":"Factorise the expression x² − x − 6, writing it as a product of two brackets.","steps":["This has the form x² + bx + c with b = −1 and c = −6, so two numbers are needed with product −6 and sum −1","The factor pairs of 6 are 1 × 6 and 2 × 3; one number must be negative, and −3 with 2 gives product −6","Check the sum: −3 + 2 = −1, which matches the coefficient of x","So the factorised form is x² − x − 6 = (x − 3)(x + 2)"],"cues":["What must the two numbers multiply to, and what must they add to?","Why must one of the two numbers be negative here?","Which pair still needs to be tested against the middle term?","Which number sits in the bracket with the plus sign?"]},{"title":"Formulating a cost formula and using it","setup":"A car hire firm charges £50 per day plus 10p per mile driven. (a) Write a formula for the total cost C, in pounds, of hiring the car for d days and driving m miles. (b) Work out the cost of hiring the car for 3 days and driving 120 miles.","steps":["Write the daily charge as 50d and the mileage charge, 10p = £0.10 per mile, as 0.1m, so C = 50d + 0.1m","Substitute d = 3 and m = 120 into the formula: C = 50 × 3 + 0.1 × 120","Work out each part: 50 × 3 = 150 and 0.1 × 120 = 12","Add the two parts: C = 150 + 12 = £162"],"cues":["How is each of the two charges written as a term in d or m?","What replaces d and what replaces m for this hire?","Which two products does the formula need worked out?","What single step turns the two parts into the total cost?"]},{"title":"Changing the subject when the letter appears twice","setup":"Rearrange the formula 2ty = t + 1 to make t the subject.","steps":["Subtract t from both sides so that both t terms are on the same side: 2ty − t = 1","Take out the common factor t on the left-hand side: t(2y − 1) = 1","Divide both sides by (2y − 1): t = 1/(2y − 1)"],"cues":["What has to happen before the two t terms can be dealt with together?","What single letter can be factored out of both terms on the left?","What is still multiplying t, and how is it removed?"]},{"title":"Solving a quadratic equation by factorising","setup":"Solve the equation x² − 5x − 14 = 0 by factorising, giving both solutions.","steps":["Find two numbers with product −14 and sum −5: they are −7 and 2","Write the left-hand side as two brackets: (x − 7)(x + 2) = 0","Use the zero product rule: x − 7 = 0 or x + 2 = 0","Solve each bracket: x = 7 or x = −2"],"cues":["What two conditions must the pair of numbers satisfy?","How are those two numbers written into the factorised form?","Why does a product equalling zero help here?","What value of x does each bracket give on its own?"]},{"title":"Deducing the nth term of a descending sequence","setup":"A sequence begins 40, 37, 34, 31, … Deduce an expression for the nth term of the sequence.","steps":["Find the common difference: 37 − 40 = −3, so the expression begins with −3n","The multiples of −3 are −3, −6, −9, −12, … and each term of the sequence is 43 more than the matching multiple, so the nth term is 43 − 3n","Test it on the first term: 43 − 3 × 1 = 40, which matches","Test it on the second term: 43 − 3 × 2 = 37, which also matches, so the nth term is 43 − 3n"],"cues":["What is subtracted each time to get the next term, and what does that give the coefficient of n?","How does each term compare with the matching multiple of −3?","Which position number checks the expression against the first term?","Which position number checks it against the second term?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Show that 3(x + 2) − x is equivalent to 2x + 6, and say whether this is an equation or an identity.","a":"3(x + 2) − x = 3x + 6 − x = 2x + 6, so both sides are equal for every x: it is an identity."},{"q":"Use v = u + at to find v when u = 10, a = −2 and t = 4.","a":"v = 10 + (−2)(4) = 10 − 8 = 2"},{"q":"The graphs of y = x + 2 and y = 8 − x cross at (3, 5). Write down the approximate solution of x + 2 = 8 − x.","a":"x = 3"},{"q":"Solve 2x + 3 > 11.","a":"x > 4"},{"q":"Simplify 6a⁵ ÷ 2a².","a":"3a³"}]},"room":{"facts":["OCR's papers: three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes — Foundation J560/01-03, Higher J560/04-06; Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator; grades 5-1 on Foundation and 9-4 on Higher.","Notation: ab means a × b; 3y means y + y + y and 3 × y; a² means a × a; a³ means a × a × a; a²b means a × a × b; a/b means a ÷ b.","Laws of indices: xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1, with coefficients multiplied or divided separately.","x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q) where p × q = c and p + q = b; the difference of two squares is x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a).","To change the subject, undo the operations on the wanted letter in reverse order on both sides; if it appears twice, collect its terms on one side and factorise it out before dividing.","Zero product rule: if (x + p)(x + q) = 0 then x + p = 0 or x + q = 0, so x = −p or x = −q — a quadratic has two roots and both must be given.","Simultaneous equations: make one letter's coefficients equal in size, add when their signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same, then substitute back and check in the other equation.","Inequalities: multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses <, >, ≤ or ≥; an open circle on a number line excludes the endpoint, a closed circle includes it.","The nth term of a linear sequence is dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term.","Special sequences: triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, …; square numbers n²; cube numbers n³; a Fibonacci-type sequence adds the two terms before it; a geometric progression multiplies by a fixed ratio r > 0; a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference."],"questions":[{"q":"Simplify 6a + 2b − 4a + 5b. (non-calculator)","a":"2a + 7b","sol":"Collect the a terms, each keeping its sign: 6a − 4a = 2a. B1 for 2a. Collect the b terms: 2b + 5b = 7b. B1 for 7b. Simplified expression: 2a + 7b. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only terms with exactly the same letter can be joined together.","Deal with the a terms and the b terms separately, keeping the sign in front of each term with it.","The first line groups the terms as (6a − 4a) + (2b + 5b)."],"codes":["6.01b"]},{"q":"Simplify (a) 4x³ × 3x²   (b) 10y⁶ ÷ 2y². (non-calculator)","a":"(a) 12x⁵   (b) 5y⁴","sol":"(a) Multiply the coefficients and add the indices: 4 × 3 = 12, x³ × x² = x³⁺² = x⁵, giving 12x⁵. B1 for 12x⁵. (b) Divide the coefficients and subtract the indices: 10 ÷ 2 = 5, y⁶ ÷ y² = y⁶⁻² = y⁴, giving 5y⁴. B1 for 5y⁴. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each part has numbers and letters that are dealt with separately.","Use ordinary arithmetic on the coefficients and the laws of indices on the powers of the same letter.","Part (a) begins as (4 × 3) × (x³ × x²), with the indices still to be combined."],"codes":["6.01c"]},{"q":"Work out the value of 2x² − 5 when x = −3. (non-calculator)","a":"13","sol":"Substitute, writing the negative value in brackets: 2 × (−3)² − 5. M1 for this substitution line. (−3)² = 9, so 2 × 9 = 18, and 18 − 5 = 13. A1 for 13. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A power is worked out before a multiplication, and a negative value needs care when it is squared.","Replace x by −3 everywhere it appears, writing the value in brackets, then follow the order of operations.","The first line is 2 × (   )² − 5, with −3 written inside the brackets."],"codes":["6.02b"]},{"q":"Solve 4x − 7 = 13. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 5","sol":"Add 7 to both sides: 4x = 20. M1 for 4x = 20. Divide both sides by 4: x = 5. A1 for x = 5. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The unknown has had two things done to it; both can be undone while keeping the equation balanced.","Undo the subtraction first, then the multiplication.","Add 7 to both sides first, then divide both sides by the coefficient of x."],"codes":["6.03a"]},{"q":"State whether each of the following is an expression, an equation, a formula or an identity: (a) 4y − 3   (b) 4y − 3 = 9   (c) v = u + at   (d) 3(y + 2) ≡ 3y + 6. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) expression   (b) equation   (c) formula   (d) identity","sol":"(a) 4y − 3 has no equals or identity sign, so it is an expression. B1 for expression. (b) 4y − 3 = 9 is true only for one particular value of y, so it is an equation. B1 for equation. (c) v = u + at is a rule with one letter, its subject, worked out from the others, so it is a formula. B1 for formula. (d) 3(y + 2) ≡ 3y + 6 uses the identity sign, and it holds for every value of y, so it is an identity. B1 for identity. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Look at what sign, if any, connects the two sides of each part, and whether it holds for one value of the letter or for every value.","An expression has no equals sign; an equation holds for one particular value; a formula has a subject worked out from the other letters; an identity holds for every value and is written with ≡.","For part (d), expand the bracket on the left and compare it with the right-hand side."],"codes":["6.01a"]},{"q":"Factorise x² + 3x − 10. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(x + 5)(x − 2)","sol":"Two numbers with product −10 and sum 3 are 5 and −2. M1 for identifying 5 and −2, or for a partly correct factorisation. So x² + 3x − 10 = (x + 5)(x − 2). A1 for (x + 5)(x − 2). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Find the pair of numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x.","List the factor pairs of 10 and decide which pair, with one number negative, adds to 3.","Write the answer as (x + …)(x + …), with the two numbers still to be placed."],"codes":["6.01e"]},{"q":"Make r the subject of the formula C = 2πr. (non-calculator)","a":"r = C/(2π)","sol":"The right-hand side is 2π multiplied by r, so divide both sides by 2π: C/(2π) = r. M1 for dividing both sides by 2π. A1 for r = C/(2π). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The subject is the letter standing alone on one side, and here that has to become r.","Undo the operation that is applied to r by doing the same thing to both sides.","The first line of working divides both sides of C = 2πr by 2π; the right-hand side still needs simplifying from there."],"codes":["6.02c"]},{"q":"A function machine multiplies its input by 3 and then subtracts 7. (a) Work out the output when the input is 5. (b) Work out the input that gives an output of 20. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) 8   (b) 9","sol":"(a) The rule as an expression is 3x − 7. Substituting x = 5: 3 × 5 − 7 = 15 − 7 = 8. B1 for 8. (b) Run the machine backwards, undoing each step in reverse order: add 7, then divide by 3, so 3x − 7 = 20 gives 3x = 27. M1 for 3x = 27, or for both steps reversed in the correct order. Dividing by 3: x = 9. A1 for 9. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The machine is the expression 3x − 7 read as a rule turning an input into one output.","Part (a) puts the input into the rule; part (b) runs the machine backwards, undoing each operation in reverse order.","For part (b), the first line sets 3x − 7 equal to 20."],"codes":["6.05a"]},{"q":"Solve x² − 2x − 24 = 0 by factorising. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 6 or x = −4","sol":"Two numbers with product −24 and sum −2 are −6 and 4, so (x − 6)(x + 4) = 0. M1 for (x − 6)(x + 4) = 0. Setting each factor to zero gives both roots x = 6 and x = −4. A1 for both roots. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The equation is already equal to zero, so the left-hand side can be written as two brackets multiplied together.","Find the pair of numbers that multiply to give the constant term and add to give the coefficient of x.","Write the left-hand side as (x + …)(x + …) = 0, then set each bracket equal to zero in turn."],"codes":["6.03b"]},{"q":"Solve the simultaneous equations 3x + 2y = 16 and x − 2y = 0. (a calculator may be used)","a":"x = 4, y = 2","sol":"The y terms are +2y and −2y, so adding the two equations removes y: 4x = 16. M1 for adding the equations to eliminate y. Divide by 4: x = 4. A1 for x = 4. Substitute into x − 2y = 0: 4 − 2y = 0, so y = 2. A1 for y = 2. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Compare the y terms in the two equations and look at their signs.","When matching terms have opposite signs, adding the equations removes that letter.","Add the two equations term by term, and write down the single equation in x that is left."],"codes":["6.03c"]},{"q":"Solve 3x − 5 ≤ 10 and show the solution set on a number line. (non-calculator)","a":"x ≤ 5, drawn as a closed circle at 5 with the line to the left","sol":"Add 5 to both sides: 3x ≤ 15. M1 for 3x ≤ 15. Divide both sides by 3, a positive number, so the sign is unchanged: x ≤ 5. A1 for x ≤ 5. On the number line, a closed (filled) circle at 5, because 5 is included, with an arrow or shading to the left. B1 for the correct circle and direction. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An inequality is unpicked by the same balancing steps as an equation.","Undo the subtraction first, then the multiplication, and check the sign of the number you divide by.","Add 5 to each side, then divide each side by the coefficient of x, leaving an inequality rather than an equals sign."],"codes":["6.04a"]},{"q":"The graphs of y = 2x − 3 and y = x + 1 are drawn on the same axes and cross at the point (4, 5). (a) Write down the approximate solution of the simultaneous equations y = 2x − 3 and y = x + 1. A quadratic curve y = x² − 2x − 3 crosses the x-axis at x = −1 and x = 3. (b) Write down the solutions of x² − 2x − 3 = 0. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) x = 4, y = 5   (b) x = −1 or x = 3","sol":"(a) The solution of a pair of simultaneous equations is the point where their graphs cross, so x = 4 and y = 5. B1 for x = 4 and y = 5. (b) The solutions of x² − 2x − 3 = 0 are the x-coordinates where the curve crosses the x-axis, so x = −1 or x = 3. B1 for both values. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["The solution of simultaneous equations is read from where the two graphs cross; the roots of a quadratic are read from where its curve crosses the x-axis.","For part (a), read off the coordinates of the crossing point; for part (b), read off the x-values where the curve meets the x-axis.","Part (a) needs both coordinates of the point where the lines cross; part (b) needs both x-values where y = 0."],"codes":["6.03d"]},{"q":"A cyclist accelerates from rest with constant acceleration 1.5 m/s². Use v = u + at, where u is the initial velocity, v the final velocity, a the acceleration and t the time, and s = ut + ½at², where s is the distance travelled. (a) Work out the cyclist's velocity after 8 seconds. (b) Work out the distance the cyclist travels in those 8 seconds. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) 12 m/s   (b) 48 m","sol":"(a) Substitute u = 0, a = 1.5 and t = 8 into v = u + at: v = 0 + 1.5 × 8. M1 for the substitution. Working this out, v = 12 (m/s). A1 for 12 (m/s). (b) Substitute u = 0, a = 1.5 and t = 8 into s = ut + ½at²: s = 0 × 8 + ½ × 1.5 × 8². M1 for the substitution. 8² = 64, so ½ × 1.5 × 64 = 48, giving s = 48 (m). A1 for 48 (m). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Both formulae are printed in the question with what each letter stands for; the cyclist starts from rest, so u = 0.","Substitute the given values into each formula in turn, then work through the arithmetic in order, powers before multiplication.","Part (a) begins v = 0 + 1.5 × 8; part (b) begins s = 0 × 8 + ½ × 1.5 × 8²."],"codes":["6.02e"]},{"q":"Sequence P is 3, 6, 12, 24, … Sequence Q has nth term 5n − 2. (a) Name the type of sequence P and give its next term. (b) Work out the 20th term of sequence Q. (c) Work out which position in sequence Q gives the value 93. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) Geometric progression, common ratio 2, next term 48   (b) 98   (c) position 19","sol":"(a) 6 ÷ 3 = 2 and 12 ÷ 6 = 2, so P is a geometric progression with common ratio 2. B1 for geometric progression, ratio 2. Next term 24 × 2 = 48. B1 for 48. (b) Substitute n = 20 into 5n − 2: 5 × 20 − 2 = 98. M1 for the substitution. A1 for 98. (c) Set 5n − 2 = 93, so 5n = 95. M1 for 5n = 95 or an equivalent equation. Dividing by 5: n = 19. A1 for 19. Total 6 marks.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Compare each term of P with the one before it to see what it is multiplied by; sequence Q needs its rule used both forwards and backwards.","Test P by dividing consecutive terms; for Q, substitute for part (b) and form an equation in n for part (c).","Part (a) starts with 6 ÷ 3; part (c) starts by setting 5n − 2 equal to 93."],"codes":["6.06a","6.06b"]},{"q":"A rectangle has length (x + 3) cm and width x cm. Its area is 40 cm². (a) Show that x² + 3x − 40 = 0. (b) Solve the equation to find the possible values of x. (c) State the dimensions of the rectangle. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) shown   (b) x = 5 or x = −8   (c) 5 cm by 8 cm","sol":"(a) The area is length × width: x(x + 3) = x² + 3x. Setting this equal to 40 gives x² + 3x = 40, so x² + 3x − 40 = 0. B1 for the shown working. (b) Two numbers with product −40 and sum 3 are 8 and −5, so (x + 8)(x − 5) = 0. M1 for (x + 8)(x − 5) = 0. Both roots: x = −8 or x = 5. A1 for both roots. (c) A length cannot be negative, so x = 5 is the only valid solution: the width is 5 cm and the length is 5 + 3 = 8 cm. B1 for rejecting x = −8 and stating 5 cm by 8 cm. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The area of a rectangle is its length multiplied by its width, and here that product is given.","Expand the product, set it equal to the area, and rearrange so the equation equals zero before factorising.","Part (a) begins x(x + 3) = 40, and part (b) begins by factorising x² + 3x − 40."],"codes":["6.02a","6.01d","6.03b"]},{"q":"Pythagoras' theorem states a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. (a) Rearrange the formula to make b the subject. (b) A ladder of length c = 6.5 m leans against a wall with its foot a = 2.5 m from the wall. Work out b, the height the ladder reaches up the wall. (a calculator may be used)","a":"(a) b = √(c² − a²)   (b) 6 m","sol":"(a) Subtract a² from both sides: b² = c² − a². M1 for b² = c² − a². Take the square root of both sides: b = √(c² − a²). A1 for b = √(c² − a²). (b) Substitute c = 6.5 and a = 2.5: b = √(6.5² − 2.5²) = √(42.25 − 6.25). M1 for the correct substitution. √36 = 6, so b = 6 m. A1 for 6 (m). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Pythagoras' theorem connects the two shorter sides and the hypotenuse of a right-angled triangle; here two of the three are already given.","Rearrange the formula for the side that is missing, then substitute the two lengths given.","Part (a) begins by subtracting a² from both sides; part (b) begins by substituting the two given lengths into that rearranged formula."],"codes":["6.02c","6.02d"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Simplify 5x + 3y − 2x − y.","a":"3x + 2y"},{"q":"Solve 5x + 4 = 29.","a":"x = 5"},{"q":"Factorise x² − 5x − 6.","a":"(x − 6)(x + 1)"},{"q":"The nth term of a sequence is 9n − 4. Work out the 12th term.","a":"104"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Expression, equation, formula, identity","back":"Expression: no equals sign, e.g. 5y − 2. Equation: true only for particular values, e.g. 5y − 2 = 13. Formula: a rule with a subject, e.g. v = u + at. Identity (≡): true for every value.","code":"6.01a","open":false},{"front":"Term and factor","back":"A term is a part of an expression separated by + or −. A factor is something that divides an expression exactly; the factors of 6x²y include 2, 3, x and y.","code":"6.01a","open":false},{"front":"Showing two expressions are equivalent","back":"Expand every bracket and collect every like term on one side until it exactly matches the other side, then state that the statement is an identity. Testing one value is not enough.","code":"6.01a","open":false},{"front":"The laws of indices","back":"xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ; xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ; (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ; x⁰ = 1. Coefficients are multiplied or divided separately: 5c⁴ × 3c² = 15c⁶.","code":"6.01c","open":false},{"front":"Factorising x² + bx + c","back":"Find two numbers with product c and sum b; they go in the brackets: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q).","code":"6.01e","open":false},{"front":"The difference of two squares","back":"x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a). The brackets hold the same two terms, one added and one subtracted.","code":"6.01e","open":false},{"front":"Changing the subject of a formula","back":"Undo the operations applied to the wanted letter, in reverse order, on both sides. If the letter appears twice, collect its terms on one side and factorise it out before dividing.","code":"6.02c","open":false},{"front":"Circle formulae (6.02d)","back":"Circumference 2πr = πd; area πr². Given on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, so not recalled from memory on the day.","code":"6.02d","open":false},{"front":"Pythagoras and the trigonometry ratios (6.02d)","back":"a² + b² = c²; sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent. Given on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, so not recalled from memory.","code":"6.02d","open":false},{"front":"Zero product rule","back":"If two factors multiply to give zero, one of them must be zero: (x + p)(x + q) = 0 gives x = −p or x = −q. Both roots of a quadratic must be given.","code":"6.03b","open":false},{"front":"Elimination method for simultaneous equations","back":"Make the coefficients of one letter equal in size, then add the equations if those terms have opposite signs and subtract if they have the same sign.","code":"6.03c","open":false},{"front":"Rules for solving and showing an inequality","back":"Multiplying or dividing both sides by a negative number reverses <, >, ≤ or ≥. An open circle excludes the endpoint; a closed circle includes it.","code":"6.04a","open":false},{"front":"An expression read as a function","back":"A rule turning each input into exactly one output, e.g. 3x − 5 multiplies by 3 then subtracts 5. Undoing the steps in reverse order takes an output back to its input.","code":"6.05a","open":false},{"front":"The nth term of a linear sequence","back":"nth term = dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term. For 3, 7, 11, … this gives 4n − 1.","code":"6.06a","open":false},{"front":"Special sequences: triangular, square, cube, Fibonacci, geometric, quadratic","back":"Triangular 1, 3, 6, 10, …; square n²; cube n³; a Fibonacci-type sequence adds the two terms before it; a geometric progression multiplies by a fixed ratio r > 0; a quadratic sequence has a constant second difference.","code":"6.06b","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["How to read this list: 6.02d is the specification's own list of formulae Foundation learners must recall from memory — the circumference and area of a circle, and, Foundation tier also, Pythagoras' theorem and the sine, cosine and tangent ratios. Appendix.md section 3 records that OCR's 2026 Foundation tier formulae sheet, issued with every paper since 2022, prints all of this room's 6.02d results — the circle, Pythagoras and trigonometry formulae — alongside other results this room does not need, such as the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, compound interest and the probability addition rule; and where the specification says a result must be known but the sheet prints it, the sheet governs on the day — so none of 6.02d's formulae has to be recalled at Foundation tier.","Given on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, not memorised (6.02d): circumference of a circle, 2πr = πd; area of a circle, πr².","Given on the Foundation tier formulae sheet, not memorised (6.02d, Foundation tier also): Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c²; the trigonometry ratios sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent.","Given in the question, not memorised (6.02e): the kinematics formulae v = u + at, s = ut + ½at² and v² = u² + 2as. The specification itself says knowledge of what each letter stands for will not be required, so the formula and the meaning of its letters both arrive with the question.","Must be recalled — not listed in 6.02d and not on the formulae sheet: the notation conventions ab = a × b, 3y = y + y + y, a² = a × a, a²b = a × a × b, a/b = a ÷ b.","Must be recalled: the laws of indices, xᵃ × xᵇ = xᵃ⁺ᵇ, xᵃ ÷ xᵇ = xᵃ⁻ᵇ, (xᵃ)ᵇ = xᵃᵇ and x⁰ = 1, with coefficients multiplied or divided separately.","Must be recalled: x² + bx + c = (x + p)(x + q), where p × q = c and p + q = b; the difference of two squares, x² − a² = (x + a)(x − a); and x² + bx = x(x + b).","Must be recalled: to change the subject, apply the inverse operation to both sides in reverse order; where the wanted letter appears twice, collect its terms on one side and factorise it out before dividing.","Must be recalled: the zero product rule — if (x + p)(x + q) = 0 then x + p = 0 or x + q = 0, so x = −p or x = −q — and the elimination rule for simultaneous equations: make one letter's coefficients equal in size, then add when the signs are opposite and subtract when they are the same.","Must be recalled: multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses <, >, ≤ or ≥; on a number line an open circle marks a strict inequality and a closed circle marks ≤ or ≥.","Must be recalled: the nth term of a linear sequence is dn + (a − d), where d is the common difference and a is the first term.","Must be recalled: the special sequences — triangular numbers 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, …; square numbers n²; cube numbers n³; a Fibonacci-type sequence (each term is the sum of the two before it); a geometric progression (each term × a fixed common ratio r > 0); a quadratic sequence (constant second difference, continued by continuing the first differences)."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:50:05.029Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Mathematically clean - all 16 bank answers, 6 worked examples, 5 check questions and 4 exit answers re-solved in node and every one is correct, every sol tally adds to its m, nine questions are marked non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, difficulties split 5/7/4, and nothing Higher-only (no quadratic formula, completing the square, quadratic simultaneous equations or f(x) notation) has crept in; fix the facts line, the \"prints exactly those results\" wording and the unsourced Notes claim in why and it is ready to publish."}}},"algebra-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"algebra-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room holds the techniques that mark out the top grades on the Higher tier: a quadratic that will not factorise needs completing the square or the formula; a line meeting a curve needs simultaneous equations with one quadratic; a cubic with no algebraic method needs iteration; and a height, an area or a profit that must stay above or below a value needs a quadratic inequality. The four results in 6.02d — the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule and the area of a triangle — are in fact printed on OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet, so it is the method of using them, not memorising them, that is examined. Papers 4 and 6 allow a calculator; Paper 5 does not, so exact surd and fraction answers are expected there, and the harder factorising, algebraic fractions and proof in this room are often set without one. Functions, sequences and inequalities each appear as short standalone questions and inside longer AO3 problems that combine several rooms at once.","idea":[{"h":"Algebraic proof","t":"Checking a statement for a few numbers is not a proof, because it might fail on the next number tried. A proof must hold for every value, so before any working starts the numbers are replaced by a general algebraic form: any integer is n, consecutive integers are n, n+1, n+2, an even number is 2n, an odd number is 2n+1, two consecutive odd (or even) numbers differ by 2, and a multiple of 5 is 5n. Expand and collect, then finish by writing the result in a form that shows the conclusion — a multiple of 3 as 3(…), an even number as 2(…), an odd number as 2(…)+1 — and say in words what that form proves. The closing sentence is worth its own mark.","code":"6.01a"},{"h":"Indices with negative and fractional powers","t":"The laws of indices from Foundation — aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ ÷ aⁿ = aᵐ⁻ⁿ, (aᵐ)ⁿ = aᵐⁿ — apply exactly the same way when the powers are negative or fractional. A negative power means a reciprocal, a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ, and a fractional power means a root: a^(1/n) = ⁿ√a, so a^(m/n) = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ. To simplify a product or quotient, add or subtract the indices as usual, keeping careful track of the sign: a^(1/2) × 2a⁻³ = 2a^(1/2 − 3) = 2a^(−5/2), and 2a²b³ ÷ 4a⁻³b = (1/2)a^(2−(−3))b^(3−1) = (1/2)a⁵b². Coefficients are simplified separately from the indices themselves.","code":"6.01c"},{"h":"Expanding a product of more than two binomials","t":"Three or more brackets cannot be multiplied all at once. Deal with any two of them first, expand and collect to get a single quadratic, then multiply that quadratic by whichever bracket is left, term by term, and collect again. (x+1)(x−1)(2x+1): the first pair gives x²−1, and multiplying by (2x+1) gives 2x³+x²−2x−1. The mark scheme wants both stages of expansion to be seen, not just the final line, so keep the intermediate quadratic written down.","code":"6.01d"},{"h":"Factorising ax²+bx+c when a is not 1","t":"When the coefficient of x² is not 1, the two brackets are found by the product-and-sum method: multiply a by c, find two numbers with that product and with sum b, split the middle term into those two x-terms, then factorise in pairs so a bracket is shared and can be taken out. For 2x²+3x−2: a×c = −4, the pair 4 and −1 works, so 2x²+4x−x−2 = 2x(x+2)−1(x+2) = (2x−1)(x+2). Always check by expanding the answer back out.","code":"6.01e"},{"h":"Completing the square","t":"Any quadratic x²+bx+c can be written as (x+b/2)²−(b/2)²+c: halve the coefficient of x for the bracket, then subtract the square of that half to correct for the extra the bracket introduces. x²+4x−6 = (x+2)²−4−6 = (x+2)²−10. When the x² coefficient is not 1, take it out of the first two terms before completing the square inside: 2x²+5x+1 = 2(x²+5x/2)+1 = 2[(x+5/4)²−25/16]+1 = 2(x+5/4)²−17/8. This form gives the equation's exact solutions and the turning point of the graph without drawing it.","code":"6.01f"},{"h":"Simplifying and combining algebraic fractions","t":"To simplify an algebraic fraction, factorise the top and the bottom fully and cancel any factor common to both — only whole factors cancel, never single terms. (n²+2n)/(n²+n−2) = n(n+2)/[(n+2)(n−1)] = n/(n−1). To add or subtract two fractions, write both over a common denominator (usually the product of the two denominators), combine the numerators, then simplify: 1/(n−1) + n/(n+1) = [(n+1) + n(n−1)]/[(n−1)(n+1)] = (n²+2)/(n²−1). The same factorising and cancelling skills apply when a fraction involves surds.","code":"6.01g"},{"h":"Formulating a formula from a proportion or growth statement","t":"Where a quantity is described as proportional to another — directly, inversely, or to a power — the words translate straight into an algebraic formula with an unknown constant: 'y is inversely proportional to the square of x' becomes y = k/x², and one pair of matching values is substituted to find k. The same skill sets up a growth or decay formula, such as a value that multiplies by a fixed factor each year. Once the formula is formed, it is used exactly like any other formula to find an unknown value. This idea leans on the proportion and growth-and-decay rooms; what is new here is writing the formula itself.","code":"6.02a"},{"h":"Changing the subject when the formula contains an algebraic fraction","t":"Rearranging a formula that contains a fraction usually starts by clearing the fraction: multiply both sides by the denominator before doing anything else, then collect every term containing the target letter on one side and factorise it out if it appears more than once. To make x the subject of y = 3/(x−2): multiply by (x−2) to give y(x−2) = 3, expand to yx − 2y = 3, add 2y to both sides, then divide by y: x = (3+2y)/y. The result is often itself a fraction, and it should be simplified as far as it will go.","code":"6.02c"},{"h":"The formulae you must recall: quadratic formula, sine rule, cosine rule, area of a triangle","t":"Four results are named in the specification as ones to know by heart at Higher tier: the quadratic formula x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a for solving ax²+bx+c=0; the sine rule a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC for a side or an angle in any triangle; the cosine rule a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA for a side, or its rearrangement cosA = (b²+c²−a²)/2bc for an angle, when two sides and the included angle (or all three sides) are known; and the area of a triangle = ½ab sinC when two sides and the included angle are known but not the height. All four of these are in fact printed on OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet, so on the day they are given — but they still need to be understood well enough to substitute into correctly and to choose between.","code":"6.02d"},{"h":"Linear equations that contain an algebraic fraction","t":"A linear equation with the unknown in a denominator is cleared before it is solved: multiply every term by whatever will remove all the denominators at once, then solve the resulting equation as usual, checking the solution does not make any original denominator zero. Some equations of this kind, once cleared, turn out to be quadratic rather than linear — the method of multiplying through by the denominators is what matters, not the shape of the equation that results.","code":"6.03a"},{"h":"Solving a quadratic equation: factorising, completing the square, or the formula","t":"Every quadratic equation is first rearranged to read ax²+bx+c=0. If it factorises, factorising is quickest. If it does not, or the question does not simplify to whole numbers, complete the square or use the quadratic formula — both always work. The quadratic formula in particular handles any quadratic directly: substitute a, b and c with their signs, and take care that −4ac comes out with the right sign when c is negative. On the non-calculator paper the answer is often left as an exact surd; with a calculator it is rounded to the accuracy the question states.","code":"6.03b"},{"h":"Simultaneous equations where one equation is a quadratic","t":"Two linear equations are solved by elimination, but a linear and a quadratic (or two quadratics) are solved by substitution: rearrange the linear equation to make one letter the subject, substitute that expression into the other equation, and solve the single equation in one unknown that results. Each solution for that letter is then substituted back into the linear equation — never the quadratic — to find its matching partner, and the answers are given as pairs. A pair of equations like x²+y²=50 and 2y=x+5 usually gives two pairs of solutions.","code":"6.03c"},{"h":"What the points of intersection of a line and a curve mean","t":"Solving a pair of simultaneous equations algebraically and finding where their graphs cross are the same problem seen two ways: the coordinates of every point where a line and a curve meet are exactly the solutions of the two equations taken together. If the algebra gives one repeated root the line touches the curve at a single point and is a tangent; if it gives no real solutions the line and the curve never meet.","code":"6.03d"},{"h":"Approximate solutions by iteration","t":"When an equation has no algebraic method of solution, a root is located numerically instead. First show a root exists in an interval: work out the value of f(x) at each end, and a change of sign (with a continuous graph) proves a root lies between them. Then rearrange the equation into the form x = g(x) and use the iterative formula xₙ₊₁ = g(xₙ) from a given starting value x₀, feeding each answer straight back in, until consecutive values agree to the accuracy asked for. A systematic decimal search — repeatedly testing values to narrow the interval — is an alternative that needs no rearrangement at all.","code":"6.03e"},{"h":"Quadratic inequalities and set notation","t":"A quadratic inequality is solved through a sketch, not the algebra alone: rearrange so one side is zero, factorise to find the critical values, then picture the curve — with a positive x² coefficient it is U-shaped, below the axis between its two roots and above the axis outside them. x²−2x<3 rearranges to x²−2x−3<0, factorises to (x−3)(x+1)<0, with critical values −1 and 3, and the U-shape is below the axis between them: −1<x<3. Set notation is required at Higher tier: {x : −1<x<3} for one stretch, or {x : x<a} ∪ {x : x>b} when the solution has two separate pieces.","code":"6.04a"},{"h":"Inequalities in two variables and their region","t":"Several linear inequalities in two variables together define a region of the coordinate grid. Draw each boundary line — dashed for a strict inequality (< or >), solid where it is included (≤ or ≥) — then decide which side of each line satisfies its inequality, usually by testing a point such as the origin. The solution is wherever every inequality holds at once, so it is checked by substituting a point's coordinates into all the inequalities together, not just one.","code":"6.04b"},{"h":"Inverse and composite functions","t":"A composite function is the succession of two functions: fg(x) means apply g first, then apply f to that output, so fg(x) and gf(x) are usually different — the order is where marks are commonly lost. An inverse function reverses a function's process, so f⁻¹ takes an output of f back to the input that produced it. To find f⁻¹(x) for a function given as y = f(x), rearrange to make x the subject, then rewrite the rule in x. Function notation itself is not formally examined, but the two ideas — reversing and combining — are.","code":"6.05a"},{"h":"Subscript notation and the nth term of a quadratic sequence","t":"Subscript notation names a sequence's terms directly: xₙ is the term in position n, and a rule can be given position-to-term (xₙ = n+2) or term-to-term (xₙ₊₁ = 2xₙ−3, built from the term before it). For a quadratic sequence — one whose first differences are not constant but whose second differences are — the second difference equals twice the coefficient of n² in the nth term. Halve it to get the n² part, subtract those values from the sequence to leave a linear sequence, find that sequence's nth term as at Foundation, and add the two parts together: 0, 3, 10, 21, … has second difference 4, so the n² part is 2n², and the full nth term is 2n²−3n+1.","code":"6.06a"},{"h":"Sequences that are not arithmetic, quadratic or geometric","t":"Some Higher-tier sequences follow none of the named families, and the rule for them is always given or has to be spotted from the pattern of the terms rather than recalled. A progression with a surd common ratio, such as 1, √2, 2, 2√2, …, multiplies by √2 each time, so whole-number and surd terms alternate, since √2×√2=2. A sequence such as 1/2, 2/3, 3/4, … is spotted by comparing the numerator and denominator with the position number: nth term = n/(n+1). Whatever the pattern, checking the rule against every term given is essential before writing an nth term down.","code":"6.06b"}],"examples":[{"title":"Using the cosine rule and the area formula in a triangle","setup":"Triangle PQR has PQ = 10 cm, PR = 6 cm and angle QPR = 40°. Work out the length QR, and work out the area of triangle PQR, each to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Two sides and the included angle are known, so use the cosine rule: QR² = PQ² + PR² − 2 × PQ × PR × cos(QPR)","Substitute the numbers: QR² = 10² + 6² − 2 × 10 × 6 × cos40°","Work out the right-hand side: QR² = 100 + 36 − 120 × 0.766… = 44.07…","Square-root to find QR: QR = √44.07… = 6.6 cm (1 dp)","For the area, use Area = ½ × PQ × PR × sin(QPR) = ½ × 10 × 6 × sin40°","Work this out: Area = 30 × 0.643… = 19.3 cm² (1 dp)"],"cues":["Which rule applies when two sides and the angle between them are known?","What do the three known values become once they are written into the rule?","What does the right-hand side come to before the square root is taken?","What operation turns QR² into QR?","Which formula finds the area from two sides and the included angle?","What does that formula come to once the values are substituted?"]},{"title":"Completing the square to solve exactly","setup":"Solve x² + 6x − 2 = 0 by completing the square, giving your answers in exact surd form. No calculator may be used.","steps":["Halve the coefficient of x, which gives 3, so the bracket is (x + 3)² and that expands to x² + 6x + 9","Take off the extra 9 to keep the value the same: x² + 6x − 2 = (x + 3)² − 9 − 2","Collect the numbers, so the equation becomes (x + 3)² − 11 = 0, that is (x + 3)² = 11","Square-root both sides, keeping both signs: x + 3 = ±√11","Subtract 3 from both sides: x = −3 + √11 or x = −3 − √11"],"cues":["Which number goes inside the bracket, and where does it come from?","Why can the bracket not simply replace the first two terms on its own?","What does the equation look like once the numbers are collected?","What must be written on the right-hand side once a square root is taken?","What single step finishes the solution?"]},{"title":"A line and a circle: linear/quadratic simultaneous equations","setup":"Solve the simultaneous equations x² + y² = 50 and 2y = x + 5 algebraically, giving both pairs of values.","steps":["Rearrange the linear equation to make x the subject: x = 2y − 5","Substitute into the quadratic equation: (2y − 5)² + y² = 50","Expand the bracket: 4y² − 20y + 25 + y² = 50","Collect and simplify to a quadratic in y: 5y² − 20y − 25 = 0, that is y² − 4y − 5 = 0","Factorise and solve: (y − 5)(y + 1) = 0, so y = 5 or y = −1","Substitute each value into x = 2y − 5: y = 5 gives x = 5; y = −1 gives x = −7"],"cues":["Which of the two equations can safely be rearranged for a single letter?","What happens to the quadratic equation once that expression is substituted in?","What does the squared bracket expand to?","What single equation is left once everything is collected?","Which two numbers factorise that quadratic?","Which equation is it safer to substitute each value back into?"]},{"title":"Locating a root by decimal search","setup":"Show that x³ − x − 1 = 0 has a root between x = 1 and x = 2, then use a decimal search to find this root to 2 decimal places. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Write f(x) = x³ − x − 1 and test the ends of the interval: f(1) = 1 − 1 − 1 = −1 and f(2) = 8 − 2 − 1 = 5","The two values have opposite signs, so the continuous graph crosses the x-axis between x = 1 and x = 2, and a root lies there","Narrow the interval: f(1.5) = 3.375 − 1.5 − 1 = 0.875, positive, so the root lies between 1 and 1.5","Test x = 1.3: f(1.3) = 2.197 − 1.3 − 1 = −0.103, negative, so the root lies between 1.3 and 1.5","Test x = 1.33: f(1.33) ≈ 0.023, positive, so the root lies between 1.3 and 1.33","Test x = 1.32: f(1.32) ≈ −0.020, negative, so the root lies between 1.32 and 1.33","The interval (1.32, 1.33) alone does not decide the 2 dp answer, so test the midpoint: f(1.325) ≈ 0.0012, positive, so the root lies between 1.32 and 1.325 — below the midpoint, so to 2 decimal places the root is x = 1.32"],"cues":["What two values would show a root is trapped somewhere in the interval?","What is it about the pair of answers that proves a crossing?","Which single new value splits the interval in half?","Which end of the new, narrower interval does 1.3 replace?","Which narrower interval does testing x = 1.33 give?","Which even narrower interval does testing x = 1.32 give?","Which value, exactly halfway between 1.32 and 1.33, decides which one the root rounds to?"]},{"title":"Composite and inverse functions","setup":"f is the function that doubles a number then adds 3, and g is the function that subtracts 1 then squares the result. Work out fg(2), work out gf(2), and find an expression for f⁻¹(x).","steps":["In fg(2) the function nearer the number acts first, so start with g(2) = (2 − 1)² = 1","Now put that output into f: f(1) = 2 × 1 + 3 = 5, so fg(2) = 5","In gf(2) the order is reversed: f(2) = 2 × 2 + 3 = 7, and then g(7) = (7 − 1)² = 36, so gf(2) = 36","For the inverse, write y = 2x + 3 and rearrange to make x the subject: y − 3 = 2x, so x = (y − 3)/2","Writing the rule in terms of x gives f⁻¹(x) = (x − 3)/2"],"cues":["Which of the two functions is applied to the number first in fg(2)?","What is done with the number the first function produced?","Which function acts on 2 first in gf(2)?","What has to be made the subject before the inverse can be written down?","Which letter should the final answer be written in?"]},{"title":"Deducing the nth term of a quadratic sequence","setup":"The quadratic sequence 0, 3, 10, 21, … continues in the same way. Deduce an expression for the nth term of the sequence.","steps":["First differences: 3 − 0 = 3, 10 − 3 = 7 and 21 − 10 = 11","Second differences: 7 − 3 = 4 and 11 − 7 = 4 — constant, so the n² coefficient is 4 ÷ 2 = 2","Subtract the values of 2n² (2, 8, 18, 32) from the sequence (0, 3, 10, 21) to leave −2, −5, −8, −11, a linear sequence with nth term −3n + 1","Combine the two parts: the nth term is 2n² + (−3n + 1) = 2n² − 3n + 1; check at n = 4: 32 − 12 + 1 = 21, the term given"],"cues":["What is the gap between each pair of terms next to each other?","Do those gaps themselves go up by the same amount each time?","What happens when the values of 2n² are taken away from the sequence?","Which term that you were given could test the finished expression?"]}],"check":[{"q":"y is inversely proportional to x². When x = 3, y = 4. Find a formula for y in terms of x, and work out y when x = 6.","a":"y = 36/x²; y = 1 when x = 6"},{"q":"Make x the subject of y = 5/(x + 1).","a":"x = (5 − y)/y"},{"q":"Solve x² + 8x − 5 = 0 by completing the square, giving exact answers.","a":"x = −4 + √21 or x = −4 − √21"},{"q":"Prove that the sum of two consecutive odd numbers is always even.","a":"(2n + 1) + (2n + 3) = 4n + 4 = 2(2n + 2), which is 2 times an integer, so the sum is always even."},{"q":"A sequence is defined by u₁ = 2 and uₙ₊₁ = 3uₙ + 1. Work out u₃.","a":"22"}]},"room":{"facts":["The quadratic formula: for ax²+bx+c=0 (a≠0), x = (−b ± √(b²−4ac))/2a — printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, but rearrange to = 0 and read off a, b, c with their signs before using it.","The sine rule: a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC, for finding a side or an angle in any triangle; the cosine rule: a² = b²+c²−2bc cosA, rearranged as cosA = (b²+c²−a²)/(2bc) to find an angle — both printed on the Higher tier sheet.","Area of a triangle = ½ab sinC, using two sides and the included angle — also printed on the Higher tier sheet.","Completing the square: x²+bx+c = (x+b/2)²−(b/2)²+c; when the x² coefficient is not 1, take it out of the x² and x terms first.","The discriminant b²−4ac: positive gives two different roots, zero gives one repeated root (a tangent, where the quadratic comes from a line meeting a curve), negative gives no real solutions.","Iteration: rearrange f(x)=0 into x=g(x) and use xₙ₊₁=g(xₙ) from a starting value x₀; a sign change in f(a) and f(b), with a continuous graph, proves a root lies between a and b.","A quadratic sequence's nth term is an²+bn+c, where the constant second difference equals 2a; a geometric progression's nth term is arⁿ⁻¹, where r may be a surd at Higher tier.","The standard algebraic forms for proof: any integer n; consecutive integers n, n+1, n+2; an even number 2n; an odd number 2n+1; a multiple of 5 is 5n — and a proof ends by writing the result in a form that shows the conclusion.","Laws of indices with negative and fractional powers: aᵐ×aⁿ=aᵐ⁺ⁿ, aᵐ÷aⁿ=aᵐ⁻ⁿ, a⁻ⁿ=1/aⁿ, a^(1/n)=ⁿ√a.","Inverse function: f⁻¹(x) is found by writing y=f(x), making x the subject, then rewriting in x; f⁻¹f(x)=x. Composite function: fg(x)=f(g(x)), with g acting first.","OCR's Higher tier assessment: three papers, J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes long. Paper 5 (J560/05) is non-calculator; Papers 4 and 6 allow a calculator. Grades 9–4 are available on the Higher tier (Foundation, J560/01–03, is graded 5–1, with Paper 2 non-calculator)."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Factorise 6x² + 7x − 3 fully.","a":"(2x + 3)(3x − 1)","sol":"a × c = 6 × (−3) = −18; two numbers with product −18 and sum 7 are 9 and −2. Split the middle term: 6x² + 9x − 2x − 3 = 3x(2x + 3) − 1(2x + 3) (M1 for splitting into 9x and −2x, or equivalent). = (2x + 3)(3x − 1) (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The coefficient of x² is not 1, so the two numbers you need are not simply a factor pair of the constant term.","Multiply the coefficient of x² by the constant term, find two numbers with that product and with sum 7, then use them to split the middle term.","Write down (coefficient of x²) × (constant term) — that calculation gives the product the two numbers must have."],"codes":["6.01e"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Simplify a^(1/2) × 6a⁻⁴, writing your answer as a single power of a with a numerical coefficient.","a":"6a^(−7/2)","sol":"Add the indices: 1/2 + (−4) = −7/2 (M1). So a^(1/2) × 6a⁻⁴ = 6a^(−7/2) (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Only the powers of a combine when two terms multiply; the coefficient is handled separately.","Add the two indices, keeping their signs, to get the power of a in the answer.","Write the calculation as 6 × a^(1/2 + (−4)) before simplifying the index."],"codes":["6.01c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write x² − 8x + 3 in the form (x + p)² + q.","a":"(x − 4)² − 13","sol":"Half of −8 is −4, so (x − 4)² = x² − 8x + 16 (M1). x² − 8x + 3 = (x − 4)² − 16 + 3 = (x − 4)² − 13 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The first two terms come from squaring a bracket; only the constant needs correcting afterwards.","Halve the coefficient of x for the number in the bracket, then compare its expansion with the expression given.","Start from (x − 4)² and work out how much too much or too little it gives before adding the 3."],"codes":["6.01f"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sequence is defined by x₁ = 5 and xₙ₊₁ = 2xₙ − 3. Work out x₃.","a":"11","sol":"x₂ = 2 × 5 − 3 = 7 (M1). x₃ = 2 × 7 − 3 = 11 (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each term is built from the one before it, so the terms have to be found in order.","Substitute the value you have into the rule to get the next term, then repeat once more.","Write down x₂ = 2 × x₁ − 3 first, then substitute the value you were given for x₁."],"codes":["6.06a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve x² < 16, giving your answer in set notation.","a":"{x : −4 < x < 4}","sol":"x² − 16 < 0 factorises as (x − 4)(x + 4) < 0, with critical values x = 4 and x = −4 (M1). Solution set: {x : −4 < x < 4} (A1). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which values of x make x² smaller than 16, not just where it equals 16.","Find the two values where x² = 16, then decide which side of them satisfies the inequality.","The two critical values satisfy x² = 16, so x = 4 or x = −4."],"codes":["6.04a"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) A surveyor measures a triangular field PQR, with PQ = 15 m, QR = 11 m and angle PQR = 55°. (a) Use the cosine rule to work out the length PR, to 1 decimal place. (b) Use the sine rule to work out the size of angle QPR, to 1 decimal place.","a":"PR = 12.5 m; angle QPR = 46.0° (accept 46.0° or 46.1° as follow-through from the rounded PR)","sol":"(a) PR² = PQ² + QR² − 2 × PQ × QR × cos(PQR) = 15² + 11² − 2 × 15 × 11 × cos55° (M1 for correct substitution into the cosine rule). = 225 + 121 − 330 × 0.5736… = 156.7…, so PR = √156.7… = 12.5188… = 12.5 m (1 dp) (A1). (b) The sine rule links QR with its opposite angle QPR: sin(QPR)/QR = sin(PQR)/PR (M1 for the sine rule set up correctly). Using the unrounded PR = 12.5188…: sin(QPR) = 11 × sin55°/12.5188… = 0.7198… (M1 for rearranging and evaluating). QPR = sin⁻¹(0.7198…) = 46.0° (1 dp) (A1); working from the rounded PR = 12.5 m instead gives sin(QPR) = 0.7209… and QPR = 46.1°, so accept 46.0° or 46.1° as follow-through from part (a). Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two sides and the angle between them give the third side by one particular rule; once all three sides and one angle are known, a second rule links any other side with the angle opposite it.","Substitute the two known sides and the included angle into the cosine rule for PR, then use the sine rule with QR and its opposite angle QPR.","For part (a), write PR² = 15² + 11² − 2 × 15 × 11 × cos(PQR), using the given angle, and work out the right-hand side before square-rooting."],"codes":["6.02d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The time T minutes for a repair job is inversely proportional to (w + 2), where w is the number of workers sent. When w = 3, T = 10. (a) Find a formula for T in terms of w. (b) Rearrange your formula to make w the subject.","a":"T = 50/(w + 2); w = (50 − 2T)/T","sol":"(a) T = k/(w + 2) (M1 for the correct form of an inverse proportion). Substituting w = 3, T = 10: 10 = k/5, so k = 50 (M1). T = 50/(w + 2) (A1). (b) Multiply both sides by (w + 2): T(w + 2) = 50, so Tw + 2T = 50 (M1). Collect the w-term: Tw = 50 − 2T (M1). Divide by T: w = (50 − 2T)/T (A1). Total 6 marks.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["\"Inversely proportional\" means T is written over the other quantity, with an unknown constant to find.","Write T = k/(w + 2), substitute the given pair of values to find k, then clear the fraction before rearranging for w.","Write down T = k/(w + 2); this is the equation the values you were given for T and w get substituted into to find k."],"codes":["6.02a","6.02c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Expand and simplify (x − 2)(x + 3)(2x − 1).","a":"2x³ + x² − 13x + 6","sol":"(x − 2)(x + 3) = x² + x − 6 (M1). Multiplying by (2x − 1): x²(2x − 1) + x(2x − 1) − 6(2x − 1) = 2x³ − x² + 2x² − x − 12x + 6 (M1 for multiplying every term of the quadratic by both terms of the remaining bracket). = 2x³ + x² − 13x + 6 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Three brackets cannot be multiplied all at once; take two of them together first.","Expand a pair of brackets and collect, then multiply the resulting quadratic by the bracket that is left.","Expand (x − 2)(x + 3) first to get a single quadratic."],"codes":["6.01d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A first function multiplies a number by 3 and then subtracts 2. A second function squares a number and then adds 4. Find, in terms of x, an expression for the result of applying the first function to x and then the second function to that result. Find, in terms of x, an expression for the result of applying the second function to x and then the first function to that result.","a":"9x² − 12x + 8; 3x² + 10","sol":"First function to x gives 3x − 2; applying the second function to that: (3x − 2)² + 4 (M1). Expand: 9x² − 12x + 4 + 4 = 9x² − 12x + 8 (A1). Second function to x gives x² + 4; applying the first function to that: 3(x² + 4) − 2 (M1). Expand: 3x² + 12 − 2 = 3x² + 10 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The order the two functions act in is not interchangeable, so read carefully which one is applied to x first each time.","Write down what the first function does to x, then feed that whole expression into the second function as its input.","For the first case, write (3x − 2)² + 4 and expand the brackets before simplifying."],"codes":["6.05a"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) Solve 2x² − 3x − 4 = 0 using the quadratic formula, giving your solutions to 2 decimal places.","a":"x = 2.35 or x = −0.85","sol":"With a = 2, b = −3, c = −4: b² − 4ac = (−3)² − 4×2×(−4) = 9 + 32 = 41 (M1). x = (3 ± √41)/4 (M1). √41 = 6.4031…, so x = 9.4031…/4 = 2.35 or x = −3.4031…/4 = −0.85 (2 dp) (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The equation is already in the form ax²+bx+c=0, so the coefficients can be read straight off, with their signs.","Work out the value under the square root on its own first, before substituting into the rest of the formula.","Write b²−4ac = (−3)² − 4×2×(−4) and work it out."],"codes":["6.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sequence begins 3, 3√2, 6, 6√2, 12, … and continues in the same pattern. (a) Write down the next term. (b) Work out the 8th term of the sequence, giving your answer exactly.","a":"next term = 12√2; 8th term = 24√2","sol":"3√2 ÷ 3 = √2, so the common ratio is r = √2 (M1). The next term after 12 is 12 × √2 = 12√2 (A1). Using arⁿ⁻¹ to reach the 8th term: 8th term = 3 × (√2)⁷ = 3 × 8√2 = 24√2 (M1 for using the ratio to reach the 8th position, A1 for 24√2). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Divide a term by the one before it to see what the sequence is being multiplied by each time.","Once the ratio is known, keep multiplying term by term, or use the nth term formula arⁿ⁻¹, to reach the position asked for.","Work out 3√2 ÷ 3 to find the common ratio."],"codes":["6.06b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Prove that the difference between the squares of any two consecutive even numbers is always a multiple of 4.","a":"Proved: the difference is 4(2n + 1), a multiple of 4","sol":"Let the two consecutive even numbers be 2n and 2n + 2, where n is an integer (B1 for both numbers in a correct general form). Their squares are (2n)² = 4n² and (2n + 2)² = 4n² + 8n + 4 (M1 for expanding (2n + 2)² correctly). The difference is (4n² + 8n + 4) − 4n² = 8n + 4 = 4(2n + 1), which is 4 times an integer, so the difference is always a multiple of 4 (A1). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["Testing a few pairs of even numbers will not be enough; the argument has to hold for every pair.","Write the two consecutive even numbers in terms of the same letter, square them, subtract, and look at the factor that appears.","Let the two even numbers be 2n and 2n + 2, and write down their squares."],"codes":["6.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve 2/x − 2/(x + 1) = 1.","a":"x = 1 or x = −2","sol":"Multiply every term by x(x + 1): 2(x + 1) − 2x = x(x + 1) (M1). Simplify the left-hand side: 2x + 2 − 2x = 2, so 2 = x² + x, that is x² + x − 2 = 0 (A1). Factorise: (x + 2)(x − 1) = 0 (M1), so x = −2 or x = 1 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Nothing can be solved until the fractions are cleared from the equation.","Multiply every term by x(x + 1), the product of the two denominators, then simplify before solving.","Write 2(x + 1) − 2x = x(x + 1) once the denominators have been cleared."],"codes":["6.01g","6.03a","6.03b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Solve the simultaneous equations y = x² − x − 1 and y = 2x − 3 algebraically, and hence write down the coordinates of the points where the line meets the curve.","a":"(1, −1) and (2, 1)","sol":"Both expressions equal y, so x² − x − 1 = 2x − 3 (M1). Collecting on the left: x² − 3x + 2 = 0 (A1). Factorising: (x − 1)(x − 2) = 0, so x = 1 or x = 2 (M1). Substituting into y = 2x − 3: x = 1 gives y = −1, x = 2 gives y = 1 (M1). The line meets the curve at (1, −1) and (2, 1) (A1). Total 5 marks.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Two expressions that are both equal to y are equal to each other, which removes one of the letters.","Form a single quadratic equal to zero, solve it, then substitute each value into the linear equation for its matching y.","Write x² − x − 1 = 2x − 3 and collect every term on one side."],"codes":["6.03c","6.03d"]},{"q":"(a calculator may be used) Show that x³ + x − 4 = 0 has a root between x = 1 and x = 1.5. Starting with x₀ = 1.3, use the iteration xₙ₊₁ = ∛(4 − xₙ) to find this root to 3 decimal places.","a":"x = 1.379","sol":"Let f(x) = x³ + x − 4. f(1) = 1 + 1 − 4 = −2 and f(1.5) = 3.375 + 1.5 − 4 = 0.875 (M1 for both values). The values have opposite signs and the cubic is continuous, so a root lies between x = 1 and x = 1.5 (A1). Rearranging x³ + x − 4 = 0 gives x³ = 4 − x, so x = ∛(4 − x) and xₙ₊₁ = ∛(4 − xₙ). Starting from x₀ = 1.3: x₁ = ∛2.7 = 1.392477, x₂ = ∛2.607523 = 1.376394, x₃ = ∛2.623606 = 1.379218, x₄ = ∛2.620782 = 1.378723 (M1 for correctly iterating at least twice). x₃ and x₄ both round to 1.379 (3 dp), so the root is x = 1.379 (A1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A change of sign between the two ends of an interval, on a continuous graph, shows a root is trapped inside it.","Rearrange the equation into the form x = g(x), then use the iterative formula repeatedly, feeding each answer back in.","Work out f(1) and f(1.5) to show the sign change before anything else."],"codes":["6.03e"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A region R on a graph is defined by y ≤ 2x, x + y < 8 and y > 2. State which of the points A(3, 3), B(1, 5) and C(4, 1) lie in R, and say which of the three boundary lines are drawn dashed.","a":"Only A(3, 3) lies in the region; x + y = 8 and y = 2 are dashed","sol":"Test A(3, 3): y ≤ 2x → 3 ≤ 6 true; x + y < 8 → 6 < 8 true; y > 2 → 3 > 2 true (M1 for testing a point against all three inequalities), so A lies in the region (A1). Test B(1, 5): 5 ≤ 2×1 is false, so B is outside; test C(4, 1): 1 > 2 is false, so C is outside (A1). A strict inequality has a dashed boundary, so the lines x + y = 8 and y = 2 are dashed, while y = 2x (≤) is solid (B1). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A point lies in the region only if it satisfies every one of the inequalities at once.","Substitute each point's coordinates into all three inequalities in turn and see which, if any, fail.","For A(3, 3), check whether 3 ≤ 2×3, whether 3+3 is less than 8, and whether 3 is greater than 2."],"codes":["6.04b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Factorise 4x² + 4x − 3.","a":"(2x + 3)(2x − 1)"},{"q":"Solve x² − 7x + 10 = 0.","a":"x = 5 or x = 2"},{"q":"A function multiplies a number by 3 and then subtracts 2, giving an output y. Find a formula, in terms of y, for the number that produces that output (the inverse function).","a":"(y + 2)/3"},{"q":"Solve the inequality x² − 4 > 0, giving your answer in set notation.","a":"{x : x < −2} ∪ {x : x > 2}"}]},"cards":[{"front":"The standard algebraic forms used in proof","back":"Any integer n; consecutive integers n, n+1, n+2; even 2n; odd 2n+1; consecutive even or odd numbers differ by 2; a multiple of 5 is 5n. A proof ends by writing the result in a form that shows the conclusion.","code":"6.01a","open":false},{"front":"Laws of indices with negative and fractional powers","back":"aᵐ×aⁿ=aᵐ⁺ⁿ; aᵐ÷aⁿ=aᵐ⁻ⁿ; a⁻ⁿ=1/aⁿ; a^(1/n)=ⁿ√a, so a^(m/n)=(ⁿ√a)ᵐ. Add or subtract the indices, keeping their signs; simplify the coefficient separately.","code":"6.01c","open":false},{"front":"Expanding a product of three or more binomials","back":"Multiply any two brackets and collect to get a quadratic, then multiply that quadratic by the remaining bracket, term by term, and collect again. Both stages of expansion must be seen.","code":"6.01d","open":false},{"front":"Factorising ax²+bx+c when a ≠ 1","back":"Find two numbers with product a×c and sum b; split the middle term into those two x-terms; factorise in pairs; the repeated bracket and the outside factors form the answer.","code":"6.01e","open":false},{"front":"Completing the square","back":"x²+bx+c = (x+b/2)²−(b/2)²+c. When the x² coefficient is not 1, take it out of the x² and x terms first, complete the square inside, then multiply the correction back out.","code":"6.01f","open":false},{"front":"Simplifying and combining algebraic fractions","back":"Factorise the numerator and denominator fully, then cancel a shared bracket — never a single term. To add or subtract, write both fractions over a common denominator first.","code":"6.01g","open":false},{"front":"The quadratic formula","back":"For ax²+bx+c=0 (a≠0): x=(−b±√(b²−4ac))/2a. Printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, but rearrange to =0 and read off a, b, c with their signs before using it.","code":"6.02d","open":false},{"front":"The sine rule","back":"a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC, linking each side with the angle opposite it — used for a missing side or angle when a side and its opposite angle are both known. On the Higher tier sheet.","code":"6.02d","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule","back":"a²=b²+c²−2bc cosA, for a side when two sides and the included angle are known, or rearranged as cosA=(b²+c²−a²)/(2bc) for an angle when all three sides are known. On the Higher tier sheet.","code":"6.02d","open":false},{"front":"Area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle","back":"Area = ½ab sinC, used when the height is not known but two sides and the angle between them are. Printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet.","code":"6.02d","open":false},{"front":"Solving simultaneous equations where one is quadratic","back":"Rearrange the linear equation for one letter, substitute into the quadratic equation, solve the single equation that results, then substitute each value back into the linear equation for its pair.","code":"6.03c","open":false},{"front":"Iteration and the sign-change test","back":"If f(a) and f(b) have opposite signs and y=f(x) is continuous between them, a root of f(x)=0 lies there. Rearrange to x=g(x) and use xₙ₊₁=g(xₙ) from a given x₀.","code":"6.03e","open":false},{"front":"Inverse and composite functions","back":"fg(x)=f(g(x)): g acts first. f⁻¹ reverses f, so f⁻¹f(x)=x; find it by writing y=f(x), making x the subject, then rewriting the rule in x.","code":"6.05a","open":false},{"front":"The nth term of a quadratic sequence","back":"an²+bn+c, where the constant second difference equals 2a. Halve it for a, subtract the an² values from the terms, then find the nth term of the linear sequence left. xₙ names the term in position n.","code":"6.06a","open":false},{"front":"Give an example of a sequence that is not arithmetic, quadratic or geometric with a rational ratio","back":"1, √2, 2, 2√2, … (ratio √2, surd and whole-number terms alternate); 1/2, 2/3, 3/4, … (nth term n/(n+1)); 1, 1/2, 1/3, 1/4, … (nth term 1/n)","code":"6.06b","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["How to read this list: this is a Higher tier room, so OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet (read 19 September 2026 at ocr.org.uk/Images/742417-higher-tier-formulae-sheet.pdf) is the one that applies. The specification's own rule (2b) says no formula sheet was originally planned and that 6.02d's four results must be recalled — but the sheet that is actually issued each series prints all four of them, so it governs what is in front of you on the day. 'Given on the Higher sheet' below means it is printed on the insert; 'Recall' means you must know it from memory, because neither the specification's own rule nor the sheet provides it.","Given on the Higher sheet, though 6.02d says to recall it: the quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) / 2a, for solving ax²+bx+c = 0.","Given on the Higher sheet, though 6.02d says to recall it: the sine rule, a/sinA = b/sinB = c/sinC.","Given on the Higher sheet, though 6.02d says to recall it: the cosine rule, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cosA.","Given on the Higher sheet, though 6.02d says to recall it: the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, Area = ½ab sinC.","Recall — not on either tier's sheet and not listed in the specification's own formulae rule: completing the square, x²+bx+c=(x+b/2)²−(b/2)²+c, and the discriminant b²−4ac (positive: two roots; zero: one repeated root; negative: no real roots).","Recall: the laws of indices with negative and fractional powers (a⁻ⁿ=1/aⁿ, a^(1/n)=ⁿ√a), and the method for factorising ax²+bx+c and expanding a product of more than two binomials.","Recall: the method for simplifying and combining algebraic fractions, and for clearing an algebraic fraction from an equation by multiplying every term by the denominator(s).","Recall: the method for solving simultaneous equations where one is a quadratic (substitute the linear equation into the quadratic), and that the points of intersection of a line and a curve are the solutions of the pair of equations.","Recall: iteration — rearranging f(x)=0 into x=g(x), the iterative formula xₙ₊₁=g(xₙ), and the sign-change test for locating a root.","Recall: the method for solving a quadratic inequality (factorise, find the critical values, read the sketch) and set notation for the solution; the conventions for a region defined by inequalities in two variables (dashed for a strict inequality, solid where it is included).","Recall: function notation — fg(x)=f(g(x)) with g acting first, and finding f⁻¹(x) by making x the subject of y=f(x) — and the method for the nth term of a quadratic sequence (halve the constant second difference) and for a sequence that is none of the named families, whose rule is always given or found from the pattern.","Also on the Higher sheet but not needed in this room: the area of a trapezium, the volume of a prism, the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle, compound interest, and the two probability rules — see appendix.md section 3 for the complete list, which is reissued each series."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:11:46Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Every fix in 'fixed' checks out: all 16 answer keys, 5 check questions, 4 exit items and 6 worked examples re-solved with sympy and correct (PR = 12.5 m and 46.0°, y = 8/(x+3) and x = (8−3y)/y, 9x²−12x+8 and 3x²+10, f(1.325) = +0.0012, inverse (y+2)/3); marks tally to m everywhere, 13 questions are non-calculator and hand-workable, all 19 statements now carry a bank question, the paper numbers are in room.facts and the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md; only a misaligned cue in the decimal-search example and a rounding caveat on the surveyor question remain."}}},"graphs":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"graphs","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Graphs are where an equation, a table of values and a real situation meet, and OCR 7 threads through every paper at Foundation tier: plotting a point in any of the four quadrants, building a table of values, reading a gradient and a y-intercept from y = mx + c, finding the roots and turning point of a parabola, recognising a cubic or reciprocal shape on sight, and reading a speed off a distance-time or conversion graph. A sign slip in a coordinate or a gradient read the wrong way round costs the accuracy mark however sound the rest of the method is, so this room drills the habit of substituting carefully and reading axes with their units before answering.","idea":[{"h":"Points on the plane: coordinates in all four quadrants","t":"A point is fixed by an ordered pair (x, y): the x-coordinate is read first, along the horizontal axis from the origin (0, 0); the y-coordinate is read second, up or down the vertical axis. To the left of the y-axis x is negative; below the x-axis y is negative, so the four quadrants carry the sign pairs (+, +) top right, (−, +) top left, (−, −) bottom left and (+, −) bottom right. The exam asks you to plot points in all four quadrants, to write down the coordinates of a point already marked, and to read a value off a table before it is plotted. A point misplotted by more than half a square, or with x and y swapped, loses the accuracy mark even when the arithmetic used to find it is correct.","code":"7.01a"},{"h":"Building and reading a table of values","t":"A table of values substitutes each given x in turn into the equation to work out the matching y, so that the pairs can be plotted and joined to draw the graph; the table itself carries the method mark, so show every substitution. This works for a linear graph such as y = 2x + 3, a quadratic such as y = 2x² + 1, and at Foundation tier also for other polynomials and reciprocals such as y = x³ − 2x, y = x + 1/x and an equation not yet arranged for y, such as 2x + 3y = 6, which must first be rearranged or substituted directly. Work through the table left to right, checking each pair against the shape you expect before joining the points with a smooth curve or a ruled straight line.","code":"7.01b"},{"h":"Recognising and sketching linear, quadratic, cubic and reciprocal graphs","t":"The highest power of x decides the shape. A linear function is a straight line; a quadratic (highest power x²) is a U-shaped parabola, upside down if the x² term is negative and symmetrical about a vertical line. y = x³ rises from the bottom left, flattens near the origin and rises again to the top right. y = 1/x, with x ≠ 0, is a pair of separate branches, approaching but never touching the axes, its asymptotes. For a quadratic, the y-intercept is found by putting x = 0; the roots are found by putting y = 0 and factorising; and because the curve is symmetrical, its turning point lies midway between the roots, at x = (one root + the other root) ÷ 2, with its y-coordinate found by substituting that x back in.","code":"7.01c"},{"h":"Straight-line graphs: gradient, intercept and y = mx + c","t":"Written as y = mx + c, m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept, the point (0, c) where the line crosses the y-axis; rewrite the x term first if it is not already so, as for y = 5 − 3x, where m = −3 and c = 5. Given two points, the gradient is the change in y divided by the change in x; substitute that m and either point back into y = mx + c to find c. Given one point and a gradient, the same substitution finds c directly. A positive gradient rises from left to right, a negative gradient falls, and a gradient of 0 is a horizontal line; always finish by writing the equation in the form the question asks for.","code":"7.02a"},{"h":"Parallel lines","t":"Two lines are parallel exactly when their gradients are equal, whatever their y-intercepts are: y = 2x − 3 and y = 2x + 5 are parallel because both have m = 2, and they never meet because they keep the same vertical distance apart. To find the equation of a line parallel to a given one, copy its gradient and then use a given point, or a given y-intercept, to fix c in y = mx + c. An equation may need rearranging into y = mx + c form first before its gradient can be compared with another line's, for example by dividing every term by the coefficient of y.","code":"7.02b"},{"h":"Graphs of real-world situations, including direct and inverse proportion","t":"Real quantities can be plotted against each other and read back: a distance-time graph, a cost graph, a money-conversion or temperature-conversion graph. A straight-line real-life graph has a fixed starting value as its intercept and a rate as its gradient. When the line passes through the origin, the two quantities are in direct proportion, and the gradient is the constant rate connecting them; a graph that curves and levels off without ever reaching the axis, such as time against number of workers, shows inverse proportion instead. Where a question says 'use the graph', the reading off the axis is the mark, so draw the dashed lines you used to get it.","code":"7.04a"},{"h":"Gradient as a rate of change, and its link to ratio","t":"The gradient of a real-life straight-line graph is a rate of change: the amount the vertical quantity changes for one unit of the horizontal quantity, carrying a unit such as km/h, litres per minute or pounds per hour, and it is closely linked to a ratio between the two quantities, since a steeper gradient is a larger ratio of vertical change to horizontal change. On a distance-time graph the gradient of each stage is the speed; a horizontal section means the object is stationary. Average speed for a whole journey is total distance divided by total time, not the average of the separate stage speeds, because the stages do not usually take equal amounts of time.","code":"7.04b"}],"examples":[{"title":"A table of values, and testing whether a point is on the line","setup":"The equation of a straight line is y = 3x − 1. A table of values is being filled in for x = −2, x = 0 and x = 4. A point Q has coordinates (−3, −11). Work out the three y-values for the table, and decide whether Q lies on the line, showing how you decide.","steps":["Substitute x = −2: y = 3 × (−2) − 1 = −6 − 1 = −7, giving the pair (−2, −7).","Substitute x = 0: y = 3 × 0 − 1 = −1, giving the pair (0, −1).","Substitute x = 4: y = 3 × 4 − 1 = 12 − 1 = 11, giving the pair (4, 11).","Test Q by substituting its x-coordinate, −3, and compare the result with its given y-coordinate, −11: y = 3 × (−3) − 1 = −9 − 1 = −10.","The rule gives −10, which does not match Q's y-coordinate of −11, so Q does not lie on the line."],"cues":["Which operation is done first, the multiplying or the subtracting?","What happens to the 3x term when x is zero?","Does a positive x here give a positive or a negative y?","What must you do with the x-coordinate of Q before you can compare anything?","What has to be true of the two y-values for Q to be on the line?"]},{"title":"The equation of a line through two points, and a parallel line","setup":"A straight line passes through the points A(2, 5) and B(5, 17). Find the equation of this line in the form y = mx + c. Then write down the equation of the line that is parallel to it and crosses the y-axis at (0, −7).","steps":["Gradient m = (17 − 5) ÷ (5 − 2) = 12 ÷ 3 = 4.","Substitute m = 4 and the point A(2, 5) into y = mx + c: 5 = 4 × 2 + c.","So c = 5 − 8 = −3, and the line is y = 4x − 3.","A parallel line shares the same gradient, m = 4, and must pass through the given y-intercept (0, −7).","So c = −7 for this line, giving the parallel line y = 4x − 7."],"cues":["Which difference goes on top, the y-values or the x-values?","Which of the two given points may be substituted?","What is left to find once m is known?","What do parallel lines always share, and what extra information fixes this particular line?","Where does the y-intercept of a line appear in y = mx + c?"]},{"title":"Reading a parabola: y-intercept, roots and turning point","setup":"The graph of y = x² − 2x − 3 is drawn for values of x from −2 to 4. Write down the coordinates of its y-intercept, find the coordinates of the points where it crosses the x-axis by factorising, and use the symmetry of the curve to find the coordinates of its turning point.","steps":["The y-intercept is where x = 0: y = 0² − 2 × 0 − 3 = −3, so the curve crosses at (0, −3).","Factorise the expression: x² − 2x − 3 = (x + 1)(x − 3).","Roots: (x + 1)(x − 3) = 0 gives x = −1 or x = 3, so the crossings are (−1, 0) and (3, 0).","By symmetry the turning point lies midway between the roots: x = (−1 + 3) ÷ 2 = 1.","Substituting: y = 1² − 2 × 1 − 3 = 1 − 2 − 3 = −4, so the turning point is (1, −4)."],"cues":["What is always true of the x-coordinate on the y-axis?","Which two numbers multiply to give the constant and add to give the x coefficient?","What must y be for a point on the x-axis?","Where does the line of symmetry of a parabola sit relative to its roots?","How do you turn that x-coordinate into the missing y-coordinate?"]},{"title":"Matching equations to graph shapes","setup":"Four equations are given: y = 7 − 3x, y = x² − 16, y = x³ + 2x, and y = 4/x with x ≠ 0. Four sketches are described: a straight line falling from left to right; a U-shaped curve with a minimum; a curve that rises, flattens near the origin and rises again; and a curve made of two separate branches. Match each equation to its sketch, and state where y = x² − 16 crosses the x-axis.","steps":["y = 7 − 3x has x as its highest power and a negative coefficient, so it matches the straight line falling from left to right.","y = x² − 16 has highest power x², so it matches the U-shaped curve with a minimum.","y = x³ + 2x is cubic, so it matches the curve that rises, flattens near the origin and rises again.","y = 4/x is the reciprocal function, undefined when x is zero, so it matches the two separate branches, with the axes as asymptotes.","For the parabola, y = 0 gives x² − 16 = 0, so x² = 16 and x = 4 or x = −4: it crosses at (4, 0) and (−4, 0)."],"cues":["What does the sign of the x coefficient do to a straight line?","Which power of x produces a curve with one turning point?","Which of the remaining equations has the highest power of all?","Why can one of these graphs have no point on the y-axis?","What is the y-coordinate everywhere on the x-axis?"]},{"title":"Reading a distance-time graph for a train journey","setup":"A train journey is drawn on a distance-time graph as three straight stages. Stage 1 goes from (0 h, 0 km) to (0.75 h, 60 km). Stage 2 goes from (0.75 h, 60 km) to (1.25 h, 60 km). Stage 3 goes from (1.25 h, 60 km) to (2 h, 90 km). Find the speed on stage 1 in km/h, say what stage 2 shows, and find the average speed for the whole journey in km/h.","steps":["Stage 1 speed = gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time = 60 ÷ 0.75 = 80 km/h.","Stage 2 is horizontal, so the distance stays at 60 km: the train is stationary for 1.25 − 0.75 = 0.5 h.","For the whole journey, the total distance is the final reading, 90 km, and the total time is 2 h.","Average speed = total distance ÷ total time = 90 ÷ 2 = 45 km/h."],"cues":["Which quantity is on which axis, and which way round does the division go?","What is the gradient of a horizontal line, and what does that mean here?","Is the total distance the sum of the stages or the last reading on the axis?","Why is this not the average of the stage speeds?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Write down the gradient and the y-intercept of the line y = 8 − 5x.","a":"Gradient −5; y-intercept (0, 8)"},{"q":"In which quadrant does the point (6, −2) lie?","a":"The fourth quadrant, bottom right, where x is positive and y is negative"},{"q":"The curve y = (x − 5)(x + 3) meets the x-axis at two points. What is the x-coordinate of its turning point?","a":"x = 1, midway between the roots 5 and −3"},{"q":"A distance-time graph is a straight line from (0 h, 0 km) to (3 h, 90 km). What is the speed?","a":"30 km/h"}]},"room":{"facts":["Gradient: m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁).","A straight line has equation y = mx + c, where m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept; parallel lines have equal m.","The roots of a quadratic graph are the x-values where the curve crosses the x-axis: put y = 0 and factorise.","A parabola is symmetrical, so its turning point lies midway between its roots: x = (one root + the other root) ÷ 2.","On a distance-time graph the gradient is the speed; a horizontal section means stationary, and average speed = total distance ÷ total time.","y = 1/x, with x ≠ 0, has two separate branches with the x-axis and the y-axis as asymptotes; a straight line through the origin shows direct proportion.","OCR sets three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes: J560/01–03 Foundation (Paper 2 non-calculator) and J560/04–06 Higher (Paper 5 non-calculator); Foundation is graded 5–1 and Higher 9–4."],"questions":[{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the point that is 4 units to the left of the origin and 6 units above it. (non-calculator)","a":"(−4, 6)","sol":"Left of the origin means a negative x-coordinate and above the origin means a positive y-coordinate, so the point is (−4, 6). B1 for the correct ordered pair.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Moving left changes the sign of one coordinate and moving up fixes the sign of the other.","The horizontal move gives the x-coordinate and the vertical move gives the y-coordinate, written in that order.","Write the point as (x, y), then settle the sign of each coordinate from its direction out of the origin before filling in its size."],"codes":["7.01a"]},{"q":"The point (5, k) lies on the line y = 4x − 3. The point (h, 9) lies on the same line. Work out the value of k and the value of h. (non-calculator)","a":"k = 17 and h = 3","sol":"The first point gives x, so substitute it: k = 4 × 5 − 3 = 20 − 3 = 17. The second point gives y, so solve for x: 9 = 4h − 3, 4h = 9 + 3 = 12, h = 3. M1 for a correct substitution into y = 4x − 3 for either point. A1 for both k = 17 and h = 3. (Check: 4 × 3 − 3 = 9.)","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A point lies on a line exactly when its coordinates fit the equation of that line.","One point tells you x and leaves y to be found; the other tells you y, so that one needs the equation solved for x.","Write y = 4 × x − 3 with the x-coordinate of the first point in place, then write the equation again with 9 in place of y."],"codes":["7.01a","7.02a"]},{"q":"Write down the gradient and the y-intercept of the line y = 9 − 4x. (non-calculator)","a":"Gradient −4; y-intercept (0, 9)","sol":"Rewrite with the x term first, in the form y = mx + c: y = −4x + 9. B1 for the gradient m = −4. B1 for the y-intercept c = 9, that is the point (0, 9).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both numbers can be read off directly once the equation is arranged in the usual way.","In y = mx + c the coefficient of x is the gradient and the number on its own is the y-intercept.","Rewrite the right-hand side with the x term written first, then match it term by term against y = mx + c."],"codes":["7.02a"]},{"q":"Which one of these equations has a graph that is a cubic curve: y = 6 − x, y = x² + 2x, y = 3/x, y = x³ + 3x? A calculator may be used.","a":"y = x³ + 3x","sol":"The shape of a graph is decided by the highest power of x in its equation; a cubic curve comes from an equation whose highest power is x³. Of the four equations only y = x³ + 3x is cubic. B1.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The shape of a graph is decided by the highest power of x in its equation.","A cubic curve rises, flattens near the origin, then rises again.","Take each of the four equations in turn and write down the highest power of x it contains."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"The graph of y = (x + 4)(x − 9) crosses the x-axis at two points. Write down the coordinates of both points. A calculator may be used.","a":"(−4, 0) and (9, 0)","sol":"On the x-axis y = 0, so (x + 4)(x − 9) = 0. M1 for setting each bracket equal to zero, giving x = −4 or x = 9. A1 for both points written as coordinates, (−4, 0) and (9, 0).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A graph crosses the x-axis where its y-coordinate is zero.","A product of two brackets is zero only when one of the brackets is zero.","Write (x + 4)(x − 9) = 0, then set each bracket equal to zero in turn."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"A table of values for y = x + 1/x is being completed for x = 1, x = 2 and x = 4. Work out the three y-values, giving each as a whole number or a decimal. (non-calculator)","a":"y = 2 when x = 1; y = 2.5 when x = 2; y = 4.25 when x = 4","sol":"Substitute x = 1: y = 1 + 1 ÷ 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. B1. Substitute x = 2: y = 2 + 1 ÷ 2 = 2 + 0.5 = 2.5. B1. Substitute x = 4: y = 4 + 1 ÷ 4 = 4 + 0.25 = 4.25. B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each y-value needs the given x substituted into the whole expression, including the fraction.","Work out x, then work out 1 ÷ x, then add the two together.","Write y = x + 1 ÷ x, and replace x by the first value in the table."],"codes":["7.01b"]},{"q":"A straight line has a gradient of −2 and passes through the point (4, 5). Work out the equation of the line, and write down the coordinates of the point where it crosses the y-axis. (non-calculator)","a":"y = −2x + 13; it crosses the y-axis at (0, 13)","sol":"The gradient is given, so y = −2x + c. Substituting the point (4, 5): 5 = −2 × 4 + c, so 5 = −8 + c and c = 13. M1 for substituting m = −2 and (4, 5) into y = mx + c. A1 for y = −2x + 13. B1 for the y-intercept (0, 13). (Check: −2 × 4 + 13 = 5.)","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The gradient is already known, so the only thing missing from y = mx + c is c.","Put the gradient and the coordinates of the given point into y = mx + c, then solve the resulting equation for c.","Start by writing y = −2x + c, then replace x and y by the coordinates of the point you are given."],"codes":["7.02a"]},{"q":"The line L has equation y = 6x − 5. The line N has equation 3y = 18x + 21. Show that L and N are parallel, and write down the coordinates of the y-intercept of N. A calculator may be used.","a":"Both gradients are 6, so L and N are parallel; the y-intercept of N is (0, 7)","sol":"Make y the subject of N: dividing 3y = 18x + 21 by 3 gives y = 6x + 7. M1. The gradient of N is 6 and the gradient of L is 6, and lines with equal gradients are parallel. A1. Its y-intercept is c = 7, the point (0, 7). B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["One of the two equations is not yet in the arrangement that shows its gradient.","Make y the subject of the second equation, then compare the coefficients of x: equal gradients mean the lines are parallel.","Divide every term of 3y = 18x + 21 by 3."],"codes":["7.02a","7.02b"]},{"q":"The curve y = 2x² − 3x + 5 is drawn. Write down the coordinates of its y-intercept, and state whether the curve has a minimum or a maximum turning point, giving a reason. (non-calculator)","a":"y-intercept (0, 5); a minimum turning point, because the coefficient of x² is positive","sol":"The y-intercept is where x = 0: y = 2 × 0² − 3 × 0 + 5 = 5, so the curve crosses at (0, 5). B1. The coefficient of x² is 2, which is positive, so the curve is U-shaped. B1. A U-shaped curve has a minimum turning point. B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The y-intercept is the value of y when x is set to zero.","Look at the sign of the number in front of x² to decide which way up the curve opens.","Substitute x = 0 into y = 2x² − 3x + 5, then compare the sign of the x² coefficient with the two possible shapes described in the room."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"A conversion graph for kilometres (k) against miles (m) is a straight line through the origin. It passes through the point (50 km, 31 miles). State, with a reason, whether kilometres and miles are in direct proportion, then use the graph's gradient to work out how many miles are equivalent to 120 km. A calculator may be used.","a":"Yes, they are in direct proportion because the line passes through the origin; 120 km is equivalent to 74.4 miles","sol":"A straight-line graph through the origin shows two quantities are in direct proportion, and this one passes through (0, 0). B1. Gradient (miles per km) = 31 ÷ 50 = 0.62. M1. Miles for 120 km = 0.62 × 120 = 74.4 miles. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A graph shows direct proportion when it is a straight line through one particular point.","Find the rate (miles per kilometre) from the given point, then scale it up to the new distance.","Write miles ÷ km using the given point, then multiply that rate by 120."],"codes":["7.04a"]},{"q":"A candle burns down at a steady rate. A graph of its height h cm against time t minutes is a straight line from (0 min, 20 cm) to (8 min, 4 cm). Work out the rate at which the candle burns, in cm per minute, and use it to find how many minutes it takes for the candle to burn out completely. A calculator may be used.","a":"The candle burns at 2 cm per minute, and it takes 10 minutes to burn out completely","sol":"Rate = gradient = change in height ÷ change in time = (4 − 20) ÷ (8 − 0) = −16 ÷ 8 = −2, so the candle burns down at 2 cm per minute. M1 A1. Starting at 20 cm, the candle burns out (height 0) after 20 ÷ 2 = 10 minutes. B1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The candle gets shorter over time, so the gradient of the graph is negative.","The rate is the gradient: change in height divided by change in time.","Write rate = (change in height) ÷ (change in time) using the two given points, then divide the starting height by that rate."],"codes":["7.04a","7.04b"]},{"q":"The graph of y = x² + bx + c crosses the x-axis at (−6, 0) and (2, 0). Find the value of b and the value of c, and write down the x-coordinate of the turning point. (non-calculator)","a":"b = 4, c = −12; turning point at x = −2","sol":"The roots are x = −6 and x = 2, so y = (x + 6)(x − 2). M1. Expanding: x² − 2x + 6x − 12 = x² + 4x − 12, so b = 4. A1. And c = −12. A1. By symmetry the turning point is midway between the roots: x = (−6 + 2) ÷ 2 = −2. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Knowing where a quadratic curve crosses the x-axis tells you its factorised form.","Write the equation as a product of two brackets built from the roots, expand it, then compare it with x² + bx + c.","Write y = (x − first root)(x − second root), putting each root into its bracket with its sign changed."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"A plumber charges a fixed call-out fee plus a fixed amount for each hour worked. A 3-hour job costs £108 and a 6-hour job costs £198. With C the cost in pounds and h the number of hours, find the equation connecting them in the form C = mh + c, and use it to find the cost of a 9-hour job. A calculator may be used.","a":"C = 30h + 18, and a 9-hour job costs £288","sol":"Gradient m = (198 − 108) ÷ (6 − 3) = 90 ÷ 3 = 30, so the hourly rate is £30. M1 A1. Substituting h = 3, C = 108: 108 = 30 × 3 + c = 90 + c, so c = 18 and C = 30h + 18. M1 A1. When h = 9: C = 30 × 9 + 18 = 270 + 18 = 288, so the job costs £288. A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["A fixed fee plus a fixed hourly rate is a straight-line relationship between the cost and the hours.","The hourly rate is the gradient between the two jobs and the call-out fee is the intercept c; then substitute the new number of hours.","Write m = (difference between the two costs) ÷ (difference between the two times)."],"codes":["7.02a","7.04a","7.04b"]},{"q":"The curve y = x² − 6 and the line y = 30 are drawn on the same axes. Write down the coordinates of the two points where they meet, and write down the y-coordinate of the turning point of the curve. (non-calculator)","a":"(6, 30) and (−6, 30); the turning point has y = −6","sol":"Where the graphs meet the y-values are equal, so x² − 6 = 30. M1. Then x² = 36, so x = 6 or x = −6. A1. The meeting points are (6, 30) and (−6, 30). A1. The curve y = x² − 6 has its minimum where x = 0, giving y = 0 − 6 = −6. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Where two graphs meet, the two expressions for y must be equal.","Set the two expressions equal and solve, remembering that a positive number has two square roots; the minimum of this curve sits on the y-axis.","Write x² − 6 = 30 and add 6 to both sides."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"The time t hours a job takes is inversely proportional to the number of workers n, given by t = 40/n. Find t when n = 5, find n when t = 2, and explain why the graph of t against n never meets the n-axis. A calculator may be used.","a":"t = 8 hours; n = 20 workers; because 40 ÷ n is never zero, however large n is","sol":"n = 5: t = 40 ÷ 5 = 8 hours. B1. t = 2: 2 = 40 ÷ n, so n = 40 ÷ 2 = 20 workers. M1 A1. Dividing 40 by n never gives zero, however many workers there are, so t stays above zero and the curve approaches the n-axis without ever meeting it. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The rule links the two quantities by a division, so its graph is a reciprocal curve with a branch that levels off.","Substitute the given number of workers to find the time; rearrange the rule to go the other way; then ask whether the division can ever give zero.","Write t = 40 ÷ n and put in the number of workers you are given."],"codes":["7.01c","7.04a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"For y = 2x² + 1, work out the y-value in the table when x = 3.","a":"y = 19"},{"q":"Are the lines y = 5x − 2 and y = 5x + 9 parallel? Give a reason.","a":"Yes, because both have gradient 5"},{"q":"A graph of cost against number of tickets sold is a straight line through the origin. What does this show about cost and number of tickets?","a":"They are in direct proportion"},{"q":"A section of a distance-time graph is horizontal. What is happening to the object?","a":"It is stationary (not moving)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Coordinates (x, y)","back":"An ordered pair fixing a point: x is read first along the horizontal axis from the origin, y second up or down the vertical axis. The order matters.","code":"7.01a","open":false},{"front":"The signs of the coordinates in the four quadrants","back":"Top right (+, +); top left (−, +); bottom left (−, −); bottom right (+, −). x is negative left of the y-axis, y is negative below the x-axis.","code":"7.01a","open":false},{"front":"A table of values","back":"x-values with their matching y-values, found by substituting each x into the equation; plotting these pairs and joining them draws the graph.","code":"7.01b","open":false},{"front":"The method mark on a plotted graph","back":"Shown by correctly working out each pair of values in the table: every correctly substituted pair earns credit, even before the points are plotted or joined.","code":"7.01b","open":false},{"front":"Roots of a quadratic graph","back":"The x-values where the curve crosses the x-axis, so where y = 0. Found by putting y = 0, factorising, and setting each bracket equal to zero.","code":"7.01c","open":false},{"front":"Position of the turning point of a parabola","back":"Midway between the roots, because the curve is symmetrical: average the two roots for its x-coordinate, then substitute that x into the equation for its y-coordinate.","code":"7.01c","open":false},{"front":"The shape of the graph of y = x³","back":"A cubic curve: it rises from the bottom left, flattens near the origin and then rises again to the top right.","code":"7.01c","open":false},{"front":"The shape of the graph of y = 1/x, x ≠ 0","back":"The reciprocal function: two separate branches, one in the first quadrant and one in the third, with the x-axis and the y-axis as asymptotes.","code":"7.01c","open":false},{"front":"The form y = mx + c","back":"The equation of a straight line: m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept, the y-value where the line crosses the y-axis at (0, c).","code":"7.02a","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of the line through two points","back":"m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁): rise over run, with both points taken in the same order top and bottom.","code":"7.02a","open":false},{"front":"Condition for two lines to be parallel","back":"Their gradients are equal. Written as y = mx + c they have the same m and a different c, so they keep the same distance apart and never meet.","code":"7.02b","open":false},{"front":"Testing a graph for direct proportion","back":"The graph is a straight line through the origin, (0, 0); its gradient is the constant rate connecting the two quantities.","code":"7.04a","open":false},{"front":"The fixed value and the rate on a real-life straight-line graph","back":"The y-intercept is the starting or fixed value, when the other quantity is zero; the gradient is the rate of change, with its unit.","code":"7.04a","open":false},{"front":"The gradient of a distance-time graph","back":"The speed. A steeper section means a faster speed and a horizontal section means the object is stationary.","code":"7.04b","open":false},{"front":"Average speed for a whole journey","back":"Total distance ÷ total time for the journey, not the average of the separate stage speeds.","code":"7.04b","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Gradient of a straight line: m = (change in y) ÷ (change in x) = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁). Not part of 6.02d's list of formulae learners are required to know, and not printed on the OCR Foundation tier formulae sheet (J560/01–03): it is not given on the day, so recall it.","Equation of a straight line: y = mx + c, with m the gradient and c the y-intercept. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Foundation formulae sheet: recall it.","Parallel lines: equal gradients, m₁ = m₂. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Foundation formulae sheet: recall it.","Roots of a quadratic graph: the solutions of y = 0, found by factorising. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Foundation formulae sheet: recall it.","Symmetry of a parabola: the turning point sits at x = (one root + the other root) ÷ 2. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Foundation formulae sheet: recall it.","Speed from a distance-time graph: speed = distance ÷ time, and average speed = total distance ÷ total time. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Foundation formulae sheet: recall it.","The quadratic formula, x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac)) ÷ 2a, is a Higher-tier-only requirement in 6.02d and appears only on the Higher formulae sheet (J560/04–06); it is not needed in this Foundation room, where roots are found by factorising.","Under 2b the assessment 'will not include a formula sheet' beyond 6.02d, but since 2022 Ofqual has required one and OCR issues one per tier each series. The Foundation tier sheet (read 19 September 2026) prints the trapezium, the prism, circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, compound interest and the addition rule for probability — none of the six graph results above is on it, so this room's working assumes those six are written from memory. The sheet is reissued each series, so check the one at the front of the paper on the day."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:48:29Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark tally and worked-example step re-solved in node and correct, the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md exactly (none of the six graph results is on the 2026 Foundation sheet) and nothing Higher-only has crept in — but the facts omit OCR's paper numbers and the exit ticket is a numbers-swapped copy of the check, so fix those before publishing."}}},"graphs-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"graphs-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room carries the ten Higher-tier-only statements of OCR 7 Graphs of equations and functions: perpendicular lines and the tangent to a circle, turning points found by completing the square, the shapes of exponential and trigonometric graphs, the equation of a circle centred at the origin, shading the solution set of a linear inequality, translating and reflecting a given graph, and reading gradients and areas from a graph as rates of change and totals. It builds directly on the Foundation graphs room, where you met coordinates, tables of values, y = mx + c, parabolas and distance-time graphs — everything here reuses that ground. These ideas appear on all three Higher papers, including Paper 5 without a calculator, where an exact fraction is the required form, and they carry a large share of the AO2 reasoning and AO3 problem-solving marks the Higher tier weights above the Foundation tier.","idea":[{"h":"Plotting an exponential graph from a table of values","t":"An exponential equation such as y = 3 × 1.1ˣ is plotted the same way any other graph is: substitute each given x-value in turn to build a table of y-values, then plot the points and join them with a smooth curve. Because the base is greater than 1, each y-value is 1.1 times the one before it, so the values grow slowly at first and then increasingly quickly — the table itself should look like it is accelerating. Work systematically left to right through the table, keeping a consistent number of decimal places, and check that the shape you plot rises without ever crossing the x-axis, since a positive base raised to any power stays positive.","code":"7.01b"},{"h":"Turning points of a quadratic by completing the square","t":"Writing a quadratic as y = (x + p)² + q puts its turning point on display. A squared bracket can never be negative, so its smallest possible value is 0, reached when x + p = 0, that is x = −p; there y = q. The turning point is therefore (−p, q), and by the curve's symmetry the line of symmetry is x = −p. To reach that form, halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, then compare the expanded bracket's constant with the one in the original expression and add or subtract the difference. The same form also settles whether the equation y = 0 has real solutions: if q is positive, y is never 0 and the curve never meets the x-axis.","code":"7.01c"},{"h":"The graph of y = kˣ for positive k","t":"y = kˣ, with k a positive number, is an exponential curve. It always passes through (0, 1), because any positive number raised to the power 0 is 1, and it never touches the x-axis, which is an asymptote it approaches but never reaches. For k greater than 1 the curve climbs, slowly at first and then ever more steeply, as x increases; for k between 0 and 1 the curve instead falls away to the right, getting closer and closer to zero without ever reaching it. The larger k is, the more sharply the curve bends upward once x is positive, and every such graph lies entirely above the x-axis.","code":"7.01d"},{"h":"The graphs of y = sin x, y = cos x and y = tan x","t":"These graphs use angles in degrees and repeat for values of any size, positive or negative. y = sin x passes through the origin, rises to a maximum of 1 at 90°, returns to 0 at 180°, falls to a minimum of −1 at 270° and is back to 0 at 360°; its period is 360°. y = cos x is the same wave shifted so that it starts at its maximum: it passes through (0, 1), is 0 at 90° and 270°, reaches −1 at 180° and returns to 1 at 360°, also with period 360°. y = tan x passes through the origin, has period 180°, and is broken by vertical asymptotes at 90°, 270° and every 180° beyond, where the curve is undefined. Exams ask for a sketch showing these key features, and for solutions read off the curve, such as the values of x where sin x = −1.","code":"7.01e"},{"h":"The equation of a circle centred at the origin","t":"A circle centred at the origin with radius r has equation x² + y² = r². This comes straight from Pythagoras' theorem applied to the right-angled triangle formed by the origin, the point (x, y) and the two axes, so the number on the right is the radius squared, not the radius itself. x² + y² = 81 is therefore the circle of radius 9, since 9² = 81; to find the radius, take the square root of the number on the right. A point lies on the circle exactly when substituting its coordinates makes the equation true, which lets you check a given point without drawing anything.","code":"7.01f"},{"h":"Linear inequalities in two variables on a graph","t":"An inequality such as y < 2x + 1 or y ≥ x − 3 describes a whole region of the coordinate plane, not a single line. Start by treating the inequality as an equation to draw its boundary line, then use the inequality sign to decide which side to shade: pick a test point not on the line, such as (0, 0), substitute its coordinates, and shade the side where the inequality holds. The boundary line itself is drawn dashed for a strict inequality (< or >), because points exactly on the line are not included, and solid for ≤ or ≥, because they are. Where a region must satisfy several inequalities at once, its solution set is the overlap of all the individual shaded regions.","code":"7.02a"},{"h":"Perpendicular lines, and the tangent to a circle","t":"Two lines are perpendicular exactly when the product of their gradients is −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other: turn the fraction upside down and change its sign. A gradient of 3/5 gives −5/3; a gradient of −2 gives 1/2. Given a line and a point, find the equation of the perpendicular through that point by taking the negative reciprocal of the given gradient, then substituting the point into y = mx + c to solve for c. This is exactly how a tangent to a circle is found: a tangent meets the circle at one point and is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, so work out the radius' gradient from the origin and the point of contact, take its negative reciprocal for the tangent's gradient, then substitute the point into y = mx + c.","code":"7.02b"},{"h":"Translations and reflections of a graph","t":"A change made outside the function moves the graph vertically: y = f(x) + a translates it a units up (a units down if a is negative). A change made inside the brackets moves the graph horizontally, in the opposite direction to the sign: y = f(x + a) translates it a units to the left, so y = f(x − 3) moves it 3 units to the right. Reflections follow the same inside/outside rule: y = −f(x) reverses every y-value, a reflection in the x-axis, turning a maximum into a minimum; y = f(−x) reverses every x-value, a reflection in the y-axis. Given one key point on y = f(x) — a maximum, minimum or intercept — track that single point through the rule to find its image, and the rest of the sketch follows.","code":"7.03a"},{"h":"Gradients of graphs: average and instantaneous rate of change","t":"The gradient of a straight section of a graph is a rate of change: on a distance-time graph it is speed, on a velocity-time graph it is acceleration, on a graph of an investment's value it is pounds per year. On a curve the gradient changes from point to point, so the instantaneous rate of change at one point is found by drawing a tangent that just touches the curve there and finding that tangent's gradient from two points on it, far enough apart to read accurately. Where a tangent cannot be drawn accurately, a chord joining two nearby points on the curve gives a good estimate of the average rate of change over that short interval instead, and the calculation is the same one used for the gradient between two points.","code":"7.04b"},{"h":"Areas under graphs by estimation","t":"The area between a graph and the horizontal axis also has a meaning: the area under a velocity-time graph is the distance travelled, and the area under a graph of rate against time in general is the total amount accumulated. When the graph is made of straight sections, split the region into triangles, rectangles and trapezia and add their areas exactly. When the graph is a curve, split it into strips of equal width and estimate each strip's area using the area of a trapezium, ½(a + b)h, which the Higher formulae sheet prints, with a and b the two parallel readings and h the strip's width, or by counting squares. Because chords across a curve that bends upward lie above it, trapezia in that case overestimate the true area, so always state whether the estimate is too large or too small, with a unit and a sentence in context.","code":"7.04c"}],"examples":[{"title":"The equation of a perpendicular line through a given point","setup":"The line L has equation y = 3x − 2. Find the equation of the line perpendicular to L that passes through the point (9, 4). Give your answer in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["L has gradient 3, read directly from y = 3x − 2.","The perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal of 3, which is −1/3.","Substitute the point (9, 4) into y = −1/3 x + c: 4 = −1/3 × 9 + c, so 4 = −3 + c.","c = 7, so the perpendicular line is y = −1/3 x + 7."],"cues":["Which number in y = 3x − 2 is the gradient of L?","What must be done to a gradient to get the gradient of a perpendicular line?","Which pair of coordinates goes into y = mx + c once the new gradient is known?","What is left to state once the value of c is found?"]},{"title":"Completing the square to find a turning point","setup":"A curve has equation y = x² + 18x + 92. Write it in the form (x + p)² + q, and use that form to write down the coordinates of the turning point.","steps":["Halve the coefficient of x: 18 ÷ 2 = 9, so the bracket must be (x + 9)².","Expanding gives (x + 9)² = x² + 18x + 81, which is 11 less than x² + 18x + 92, so y = (x + 9)² + 11.","A squared bracket is smallest, and equal to 0, when x + 9 = 0, that is x = −9.","At that value of x, y = 0 + 11 = 11, so the turning point is (−9, 11)."],"cues":["Which term of the quadratic decides the number placed inside the bracket?","How does the constant in the expanded bracket compare with the constant needed, and what must be done about the gap?","What is the smallest value a squared bracket can take, and when does it happen?","With the bracket at its smallest, what is left of the right-hand side?"]},{"title":"Tracking one point through four transformations","setup":"The graph of y = f(x) has a single maximum point at (5, 7). Write down the coordinates of the image of that maximum point on each of the graphs y = f(x) + 3, y = f(x − 4), y = −f(x) and y = f(−x).","steps":["Adding 3 outside the function adds 3 to every y-value, moving the maximum to (5, 10).","Replacing x by x − 4 slides the curve 4 units to the right, moving the maximum to (9, 7).","The minus sign outside reverses the sign of every y-value, a reflection in the x-axis, so the point maps to (5, −7) and it is now a minimum.","The minus sign inside reverses the sign of every x-value, a reflection in the y-axis, so the point maps to (−5, 7)."],"cues":["Is a number added outside the function a vertical or a horizontal change?","A change inside the brackets moves the curve sideways — which way does a minus sign send it?","Which coordinate changes sign when the whole function is negated, and what does that do to a maximum?","Which coordinate changes sign when the input is negated instead?"]},{"title":"The tangent to a circle at a given point","setup":"A circle has equation x² + y² = 50. The point T(5, 5) lies on the circle. Find the equation of the tangent to the circle at T, giving your answer in the form y = mx + c.","steps":["The centre is the origin O(0, 0), so the radius OT has gradient 5 ÷ 5 = 1.","A tangent meets its radius at a right angle, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of 1, which is −1.","Substitute T(5, 5) and that gradient into y = mx + c: 5 = −1 × 5 + c, so 5 = −5 + c.","c = 10, so the tangent is y = −x + 10."],"cues":["Where is the centre of a circle whose equation has no x or y term outside the squares?","What is the angle between a tangent and the radius at the point of contact?","Which point must lie on the tangent, and what do you do with its coordinates?","What is left to state once the intercept has been found?"]},{"title":"Acceleration and distance from a velocity-time graph","setup":"A motorbike's velocity-time graph is made of two straight sections. It rises in a straight line from (0 s, 0 m/s) to (6 s, 18 m/s), then stays constant at 18 m/s from (6 s, 18 m/s) to (14 s, 18 m/s). Work out the acceleration during the first 6 seconds, and the total distance travelled in the 14 seconds.","steps":["Gradient of the rising section = change in velocity ÷ change in time = (18 − 0) ÷ (6 − 0) = 3 m/s², which is the acceleration.","The region under the rising section is a triangle: area = ½ × 6 × 18 = 54 m.","The constant section stands on a base of 14 − 6 = 8 s and a height of 18 m/s, so it is a rectangle: area = 8 × 18 = 144 m.","Total distance = 54 + 144 = 198 m."],"cues":["Which feature of a velocity-time graph gives the acceleration?","What shape is the region under the rising section, and what is its area formula?","What shape is the region under a horizontal section, and how long does it last?","What must be done with the two areas to answer the second part?"]},{"title":"A table of values for an exponential graph","setup":"Complete a table of values for y = 3 × 1.1ˣ, for x = 0, x = 5 and x = 10, giving each y-value to 1 decimal place, then describe what happens to y as x increases without limit.","steps":["Substitute x = 0: y = 3 × 1.1⁰ = 3 × 1 = 3.0.","Substitute x = 5: y = 3 × 1.1⁵ = 3 × 1.61051 = 4.8 to 1 d.p.","Substitute x = 10: y = 3 × 1.1¹⁰ = 3 × 2.59374… = 7.8 to 1 d.p.","As x increases without limit, y keeps increasing too, more and more steeply, since the base 1.1 is greater than 1."],"cues":["What is any positive number raised to the power zero?","Which calculator key deals with the power in this expression?","Does a larger power of a number greater than 1 give a bigger or a smaller result?","Is the rate at which y grows staying the same, slowing down, or speeding up?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Two lines are perpendicular and one of them has gradient 2. What is the gradient of the other?","a":"−1/2"},{"q":"What are the coordinates of the turning point of y = (x + 4)² − 3, and what is its line of symmetry?","a":"Turning point (−4, −3); line of symmetry x = −4"},{"q":"Does the point (5, 12) lie on the circle x² + y² = 169?","a":"Yes: 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169"},{"q":"Should the boundary line of the region y ≥ 3x − 2 be drawn solid or dashed, and why?","a":"Solid, because points exactly on the line satisfy y ≥ 3x − 2 and so are included"}]},"room":{"facts":["Perpendicular lines: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other.","Completed square form: y = (x + p)² + q has turning point (−p, q) and line of symmetry x = −p.","A circle centred on the origin with radius r has equation x² + y² = r²; a tangent at a point on the circle is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point.","An inequality's boundary is drawn dashed for < or > (points on the line excluded) and solid for ≤ or ≥ (points on the line included).","y = kˣ (k positive) passes through (0, 1) with the x-axis as an asymptote; y = sin x and y = cos x have period 360° and run between −1 and 1; y = tan x has period 180° with asymptotes at 90°, 270° and every 180° beyond.","y = f(x) + a moves a curve a up; y = f(x + a) moves it a to the left; y = −f(x) reflects it in the x-axis; y = f(−x) reflects it in the y-axis.","The gradient of a tangent to a curve is the instantaneous rate of change at that point; a chord between two nearby points estimates the average rate of change.","The area under a velocity-time graph is the distance travelled; estimate a curved region with the area of a trapezium, ½(a + b)h, which the Higher formulae sheet prints, for each strip.","OCR Mathematics has three papers per tier: Higher tier is assessed by J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 5 (J560/05) is non-calculator, and Higher tier reports grades 9 to 4."],"questions":[{"q":"A line has gradient 3/5. Write down the gradient of any line perpendicular to it. (non-calculator)","a":"−5/3","sol":"Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1, so the gradient wanted is the negative reciprocal of 3/5: turn the fraction upside down and change its sign, giving −5/3. B1 for −5/3.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Perpendicular lines do something very particular to a gradient, and it is more than a change of sign.","The two gradients have a fixed product, so find the value that multiplies with 3/5 to give it.","Turn the fraction 3/5 upside down, then change the sign of the result."],"codes":["7.02b"]},{"q":"Write down the radius of the circle with equation x² + y² = 64. (non-calculator)","a":"8","sol":"A circle centred on the origin has equation x² + y² = r², so here r² = 64 and r = √64 = 8. B1 for 8.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the equation with the standard form of a circle centred on the origin.","The number on the right-hand side is not the radius itself; it is the radius squared.","Set r² equal to 64 and then undo the squaring."],"codes":["7.01f"]},{"q":"Write down the coordinates of the point where the graph of y = 7ˣ crosses the y-axis. (non-calculator)","a":"(0, 1)","sol":"Every point on the y-axis has x = 0, and 7⁰ = 1, so the graph crosses the y-axis at (0, 1). B1 for the ordered pair.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Every graph crosses the y-axis where the x-coordinate takes one particular value.","Put that value of x into y = 7ˣ and use the index law for a power of zero.","Write the point as (x, y) with the x-coordinate already settled by the axis."],"codes":["7.01d"]},{"q":"State whether the boundary line of the region y < 4x + 1 should be drawn solid or dashed, and give a reason. (non-calculator)","a":"Dashed, because points exactly on the line y = 4x + 1 do not satisfy the strict inequality","sol":"The inequality is strict (< rather than ≤), so points lying exactly on the boundary line are not part of the solution set. B1 for dashed. B1 for a reason referring to the strict inequality excluding the line itself.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Whether a boundary is drawn solid or dashed depends on the type of inequality sign used.","A strict inequality treats the boundary differently from an inequality that also allows equality.","Decide whether points exactly on the line y = 4x + 1 make the statement y < 4x + 1 true."],"codes":["7.02a"]},{"q":"Line A has equation y = 4x + 3 and line B has equation 4y + x = 8. Show that line A and line B are perpendicular. (non-calculator)","a":"Gradient of A is 4; gradient of B is −1/4; 4 × (−1/4) = −1, so A and B are perpendicular","sol":"Line A is already in the form y = mx + c, so its gradient is 4. M1. Rearrange line B: 4y + x = 8 gives 4y = 8 − x, so y = 2 − 1/4 x, and its gradient is −1/4. M1. The product of the gradients is 4 × (−1/4) = −1, so line A and line B are perpendicular. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A line's gradient can be read directly once its equation is written in the form y = mx + c.","Rearrange the second equation into y = mx + c form before comparing gradients.","Perpendicular lines have gradients whose product is a fixed value — work out that product for these two gradients."],"codes":["7.02b"]},{"q":"A curve has equation y = x² − 8x + c, where c is a constant. Given that the turning point of the curve is at (4, 5), find the value of c. (non-calculator)","a":"c = 21","sol":"Halve the coefficient of x: −8 ÷ 2 = −4, so the bracket is (x − 4)². M1. Expanding, (x − 4)² = x² − 8x + 16, which is 16 less than x² − 8x + c, so y = (x − 4)² + (c − 16); the turning point is therefore (4, c − 16). M1. Since the turning point is (4, 5), c − 16 = 5, so c = 21. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Completing the square puts the turning point's coordinates on display in terms of c.","Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, then compare the expanded bracket with x² − 8x + c.","Set the y-coordinate of the turning point you get in terms of c equal to 5, then solve for c."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"Using the graph of y = cos x, write down all the solutions of cos x = 0 for 0° ≤ x ≤ 360°. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 90° and x = 270°","sol":"The cosine curve starts at its maximum and crosses zero twice in one full period. In the first quarter-turn it reaches 0 at x = 90°. B1. By the symmetry of the wave it is also 0 at x = 270°, before returning to its maximum at 360°. B1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Picture one full cosine wave and ask where it crosses the x-axis.","A wave starting at its maximum crosses zero on the way down and again on the way back up.","Find the two angles between 0° and 360° where the graph has height 0."],"codes":["7.01e"]},{"q":"The graph of y = f(x) has a minimum point at (3, −2). Write down the coordinates of the minimum point on the graph of y = f(x) − 6, and on the graph of y = f(x + 5). (non-calculator)","a":"(3, −8) and (−2, −2)","sol":"Subtracting 6 outside the function subtracts 6 from every y-value, so y = f(x) − 6 has its minimum at (3, −8). B1. Replacing x by x + 5 translates the curve 5 units to the left, so y = f(x + 5) has its minimum at (3 − 5, −2) = (−2, −2). B1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Decide for each rule whether the change acts on the input or the output of the function.","A number added or subtracted outside the function moves the curve vertically; one added inside moves it horizontally, and not in the direction the sign first suggests.","Change only the y-coordinate for the first graph, and only the x-coordinate for the second."],"codes":["7.03a"]},{"q":"A car's value in pounds is modelled by V = 18000 × 0.85ᵗ, where t is its age in years. By finding the value at t = 2 and at t = 4, estimate the gradient of the chord joining these two points, to the nearest £1, and state what it represents. A calculator may be used.","a":"About −£1804 per year: the car's value is falling by roughly £1804 a year around this time","sol":"At t = 2, V = 18000 × 0.85² = £13005. M1. At t = 4, V = 18000 × 0.85⁴ = £9396.11 to the nearest penny. M1. Gradient of the chord = (9396.11 − 13005) ÷ (4 − 2) = −3608.89 ÷ 2 = −1804.44. M1. To the nearest pound the gradient is −£1804, so the car's value is decreasing by about £1804 per year around this time. A1.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The gradient of this graph is a rate of change, measured in pounds per year.","A chord joining two points either side of the time you want gives a good estimate of the gradient there.","Work out the value of the car at t = 2 and at t = 4 before doing anything with a gradient."],"codes":["7.04b"]},{"q":"A tap fills a bath at a rate, in litres per minute, that rises in a straight line from 0 L/min at t = 0 min to 6 L/min at t = 3 min, then falls in a straight line back to 0 L/min at t = 9 min. Find the total volume of water added to the bath during the 9 minutes. A calculator may be used.","a":"27 litres","sol":"The volume added is the area under the rate-time graph. The first part is a triangle of base 3 min and height 6 L/min: area = ½ × 3 × 6 = 9 litres. M1. The second part is a triangle of base 9 − 3 = 6 min and height 6 L/min: area = ½ × 6 × 6 = 18 litres. M1. Total volume = 9 + 18 = 27 litres. A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The volume added is not read off the vertical axis; it is hidden in the region under the graph.","Split the region into two triangles and find the area of each.","The first triangle has base 3 min and height 6 L/min, so deal with its area first."],"codes":["7.04c"]},{"q":"The point A(4, 3) lies on the circle x² + y² = 25. The tangent to the circle at A crosses the y-axis at the point Q. Find the coordinates of Q. (non-calculator)","a":"(0, 25/3)","sol":"The centre is the origin, so the radius OA has gradient 3 ÷ 4 = 3/4. M1. The tangent is perpendicular to OA, so its gradient is −4/3. M1. Substitute A(4, 3) into y = −4/3 x + c: 3 = −16/3 + c. M1. So c = 3 + 16/3 = 25/3, and the tangent is y = −4/3 x + 25/3. A1. Q is where the tangent crosses the y-axis, at x = 0, which is the point (0, 25/3). B1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The tangent is perpendicular to the radius drawn from the centre to A.","Find the equation of the tangent first, then recall what x equals on the y-axis.","The radius runs from the origin to A, so work out its gradient from those two points before touching y = mx + c."],"codes":["7.01f","7.02b"]},{"q":"By completing the square, find the minimum value of y = x² − 10x + 30 and the value of x at which it occurs. Hence explain why the equation x² − 10x + 30 = 0 has no real solutions. (non-calculator)","a":"Minimum y = 5 at x = 5; since (x − 5)² ≥ 0, y ≥ 5 for every x, so y is never 0","sol":"Half of −10 is −5, so the bracket is (x − 5)². M1. Expanding, (x − 5)² = x² − 10x + 25, which is 5 less than x² − 10x + 30, so y = (x − 5)² + 5. A1. A square is never negative, so the least value of the bracket is 0, reached when x = 5, giving a minimum value of y = 5. A1. Since y ≥ 5 for every value of x, y can never equal 0, so x² − 10x + 30 = 0 has no real solutions. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A squared bracket can never be negative, and that fact is the whole of the explanation needed.","Rewrite the quadratic as (x + p)² + q, then say what the smallest possible value of the bracket is.","Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, then correct the constant term."],"codes":["7.01c"]},{"q":"A colony of bacteria is modelled by N = 200 × 1.15ᵗ, where t is the time in hours. A graph of N against t is drawn for 0 ≤ t ≤ 10 by plotting (0, 200), (2, 264.5), (4, 349.8), (6, 462.6), (8, 611.8) and (10, 809.1) and joining them with a smooth curve. Use the graph to find an approximate solution to 200 × 1.15ᵗ = 600, and say what it means, giving t to the nearest hour. A calculator may be used.","a":"t ≈ 7.8, so to the nearest hour the colony reaches 600 bacteria after about 8 hours","sol":"A solution of 200 × 1.15ᵗ = 600 is a value of t where the curve reaches N = 600, so draw the line N = 600 across to the curve. M1. The plotted values put the curve at 462.6 when t = 6 and at 611.8 when t = 8, so N = 600 is reached between these two points; read down to the t-axis. M1. The reading is t ≈ 7.8. A1 (accept 7.6 to 8.1). Since 7.8 is well clear of the half-hour boundary 7.5, to the nearest hour the answer is 8: the colony reaches 600 bacteria after about 8 hours. B1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A solution read from this graph is a value of t, not a value of N.","Find the place on the curve where N equals the number on the right of the equation, then read down to the t-axis.","Draw the horizontal line at N = 600 across to the curve before reading anything off the t-axis."],"codes":["7.01b"]},{"q":"A region R satisfies all three inequalities y ≤ 2x + 3, y > −1 and x < 4. Determine, with reasons, whether each of the points P(3, 4) and Q(4, 0) lies inside R. A calculator may be used.","a":"P(3, 4) lies inside R; Q(4, 0) does not, because it fails x < 4","sol":"Test P(3, 4): 4 ≤ 2 × 3 + 3 = 9 is true; 4 > −1 is true; 3 < 4 is true. All three hold, so P lies inside R. M1 A1. Test Q(4, 0): 0 ≤ 2 × 4 + 3 = 11 is true; 0 > −1 is true; but 4 < 4 is false, since 4 is not strictly less than 4. Because Q lies exactly on the dashed boundary x = 4, it is not included in R. M1 A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A point lies in the region only when its coordinates satisfy every one of the given inequalities.","Substitute each point's coordinates into all three inequalities in turn and check whether each one is true.","For Q, look carefully at the inequality that uses a strict sign and compare it with Q's own x-coordinate."],"codes":["7.02a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Two lines have gradients 6 and −1/6. Are the lines perpendicular? Give a reason.","a":"Yes, because 6 × (−1/6) = −1"},{"q":"Write y = x² + 8x + 9 in the form y = (x + p)² + q.","a":"y = (x + 4)² − 7"},{"q":"Write down the radius of the circle x² + y² = 121.","a":"11"},{"q":"Does the point (3, 2) satisfy the inequality y ≤ x + 1?","a":"Yes, because 2 ≤ 3 + 1 = 4"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Perpendicular gradients","back":"If two lines are perpendicular their gradients multiply to −1: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so each gradient is the negative reciprocal of the other.","code":"7.02b","open":false},{"front":"Tangent to a circle at a point","back":"It touches the circle there and is perpendicular to the radius drawn to that point, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of the radius' gradient.","code":"7.02b","open":false},{"front":"Completed square form of a quadratic","back":"y = (x + p)² + q. The turning point is at (−p, q) and the line of symmetry is x = −p.","code":"7.01c","open":false},{"front":"Completing the square: the method","back":"Halve the coefficient of x to fill the bracket, expand the bracket, then add or subtract a constant so the expression matches the original.","code":"7.01c","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = kˣ for positive k","back":"An exponential curve through (0, 1), with the x-axis as an asymptote it never reaches. It rises ever more steeply for k greater than 1, and falls towards zero for k between 0 and 1.","code":"7.01d","open":false},{"front":"Table of values for an exponential graph","back":"Substitute each given x into the equation to find the matching y, then plot and join the points; each y-value is the previous one multiplied by the base.","code":"7.01b","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = sin x","back":"A wave of period 360° through (0, 0), rising to 1 at 90°, back to 0 at 180°, down to −1 at 270° and 0 at 360°. It repeats for angles of any size.","code":"7.01e","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = cos x","back":"The sine wave shifted so it starts at its maximum: (0, 1), 0 at 90° and 270°, −1 at 180°, back to 1 at 360°. Period 360°.","code":"7.01e","open":false},{"front":"Graph of y = tan x","back":"Period 180°, passing through (0, 0), and broken by vertical asymptotes at 90°, 270° and every 180° from there, where the graph is undefined.","code":"7.01e","open":false},{"front":"Equation of a circle centred at the origin","back":"x² + y² = r², where r is the radius. A point lies on the circle exactly when its coordinates satisfy the equation.","code":"7.01f","open":false},{"front":"Solid or dashed boundary line","back":"Dashed for a strict inequality (< or >), since points on the line are excluded; solid for ≤ or ≥, since points on the line are included.","code":"7.02a","open":false},{"front":"y = f(x) + a and y = f(x + a)","back":"f(x) + a translates the curve a units up (down if a is negative). f(x + a) translates it a units to the left (right if a is negative).","code":"7.03a","open":false},{"front":"y = −f(x) and y = f(−x)","back":"−f(x) reverses the sign of every y-value, a reflection in the x-axis. f(−x) reverses the sign of every x-value, a reflection in the y-axis.","code":"7.03a","open":false},{"front":"Gradient of a curve at a point","back":"Draw a tangent touching the curve there and find its gradient from two points on it. It gives the instantaneous rate of change at that point.","code":"7.04b","open":false},{"front":"Area under a velocity-time graph","back":"The distance travelled. For straight sections, split into triangles, rectangles and trapezia; for a curve, use the area of a trapezium, ½(a + b)h (on the Higher sheet), for each strip, or count squares.","code":"7.04c","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Perpendicular lines: m₁ × m₂ = −1, so the perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal. Not among the results in 6.02d, and not printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet: recall it.","Equation of a straight line: y = mx + c, with m the gradient and c the y-intercept. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Gradient between two points: m = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁). Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Completed square form: y = (x + p)² + q, with turning point (−p, q) and line of symmetry x = −p. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Circle centred at the origin: x² + y² = r², with r the radius. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Tangent to a circle: perpendicular to the radius drawn to the point of contact, so its gradient is the negative reciprocal of the radius' gradient. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Boundary convention for a linear inequality: dashed for < or >, solid for ≤ or ≥. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Vertical translation of a graph: y = f(x) + a is y = f(x) translated by the vector (0, a). Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Horizontal translation of a graph: y = f(x + a) is y = f(x) translated by the vector (−a, 0). Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Reflection in the x-axis: y = −f(x) reflects y = f(x) in the x-axis, so every y-coordinate changes sign. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Reflection in the y-axis: y = f(−x) reflects y = f(x) in the y-axis, so every x-coordinate changes sign. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Exponential growth and decay: y = abˣ, with a the value at x = 0 and b the multiplier per unit of x. Not in 6.02d in this general form, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Speed from a distance-time graph, and acceleration from a velocity-time graph: each is the gradient of the graph, so speed = distance ÷ time and acceleration = change in velocity ÷ time taken. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Rate of change on a curve: the gradient of the tangent drawn at a point is the rate of change at that point. Not in 6.02d, and not on the Higher tier sheet: recall it.","Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, used here for each strip when estimating the area under a curve. Not in 6.02d, but section 3 of appendix.md records it as printed on the OCR Higher tier formulae sheet (J560/04–06): on the day the sheet governs and it IS given — recall or derive it anyway, since the sheet is reissued each series.","Since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper, and OCR issues one for each tier for every series. This is a Higher tier room, so the Higher insert (J560/04–06) is the one that matters; per appendix.md it prints the trapezium, the prism, the circumference and area of a circle, the quadratic formula, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, compound interest, and the addition and conditional multiplication rules for probability. Nothing in items 1 to 14 above is on it, so those results are written from memory; the quadratic formula and compound interest are also on the sheet but are not needed in this room, where turning points come from completing the square. The sheet is reissued each series, so check the one at the front of the paper before relying on recall."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:02:06Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit: every one of the 14 questions and 6 worked examples re-solved clean with node, every sol tally adds to its m, and question 12's rewritten target (200 × 1.15ᵗ = 600, exact t = 7.861, chord reading 7.842) now rounds to 8 hours across the whole accepted 7.6-8.1 band, so the earlier ambiguity is gone; the trapezium naming, the missing OCR paper numbers and the example-reuse pairs are all fixed, and the Formulae sheet extra now matches appendix.md exactly - trapezium area, quadratic formula and compound interest all correctly stated as printed on the Higher insert. Publishable."}}},"basic-geometry":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"basic-geometry","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Basic geometry is the language every other topic in the specification is written in: a mensuration or trigonometry question cannot be started until the angles and shape properties in its figure are known, and OCR awards marks for naming the property behind each step, not only for the number reached at the end. Much of this room is tested without a calculator, so the constructions, the angle facts and the vocabulary of shapes, circles and solids must be secure by hand and stated in the board's own words.","idea":[{"h":"Points, lines, vertices, edges, planes and the parallel/perpendicular marks","code":"8.01a","t":"Points are fixed positions, named with a capital letter, and a line segment is named by its two end points, for example the segment AB. A vertex is a corner and an edge is a straight boundary where two flat parts of a figure meet; a plane is a flat surface. Two lines that never meet, however far extended, are parallel, shown on a diagram by matching arrows; two lines that cross at a right angle are perpendicular, shown by a small square at the crossing. These conventions are used to read every diagram in the exam, so questions expect them to be read correctly rather than re-taught each time."},{"h":"Acute, obtuse, right and reflex angles, and labelling a triangle","code":"8.01b","t":"An angle is acute if it is less than 90°, right if it is exactly 90°, obtuse if it is between 90° and 180°, and reflex if it is more than 180°. Triangles are labelled with capital letters at the vertices, and the side opposite each vertex takes the matching small letter: in triangle ABC the side opposite vertex A is called a. The angle at a vertex is written with the vertex as the middle letter, so angle ABC (also written ∠ABC) means the angle at B, between the lines BA and BC. Getting this order right matters, because a mark scheme reads the letters in exactly this way."},{"h":"Naming triangles, quadrilaterals and polygons","code":"8.01c","t":"A regular polygon has every side the same length and every angle the same size. Triangles are named by their sides: scalene (no sides equal), isosceles (two sides equal) and equilateral (three sides equal). The named quadrilaterals are the square, rectangle, kite, rhombus, parallelogram and trapezium, each told apart by its own sides, angles and diagonals. Polygons with more sides also have names to know: pentagon (5 sides), hexagon (6 sides) and octagon (8 sides). A question may simply ask you to name a shape from a diagram or a description, so these names must be given exactly, not described."},{"h":"Faces, surfaces, edges and vertices, and the names of solids","code":"8.01d","t":"A face is a flat surface of a solid, a surface can also be curved (as on a cylinder or a sphere), an edge is a line where two faces meet, and a vertex is a corner where edges meet. The named solids are the cube, cuboid, prism, cylinder, pyramid, cone and sphere, and each must be recognised from a picture, a description or a set of views. Curved surfaces are not counted as faces, which is the single most common source of a wrong count, so always separate the flat faces of a solid from any curved surface before counting either of them."},{"h":"Drawing an accurate diagram from a written description","code":"8.01e","t":"Some questions describe a figure in words and ask for an accurate diagram, or give a diagram with facts stated separately and expect them marked on. Every stated fact should appear on the diagram as a mark before any calculation starts: matching dashes for sides stated to be equal, matching arrows for lines stated to be parallel, and a small square for any angle stated to be a right angle. A diagram drawn without these marks can hide the very facts a question relies on, so build the marked diagram first and use it to see which angle fact or property applies."},{"h":"Using a ruler, protractor and compasses","code":"8.01f","t":"A ruler is used to draw and measure straight lines, reading lengths to the nearest millimetre. A protractor is used to draw and measure angles: its centre sits exactly on the vertex of the angle, its base line lies along one arm, and the size is read where the other arm crosses the scale, to the nearest degree. Compasses are opened to a set radius and used to draw circles or arcs of that exact radius, and are also the tool behind every ruler-and-compass construction in this room. Accuracy with all three instruments is assessed directly, not just used to produce other answers."},{"h":"Using x- and y-coordinates in plane geometry","code":"8.01g","t":"Once a shape is placed on a grid, every vertex has an x-coordinate and a y-coordinate, and geometric facts can be read from those numbers: an equal step across and up between two pairs of vertices means the two sides they lie on are equal and parallel, which is exactly the test for shapes such as a parallelogram. Coordinates are also the language used to describe transformations of a shape, such as a reflection or a rotation, but that full technique belongs to the Congruence and similarity room; here the skill is plotting points accurately and using the coordinates themselves to support ordinary plane geometry reasoning."},{"h":"Constructing the perpendicular bisector and midpoint of a line segment","code":"8.02a","t":"The perpendicular bisector of a line segment AB is the straight line that crosses AB at its exact midpoint and at a right angle. To construct it, open the compasses to a radius that is more than half of AB, draw an arc from A and an arc from B without changing the radius, and join the two points where the arcs cross; that line is the perpendicular bisector, and its crossing point with AB is also the midpoint. Leave the arcs on the page: rubbing them out removes the evidence of a correct method even when the final line is accurately drawn."},{"h":"Constructing the bisector of an angle","code":"8.02b","t":"The bisector of an angle is the straight line from its vertex that splits it into two equal halves. To construct it, draw an arc centred on the vertex that cuts both arms of the angle, then draw two more arcs of the same radius, one centred on each of those two crossing points, and join the vertex to where these last two arcs cross. That line is the angle bisector, and, like every other construction in this room, the arcs must be left visible on the diagram for the method to be credited."},{"h":"Constructing a perpendicular to a line, from a point or at a point","code":"8.02c","t":"Two related constructions use a perpendicular: one draws the perpendicular from a point that is not on a line down to that line, and the other draws the perpendicular to a line at a point that is already on it; both use arcs from the given point to find two points on the line equidistant from it, then equal arcs from those two points to fix the perpendicular. Either construction matters because the perpendicular distance from a point to a line is always the shortest distance between them, so any question asking for the shortest route to a line is really asking for this construction."},{"h":"Loci and the word equidistant","code":"8.02d","t":"A locus is the complete set of points that obey a stated rule; equidistant means an equal distance from each of the things named. Three standard loci follow directly from the earlier constructions: the points equidistant from two fixed points form the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them, the points equidistant from two lines that meet form the bisector of the angle between them, and the points a fixed distance from a single point form a circle centred on that point. Real-world problems dress these up as a region a device can reach, a fair boundary between two towns, or a safety zone round a fixed hazard."},{"h":"Angles at a point","code":"8.03a","t":"Angles that meet at a single point, with nothing else there, always add up to 360°, a full turn. This is one of the basic angle facts used, together with the others in this room, to work out an unknown angle in a figure and to justify a stated result rather than simply measure it. Whenever several angles are drawn meeting at one point, check first whether they use up the whole 360° or only part of it, since some questions leave a right angle or a straight line unlabelled that still has to be accounted for."},{"h":"Angles at a point on a straight line","code":"8.03b","t":"Angles that meet at a point lying on a straight line, on one side of it, always add up to 180°. This fact is used constantly alongside the angles-at-a-point fact, and the two are easy to confuse: check whether the angles surround the point completely (360°) or only fill one side of a straight line through it (180°) before choosing which total to subtract from. Combined with the triangle angle sum, this fact is also what lets an exterior angle of a triangle or polygon be found from its interior angle."},{"h":"Vertically opposite, alternate and corresponding angles","code":"8.03c","t":"Where two straight lines cross, the two angles opposite each other are called vertically opposite angles, and they are always equal. Where a straight line crosses a pair of parallel lines, alternate angles — between the parallels, on opposite sides of the crossing line — are equal, and corresponding angles — in matching positions on the same side — are equal. State the property by its full name in your working, since the marks are for the named fact and not only for the number it gives."},{"h":"Interior and exterior angles of a polygon","code":"8.03d","t":"The angle sum of a triangle, 180°, can be derived from the parallel-line facts by drawing a line through one vertex parallel to the opposite side, and every other polygon result follows from it: a polygon with n sides splits into (n − 2) triangles from one vertex, so its interior angles add up to (n − 2) × 180°, and one interior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is that total shared equally, (n − 2) × 180° ÷ n. The exterior angles of any polygon, regular or not, always add up to 360°, and at each vertex the interior and exterior angles add up to 180°."},{"h":"Properties of isosceles, equilateral and right-angled triangles","code":"8.04a","t":"An isosceles triangle has two equal sides, and the two angles opposite those sides — its base angles — are equal because the triangle has a line of symmetry down the middle. An equilateral triangle has three equal sides and, by the same reasoning applied three times over, three equal angles of 60° each. A right-angled triangle has one angle of exactly 90°. These properties let a missing length or angle be found, or a stated result justified, without measuring: state the property being used as part of the working, since the reasoning is what is credited."},{"h":"Properties of the square, rectangle, parallelogram, trapezium, kite and rhombus","code":"8.04b","t":"A parallelogram has both pairs of opposite sides parallel and equal, and both pairs of opposite angles equal. A rhombus is a parallelogram with all four sides equal, whose diagonals cross at right angles. A kite has two pairs of equal adjacent sides and one line of symmetry, with diagonals that cross at right angles. A trapezium has exactly one pair of parallel sides. Each property can be used to find an unknown length or angle in a figure, or to justify why a shape must be one of these types rather than another."},{"h":"Reflection and rotation symmetry of polygons","code":"8.04c","t":"A shape has a line of symmetry if it can be folded along that line so the two halves match exactly, and it has rotational symmetry of order n if turning it through a full circle brings it back to its starting appearance n times, counting the starting position. A regular polygon with n sides always has n lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry of order n. Irregular shapes usually have fewer: an isosceles triangle that is not equilateral has exactly one line of symmetry and no rotational symmetry beyond order 1."},{"h":"The parts of a circle","code":"8.05a","t":"The centre of a circle is its fixed middle point, and the radius is the distance from the centre to the curve. The diameter is a straight line through the centre joining two points on the curve, twice the radius, and the circumference is the curve itself, right the way round. A chord is any straight line joining two points on the circumference, so the diameter is the longest possible chord; a tangent is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point. An arc is part of the circumference, a sector is bounded by two radii and an arc, and a segment is bounded by a chord and an arc."},{"h":"Properties of 3-dimensional solids","code":"8.06a","t":"The cube has 6 equal square faces, 12 edges and 8 vertices, and the cuboid has the same counts with three pairs of equal rectangular faces. A prism has two congruent parallel end faces joined by rectangles running between them, so a triangular prism has 5 faces, 9 edges and 6 vertices, and a pyramid has one base face with triangular faces meeting at a single apex, so a square-based pyramid has 5 faces, 8 edges and 5 vertices. A cylinder has two flat circular faces and one curved surface; a cone has one flat circular face, one curved surface, one vertex and one edge; a sphere is a single curved surface with no edges and no vertices."},{"h":"Plans and elevations","code":"8.06b","t":"A solid can be drawn as three flat views, all to the same scale: the plan is the view looking straight down from above, the front elevation is the view looking horizontally from the front, and the side elevation is the view looking horizontally from one side. Each view shows only outlines and the lines where a surface steps, with no perspective, so a solid built from cubes gives views made of whole squares; questions also work the other way, giving the views and asking for the solid, or for a drawing of it on isometric paper."}],"examples":[{"title":"Angles where a transversal crosses two parallel lines","setup":"The lines JK and LM are parallel, with J and L on the left. A straight line crosses JK at the point P and crosses LM at the point Q, with Q below P and to the right. Angle JPQ = 71°. The angle PQM is marked x and the angle KPQ is marked y. Work out x and y, naming the angle fact used for each.","steps":["Angle JPQ and angle PQM both lie between the two parallel lines, on opposite sides of the crossing line PQ, so they are a pair of alternate angles.","Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal, so x = angle JPQ = 71°.","Angle JPQ and angle KPQ sit side by side at the point P on the straight line JK, so they are angles at a point on a straight line.","y = 180° − 71° = 109°."],"cues":["Which two marked angles lie between the parallels, on opposite sides of PQ?","What does the alternate angles fact say about the sizes of that pair?","Which fact links the two angles that meet at P along JK?","What single subtraction now gives y?"]},{"title":"Two constructions used to place a footpath sign","setup":"A rectangular field ABCD is drawn to a scale of 1 cm to 5 m, with AB = 8 cm and BC = 6 cm on the drawing. A signpost T must stand inside the field so that it is the same distance from the side AB as from the side AD, and also the same distance from the corner A as from the corner B. Describe the two constructions needed to fix the position of T, and work out the real distance of T from the side AB, in metres.","steps":["Same distance from A as from B: draw arcs of the same radius, more than half of AB, centred on A and on B, and join the two crossing points — this is the perpendicular bisector of AB.","That bisector crosses AB at its midpoint, 8 ÷ 2 = 4 cm from A along the drawing.","Same distance from side AB as from side AD: draw an arc centred on A cutting both sides, then equal arcs from those two cuts, and join A to where they cross — this is the bisector of angle DAB.","Angle DAB is a right angle (a rectangle's corner), so its bisector leaves A at 45°, and every point on it is as far from AB as it is along AB; T is therefore 4 cm from AB on the drawing.","The scale is 1 cm to 5 m, so the real distance is 4 × 5 = 20 m."],"cues":["Which locus holds every point equally far from two fixed corners?","Whereabouts on AB does this locus cross it?","Which locus holds every point equally far from the two sides that meet at A?","What size is angle DAB in a rectangle, and what does bisecting it give?","What does one centimetre on this drawing represent?"]},{"title":"Finding the number of sides of a regular polygon from its interior angle","setup":"A regular polygon has each interior angle equal to 135°. Work out the number of sides the polygon has, and give its name.","steps":["Each interior angle and its neighbouring exterior angle lie on a straight line, so they add up to 180°; the exterior angle is 180° − 135° = 45°.","The exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, so the number of sides is 360° ÷ 45°.","360° ÷ 45° = 8, so the polygon has 8 sides.","A polygon with 8 sides is called an octagon."],"cues":["What is true of an interior angle and the exterior angle next to it?","What do all the exterior angles of a polygon add up to, however many sides it has?","What division now gives the number of sides?","What is the name for an 8-sided polygon?"]},{"title":"Using the properties of a rhombus to find missing angles","setup":"WXYZ is a rhombus. Angle WXY = 64°. Work out the size of angle XYZ and the size of angle YZW, naming the properties you use.","steps":["A rhombus is a parallelogram, so opposite angles are equal: angle YZW = angle WXY = 64°.","Adjacent angles in a parallelogram lie between a pair of parallel sides on the same side of the crossing side, so angle WXY and angle XYZ add up to 180°.","angle XYZ = 180° − 64° = 116°.","So angle XYZ = 116° and angle YZW = 64°, the two angles the question asks for."],"cues":["Which pair of angles in a rhombus are always equal to angle WXY?","What is true of two angles that sit next to each other in a parallelogram?","What single subtraction gives angle XYZ?","What are the two angles the question asks for, now that each has been found?"]},{"title":"Naming a solid and counting its faces, edges and vertices from a description","setup":"A solid has one flat face that is a square and four flat triangular faces that all meet at a single point above the square. Name the solid, and write down its numbers of faces, edges and vertices.","steps":["One square face with four triangular faces meeting at a point above it is the description of a pyramid with a square base, so the solid is a square-based pyramid.","Count the faces: the square base plus the four triangular sides gives 1 + 4 = 5 faces.","Count the vertices: the four corners of the square base plus the one point at the top gives 4 + 1 = 5 vertices.","Count the edges: the four sides of the square base plus the four sloping edges up to the top point gives 4 + 4 = 8 edges."],"cues":["Which named solid has one square face and triangular faces meeting at a single top point?","How many faces does the base contribute, and how many do the triangles contribute?","How many corners does the square base have, and how many more does the solid have above it?","How many edges does the square base have, and how many edges run up to the top point?"]},{"title":"Using coordinates to find a missing vertex of a parallelogram","setup":"Three vertices of a parallelogram EFGH, named in order round the shape, are E (1, 1), F (5, 1) and G (7, 4). Plot the points and work out the coordinates of the fourth vertex H.","steps":["In a parallelogram EFGH the side HG is parallel and equal to the side EF, so the step from H to G is the same as the step from E to F.","The step from E to F is 4 across and 0 up (5 − 1 = 4, 1 − 1 = 0).","The same step from H must reach G, so H is 4 across and 0 up less than G: H = (7 − 4, 4 − 0).","H = (3, 4)."],"cues":["Which side of the parallelogram is parallel and equal to EF?","How far across and how far up is it from E to F?","If that step starts at H and ends at G, how do you get back from G to H?","What single pair of coordinates does that subtraction give?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Two angles meet at a point, with nothing else there. One is 250°. What is the other?","a":"110°, because angles at a point add up to 360°."},{"q":"What is the sum of the exterior angles of any polygon, whatever its number of sides?","a":"360°."},{"q":"Which construction gives every point that is the same distance from two fixed points A and B?","a":"The perpendicular bisector of the line segment AB."},{"q":"A shape has exactly one line of symmetry and no rotational symmetry beyond order 1. Name a triangle type that could have exactly this symmetry.","a":"An isosceles triangle that is not equilateral."}]},"room":{"facts":["Angles at a point add up to 360°.","Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°, and vertically opposite angles are equal.","Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal, and corresponding angles on parallel lines are equal — name the property in full in your working.","The angles of a triangle add up to 180°, and the angles of a quadrilateral add up to 360°.","The interior angles of a polygon with n sides add up to (n − 2) × 180°; the exterior angles of any polygon add up to 360°, so one exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon is 360° ÷ n, and interior angle + exterior angle = 180° at each vertex.","The properties of a parallelogram, rhombus, kite and trapezium — their sides, angles, diagonals and symmetry.","The perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance from that point to the line.","The three standard loci: equidistant from two points is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them; equidistant from two lines that meet is the bisector of the angle between them; a fixed distance from a point is a circle centred on that point.","OCR sets three papers per tier: J560/01–03 for Foundation and J560/04–06 for Higher, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 2 and Paper 5 are non-calculator; Foundation is graded 5–1 and Higher is graded 9–4."],"questions":[{"q":"Three angles meet at a point, with nothing else there. Two of them are 95° and 140°. Work out the third angle, and name the angle fact you use. (non-calculator)","a":"125° (angles at a point add up to 360°)","sol":"The three angles fill a complete turn. B1 for the reason: angles at a point add up to 360°, so the third angle = 360° − 95° − 140°. B1 for 125°.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what a complete turn round a single point adds up to.","Two of the three angles are already given, so the third is the whole turn minus both of them.","Write 360° minus the two given angles as one subtraction, and name the fact beside your answer."],"codes":["8.03a","8.01a"]},{"q":"A circle has centre O. Name the region enclosed by two radii and the arc between them, and name the region cut off by a chord and the arc it cuts off. A calculator may be used.","a":"sector; segment","sol":"B1 for sector — the region between two radii and the arc they enclose. B1 for segment — the region between a chord and the arc it cuts off.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both are regions of a circle, but one is bounded by two straight radii and the other by a single straight chord.","Picture a slice of a circular cake for one of them, and the area cut off by a straight cut across the circle for the other.","Give one word for each, taken from the standard names for the parts of a circle."],"codes":["8.05a"]},{"q":"A cuboid has three pairs of congruent rectangular faces. Write down the number of edges and the number of vertices of a cuboid. A calculator may be used.","a":"12 edges and 8 vertices","sol":"B1 for 12 edges — 4 round the top face, 4 round the bottom face and 4 joining the matching corners. B1 for 8 vertices — 4 on the top face and 4 on the bottom face.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["An edge is where two faces meet and a vertex is where edges meet at a corner.","Deal with the top and bottom faces first, then count what joins their corners together.","Count the edges of one rectangular face, double it, then add the joining edges; do the same style of count for the corners."],"codes":["8.06a","8.01d"]},{"q":"Write down the number of lines of symmetry of a regular pentagon and the order of its rotational symmetry. A calculator may be used.","a":"5 lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry of order 5","sol":"A regular pentagon has 5 equal sides and 5 equal angles. B1 for 5 lines of symmetry — each through one vertex and the midpoint of the opposite side. B1 for rotational symmetry of order 5.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A regular polygon has all its sides equal and all its angles equal, and its symmetry follows from that.","Each line of symmetry of a regular pentagon runs from one corner to the midpoint of the side opposite it.","Count the folds of that kind round the whole shape, then turn the pentagon about its centre and count how many positions in one full turn look identical to the start."],"codes":["8.04c","8.01c"]},{"q":"An angle measures 156°. State whether it is acute, obtuse, right or reflex. In triangle DEF, write down the letter that names the side opposite angle D. (non-calculator)","a":"obtuse; side d","sol":"B1 for obtuse — greater than 90° and less than 180°. B1 for side d — the side opposite a vertex takes the matching small letter.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the angle with 90° and with 180° to place it in the right group.","The standard convention gives every side a letter that matches the vertex directly across from it.","State the type of angle first, then the single lower-case letter for the side."],"codes":["8.01b"]},{"q":"The interior angles of a heptagon (a 7-sided polygon) are all shown on a diagram; six of them are each 130° and the seventh is x°. Work out x. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 120","sol":"M1 for the interior angle sum of a 7-sided polygon, (7 − 2) × 180° = 900°. M1 for 900° − (6 × 130°) = 900° − 780° = 120°. A1 for x = 120.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A polygon with 7 sides can be split into triangles from one vertex; use how many triangles that gives.","Find the total of all seven angles, then take away the six you already know.","Write the interior angle sum of a 7-sided polygon on one line, then subtract six lots of 130°."],"codes":["8.03d","8.01c"]},{"q":"In triangle RST, the point U lies on RS and the point V lies on RT, with UV parallel to ST. Angle RUV = 68° and angle RVU = 77°. Work out angle RST, naming the angle fact you use, and hence work out angle SRT. (non-calculator)","a":"angle RST = 68° and angle SRT = 35°","sol":"B1 for angle RST = 68°. B1 for the reason: angle RUV and angle RST are corresponding angles on the parallel lines UV and ST, so they are equal. M1 for 180° − 68° − 77°, using the angle sum of triangle RST with angle RTS = 77° (also corresponding angles). A1 for angle SRT = 35°.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["RS is a straight line crossing the two parallel lines UV and ST — look at the angles it makes with each of them.","Corresponding angles sit in matching positions at the two parallel lines; find the one matching angle RUV, then do the same along RT.","State the two angles of triangle RST you now know, then use the angle sum of a triangle on one line of working."],"codes":["8.03c","8.03d","8.01a"]},{"q":"A quadrilateral has both pairs of opposite sides parallel and equal, and its diagonals are unequal in length and do not cross at right angles. Name this quadrilateral, and state the two marks you would put on a sketch of it to show its equal and parallel sides. A calculator may be used.","a":"parallelogram; matching arrows on each pair of parallel sides, and matching dash marks on each pair of equal sides","sol":"B1 for parallelogram — both pairs of opposite sides parallel and equal, with the unequal, non-perpendicular diagonals ruling out a rectangle or a rhombus. B1 for arrows marking each pair of parallel sides. B1 for dash marks on each pair of equal sides.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both pairs of sides being parallel and equal fits more than one shape — use what is NOT true (the diagonals) to narrow it down.","Recall the standard diagram marks: one symbol shows parallel lines and a different symbol shows equal lengths.","Name the quadrilateral first, then state the two separate marks, one for the parallel sides and one for the equal sides."],"codes":["8.04b","8.01e"]},{"q":"Two straight paths meet at a point K, with an angle of 82° between them. A new lamp-post line is to be laid from K, so that every point on it is the same distance from each path. (a) Name the construction that fixes the direction of the line, and describe how to carry it out using compasses. (b) Work out the size of the angle between the new line and each path. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) the bisector of the angle between the two paths at K; (b) 41°","sol":"(a) B1 for naming the construction as the bisector of the angle between the two paths at K. B1 for describing the method: draw an arc centred on K that cuts both paths, then two further equal arcs, one centred on each of those two crossing points, and join K to the point where these last two arcs cross. (b) M1 for 82° ÷ 2. A1 for 41°.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Think about which construction gives every point that is the same distance from two lines that meet at a point.","That construction begins with an arc centred on the vertex K, cutting both paths.","Once you can name the construction, halve the angle between the two paths to find the angle it makes with each one."],"codes":["8.02b","8.02d"]},{"q":"A solid is built from identical cubes standing on a table. The bottom layer is completely filled, 3 cubes from left to right and 3 cubes from front to back. A second layer of cubes is placed on top of the middle row only (the middle of the three rows from front to back), and nothing else is added. Work out the number of cubes in the solid, the number of squares in the plan, and the number of squares in the front elevation. A calculator may be used.","a":"12 cubes; 9 squares in the plan; 6 squares in the front elevation","sol":"B1 for 12 cubes — the bottom layer is 3 × 3 = 9 cubes, plus one extra cube on each of the 3 middle-row positions, 9 + 3 = 12. B1 for 9 squares in the plan — the bottom layer already fills every one of the 3 by 3 positions, so the view from above covers all 9. B1 for 6 squares in the front elevation — every one of the 3 left-to-right positions rises to height 2 at the middle depth, so the front view is a solid rectangle 3 wide by 2 high, 3 × 2 = 6.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Work out the bottom layer first, then see how many more cubes the second layer adds.","The plan looks straight down, so it shows every position that has at least one cube on it.","For the front elevation, work out the tallest height standing at each of the 3 positions from left to right, then count the squares that makes."],"codes":["8.06b","8.06a"]},{"q":"A triangle is drawn accurately. Using a protractor, two of its angles are measured as 42° and 96°. (a) Work out the third angle. (b) State whether the triangle is acute-angled, right-angled or obtuse-angled, giving a reason. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 42°; (b) obtuse-angled, because one angle (96°) is greater than 90°","sol":"(a) M1 for 180° − 42° − 96°, using the angle sum of a triangle. A1 for 42°. (b) B1 for obtuse-angled. B1 for the reason: a triangle is obtuse-angled when one of its angles is greater than 90°, and 96° is greater than 90°.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Every triangle's three angles add up to a value you should know.","Subtract the two measured angles from that total to find the third.","Compare the largest of the three angles with 90° to decide which of the three angle-types names the triangle."],"codes":["8.04a","8.01b"]},{"q":"(a) Each interior angle of a regular polygon P is 162°. Work out how many sides P has. (b) A regular hexagon, a square and a third regular polygon Q fit exactly around a point with no gaps and no overlaps. Work out how many sides Q has. (non-calculator)","a":"P has 20 sides and Q has 12 sides","sol":"(a) M1 for the exterior angle 180° − 162° = 18°. A1 for 360° ÷ 18° = 20 sides. (b) M1 for 360° − 120° − 90° = 150°, the interior angle of Q, using 120° for a regular hexagon and 90° for a square. M1 for the exterior angle 180° − 150° = 30° and then 360° ÷ 30°. A1 for 12 sides.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The exterior angles of any polygon have a fixed total, however many sides it has.","For each part, turn an interior angle into an exterior angle first, then use that total.","For part (b) write down the interior angles of a regular hexagon and of a square, and see what is left of the angles round a point."],"codes":["8.03d","8.01c"]},{"q":"A straight fence MN is marked on a plan drawn to a scale of 1 cm to 25 m. A tree T stands on the plan, 6 cm from the fence and to one side of it. (a) A gate is to be built on the fence, at the point closest to the tree. Name the construction that fixes the position of the gate, explain briefly why that point is the closest, and work out the real distance from the tree to the gate, in metres. (b) A sprinkler must water every point within 80 m of the tree. Describe the shape and size of this region, giving its radius as drawn on the plan. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) the perpendicular from T to the line MN; 150 m; (b) a circular region centred on T, of radius 3.2 cm on the plan","sol":"(a) B1 for naming the construction as the perpendicular from the point T to the line MN — an arc centred on T cutting MN twice, then equal arcs from those two cuts, joined back to T. B1 for the reason: the perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance to that line. M1 for 6 × 25. A1 for 150 m. (b) B1 for a circular region centred on T, radius 80 ÷ 25 = 3.2 cm on the plan.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Each part is a standard locus — decide what set of points each description picks out before working with lengths.","'Closest point on a line' and 'a fixed distance from a point' are two different loci; picture what each one looks like drawn on the plan.","For (a) turn the 6 cm into metres using the scale; for (b) turn the 80 m back into centimetres using the same scale."],"codes":["8.02c","8.02d","8.01f"]},{"q":"In triangle XYZ, XY = XZ, so the triangle is isosceles, and angle YXZ = 48°. The line XZ is extended beyond Z to the point W. Work out the size of angle XYZ and the size of angle YZW (the exterior angle at Z), naming the facts you use. (non-calculator)","a":"angle XYZ = 66° and angle YZW = 114°","sol":"Since XY = XZ, triangle XYZ is isosceles, so its base angles are equal: angle XYZ = angle XZY. M1 for (180° − 48°) ÷ 2, using the angle sum of a triangle. A1 for angle XYZ = 66°. B1 for the reason that angle XZY and angle YZW are angles at the point Z on the straight line XZW, so they add up to 180°. A1 for angle YZW = 180° − 66° = 114°.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Two equal sides mean two equal angles opposite them — write down which two angles of the triangle must match.","Use the angle sum of a triangle to share the remaining angle equally between those two base angles.","The exterior angle at Z sits on a straight line with the interior angle at Z; one subtraction from 180° finishes the question."],"codes":["8.04a","8.03b"]},{"q":"The points P (−2, −1), Q (4, −1) and R (6, 3) are three vertices of a parallelogram PQRS, named in order round the shape. (a) Explain why the diagonals PR and QS of a parallelogram must have the same midpoint. (b) Work out the midpoint of PR. (c) Use your answer to part (b) to work out the coordinates of the fourth vertex S. (d) Check your answer by showing that QS also has this midpoint. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so they share the same midpoint; (b) (2, 1); (c) S = (0, 3); (d) the midpoint of QS is also (2, 1)","sol":"(a) B1 for the property that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other, so PR and QS must cross at the same point, their shared midpoint. (b) M1 for ((−2 + 6) ÷ 2, (−1 + 3) ÷ 2). A1 for (2, 1). (c) M1 for S = 2 × (2, 1) − Q, using that (2, 1) is also the midpoint of QS. A1 for S = (0, 3). (d) B1 for the midpoint of QS = ((4 + 0) ÷ 2, (−1 + 3) ÷ 2) = (2, 1), the same point, confirming S is correct.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Recall what a parallelogram's diagonals do to each other — that gives the key link between PR and QS.","Average the x-coordinates and the y-coordinates of P and R to find where the diagonals cross.","Use that same crossing point as the midpoint of QS to work backwards from Q to S."],"codes":["8.01g","8.04b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Two angles meet at a point on a straight line. One of them is 118°. What is the other?","a":"62°"},{"q":"What do the interior angles of a pentagon add up to?","a":"(5 − 2) × 180° = 540°"},{"q":"Name the locus of points that are the same distance from two given lines that meet.","a":"The bisector of the angle between the two lines."},{"q":"Write down the number of faces and the number of vertices of a triangular-based pyramid (a tetrahedron).","a":"4 faces and 4 vertices"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Points, lines, vertices and planes","back":"A point is a fixed position, capital-lettered. A line segment is named by its two ends (AB). A plane is a flat surface. Arrows on lines mean parallel; a small square marks perpendicular lines (a right angle).","code":"8.01a","open":false},{"front":"Types of angle, and the standard triangle-labelling convention","back":"Acute: less than 90°. Right: exactly 90°. Obtuse: between 90° and 180°. Reflex: more than 180°. In triangle ABC, the side opposite vertex A is a; angle ABC (∠ABC) is the angle at the middle letter, B.","code":"8.01b","open":false},{"front":"Names of triangles, quadrilaterals and polygons","back":"Scalene: no equal sides. Isosceles: two equal sides. Equilateral: three equal sides. Quadrilaterals: square, rectangle, parallelogram, trapezium, kite, rhombus. Regular polygon: all sides and angles equal. Pentagon 5 sides, hexagon 6, octagon 8.","code":"8.01c","open":false},{"front":"Solids: names and their face, edge, vertex counts","back":"Cube: 6 faces, 12 edges, 8 vertices. Cuboid: same counts, rectangular faces. Triangular prism: 5 faces, 9 edges, 6 vertices. Square-based pyramid: 5 faces, 8 edges, 5 vertices. Cylinder, cone and sphere have curved surfaces, not flat faces.","code":"8.06a","open":false},{"front":"Using a ruler, protractor and compasses","back":"A ruler draws and measures straight lines, to the nearest millimetre. A protractor measures and draws angles, placed with its centre on the vertex and its base line along one arm. Compasses draw circles and arcs of a fixed radius.","code":"8.01f","open":false},{"front":"Perpendicular bisector and angle bisector constructions","back":"Perpendicular bisector of AB: equal arcs, radius over half of AB, centred on A and B, joined through both crossings. Angle bisector: an arc from the vertex cutting both arms, then equal arcs from those cuts, joined to the vertex.","code":"8.02a","open":false},{"front":"Perpendicular from a point, and the three standard loci","back":"Perpendicular from a point to a line: the shortest route to it. Loci: equidistant from two points is their perpendicular bisector; equidistant from two lines is the angle bisector; a fixed distance from a point is a circle.","code":"8.02c","open":false},{"front":"Angles at a point, on a line, and vertically opposite","back":"Angles at a point add up to 360°. Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°. Vertically opposite angles, made where two straight lines cross, are equal.","code":"8.03a","open":false},{"front":"Alternate and corresponding angles on parallel lines","back":"Alternate angles lie between the parallel lines, on opposite sides of the crossing line, and are equal. Corresponding angles lie in matching positions, on the same side, and are equal.","code":"8.03c","open":false},{"front":"Interior and exterior angles of a polygon with n sides","back":"Interior angles add up to (n − 2) × 180°. Exterior angles add up to 360°. At each vertex, interior + exterior = 180°. For a regular polygon, divide each total by n.","code":"8.03d","open":false},{"front":"Properties of isosceles, equilateral and right-angled triangles","back":"Isosceles: two equal sides and the two base angles opposite them are equal. Equilateral: three equal sides and three equal 60° angles. Right-angled: one angle of exactly 90°.","code":"8.04a","open":false},{"front":"Properties of quadrilaterals","back":"Parallelogram: opposite sides parallel and equal, opposite angles equal. Rhombus: parallelogram with four equal sides, diagonals crossing at right angles. Kite: two pairs of equal adjacent sides. Trapezium: one pair of parallel sides.","code":"8.04b","open":false},{"front":"Symmetry of polygons","back":"A regular n-sided polygon has n lines of symmetry and rotational symmetry of order n. Irregular shapes can have fewer of each; count folds for lines of symmetry, and matching positions in one full turn for rotational order.","code":"8.04c","open":false},{"front":"The parts of a circle","back":"Radius: centre to curve. Diameter: chord through the centre, twice the radius. Chord: joins two points on the curve. Tangent: touches at one point. Arc: part of circumference. Sector: two radii and an arc. Segment: a chord and its arc.","code":"8.05a","open":false},{"front":"Plan and elevations of a solid","back":"Plan: the view from directly above. Front elevation: the view horizontally from the front. Side elevation: the view horizontally from the side. All three, and any isometric drawing, use the same scale.","code":"8.06b","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Angles at a point add up to 360° — must be recalled; not listed under 6.02d and not printed on either tier's formulae sheet.","Angles at a point on a straight line add up to 180°, and vertically opposite angles are equal — must be recalled; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","Alternate angles on parallel lines are equal, and corresponding angles on parallel lines are equal — must be recalled and named in full in your working; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","The angles of a triangle add up to 180°, and the angles of a quadrilateral add up to 360° — must be recalled; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","Sum of the interior angles of a polygon with n sides = (n − 2) × 180°, and one interior angle of a regular n-sided polygon = that total ÷ n — must be recalled or derived from the triangle angle sum; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","Sum of the exterior angles of any polygon = 360°, so one exterior angle of a regular n-sided polygon = 360° ÷ n, and interior angle + exterior angle = 180° at each vertex — must be recalled; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","The properties of the square, rectangle, parallelogram, rhombus, kite and trapezium (sides, angles, diagonals and symmetry) — must be recalled; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","The perpendicular distance from a point to a line is the shortest distance from that point to the line — must be recalled; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","The three standard loci — equidistant from two points is the perpendicular bisector of the segment joining them; equidistant from two lines that meet is the bisector of the angle between them; a fixed distance from a point is a circle centred on that point — must be recalled; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","Face, edge and vertex counts for the cube, cuboid, prism and pyramid — must be recalled, or worked out by counting from a description; not in 6.02d or on the sheet.","What the appendix and the exam formulae sheet actually cover: 6.02d lists nothing that belongs to this room — its Foundation-tier entries are the circumference and area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios, which belong to other rooms (circle measurement, right-angled triangles), not to this room's conventions, constructions, angle facts, polygon properties, circle vocabulary or solids. The Foundation formulae sheet (2026 series, outside this specification) likewise prints only circle- and triangle-measurement results, none of which this room uses. Every fact above must therefore be recalled or derived from the reasoning shown in the lesson — nothing in this room is given to you on the day."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:01:47.682299Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.86,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit: all three score-capping faults are genuinely fixed. room.facts now carries OCR's paper numbers exactly as the brief words them (J560/01-03 and /04-06, 100 marks, 1 h 30 min, Papers 2 and 5 non-calculator, grades 5-1 and 9-4); the new question 9 (index 8) is a clean angle-bisector construction at 82 degrees with no scale drawing and no shared number; the new question 15 (index 14) uses the diagonal-bisection property with all-new coordinates; the rhombus example ends on the asked-for angles; and the three code mis-cites are corrected. Re-solved all fifteen answers, six examples, four check and four exit-ticket answers with node and found none wrong; every sol tally still equals m, eight questions are marked non-calculator, difficulties are 5/6/4, no d=1 exceeds 2 marks, nothing Higher-only appears, the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md, and kit-tools check reports OK. Publishable; the three notes above are polish."}}},"basic-geometry-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"basic-geometry-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room is where Higher tier geometry stops being about finding an angle and starts being about proving it must be that angle. The same angle facts and circle results a Foundation student uses to compute a number, a Higher student must now use as named steps in a chain of reasoning: every line of a 'prove' or 'show that' answer needs its own justification, in words, and the eight standard circle theorems must be provable from radii, isosceles triangles and the exterior angle rule, not just quoted. AO2 carries 30% of the Higher tier and is marked almost entirely on this kind of chain of reasoning, so a correct angle with no theorem named earns little, and a proof that merely restates the result it was asked to prove earns nothing.","idea":[{"h":"Angles at a point in a formal proof","code":"8.03a","t":"Angles that meet at a single point always add up to 360°. At Higher tier this fact becomes one link in a longer chain: instead of computing an unknown angle directly, a formal proof states 'angles at a point sum to 360°' as the justification for one particular line of working, often alongside an unknown expressed algebraically, before that line feeds into the next step. In the exam, marks are given for the named fact and the equation it produces, not for the number alone."},{"h":"Angles on a line in a formal proof","code":"8.03b","t":"Two or more angles that sit on one side of a straight line, meeting it at the same point, add up to 180°. This fact is the standard bridge in a proof between an angle inside a shape and an angle formed by extending one of its sides, and it is exactly what turns an exterior-angle diagram into an equation. A formal answer states 'angles on a straight line sum to 180°' at the point it is used, rather than leaving the reader to infer it from a diagram."},{"h":"Vertically opposite, alternate and corresponding angles in a formal proof","code":"8.03c","t":"When two lines cross, the angles opposite one another are equal (vertically opposite); when a transversal cuts two parallel lines, corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles sum to 180°. These are the tools that let a proof move an angle from one part of a diagram to another — most famously the construction of a line parallel to one side of a triangle, used to prove its angle sum is 180°. Each use must name which of the three parallel-line facts is being applied."},{"h":"Angles in polygons in a formal proof","code":"8.03d","t":"The angle sum of a triangle is 180°, the sum of the exterior angles of any polygon is 360°, and an n-sided polygon's interior angles sum to (n − 2) × 180°, found by splitting it into (n − 2) triangles from one vertex. A key derived result is that the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two interior angles not next to it, proved from the angle sum of a triangle together with angles on a straight line. Exam questions ask for the general proof, not only a numerical instance."},{"h":"Triangle properties used inside a formal proof","code":"8.04a","t":"The properties of isosceles, equilateral and right-angled triangles — equal base angles, a line of symmetry, a right angle opposite the longest side — become building blocks inside longer proofs rather than results asked for on their own. The single most important use at Higher tier is that any triangle formed by two radii of a circle is isosceles, because both of those sides are equal; this fact underlies the proofs of the angle at the centre theorem and of the angle in a semicircle."},{"h":"Quadrilateral properties used inside a formal proof","code":"8.04b","t":"The properties of a square, rectangle, parallelogram, trapezium, kite and rhombus — equal or parallel sides, equal angles, diagonals that bisect each other or cross at right angles — can themselves be proved from more basic facts and are then used as steps inside further proofs, such as showing that the diagonals of a rhombus meet at right angles by combining the parallelogram property that they bisect each other with congruent triangles."},{"h":"The angle at the centre theorem, applied and proved","code":"8.05b","t":"The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of a circle is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the circumference. Applying it means halving or doubling a given angle and naming the theorem; proving it means drawing the two radii to the circumference point, showing the triangles they form are isosceles, and using the exterior angle of a triangle to add the two halves together. This theorem is also the tool used to prove the cyclic quadrilateral result."},{"h":"The angle in a semicircle, applied and proved","code":"8.05c","t":"The angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is always 90°. It applies whenever a triangle is drawn with one side as a diameter of a circle. It is proved either as the special case of the angle at the centre theorem where the centre angle on a diameter is a straight line (180°, so the circumference angle is 90°), or directly from the two isosceles triangles formed by the radius to the third vertex."},{"h":"Angles in the same segment, applied and proved","code":"8.05d","t":"Angles subtended at the circumference by the same arc, on the same side of that arc, are equal, because each one is half of the same angle at the centre. Applying it means spotting two angles standing on the same arc from the same side; proving it is a one-line consequence of the angle at the centre theorem, since both circumference angles equal half of the identical centre angle."},{"h":"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord, applied and proved","code":"8.05e","t":"A radius or diameter bisects a chord exactly when it is perpendicular to that chord — the property runs both ways. Applying it usually means using Pythagoras' theorem in the right-angled triangle formed by the radius, half the chord and the perpendicular distance from the centre. Proving either direction uses congruent triangles: two radii, the shared or perpendicular side, and either RHS (given perpendicularity) or SSS (given the midpoint) show the triangles match."},{"h":"The tangent and the radius, applied and proved","code":"8.05f","t":"The radius or diameter drawn to the point where a tangent touches a circle is always perpendicular to that tangent. Applying it gives a 90° angle to use inside a larger diagram. A formal proof argues by contradiction: if the radius met the tangent at any angle other than 90°, the true perpendicular from the centre to the tangent line would be shorter than the radius, placing its foot inside the circle — contradicting that every point of a tangent other than the point of contact lies outside the circle."},{"h":"The alternate segment theorem, applied and proved","code":"8.05g","t":"The angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle the chord subtends at the circumference in the alternate segment — the segment on the other side of the chord from the tangent angle. Applying it transfers a given angle to a triangle elsewhere in the circle, often combined with the angle sum of that triangle. A full proof combines the tangent–radius right angle with the angle at the centre theorem."},{"h":"Cyclic quadrilaterals, applied and proved","code":"8.05h","t":"A quadrilateral with all four vertices on a circle has opposite angles that add up to 180°. Applying it subtracts a given angle from 180° to find the opposite one. Proving it joins the centre to two opposite vertices, expresses each of the two reflex-or-obtuse angles at the centre using the angle at the centre theorem on the two arcs either side, and uses the fact that angles at a point sum to 360° to add the two doubled angles together."}],"examples":[{"title":"Proving that the angles of a triangle sum to 180°","setup":"Triangle ABC has angle BAC = a, angle ABC = b and angle BCA = c. A straight line is drawn through C, parallel to AB. Prove that the angles of a triangle sum to 180°, that is that a + b + c = 180°, using angle facts on parallel lines and on a straight line.","steps":["AC is a transversal crossing the two parallel lines (AB and the new line through C), so the angle on the far side of C, between the new line and CA, equals a, by alternate angles.","BC is also a transversal crossing the same two parallel lines, so the angle on the other side of C, between the new line and CB, equals b, by alternate angles.","The three angles now sitting at C on the straight line — the alternate angle equal to a, the triangle's own angle c, and the alternate angle equal to b — lie together along that one straight line, and angles on a straight line always sum to 180°.","Since a, c and b together make up that full 180°, a + c + b = 180°, which proves the angle sum of the triangle."],"cues":["AC crosses both parallel lines — what kind of angle pair does that make, and what is it equal to?","What does the same argument give for BC crossing the two parallel lines?","Which three angles, all sitting at the point C, does the diagram now show lying along the new straight line?","What do angles on a straight line always add up to?"]},{"title":"Proving the exterior angle of a triangle","setup":"Triangle ABC has angle BAC = x and angle ABC = y. Side BC is extended beyond C to a point D. Prove that the exterior angle ACD equals x + y.","steps":["Angle ACB and angle ACD lie together on the straight line BD, so angle ACB + angle ACD = 180°.","The angle sum of triangle ABC gives x + y + angle ACB = 180°, so angle ACB = 180° − x − y.","Substituting this into the straight-line equation: angle ACD = 180° − angle ACB = 180° − (180° − x − y).","This simplifies to angle ACD = x + y, so the exterior angle equals the sum of the two interior angles not next to it."],"cues":["Which two angles at C, together, make up the straight line BD?","Which other fact about triangle ABC gives a second equation involving angle ACB?","What happens when you replace angle ACB in the straight-line equation with the expression from the triangle?","What does the simplified expression say about angle ACD compared with x and y?"]},{"title":"Proving the angle at the centre theorem","setup":"A, B and C lie on a circle with centre O, and C is on the major arc AB. The line CO is extended to meet the circle again at D, so that D lies on the minor arc AB. Let angle ACO = p and angle BCO = q. Prove that angle AOB = 2 × angle ACB.","steps":["OA, OB and OC are all radii of the circle, so OA = OC and OB = OC, making triangles OAC and OBC isosceles.","In the isosceles triangle OAC, the base angles are equal, so angle OAC = angle OCA = p; in the isosceles triangle OBC, angle OBC = angle OCB = q.","Angle AOD is the exterior angle of triangle OAC at O, so angle AOD = p + p = 2p; similarly angle BOD = q + q = 2q.","Angle AOB is made up of angle AOD and angle BOD together, so angle AOB = 2p + 2q = 2(p + q) = 2 × angle ACB, since angle ACB = angle ACO + angle BCO = p + q."],"cues":["OA, OB and OC are all the same kind of line in this circle — what does that make triangles OAC and OBC?","What do the two equal base angles of each isosceles triangle tell you about angle OAC and angle OBC?","Angle AOD sits outside triangle OAC, at its apex O — which rule links an exterior angle to the triangle's other two angles?","How do angle AOD and angle BOD combine to make the whole of angle AOB?"]},{"title":"Proving that the angle in a semicircle is 90°","setup":"AB is a diameter of a circle with centre O, and C is any other point on the circle. OC is drawn, splitting triangle ABC into two smaller triangles. Prove that angle ACB is a right angle.","steps":["OA = OC, both being radii, so triangle OAC is isosceles; let its equal base angles be angle OAC = angle OCA = s.","OB = OC, both being radii, so triangle OBC is also isosceles; let its equal base angles be angle OBC = angle OCB = t.","Angle ACB is made up of angle OCA and angle OCB together, so angle ACB = s + t.","The angle sum of triangle ABC gives angle A + angle B + angle ACB = 180°, that is s + t + (s + t) = 180°, so 2(s + t) = 180° and s + t = 90°, meaning angle ACB = 90°."],"cues":["OA and OC are two sides of the same kind in this circle — what does that make triangle OAC, and its base angles?","What does the same argument give for triangle OBC?","How do angle OCA and angle OCB combine to make the full angle ACB?","Once triangle ABC's three angles are written in terms of s and t, what equation does its angle sum give you?"]},{"title":"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord","setup":"A circle has centre O and radius 10 cm. AB is a chord of the circle with length 16 cm. M is the point on AB for which OM is perpendicular to AB. Work out the length of OM.","steps":["The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects that chord, so AM = 16 ÷ 2 = 8 cm.","OA is a radius, so OA = 10 cm, and triangle OMA has a right angle at M.","By Pythagoras' theorem, OM² = 10² − 8² = 100 − 64 = 36.","OM = √36 = 6 cm."],"cues":["What does the perpendicular from the centre do to the chord it meets?","Which two side lengths of the right-angled triangle OMA do you now know?","Which rule connects the three sides of a right-angled triangle?","What is left to do once you have OM²?"]},{"title":"The alternate segment theorem","setup":"A tangent touches a circle at the point A. AB is a chord, and the angle between the tangent and AB, on one side, is 72°. C is a point on the circle in the alternate segment for that angle (the segment on the other side of AB from the 72° angle). Angle ABC = 48°. Work out the size of angle BAC.","steps":["By the alternate segment theorem, the angle between the tangent and the chord AB equals the angle AB subtends in the alternate segment, so angle ACB = 72°.","Triangle ABC now has two known angles: angle ACB = 72° and angle ABC = 48°.","The angle sum of a triangle is 180°, so angle BAC = 180° − 72° − 48°.","angle BAC = 60°."],"cues":["What does the alternate segment theorem let you copy from the tangent–chord angle into the triangle?","Once angle ACB is known, how many of triangle ABC's three angles are now known?","Which rule finds the third angle of a triangle once the other two are known?","What number does that subtraction give?"]}],"check":[{"q":"What do angles on a straight line always add up to?","a":"180°."},{"q":"Which theorem gives the size of the angle at the circumference in a semicircle, and what is that angle?","a":"The angle in a semicircle is always 90°, a corollary of the angle at the centre theorem, since a diameter subtends a straight angle (180°) at the centre."},{"q":"In a formal proof, why is a triangle formed by two radii of a circle always isosceles?","a":"Because both of those two sides are radii of the same circle, so they are equal in length, giving the triangle a pair of equal base angles."},{"q":"What is the sum of the interior angles of a regular octagon?","a":"(8 − 2) × 180° = 1080°."}]},"room":{"facts":["Angles at a point sum to 360°; angles on a straight line sum to 180°; vertically opposite angles are equal — the basic tools for building a formal proof.","On parallel lines cut by a transversal: corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles sum to 180°.","The angle sum of a triangle is 180°; the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two interior angles not next to it — both provable from the facts above.","For an n-sided polygon, the interior angles sum to (n − 2) × 180°, found by splitting the polygon into (n − 2) triangles from one vertex; the exterior angles of any polygon sum to 360°.","A triangle formed by two radii of a circle is isosceles, because radii of the same circle are equal; this is the key tool in every circle-theorem proof in this room.","Angle at the centre theorem: the angle subtended by an arc at the centre of a circle is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at the circumference; proved from the isosceles triangles formed by the radii and the exterior angle of a triangle.","Angle in a semicircle = 90°, and angles in the same segment (subtended by the same arc, same side) are equal — both corollaries of the angle at the centre theorem.","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°, proved by joining the centre to two opposite vertices and applying the angle at the centre theorem to each pair of arcs.","A radius or diameter bisects a chord if and only if it is perpendicular to it, proved using congruent triangles (RHS or SSS) formed by the two radii.","The radius or diameter drawn to a point on a circle is perpendicular to the tangent at that point.","Alternate segment theorem: the angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle the chord subtends in the alternate segment.","Properties of triangles and quadrilaterals (equal base angles of an isosceles triangle, a parallelogram's diagonals bisecting each other, a rhombus's equal sides) are themselves used as steps inside longer formal proofs.","OCR sets three exam papers per tier: J560/01–03 for Foundation and J560/04–06 for Higher, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator; grades awarded run 5–1 on Foundation tier and 9–4 on Higher tier."],"questions":[{"q":"At a point, four angles are formed: 2x°, 3x°, 4x° and (5x + 10)°. Using the fact that angles at a point sum to 360°, form and solve an equation to find x. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 25","sol":"Angles at a point sum to 360°, so 2x + 3x + 4x + (5x + 10) = 360. M1 for the equation 14x + 10 = 360. A1 for x = 25.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["All four angles are drawn round the same single point.","Add the four expressions together and set the total equal to the fact you know about angles at a point.","Write the line '2x + 3x + 4x + (5x + 10) = 360' before you collect the terms."],"codes":["8.03a"]},{"q":"Two angles on a straight line are (3x + 8)° and (2x + 12)°. Using the fact that angles on a straight line sum to 180°, form and solve an equation to find x. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 32","sol":"Angles on a straight line sum to 180°, so (3x + 8) + (2x + 12) = 180. M1 for the equation 5x + 20 = 180. A1 for x = 32.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Both expressions describe angles meeting the same straight line at one point.","Add the two expressions together and set the total equal to the fact you know about angles on a line.","Write the line '(3x + 8) + (2x + 12) = 180' before you collect the terms."],"codes":["8.03b"]},{"q":"Two parallel lines are cut by a transversal. One angle is (4x − 5)° and its corresponding angle is (2x + 25)°. Using the corresponding angles fact, form and solve an equation to find x, then state the size of each angle. (non-calculator)","a":"x = 15, and each angle is 55°","sol":"Corresponding angles on parallel lines are equal, so 4x − 5 = 2x + 25. M1 for the equation 2x = 30. A1 for x = 15 and each angle = 4(15) − 5 = 55°.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two marked angles sit in the same position at each of the two crossing points.","Set the two expressions equal to each other, since corresponding angles are equal.","Write the line '4x − 5 = 2x + 25' before you rearrange it."],"codes":["8.03c"]},{"q":"Work out the size of one interior angle of a regular nonagon (a 9-sided polygon), using the formal result for the interior angle sum of a polygon. A calculator may be used.","a":"140°","sol":"The interior angles of an n-sided polygon sum to (n − 2) × 180°. M1 for (9 − 2) × 180° = 1260°. A1 for 1260° ÷ 9 = 140°, using that all nine interior angles of a regular polygon are equal.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A nonagon can be split into triangles from one vertex — count how many.","Multiply the number of triangles by the angle sum of one triangle, then share the total equally.","Write the line '(9 − 2) × 180°' before you divide by the number of sides."],"codes":["8.03d"]},{"q":"In triangle PQR, angle QPR = 52° and angle PQR = 76°. Side QR is extended to a point S. Work out the size of the exterior angle PRS, naming the fact that gives it directly. (non-calculator)","a":"128°","sol":"B1 for naming the fact: the exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two interior angles not next to it. M1 for 52° + 76°. A1 for angle PRS = 128°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Angle PRS sits outside the triangle, at the vertex where side QR has been extended.","One rule links an exterior angle directly to two of the triangle's interior angles, without needing the third.","Write the line 'angle PRS = angle QPR + angle PQR' before you substitute the two given angles."],"codes":["8.03d"]},{"q":"AB is a diameter of a circle with centre O, and C is a point on the circumference. Angle ABC = 35°. Work out the size of angle ACB and the size of angle BAC, naming the fact that gives angle ACB. (non-calculator)","a":"angle ACB = 90° and angle BAC = 55°","sol":"B1 for angle ACB = 90°, because the angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is a right angle (angle in a semicircle). M1 for 180° − 90° − 35°, using the angle sum of triangle ABC. A1 for angle BAC = 55°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["AB being a diameter puts C on a semicircle, however it is drawn.","Once one angle of triangle ABC is known, the third comes from the angle sum of a triangle.","Write the line 'angle ACB = 90° (angle in a semicircle), so angle BAC = 180° − angle ACB − angle ABC' before substituting."],"codes":["8.05c","8.04a"]},{"q":"A, B, C and D lie on a circle with centre O. B and D both lie on the major arc AC. The angle AOC at the centre, standing on the minor arc AC, is 132°. Work out the size of angle ABC and the size of angle ADC, naming the circle theorem you use for each. (non-calculator)","a":"angle ABC = 66° and angle ADC = 66°","sol":"M1 for angle ABC = ½ × 132°, using the angle at the centre theorem (angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference), since both stand on the minor arc AC. A1 for angle ABC = 66°. B1 for recognising angle ADC stands on the same arc, on the same side, so angle ADC and angle ABC are angles in the same segment. A1 for angle ADC = 66°.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Both angle ABC and angle AOC stand on the same arc AC — one has its point at the centre.","Once angle ABC is found, think about where D sits compared with B, relative to the arc AC.","Write the line 'angle ABC = ½ × angle AOC' first, then compare angle ADC with the answer you get."],"codes":["8.05b","8.05d"]},{"q":"PQRS is a cyclic quadrilateral, with the vertices in that order round the circle. Angle SPQ = 79° and angle PQR = 101°. Work out the size of angle QRS and the size of angle RSP, naming the property you use. (non-calculator)","a":"angle QRS = 101° and angle RSP = 79°","sol":"B1 for naming the property: opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°. M1 for 180° − 79°. A1 for angle QRS = 101° (opposite angle SPQ). B1 for using the same property for the other pair: angle RSP = 180° − 101° = 79°.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["P and R sit at opposite corners of the quadrilateral, and so do Q and S.","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral share a fixed total.","Write the line 'angle SPQ + angle QRS = 180°' and the matching line for the other pair before substituting."],"codes":["8.05h"]},{"q":"A circle has centre O and radius r cm. AB is a chord of the circle with length 30 cm, and the perpendicular distance from O to AB is 8 cm. Work out the value of r. A calculator may be used.","a":"17 cm","sol":"The perpendicular from the centre of a circle to a chord bisects that chord, so half of AB = 30 ÷ 2 = 15 cm. M1 for 15 cm. Let M be the point where the perpendicular meets AB; triangle OMA has a right angle at M, with OM = 8 cm and MA = 15 cm. M1 for r² = 8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289, by Pythagoras' theorem. A1 for r = √289 = 17 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The perpendicular from the centre does something helpful to the chord it meets.","Half the chord and the given perpendicular distance are two sides of the right-angled triangle OMA.","Write the line 'r² = OM² + AM²' before you substitute the values you know."],"codes":["8.05e"]},{"q":"A tangent touches a circle at the point A, and AB is a chord. C is a point on the circle. Angle BAC = 44° and angle ABC = 71°. Work out the size of the angle between the tangent and the chord AB on the side of AB opposite to C, naming the theorem you use. (non-calculator)","a":"65°","sol":"M1 for angle ACB = 180° − 44° − 71°, using the angle sum of triangle ABC. A1 for angle ACB = 65°. B1 for the angle between the tangent and AB being equal to angle ACB = 65°, by the alternate segment theorem.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Start with the two angles you already know inside triangle ABC.","Once you have the third angle of the triangle, think about which theorem connects it to the tangent.","Write the line 'angle ACB = 180° − angle BAC − angle ABC' before you use the alternate segment theorem."],"codes":["8.05g"]},{"q":"ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral in a circle with centre O, with the vertices in that order round the circle. Using only the angle at the centre theorem, prove that angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°. (non-calculator)","a":"2 × angle ABC + 2 × angle ADC = 360°, so angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°","sol":"Join OA and OC, giving two angles at O, one on each side of AC. B1 for the angle at the centre standing on the arc ADC, on the same side as D, being 2 × angle ABC (angle at the centre theorem). M1 for the angle at the centre standing on the arc ABC, on the same side as B, being 2 × angle ADC. M1 for those two angles at O adding to 360°, because they are angles at a point. A1 for 2 × angle ABC + 2 × angle ADC = 360°, so angle ABC + angle ADC = 180°.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Drawing the two radii OA and OC creates two angles at the centre that together make up the full turn.","Each of those two centre angles is linked to one of the angles you are asked about, by the theorem you are told to use.","Write the line 'reflex or obtuse angle AOC on D's side = 2 × angle ABC' and then the matching line for the other side."],"codes":["8.05h","8.05b"]},{"q":"ABCD is a rhombus. Its diagonals AC and BD meet at X. Using the fact that a rhombus is a parallelogram, prove that the diagonals of the rhombus meet at right angles. (non-calculator)","a":"angle AXB = angle CXB = 90°","sol":"B1 for the diagonals of a parallelogram bisecting each other, so AX = CX and BX = DX, a property the rhombus inherits. M1 for triangles ABX and CBX matching by SSS: AB = CB (sides of a rhombus are equal), AX = CX (diagonals bisect each other), and BX is common to both triangles. A1 for angle AXB = angle CXB, corresponding angles of the congruent triangles. B1 for angle AXB and angle CXB lying on the straight line AC and being equal, so each equals 180° ÷ 2 = 90°.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A rhombus is a parallelogram with the extra property that all four sides are equal — think what that gives you about triangles ABX and CBX.","Once you can match up three pairs of equal sides between two triangles, one congruence test applies.","Write the line 'AB = CB, AX = CX, BX = BX, so triangles ABX and CBX are congruent (SSS)' and then use angles on the straight line AC."],"codes":["8.04b"]},{"q":"O is the centre of a circle and P is a point on the circle. The line t is the tangent to the circle at P. Prove that OP is perpendicular to t. (non-calculator)","a":"OP is perpendicular to t","sol":"B1 for supposing, for contradiction, that OP is not perpendicular to t, and letting M be the foot of the true perpendicular from O to t, with M different from P. M1 for triangle OMP having a right angle at M, so OP (the side opposite that right angle) is the hypotenuse and is therefore longer than OM. A1 for OM being shorter than the radius OP, which would place M inside the circle, contradicting that every point of a tangent other than the point of contact lies outside the circle. B1 for concluding that no such point M can exist, so the perpendicular from O to t must meet t at P itself, meaning OP is perpendicular to t.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Suppose the radius OP were not the shortest line from O to the tangent, and think about which point would be shorter.","In the right-angled triangle formed by that shorter perpendicular, which side must be the longest?","Write the line 'OP is the hypotenuse of triangle OMP, so OP > OM' and think about what OM < radius would mean for the point M."],"codes":["8.05f"]},{"q":"A circle has centre O, and AB is a chord of the circle that is not a diameter. M is a point on AB. Prove that OM is perpendicular to AB if and only if M is the midpoint of AB. (non-calculator)","a":"OM ⟂ AB exactly when M is the midpoint of AB","sol":"Forward direction: B1 for triangles OMA and OMB both right-angled at M (given OM ⟂ AB), with OA = OB (radii) and OM common, so they are congruent by RHS. A1 for AM = MB, so M is the midpoint. Converse direction: B1 for triangles OMA and OMB with OA = OB (radii), AM = MB (given M is the midpoint) and OM common, so they are congruent by SSS. A1 for angle OMA = angle OMB, which lie on the straight line AB and are equal, so each equals 90°, giving OM ⟂ AB.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["This 'if and only if' claim needs two separate proofs, one starting from the right angle and one starting from the midpoint.","In each direction, compare triangles OMA and OMB using the sides you are given plus the two radii.","Write the line 'OA = OB, OM common' for both directions, then add the third fact each direction supplies (RHS or SSS)."],"codes":["8.05e"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Three angles at a point are 118°, 96° and x°. Write down the value of x.","a":"146°"},{"q":"An arc AB subtends an angle of 70° at the centre O of a circle. What angle does the same arc subtend at a point on the major arc?","a":"35°, half of the angle at the centre (angle at the centre theorem)."},{"q":"AB is a diameter of a circle and C is a point on the circumference with angle CAB = 40°. Write down the size of angle ACB, and hence find angle ABC.","a":"angle ACB = 90°, so angle ABC = 50°."},{"q":"State the theorem that tells you the angle between a tangent and a chord equals the angle in the alternate segment.","a":"The alternate segment theorem."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Angles at a point","back":"Angles that meet at a single point add up to 360°. Used as a named step inside longer formal proofs, not only to find a single missing angle.","code":"8.03a","open":false},{"front":"Angles on a straight line","back":"Angles on one side of a straight line, meeting it at the same point, add up to 180°.","code":"8.03b","open":false},{"front":"Vertically opposite angles","back":"When two straight lines cross, the two angles opposite one another at the crossing point are equal.","code":"8.03c","open":false},{"front":"Angles on parallel lines","back":"Cut by a transversal: corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, and co-interior angles add up to 180°.","code":"8.03c","open":false},{"front":"Exterior angle of a triangle","back":"The exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two interior angles that are not next to it.","code":"8.03d","open":false},{"front":"Angle sum of a polygon","back":"An n-sided polygon's interior angles sum to (n − 2) × 180°, found by splitting the polygon into (n − 2) triangles from one vertex; its exterior angles always sum to 360°.","code":"8.03d","open":false},{"front":"Isosceles triangles formed by radii","back":"Any triangle with two radii of the same circle as two of its sides is isosceles, because those two sides are equal. This is the key tool in every circle-theorem proof.","code":"8.04a","open":false},{"front":"Parallelogram and rhombus properties used in proofs","back":"A parallelogram's diagonals bisect each other and its opposite sides and angles are equal; a rhombus adds that all four sides are equal, which is what makes its diagonals meet at right angles.","code":"8.04b","open":false},{"front":"Angle at the centre theorem","back":"The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of a circle is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the circumference.","code":"8.05b","open":false},{"front":"Angle in a semicircle","back":"The angle subtended at the circumference by a diameter is always 90°.","code":"8.05c","open":false},{"front":"Angles in the same segment","back":"Angles subtended at the circumference by the same arc, on the same side of that arc, are equal.","code":"8.05d","open":false},{"front":"Perpendicular from the centre to a chord","back":"A radius or diameter bisects a chord exactly when it is perpendicular to that chord — each property implies the other.","code":"8.05e","open":false},{"front":"Radius and tangent","back":"The radius or diameter drawn to the point where a tangent touches a circle is always perpendicular to that tangent.","code":"8.05f","open":false},{"front":"Alternate segment theorem","back":"The angle between a tangent and a chord drawn from the point of contact equals the angle the chord subtends at the circumference in the alternate segment.","code":"8.05g","open":false},{"front":"Cyclic quadrilateral","back":"A quadrilateral with all four vertices on a circle has opposite angles that add up to 180°.","code":"8.05h","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Angles at a point sum to 360°; angles on a straight line sum to 180°; vertically opposite angles are equal — recall all three; no angle fact appears on either tier's formulae sheet.","On parallel lines cut by a transversal: corresponding angles are equal, alternate angles are equal, co-interior angles sum to 180° — recall these; not printed for you.","Angle sum of a triangle = 180°, and exterior angle of a triangle = sum of the two interior angles not next to it — recall these and be ready to prove them formally; not printed for you.","Interior angle sum of an n-sided polygon = (n − 2) × 180°; exterior angles of any polygon sum to 360° — recall these; not printed for you.","Angle at the centre theorem: angle at the centre = 2 × angle at the circumference, standing on the same arc — recall it and be ready to prove it; no circle theorem appears in the specification's 6.02d list or on either tier's formulae sheet.","Angle in a semicircle = 90° — recall it, as a corollary of the angle at the centre theorem; not printed for you.","Angles in the same segment are equal — recall it; not printed for you.","Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180° — recall it and be ready to prove it, using the angle at the centre theorem; not printed for you.","The perpendicular from the centre to a chord bisects the chord, and this works in both directions — recall it and be ready to prove both directions using congruent triangles; not printed for you.","A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact — recall it and be ready to prove it; not printed for you.","Alternate segment theorem: angle between tangent and chord = angle in the alternate segment — recall it; not printed for you.","Properties of isosceles triangles (equal base angles) and of parallelograms and rhombuses (diagonals bisecting each other, equal sides) used as proof steps — recall these from OCR 8.04; not printed for you.","Pythagoras' theorem, a² + b² = c² with c the hypotenuse, is needed in this room for chord and perpendicular-distance problems. The specification's 6.02d lists it as a Foundation-tier 'must know' formula, but OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet for exams from 2026 does print Pythagoras' theorem, and the sheet governs what is given on the day, so it is provided.","Everything else on this page must be recalled or derived: not one angle-proof fact, polygon angle result or circle theorem in this room appears in the specification's 6.02d list or on either tier's formulae sheet. Read the insert provided on the day, and expect to name every fact and theorem above in your working."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:03:22.925Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit: all seven problems from the first pass are genuinely fixed — room.facts[12] now carries OCR’s paper numbers (J560/01–03 and /04–06, 100 marks, 1 h 30 min, Papers 2 and 5 non-calculator, grades 5–1 and 9–4), the chord question now runs the other way (half-chord 15 cm and distance 8 cm to radius 17 cm, an 8–15–17 triple, no longer worked example 5’s ask), the alternate-segment question now finds the tangent–chord angle from the triangle rather than repeating worked example 6, the cyclic-quadrilateral question’s m is 4 with a B1 for angle RSP, chord AB in the iff proof is now “not a diameter”, and the exit ticket’s polygon repeat is replaced by an angle-at-the-centre item (70° → 35°). Re-solved from scratch with node: all 14 answer keys, all 6 worked examples, the 4 check questions and the 4 exit answers are correct, every sol tally now adds to m (2,2,2,2,3,3,4,4,3,3,4,4,4,4), difficulty splits 4/6/4 with no d=1 above 2 marks, 12 questions are non-calculator with hand-workable numbers, all eight circle theorems are stated correctly and all six proofs are valid, and the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md item for item. Only two wording points remain, both cosmetic; publish."}}},"congruence-similarity":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"congruence-similarity","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"OCR groups transformations, congruence, vectors and similarity into one Foundation area because every idea in it compares a shape with an image of itself. Reflection, rotation and translation make a congruent image and are tested on both the non-calculator Paper 2 and the calculator papers, always by describing the transformation fully — the mirror line, centre and angle, or the column vector, and nothing less. Congruence proofs (SSS, SAS, ASA, RHS) and their use inside triangles and quadrilaterals carry AO2 marks for naming the criterion correctly, not only for a final length or angle. Vectors are added, subtracted and scaled with the same column notation used to describe a translation, so the two ideas constantly reappear together. Similarity extends the same comparison to shapes of different sizes, where one known pair of matching lengths unlocks every other missing length.","idea":[{"h":"Describing a reflection and finding a mirror line","t":"A reflection needs a mirror line to be fully described. On a grid the line is given by its equation — x = a is vertical, y = b is horizontal, and y = x or y = −x run diagonally at 45°. To reflect a point in a vertical line x = a, keep the y-coordinate and place the x-coordinate the same distance from a but on the opposite side; a horizontal line y = b works the same way on the y-coordinate. To reflect in y = x, swap the coordinates: (x, y) → (y, x); in y = −x, swap and change both signs: (x, y) → (−y, −x). Given a shape and its image, the mirror line passes through the midpoint of the join between a vertex and its image, at right angles to that join. Every length and angle is unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object.","code":"9.01a"},{"h":"Describing a rotation and finding its centre","t":"A rotation needs three pieces of information: the centre, the angle turned and the direction, clockwise or anticlockwise. About the origin, a 90° anticlockwise turn sends (x, y) to (−y, x), a 180° turn sends (x, y) to (−x, −y), and a 90° clockwise turn sends (x, y) to (y, −x); about any other centre, find the step from the centre to the point, apply the same rule to that step, then add the step back onto the centre. Given a shape and its image, the centre is found by joining a vertex to its image, constructing the perpendicular bisector of that join, and repeating for a second vertex — the two bisectors cross at the centre. Rotation leaves every length and angle unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object; describe one rotation only.","code":"9.01b"},{"h":"Translating a shape with a column vector","t":"A translation slides every point of a shape the same distance in the same direction, and it is fully described by a single column vector: the movement across the page on top, the movement up or down underneath, right and up positive, left and down negative. To translate a point, add the vector to its coordinates: (x, y) becomes (x + a, y + b). Given an object point and its image, the vector mapping one onto the other is found by subtracting: (x of image − x of object, y of image − y of object), and that same vector applies to every other point of the shape. A translation leaves every length and angle unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object.","code":"9.01c"},{"h":"Proving two triangles congruent","t":"Two triangles are congruent when one is an exact copy of the other, so every matching side and every matching angle is equal. Four sets of conditions are enough to prove it: SSS (three pairs of sides), SAS (two pairs of sides with the angle between them), ASA (two pairs of angles with the side between them, which also covers AAS, two angles and a non-included side) and RHS (a right angle, the hypotenuse and one other side). AAA is not a congruence criterion — matching angles alone give similar shapes, which can be different sizes. Write the vertices of the second triangle in the order that corresponds to the first, so that triangle ABC congruent to triangle PQR means A matches P, B matches Q and C matches R, and the matching sides and angles can be read straight off.","code":"9.02a"},{"h":"Using congruent triangles to prove a result","t":"Once two triangles are shown to be congruent, matching sides and matching angles can be declared equal without measuring them, and this is how many geometric results are proved. A line from the apex of an isosceles triangle to the midpoint of its base, or at right angles to its base, splits it into two triangles that share a side and are congruent by SSS or RHS, which proves the two base angles are equal. Inside a parallelogram, a diagonal splits the shape into two triangles congruent by SSS, using the two pairs of equal opposite sides and the shared diagonal, which proves that opposite angles are equal. A 'prove' or 'show that' question needs every line of reasoning written out, naming the criterion and stating which matching parts follow from it — quoting the result without the working scores nothing.","code":"9.02b"},{"h":"Adding, subtracting and scaling vectors","t":"Column vectors combine one row at a time: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d). In a diagram they add nose to tail — the second arrow starts where the first ends, and the single arrow from the very start to the very end is the sum. Multiplying a vector by a number (a scalar) multiplies both components: k(a, b) = (ka, kb), which makes the arrow |k| times as long along the same line; a negative scalar reverses the direction, so −a is the same length as a but points the opposite way, and a + (−a) = (0, 0), the zero vector. Questions often combine the two operations, such as 2a − b, and the scaled vector should be written down before the adding or subtracting starts.","code":"9.03a"},{"h":"Representing and drawing column vectors","t":"A vector has both a size and a direction, and on a square or coordinate grid it is drawn as an arrow. Writing it as a column vector records the same information as two numbers: the movement across the page on top and the movement up the page underneath, right and up positive, left and down negative. Given two points, the column vector representing the journey from the first to the second is found by subtracting coordinates: (x of the second − x of the first, y of the second − y of the first). To draw a column vector on a grid, start anywhere, move across by the top number and up or down by the bottom number, and mark the arrowhead at the finishing point; every arrow with the same two numbers represents the same vector, wherever it is drawn.","code":"9.03b"},{"h":"Identifying and proving similar triangles","t":"Two triangles are similar when their three angles match, in some order (AAA), or when their three pairs of corresponding sides are all in the same ratio. Matching angles are found using angle facts already known — angles on parallel lines, the angle sum of a triangle, or a shared angle in overlapping triangles — and once all three angles are shown to match, the triangles are proved similar without measuring a single side. Unlike congruent triangles, similar triangles need not be the same size: their matching sides are all in one fixed ratio, called the scale factor, while every matching angle stays exactly equal. A 'prove' question needs every matching pair of angles justified by name, ending in the conclusion that the triangles are similar.","code":"9.04a"},{"h":"Enlarging a shape and describing an enlargement","t":"An enlargement is fully described by its scale factor and its centre. Every point moves along the straight line from the centre through that point, and its distance from the centre is multiplied by the scale factor; a point already at the centre does not move. A scale factor greater than 1 makes the image bigger, and a fractional scale factor between 0 and 1 makes the image smaller, while every angle in the shape stays the same, so the image is similar to the object, not congruent. Given a shape and its image, the scale factor is found by comparing a matching pair of lengths or a matching pair of distances from a chosen point, and the centre is found where the lines joining each vertex to its image all cross.","code":"9.04b"},{"h":"Using similar shapes to find an unknown length","t":"In similar figures every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio, called the scale factor, while every pair of corresponding angles is equal. Once one pair of corresponding lengths is known completely, dividing the two values gives the scale factor, and multiplying or dividing any other length in one shape by that same scale factor gives its corresponding length in the other. Getting the direction of the scale factor right matters: multiply by it to go from the smaller shape to the larger one, and divide by it to go the other way. Matching the vertices in the right order is essential, since a wrongly paired side gives a scale factor that looks plausible but is wrong. Similar shapes can also be compared by writing their areas or their volumes directly as a ratio, simplified in the usual way (e.g. areas of 18 cm² and 32 cm² compare as 18 : 32, which simplifies to 9 : 16). At this tier that comparison is made between two given areas or two given volumes; working out an unknown area or volume from a length scale factor needs squaring or cubing that factor, which is Higher tier only.","code":"9.04c"}],"examples":[{"title":"Reflecting a triangle in a vertical mirror line","setup":"Triangle LMN has vertices L(1, 1), M(4, 1) and N(1, 4). Triangle LMN is reflected in the line x = 3 to give triangle L′M′N′. Work out the coordinates of L′, M′ and N′.","steps":["The mirror line x = 3 is vertical, so reflecting keeps each y-coordinate the same and moves each x-coordinate to the same distance on the opposite side of x = 3.","L is at x = 1, which is 2 units left of x = 3, so its image is 2 units right of x = 3: x = 5. L′ = (5, 1).","M is at x = 4, which is 1 unit right of x = 3, so its image is 1 unit left of x = 3: x = 2. M′ = (2, 1).","N is at x = 1, the same distance from the line as L, so its image also has x = 5 by the same reasoning: N′ = (5, 4)."],"cues":["What does a vertical mirror line do to each point's y-coordinate?","How far is L from the line x = 3, and on which side must its image land?","Is M on the same side of x = 3 as L, or the opposite side?","N has the same x-coordinate as which other vertex, and what does that mean for its image?"]},{"title":"Describing a rotation from a shape and its image","setup":"Triangle A has vertices (2, 1), (2, 4) and (5, 1). Triangle B has vertices (−1, 2), (−4, 2) and (−1, 5). Triangle A maps onto triangle B under a single rotation. Describe this rotation fully.","steps":["Compare a vertex with its image: (2, 1) → (−1, 2). Swapping the two coordinates and changing the sign of one of them is the effect of a quarter turn about the origin.","Test the rule (x, y) → (−y, x) against a second vertex: (2, 4) → (−4, 2), which matches, so the centre of the rotation is the origin (0, 0).","The rule (x, y) → (−y, x) is the effect of a 90° turn in the anticlockwise direction.","So the rotation is 90° anticlockwise about the origin (0, 0)."],"cues":["What single relationship connects (2, 1) to (−1, 2)? Try swapping the coordinates and comparing the signs.","Does that same relationship hold for a second matching pair of vertices, and what does that tell you about the centre?","Which direction of quarter turn takes (x, y) to (−y, x)?","What three things does a full description of a rotation need to state?"]},{"title":"Translating a shape by a column vector","setup":"Quadrilateral PQRS has vertex P at (−3, 2) and vertex R at (0, 1). It is translated by the column vector (5, −4) to give quadrilateral P′Q′R′S′. Work out the coordinates of P′ and of R′.","steps":["A column vector (a, b) means move a across and b up (a negative b means down), applied to every point of the shape.","For P(−3, 2): add 5 to the x-coordinate and −4 to the y-coordinate: (−3 + 5, 2 + (−4)).","P′ = (2, −2).","Apply the same vector to R(0, 1): (0 + 5, 1 + (−4)) = (5, −3), so R′ = (5, −3)."],"cues":["What does each of the two numbers in a column vector tell you to do to a point's coordinates?","What do you get when you add 5 to −3 and −4 to 2?","What are the coordinates of P′?","Does the same vector apply to every vertex of the shape, including R?"]},{"title":"Proving two triangles congruent and using the result","setup":"Triangle ABC has AB = AC. M is the midpoint of BC, and AM is drawn. Prove that triangle ABM is congruent to triangle ACM, naming the criterion, and use this to explain why the base angles of triangle ABC are equal.","steps":["AB = AC is given. M is the midpoint of BC, so BM = MC.","AM is a side of both triangle ABM and triangle ACM, so AM = AM.","Three pairs of sides now match — AB with AC, BM with MC and AM with AM — so triangle ABM is congruent to triangle ACM by SSS.","Matching angles of congruent triangles are equal, and angle ABM matches angle ACM, so angle ABC = angle ACB: the base angles of the isosceles triangle are equal."],"cues":["What does M being the midpoint of BC tell you about BM and MC?","What side do triangle ABM and triangle ACM share?","How many pairs of sides now match, and which congruence criterion needs exactly that?","What does the congruence tell you about angle ABM and angle ACM?"]},{"title":"Representing a vector and combining it with another","setup":"A is the point (1, 2) and B is the point (5, 5). a is the column vector representing the journey from A to B. b is the column vector (−2, 3). Work out the column vector a, and then work out 2a − b.","steps":["The column vector from A to B is found by subtracting coordinates: (5 − 1, 5 − 2).","a = (4, 3).","Scale first: 2a = (8, 6).","Subtract b row by row: (8 − (−2), 6 − 3).","2a − b = (10, 3)."],"cues":["Which coordinates do you subtract from which to get the vector from A to B?","What column vector does that subtraction give?","What does doubling a do to each of its components?","How do you subtract one column vector from another?","What single column vector is the final answer?"]},{"title":"Enlarging a shape from a centre using a fractional scale factor","setup":"Triangle A has vertices (2, 1), (2, 5) and (4, 1). Triangle A is enlarged by scale factor 1/2 with centre (2, 1) to give triangle B. Work out the coordinates of the other two vertices of triangle B.","steps":["The centre (2, 1) is itself a vertex of A, so its image in B stays at (2, 1).","For (2, 5): the step from the centre is 0 across and 4 up. A scale factor of 1/2 makes this step 0 across and 2 up.","Adding that scaled step to the centre gives the image of (2, 5): (2, 1 + 2) = (2, 3).","For (4, 1): the step from the centre is 2 across and 0 up, which scale factor 1/2 makes 1 across and 0 up.","Adding that scaled step to the centre gives the image of (4, 1): (2 + 1, 1) = (3, 1)."],"cues":["What happens to a point that is already at the centre of enlargement?","What is the step from the centre to (2, 5), and what does scale factor 1/2 do to that step?","Adding that scaled step to the centre gives which point?","What is the step from the centre to (4, 1), scaled by 1/2?","What are the final coordinates of that image?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A shape is reflected in the line y = x. What happens to a point's coordinates?","a":"They swap over: (x, y) becomes (y, x)."},{"q":"Name the four congruence criteria for triangles.","a":"SSS, SAS, ASA (including AAS) and RHS."},{"q":"u = (3, −1) and v = (−2, 4). Work out u + v.","a":"(1, 3)."},{"q":"Two triangles are similar with scale factor 3 from the smaller triangle to the larger. One side of the smaller triangle is 5 cm. What is the matching side of the larger triangle?","a":"15 cm."}]},"room":{"facts":["A reflection needs a mirror line, given by an equation such as x = a, y = b, y = x or y = −x; a rotation needs a centre, an angle and a direction; a translation needs a column vector; an enlargement needs a scale factor and a centre.","Reflection, rotation and translation leave every length and angle unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object; enlargement multiplies every length by the scale factor while every angle stays the same, so the image is similar to the object. A scale factor between 0 and 1 makes the image smaller; describe one transformation only.","The four congruence criteria for triangles are SSS, SAS (the angle between the two given sides), ASA (the side between the two given angles, which covers AAS too) and RHS (right angle, hypotenuse, one other side). AAA is not a congruence criterion.","Two triangles are similar when their angles all match (AAA) or their corresponding sides are all in the same ratio; matching sides are then in a fixed ratio called the scale factor, while matching angles stay equal.","In similar figures corresponding lengths are in the same ratio, the scale factor; an unknown length is found by multiplying or dividing a known length by that scale factor.","Column vectors add and subtract component by component: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d), and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d).","A scalar multiplies both components of a vector: k(a, b) = (ka, kb); the arrow becomes |k| times as long, and reverses direction if k is negative.","A point (x, y) translated by the column vector (a, b) lands on (x + a, y + b); the vector mapping an object vertex onto its image vertex is found by subtracting the object's coordinates from the image's coordinates.","Vertices of congruent or similar triangles are named in matching order: triangle ABC ~ triangle PQR means A matches P, B matches Q and C matches R, so corresponding sides are AB with PQ, BC with QR and CA with RP.","OCR's Mathematics J560 assessment has three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes: J560/01–03 for Foundation tier (grades 5–1) and J560/04–06 for Higher tier (grades 9–4); Paper 2 and Paper 5 are non-calculator, the rest allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"The point (2, −5) is translated by the column vector (−3, 4). Write down the coordinates of the image. (non-calculator)","a":"(−1, −1)","sol":"Add the column vector to the point's coordinates: (2 + (−3), −5 + 4). B1 for (−1, −1). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A column vector tells you how far to move across and how far up or down.","Add the top number of the vector to the x-coordinate, and the bottom number to the y-coordinate.","First line: write 2 + (−3) for the new x-coordinate."],"codes":["9.01c"]},{"q":"Triangle A has vertices (2, 2), (6, 2) and (2, 7). Triangle A is reflected in the line y = 5 to give triangle B. Write down the coordinates of the vertex of B that corresponds to (2, 7). (non-calculator)","a":"(2, 3)","sol":"The line y = 5 is horizontal, so the x-coordinate stays the same and the y-coordinate reflects to the same distance on the other side of y = 5: 7 is 2 above y = 5, so the image is 2 below, y = 3. B1 for (2, 3). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A horizontal mirror line leaves the x-coordinate alone and reflects the y-coordinate.","Work out how far the point is above or below the line y = 5, then place the image the same distance on the other side.","First line: write the distance from y = 7 to y = 5, then use it to find the new y-coordinate."],"codes":["9.01a"]},{"q":"Triangle DEF has DE = 6 cm, EF = 8 cm and angle DEF = 90°. Triangle GHI has GH = 6 cm, HI = 8 cm and angle GHI = 90°. State the congruence criterion that shows triangle DEF is congruent to triangle GHI, using the information given. A calculator may be used.","a":"SAS","sol":"The 90° angle lies between the two given sides in each triangle (DE with EF, and GH with HI), so two sides and the included angle are equal in both triangles. B1 for SAS. Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Look at where the given right angle sits compared with the two given sides in each triangle.","Decide whether the equal angle lies between the two equal sides or not — that decides which criterion applies.","First line: name the two sides the 90° angle lies between in triangle DEF."],"codes":["9.02a"]},{"q":"a = (5, −2) and b = (−3, 6). (non-calculator) Work out a + b as a column vector.","a":"(2, 4)","sol":"Add row by row: across 5 + (−3) = 2; up −2 + 6 = 4. B1 for (2, 4). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Column vectors combine one row at a time.","The two top numbers make the top of the answer, and the two bottom numbers make the bottom.","First line: write 5 + (−3) for the top row."],"codes":["9.03a"]},{"q":"Shape P is enlarged with centre (0, 0) to give shape Q. A vertex of P at (3, 4) maps to (9, 12) on Q. Write down the scale factor of the enlargement. A calculator may be used.","a":"3","sol":"Divide the image coordinate by the matching object coordinate: 9 ÷ 3 = 3 (checked with 12 ÷ 4 = 3). B1 for scale factor 3. Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A scale factor tells you how many times bigger the image is than the object.","Compare a coordinate of the image with the matching coordinate of the object, measured from the centre.","First line: write down the image's x-coordinate, 9, which you will divide by the object's matching x-coordinate."],"codes":["9.04b"]},{"q":"P is the point (2, −1) and Q is the point (6, 5). Write the vector PQ (the journey from P to Q) as a column vector. (non-calculator)","a":"(4, 6)","sol":"Subtract P's coordinates from Q's coordinates: (6 − 2, 5 − (−1)). B1 for (4, 6). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The vector from one point to another comes from their coordinates, not from measuring.","Subtract the coordinates of the starting point from the coordinates of the finishing point.","First line: write 6 − 2 for the top row of the vector."],"codes":["9.03b"]},{"q":"Two similar garden ponds have surface areas of 20 m² and 45 m². Write the ratio of the smaller pond's area to the larger pond's area in its simplest form. (non-calculator)","a":"4 : 9","sol":"Write the areas as a ratio: 20 : 45. Divide both parts by their highest common factor, 5: 20 ÷ 5 = 4 and 45 ÷ 5 = 9. M1 for dividing both parts by 5. A1 for the ratio 4 : 9. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A ratio compares two quantities of the same kind, written with a colon.","Simplify the ratio by dividing both numbers by their highest common factor.","First line: write 20 : 45 as the un-simplified ratio of the two areas."],"codes":["9.04c"]},{"q":"In triangles ABC and XYZ, AB = XY = 8 cm, BC = YZ = 5 cm and angle ABC = angle XYZ = 65°. State which congruence criterion shows that the two triangles are congruent, giving a reason, and write down the length of XZ given that AC = 9.3 cm. A calculator may be used.","a":"SAS; XZ = 9.3 cm","sol":"B1 for SAS. B1 for the reason: the equal 65° angles lie between the two pairs of equal sides, AB with XY and BC with YZ. B1 for XZ = 9.3 cm, because A matches X and C matches Z, so AC matches XZ. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Count how many pairs of sides and how many pairs of angles are given as equal.","Check whether the equal angle lies between the two equal sides.","First line: name the criterion, then write down which side of triangle XYZ matches AC."],"codes":["9.02a","9.02b"]},{"q":"Triangle A has vertices (1, 2), (1, 5) and (3, 2). (a) Triangle A is reflected in the line x = 0 to give triangle B. Write down the coordinates of the vertex of B that corresponds to (3, 2). (b) Triangle A is instead rotated 180° about the point (1, 2) to give triangle C. Write down the coordinates of the vertex of C that corresponds to (3, 2). (non-calculator)","a":"(a) (−3, 2); (b) (−1, 2)","sol":"(a) Reflecting in x = 0 changes the sign of the x-coordinate and leaves the y-coordinate unchanged: (3, 2) → (−3, 2). B1 for (−3, 2). (b) A rotation of 180° about (1, 2) sends (x, y) to (2 × 1 − x, 2 × 2 − y) = (2 − x, 4 − y). M1 for applying this rule to (3, 2). A1 for (−1, 2). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["For each part, compare a vertex of triangle A with its image and see what has changed.","A reflection in x = 0 changes one coordinate; a 180° rotation changes both, using the centre as a mid-point.","First line for (a): write the sign change that x = 0 makes to the x-coordinate of (3, 2)."],"codes":["9.01a","9.01b"]},{"q":"Triangle PQR has angles 55°, 65° and 60°. Triangle STU has angle S = 55° and angle T = 60°. Explain why triangle PQR is similar to triangle STU. A calculator may be used.","a":"Angle U = 65°, so triangle STU has angles 55°, 60° and 65°, matching triangle PQR's angles exactly — all three pairs of angles are equal, so the triangles are similar (AAA).","sol":"The angles of triangle STU add up to 180°. M1 for angle U = 180° − 55° − 60°. A1 for stating angle U = 65°, so triangle STU also has angles 55°, 60° and 65°, exactly matching triangle PQR's angles. B1 for concluding that the triangles are similar because all three pairs of angles match (AAA). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two triangles are similar when all three of their angles match, in some order.","Find the missing angle of triangle STU first, using the angle sum of a triangle.","First line: write 180° − 55° − 60°."],"codes":["9.04a"]},{"q":"Triangle LMN is similar to triangle XYZ, with LM corresponding to XY. LM = 6 cm, XY = 15 cm and MN = 10 cm. Work out the length of YZ. (non-calculator)","a":"25 cm","sol":"M1 for the scale factor from triangle LMN to triangle XYZ, XY ÷ LM = 15 ÷ 6 = 2.5. M1 for YZ = MN × 2.5 = 10 × 2.5. A1 for YZ = 25 cm. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Similar figures have all their corresponding lengths in the same ratio.","Use the one pair of sides you know completely to find that ratio, then apply it to the other side.","First line: write 15 ÷ 6 for the scale factor from triangle LMN to triangle XYZ."],"codes":["9.04c"]},{"q":"Triangle A has vertices (0, 0), (6, 0) and (0, 9). It is enlarged with scale factor 1/3 and centre (0, 0) to give triangle B. Write down the coordinates of the three vertices of triangle B. A calculator may be used.","a":"(0, 0), (2, 0) and (0, 3)","sol":"M1 for multiplying each coordinate by the scale factor 1/3, since the centre is the origin. A1 for the vertices (0, 0), (2, 0) and (0, 3). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Every coordinate is measured from the centre of enlargement, which here is the origin.","Multiply each coordinate of every vertex by the scale factor.","First line: write 6 × 1/3 for the x-coordinate of the second vertex's image."],"codes":["9.04b"]},{"q":"Triangle ABC has a right angle at B, with AB = 12 cm and the hypotenuse AC = 13 cm. Triangle PQR has a right angle at Q, with PQ = 12 cm and the hypotenuse PR = 13 cm. (a) Prove that triangle ABC is congruent to triangle PQR, naming the criterion. (b) Work out the length of QR, giving a reason. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) congruent by RHS; (b) QR = 5 cm","sol":"(a) Angle ABC = angle PQR = 90°. AC = PR = 13 cm (the hypotenuse of each triangle). AB = PQ = 12 cm (one other side). B1 for these three facts. B1 for RHS. (b) In triangle ABC, BC² = AC² − AB² = 13² − 12² = 169 − 144 = 25, so BC = 5 cm. M1 for this use of Pythagoras' theorem. A1 for QR = 5 cm, since matching the vertices in order (A↔P, B↔Q, C↔R) makes BC correspond to QR. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["List everything equal between the two right-angled triangles, including which side in each is the hypotenuse.","One of the four congruence criteria applies whenever both triangles contain a right angle, the hypotenuse and one more equal side.","First line for (b): write BC² = 13² − 12² using Pythagoras' theorem in triangle ABC."],"codes":["9.02a","9.02b"]},{"q":"ABCD is a parallelogram, with the vertices named in order round the shape. The diagonal BD is drawn. AB = 7 cm, AD = 5 cm and angle ABD = 38°. (a) Prove that triangle ABD is congruent to triangle CDB, naming the criterion. (b) State the size of angle BDC, giving a reason. (non-calculator)","a":"(a) congruent by SSS; (b) angle BDC = 38°","sol":"Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal, so AB = CD = 7 cm and AD = CB = 5 cm. M1 for both pairs stated equal. BD = DB is common to both triangles. B1 for this. Three pairs of sides now match, so triangle ABD is congruent to triangle CDB by SSS. A1 for SSS named with the sides correctly matched. In the congruent triangles, angle ABD (at B in the first triangle) matches angle CDB (at D in the second triangle), so angle BDC = angle ABD = 38°. B1 for 38° with this reason (or: alternate angles on the parallel sides AB and DC, oe). Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Think about which sides of a parallelogram must be equal, and what the diagonal BD does for both triangles.","Three matching pairs of sides give one particular congruence criterion.","First line: write down AB and its equal side in triangle CDB."],"codes":["9.02a","9.02b"]},{"q":"Triangle A has vertices (1, 1), (3, 1) and (1, 4). Triangle A is enlarged by scale factor 2 to give triangle B with vertices (0, −1), (4, −1) and (0, 5). Work out the coordinates of the centre of enlargement. A calculator may be used.","a":"(2, 3)","sol":"M1 for using a pair of matching vertices, such as (1, 1) and its image (0, −1), and setting up that the image is twice as far from the centre as the object, on the same side of it. M1 for solving this, and checking it against a second pair such as (3, 1) and (4, −1), to find the centre. A1 for centre (2, 3). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The centre of an enlargement lies on the line through every object point and its image.","Use one pair of matching vertices, and remember the image is twice as far from the centre as the object.","First line: write an equation for the x-coordinate of the centre using one matching pair of vertices."],"codes":["9.04b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the column vector for a translation of 5 units left and 2 units up.","a":"(−5, 2)"},{"q":"A shape is enlarged by scale factor 4. Is the image congruent or similar to the object?","a":"Similar — an enlargement changes the size of the shape, so lengths are not equal, only in the same ratio."},{"q":"u = (6, −2) and v = (−1, 5). Work out u − v as a column vector.","a":"(7, −7)"},{"q":"Name the congruence criterion used when two triangles have all three pairs of sides equal.","a":"SSS"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Describing a reflection","back":"State the mirror line (e.g. x = 2, y = −1, y = x or y = −x). Every length and angle stays the same, so the image is congruent to the object.","code":"9.01a","open":false},{"front":"Describing a rotation","back":"State the centre, the angle turned and the direction (clockwise or anticlockwise). Lengths and angles are unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object.","code":"9.01b","open":false},{"front":"Describing a translation","back":"State the column vector (across, up); right and up are positive, left and down are negative. Lengths and angles are unchanged, so the image is congruent to the object.","code":"9.01c","open":false},{"front":"The four congruence criteria for triangles","back":"SSS (3 sides); SAS (2 sides with the angle between them); ASA (2 angles with the side between them, which covers AAS); RHS (right angle, hypotenuse, one other side). AAA is not a congruence criterion.","code":"9.02a","open":false},{"front":"Matching vertices of congruent triangles","back":"Name the vertices in corresponding order: triangle ABC congruent to triangle PQR means A matches P, B matches Q, C matches R, so AB = PQ, BC = QR, CA = RP and angle A = angle P.","code":"9.02a","open":false},{"front":"Using congruent triangles in a proof","back":"Show the right combination of matching sides and angles equal, name the criterion, then use 'matching sides and angles of congruent triangles are equal' to reach the result asked for.","code":"9.02b","open":false},{"front":"Adding and subtracting column vectors","back":"(a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d); (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d) — combine one row at a time.","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Multiplying a vector by a scalar","back":"k(a, b) = (ka, kb). The arrow becomes |k| times as long; a negative k reverses its direction.","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Column vector","back":"A vector written as two numbers: the movement across the page on top, the movement up the page underneath. Right and up are positive, left and down are negative.","code":"9.03b","open":false},{"front":"The vector from an object point to its image point","back":"Subtract the object's coordinates from the image's coordinates: (x of image − x of object, y of image − y of object).","code":"9.03b","open":false},{"front":"Similar triangles","back":"Triangles whose angles all match (AAA) or whose corresponding sides are all in the same ratio. Matching angles are equal; matching sides are in a fixed ratio, the scale factor.","code":"9.04a","open":false},{"front":"Proving two triangles similar","back":"Show that all three angles match, using angle facts (parallel lines, the angle sum of a triangle) or a common angle plus one more equal pair, giving AAA.","code":"9.04a","open":false},{"front":"Describing an enlargement","back":"State the scale factor and the centre of enlargement. A scale factor between 0 and 1 makes the image smaller; the image is similar, not congruent, to the object.","code":"9.04b","open":false},{"front":"Finding an unknown length in similar figures","back":"Find the scale factor from one known pair of corresponding sides, then multiply or divide the other known length by that same scale factor.","code":"9.04c","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: none of this room's results appear in 6.02d, the specification's own list of formulae learners must know (circle facts, Pythagoras' theorem and the Foundation trigonometry ratios only), so none of them is guaranteed by the specification itself. Checked against section 3 of appendix.md, the results OCR's own Foundation tier formulae sheet (742416, for exams from 2026) prints are: area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference of a circle, area of a circle, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine/cosine/tangent ratios, compound interest and the probability addition rule — no transformation, congruence, vector or similarity result is among them, so every result below must be recalled from memory; check your own paper's insert on the day.","Given: Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c² for a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse c — used in the RHS congruence question to find the third side — IS printed on OCR's Foundation tier formulae sheet (742416) and is also in 6.02d, so it is the one result in this room the exam hands you.","Recall: a reflection is described fully by its mirror line, a rotation by its centre, angle and direction, a translation by its column vector, and an enlargement by its scale factor and centre — not printed on the formulae sheet.","Recall: (x, y) translated by the column vector (a, b) lands on (x + a, y + b); the vector mapping an object point onto its image is (x of image − x of object, y of image − y of object) — not printed on the formulae sheet.","Recall: the four congruence criteria for triangles are SSS, SAS, ASA (including AAS) and RHS; AAA is not one of them — not printed on the formulae sheet.","Recall: two triangles are similar when all three angles match (AAA) or all three pairs of corresponding sides are in the same ratio — not printed on the formulae sheet.","Recall: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a + c, b + d) and (a, b) − (c, d) = (a − c, b − d) — vectors are added or subtracted component by component; not printed on the formulae sheet.","Recall: k(a, b) = (ka, kb) — a scalar multiplies both components, making the arrow |k| times as long and reversing its direction when k is negative; not printed on the formulae sheet.","Recall: in similar figures, corresponding lengths are in the same ratio (the scale factor) and corresponding angles are equal; an unknown length is found by multiplying or dividing by that scale factor — not printed on the formulae sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:58:05.963Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit: all fifteen answer keys, all fifteen sol mark tallies, all six worked examples, the four check questions and the four exit-ticket answers re-solve correctly in node, and kit-tools check reports OK. Every fault from the previous verdict is genuinely fixed — room.facts now carries OCR's paper numbers (J560/01–03 and /04–06, 100 marks, 1 h 30 min, Papers 2 and 5 non-calculator, grades 5–1 and 9–4); the isosceles-triangle duplicate is replaced by a fresh RHS-plus-Pythagoras question; questions[1] uses new vertices whose image (2, 3) is not an object vertex; the two over-generous third hints now stop at the first line; 9.04c gains an areas/volumes ratio comparison with an explicit, correct statement that squaring or cubing the length scale factor is Higher tier only, plus a matching 4 : 9 pond question. Tier remains clean and nine questions are non-calculator with hand-workable numbers; only two minor presentational points remain."}}},"congruence-similarity-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"congruence-similarity-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room carries the four pieces of shape and space that only a Higher tier candidate meets: combining reflections, rotations and translations into one described transformation; using vectors as a tool of proof rather than only of description; enlarging by a negative or negative-fractional scale factor; and comparing the lengths, areas and volumes of similar shapes. All four are marked on the chain of reasoning rather than on a bare final value — AO2 is 30% of the Higher tier, and a 'describe fully' or 'prove' question with an incomplete description, or a result quoted with no working, can score nothing even when the right idea is there. The room also draws together earlier work: transformations return to the Foundation congruence and reflection rules, vector proofs use the ratio and midpoint results from vector arithmetic, and similar-shape scale factors connect to direct proportion, so several bank questions are deliberately synoptic.","idea":[{"h":"Combining transformations, and what stays the same","code":"9.01d","t":"A shape can be moved through a sequence of reflections, rotations and translations, one after another, and OCR expects you to describe the single transformation equivalent to the whole sequence, not just to perform each step. The safe method is to follow one general point (x, y), or a single named vertex, through every move in turn and compare where it finishes with where it started; the pattern that emerges names the combined transformation. Some results are worth knowing on sight: two reflections in parallel mirror lines a distance d apart combine to a translation of 2d perpendicular to them; two reflections in mirror lines that cross combine to a rotation, about the crossing point, through twice the angle between the lines; two rotations about the same centre simply add their angles. Every reflection, rotation and translation is an isometry — it leaves every length and angle exactly unchanged — so however many of them are combined, the final image is congruent to the original shape, and any point that maps to itself, such as a rotation's centre or a point on a mirror line, is called invariant. A full description of the combined transformation still needs every parameter the single transformation requires: a mirror line, a centre with an angle and a direction, or a column vector."},{"h":"Using vectors to prove a geometric result","code":"9.03a","t":"Column vectors do more at Higher tier than describe a translation: written in terms of two vectors a and b given in a diagram, they become the tool for proving results that would otherwise need a full geometric argument. Every route is built the same way — travel only along labelled or ratio-given vectors, adding them head to tail and reversing the sign of any travelled backwards, so AB = AO + OB = b − a when OA = a and OB = b. A point P on AB with AP:PB = m:n has AP = (m/(m+n))AB, and the midpoint M of AB has OM = 1/2(a + b). Once an unknown vector is written and simplified, factorising it proves something: if one vector equals a scalar multiple of another, the two are parallel, and if in addition they share a common point, the points at their ends are collinear — the scalar multiple alone is never enough on its own for a straight-line proof. Every 'prove' or 'show that' question needs the full route written out and the conclusion stated in words, since the printed result on a 'show that' question earns nothing quoted back."},{"h":"Enlargement by a negative scale factor","code":"9.04b","t":"At Higher tier the scale factor of an enlargement may be negative, including a negative fraction such as −1/2. To enlarge a point P by scale factor k about a centre C, find the vector from C to P, multiply that vector by k, then add the result back onto C: the image of P is C + k × (vector CP). When k is negative the vector reverses direction, so the image lands on the opposite side of the centre from the object and the whole shape is turned through 180° as well as resized — object and image stay similar, with every length multiplied by the size of k (that is, |k|) and every area multiplied by k², which is always positive whatever the sign of k. A full description of an enlargement always needs three things stated together: the word enlargement, the scale factor with its sign, and the centre given as coordinates. To find the centre of an enlargement from a diagram, join each vertex to its image and extend the lines; with a negative scale factor the centre lies between the object and its image, on each of those joining lines."},{"h":"Length, area and volume scale factors of similar shapes","code":"9.04c","t":"When two shapes or solids are similar, every pair of corresponding lengths is in the same ratio, called the length scale factor. Areas do not scale the same way as lengths: multiplying every length by a scale factor k multiplies every area by k², because an area is built from two lengths multiplied together. For solids the same idea extends one dimension further: multiplying every length by k multiplies every volume by k³, because a volume is built from three lengths multiplied together. Read the direction of the scale factor from the question — from the smaller shape to the larger one it is greater than 1, and from the larger to the smaller it is less than 1 — and always take the correct root to go from an area or a volume ratio back to a length ratio: a square root for area, a cube root for volume. This links similarity directly to the direct proportion work of an earlier room, since mass, cost and other quantities that scale with volume for objects made of one material follow the same cube relationship."}],"examples":[{"title":"Two isometries that make one","setup":"Shape S has a vertex at the point (2, 5). S is reflected in the x-axis to give shape S′, and S′ is then rotated 90° clockwise about the origin to give shape S″. Describe fully the single transformation that maps S directly onto S″, and write down the image of (2, 5) on S″.","steps":["Reflecting in the x-axis sends (x, y) to (x, −y), so (2, 5) → (2, −5).","Rotating 90° clockwise about the origin sends (x, y) to (y, −x), so (2, −5) → (−5, −2).","Following a general point through both moves: (x, y) → (x, −y) → (−y, −x).","The mapping (x, y) → (−y, −x) is the effect of a reflection in the line y = −x, so S maps to S″ by a reflection in y = −x, and the image of (2, 5) is (−5, −2)."],"cues":["What does reflecting in the x-axis do to the coordinates of a point?","Where does a quarter turn clockwise about the origin send the point (2, −5)?","What happens to a general point (x, y) after both moves?","Which single transformation sends (x, y) to (−y, −x)?"]},{"title":"Proving a midpoint result with vectors","setup":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. M is the midpoint of AB. Prove that OM = 1/2(a + b).","steps":["AB = AO + OB = −a + b = b − a.","Since M is the midpoint of AB, AM = 1/2 AB = 1/2(b − a).","Route from O to M through A: OM = OA + AM = a + 1/2(b − a).","Multiply out and collect: OM = a + 1/2 b − 1/2 a = 1/2 a + 1/2 b = 1/2(a + b)."],"cues":["Which two labelled vectors make a route from A to B, and which of them do you travel backwards along?","What fraction of AB is AM, since M is the midpoint?","Which labelled vector takes you from O to A, and what carries you on to M?","What do you get when you collect the a terms and the b terms together?"]},{"title":"Proving three points lie on a straight line","setup":"Relative to the origin O, the point C has position vector OC = 4p + 6q and the point D has position vector OD = 6p + 9q, where p and q are not parallel. Show that O, C and D lie on a straight line, and find the ratio OC:OD.","steps":["Take the common factor out of OD: 6p + 9q = 3/2 × (4p + 6q).","So OD = 3/2 OC, meaning OD is a scalar multiple of OC.","OC and OD are parallel, and they both pass through the point O, so O, C and D lie on a straight line.","Since OD = 3/2 OC, the ratio OC:OD is 2:3."],"cues":["What common factor links the components of OD to the components of OC?","What does that scalar multiple tell you about the directions of OC and OD?","Parallel vectors alone are not enough for a straight line — what else do OC and OD share?","If OD is three-halves of OC, what is OC as a fraction of OD?"]},{"title":"Enlarging by a negative scale factor about a point","setup":"Triangle XYZ has vertices X(2, 1), Y(4, 1) and Z(2, 3). It is enlarged by scale factor −2 about the centre (1, 1) to give triangle X′Y′Z′. Work out the coordinates of X′, Y′ and Z′, and state what has happened to the triangle's orientation and its area.","steps":["From the centre (1, 1) to X(2, 1) the vector is (1, 0); multiplying by −2 gives (−2, 0), so X′ = (1 − 2, 1 + 0) = (−1, 1).","From the centre to Y(4, 1) the vector is (3, 0); multiplying by −2 gives (−6, 0), so Y′ = (1 − 6, 1 + 0) = (−5, 1).","From the centre to Z(2, 3) the vector is (1, 2); multiplying by −2 gives (−2, −4), so Z′ = (1 − 2, 1 − 4) = (−1, −3).","Because the scale factor is negative, the image is turned through 180° from the object, and because its size is 2, every length is doubled and the area is multiplied by (−2)² = 4."],"cues":["What is the journey from the centre (1, 1) to X, and what does multiplying it by −2 do to its direction and length?","What is the journey from the centre to Y, scaled by −2?","What is the journey from the centre to Z, scaled by −2?","What does the sign of the scale factor do to the orientation of the image, and what does its size do to lengths and area?"]},{"title":"Comparing the surface areas and volumes of similar cuboids","setup":"Two similar cuboids, cuboid P and cuboid Q, have corresponding lengths in the ratio 2:5. Find the ratio of their surface areas, and the ratio of their volumes.","steps":["Squaring each part of the given length ratio 2:5 gives the area scale factor: 2² : 5² = 4:25.","Cubing each part of the same length ratio gives the volume scale factor: 2³ : 5³ = 8:125.","So the surface areas of P and Q are in the ratio 4:25, and their volumes are in the ratio 8:125."],"cues":["What do you get when you square each part of the length ratio 2:5?","What do you get when you cube each part of the same length ratio?","What are the two final ratios, written in the same order as the lengths, 2:5?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A shape is enlarged by scale factor −3. What happens to its orientation, and what is the area scale factor?","a":"It is turned through 180° (the image lies on the opposite side of the centre from the object); the area scale factor is (−3)² = 9."},{"q":"Two rotations of 60° are performed about the same centre, one after the other. What single rotation is equivalent to the two?","a":"A rotation of 120° about that same centre — the angles simply add."},{"q":"OA = a and OB = b. Write the vector BA in terms of a and b.","a":"BA = a − b."},{"q":"A model and the real object it represents are similar with length scale factor 10. What is the volume scale factor?","a":"10³ = 1000."}]},"room":{"facts":["Under an enlargement of scale factor k about a centre C, the image of a point P is C + k × (vector CP); a negative k puts the image on the opposite side of C from the object, turned through 180°, with lengths multiplied by |k| and areas by k².","Two reflections in parallel mirror lines a distance d apart combine to a translation of 2d perpendicular to the mirrors; two reflections in mirror lines that cross combine to a rotation, about the crossing point, through twice the angle between them; two rotations about the same centre simply add their angles.","Reflections, rotations and translations, and every combination of them, are isometries: they leave every length and angle unchanged, so the final image is always congruent to the original shape; an invariant point is one that maps to itself.","A vector between two points is the finish minus the start: with OA = a and OB = b, AB = OB − OA = b − a; vectors add head to tail, AB + BC = AC, and reversing a vector reverses its sign, BA = −AB.","If a point P divides AB so that AP:PB = m:n, then AP = (m/(m+n))AB and OP = a + (m/(m+n))(b − a); the midpoint M of AB has OM = 1/2(a + b).","Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other; three points are collinear only when that scalar-multiple test holds AND the two vectors share a common point.","For similar shapes with length scale factor k, the area scale factor is k² and the volume scale factor is k³; take a square root to recover a length scale factor from an area ratio, and a cube root from a volume ratio.","OCR's Higher tier of Mathematics J560 is assessed by three papers, J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 5 (J560/05) is the non-calculator paper; Higher tier grades run from 9 down to 4."],"questions":[{"q":"A point A has coordinates (3, 1). A is enlarged by scale factor −1 about the origin (0, 0) to give the point A′. Write down the coordinates of A′. (non-calculator)","a":"(−3, −1)","sol":"The image of a point P under an enlargement of scale factor k about centre C is C + k × (vector CP); here C is the origin, so the image is simply k times the point's coordinates. B1 for (−3, −1). Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["A negative scale factor does more than change size — think about what it does to direction as well.","With the origin as centre, the image's coordinates come from multiplying the point's coordinates by the scale factor.","First line: multiply each of A's coordinates by the scale factor, one at a time."],"codes":["9.04b"]},{"q":"Shape S is reflected in the line y = 0, and the image is then reflected in the line y = 3. Describe fully the single transformation equivalent to the two reflections, giving your answer as a column vector. (non-calculator)","a":"A translation by the column vector (0, 6)","sol":"The two mirror lines are parallel and 3 units apart, so the combined transformation is a translation perpendicular to them, of twice that distance, in the direction from the first mirror to the second. B1 for translation. B1 for the column vector (0, 6). Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two mirror lines never cross, so the combined transformation is not a rotation.","A pair of reflections in parallel lines always gives the same type of single transformation — the distance apart tells you how far it moves.","First line: write down how far apart the two mirror lines are, then double it."],"codes":["9.01d"]},{"q":"AB = 6p − 9q and CD = 2p − 3q, where p and q are not parallel. (non-calculator) Explain why AB is parallel to CD.","a":"AB = 3CD, so AB is a scalar multiple of CD","sol":"Take the common factor out of AB: 6p − 9q = 3(2p − 3q) = 3CD. B1 for AB = 3CD (or CD = 1/3 AB) together with the statement that this makes the two vectors parallel. Total 1 mark.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Compare the two expressions term by term to see what links them.","Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a number times the other.","First line: take the common factor out of AB so that what is left in the bracket matches CD."],"codes":["9.03a"]},{"q":"Two similar triangles have corresponding sides in the ratio 3:4. State the ratio of their areas. A calculator may be used.","a":"9:16","sol":"The area scale factor is the square of the length scale factor. M1 for 3² and 4² (or (4/3)²). A1 for the ratio 9:16. Total 2 marks.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Area does not scale the same way that length does.","Square each part of the length ratio to get the area ratio.","First line: write 3² and 4²."],"codes":["9.04c"]},{"q":"Point B has coordinates (6, 2). B is enlarged by scale factor −3 about a centre C to give the image point B′(−6, 10). Work out the coordinates of C. A calculator may be used.","a":"(3, 4)","sol":"With a negative scale factor, C lies between B and B′ on the line joining them, dividing it in the ratio 1 : |k| = 1:3, so C = B + 1/4(B′ − B). M1 for identifying this ratio. C = (6, 2) + 1/4(−12, 8) = (6, 2) + (−3, 2). M1 for correct method. A1 for C = (3, 4). Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The centre of enlargement lies on the straight line joining an object point to its image.","With scale factor −3, the centre divides that line in the ratio 1:3, closer to the object point B.","First line: write the vector from B to B′, then take a quarter of it."],"codes":["9.04b"]},{"q":"Triangle T has vertices (0, 0), (4, 0) and (0, 6). It is enlarged by scale factor −1/2 about the origin (0, 0) to give triangle T′. Work out the area of triangle T′. (non-calculator)","a":"3 square units","sol":"The area of T is 1/2 × 4 × 6 = 12 square units. M1 for this. The area scale factor is the square of the scale factor: (−1/2)² = 1/4. M1 for this. Area of T′ = 12 × 1/4 = 3 square units. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["You don't need to find the image triangle's coordinates to answer this.","Work out the area of T first, then decide what a scale factor of −1/2 does to area rather than to length.","First line: write 1/2 × 4 × 6 for the area of T."],"codes":["9.04b"]},{"q":"Shape S is rotated 90° anticlockwise about the point (2, 1), and the image is then rotated 90° anticlockwise about the same point (2, 1) again. Describe fully the single transformation equivalent to the two rotations, and write down the coordinates of the only invariant point. (non-calculator)","a":"A rotation of 180° about (2, 1); the invariant point is (2, 1)","sol":"Both rotations are about the same centre, so the two angles add. M1 for 90° + 90° = 180°. A1 for a rotation of 180° about (2, 1) — no direction need be given for a half turn. B1 for the invariant point (2, 1), the centre of rotation, which maps to itself. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Both turns happen about the same fixed point, so that point never moves.","Two rotations about one centre can always be replaced by a single rotation about that same centre.","First line: write 90° + 90° for the total turn."],"codes":["9.01d"]},{"q":"OPQR is a parallelogram, with the vertices named in order round the shape, OP = p and OR = r. M is the midpoint of PQ, and N is the midpoint of RQ. A calculator may be used. Prove that MN is parallel to PR, and find the ratio MN:PR.","a":"MN = 1/2 PR, so MN is parallel to PR and MN:PR = 1:2","sol":"Opposite sides of a parallelogram give equal vectors, so PQ = OR = r and RQ = OP = p. Routing from O through P, OM = OP + PM = p + 1/2 r. M1. Routing from O through R, ON = OR + RN = r + 1/2 p. M1. MN = ON − OM = (r + 1/2 p) − (p + 1/2 r) = 1/2 r − 1/2 p = 1/2(r − p). M1. Since PR = OR − OP = r − p, MN = 1/2 PR: MN is a scalar multiple of PR, so MN is parallel to PR, and MN:PR = 1:2. A1. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Write OM and ON separately, each as a route through O and a side and then half of the next side.","Once you have both, MN = ON − OM; compare what you get with PR = OR − OP.","First line: write OM = OP + 1/2 PQ, using what PQ equals in the parallelogram."],"codes":["9.03a"]},{"q":"Relative to the origin O, the point G has position vector OG = 3p + 5q and the point H has position vector OH = 9p + kq, where p and q are not parallel and k is a constant. (non-calculator) Given that O, G and H lie on a straight line, work out the value of k.","a":"k = 15","sol":"For O, G and H to lie on a straight line through O, OH must be a scalar multiple of OG: OH = λOG for some number λ. M1. Comparing the p-coefficients, which contain no unknown: 9 = 3λ, so λ = 3. M1. Comparing the q-coefficients: k = 5λ = 5 × 3 = 15. A1. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["For O, G and H to lie on a straight line through the origin, OH must be a scalar multiple of OG.","Compare the p-coefficients of OG and OH first, since neither one contains k, to find that scalar multiple.","First line: write OH = λOG, and compare the p-terms: 9 = 3λ."],"codes":["9.03a"]},{"q":"Two similar cylinders have radii in the ratio 3:7. The smaller cylinder has a curved surface area of 54 cm². Work out the curved surface area of the larger cylinder. A calculator may be used.","a":"294 cm²","sol":"The area scale factor is the square of the length scale factor: (7/3)² = 49/9. M1. Larger curved surface area = 54 × 49/9. M1. A1 for 294 cm². Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Areas do not scale by the same ratio as the radii.","Square the length ratio to get the area scale factor, then apply it to the given area.","First line: write (7/3)² for the area scale factor."],"codes":["9.04c"]},{"q":"Shape S is reflected in the line x = 2, and the image is then translated by the column vector (6, 0). Point P(1, 3) is a vertex of S. Work out the coordinates of the image of P after both transformations, and describe fully the single transformation equivalent to the sequence. (non-calculator)","a":"(9, 3); the single transformation is a reflection in the line x = 5","sol":"Reflecting in x = 2 sends (x, y) to (4 − x, y), so P(1, 3) → (3, 3). M1. Translating by (6, 0) adds 6 to the x-coordinate: (3, 3) → (9, 3). M1. Following a general point, (x, y) → (4 − x, y) → (10 − x, y), which is the effect of a reflection in the line x = 5. A1 for naming this transformation. Total 3 marks.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Deal with the two transformations one at a time, in the order given.","Once you have the image of P, follow a general point (x, y) through both moves to spot the pattern.","First line: write P → (3, 3) for the reflection in x = 2."],"codes":["9.01d"]},{"q":"Box A and box B are similar cuboidal boxes made of the same card. Box A has surface area 150 cm² and volume 125 cm³. Box B has surface area 600 cm². Work out the volume of box B. A calculator may be used.","a":"1000 cm³","sol":"The area scale factor from A to B is 600 ÷ 150 = 4. M1. The length scale factor is the square root of the area scale factor: √4 = 2. M1. The volume scale factor is the cube of the length scale factor: 2³ = 8. M1. Volume of box B = 125 × 8 = 1000 cm³. A1. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["You are given an area and asked for a volume, so you need to pass through the length scale factor to get there.","Find the area scale factor first, then take the correct root of it before cubing.","First line: write 600 ÷ 150 for the area scale factor."],"codes":["9.04c"]},{"q":"In triangle OAB, OA = a and OB = b. M lies on OA with OM:MA = 1:2, and N lies on OB with ON:NB = 1:2. (non-calculator) Prove that MN is parallel to AB, and find the ratio of the area of triangle OMN to the area of triangle OAB.","a":"MN = 1/3(b − a) = 1/3 AB, so MN is parallel to AB; the area ratio is 1:9","sol":"The ratio cuts OA into three equal parts with M one part along, so OM = 1/3 a; similarly ON = 1/3 b. M1 for both. Routing from M through O to N, MN = MO + ON = −1/3 a + 1/3 b. M1. So MN = 1/3(b − a) = 1/3 AB, since AB = b − a; MN is a scalar multiple of AB, so MN is parallel to AB. A1. Triangle OMN and triangle OAB share the angle at O with sides in the ratio 1:3, so they are similar with length scale factor 3, giving an area ratio of 1²:3² = 1:9. B1. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Write OM and ON as fractions of a and b before you try to build the vector MN.","Once you have MN as a multiple of (b − a), compare it with the vector AB itself; the length ratio then tells you about area.","First line: write OM = 1/3 a and ON = 1/3 b."],"codes":["9.03a","9.04c"]},{"q":"Triangle PQR is reflected in the line y = 1 to give triangle P′Q′R′, and triangle P′Q′R′ is then rotated 180° about the point (3, 1) to give triangle P″Q″R″. Explain why triangle P″Q″R″ must be congruent to triangle PQR, and describe fully the single transformation that maps PQR directly onto P″Q″R″. (non-calculator)","a":"Congruent because a reflection and a rotation each preserve every length and angle; the single transformation is a reflection in the line x = 3","sol":"A reflection and a rotation are both isometries: each leaves every length and angle in the shape unchanged, so triangle P′Q′R′ is congruent to PQR, and triangle P″Q″R″ is congruent to P′Q′R′, hence P″Q″R″ is congruent to PQR. B1 for this reasoning. Following a general point (x, y): reflecting in y = 1 gives (x, 2 − y). M1. Rotating that 180° about (3, 1) gives (6 − x, 2 − (2 − y)) = (6 − x, y). M1. This is the effect of a reflection in the line x = 3. A1. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Before finding the single transformation, think about what a reflection and a rotation each do, or do not do, to lengths and angles.","Track a general point (x, y) through the reflection first, then through the rotation, and compare the result with where it started.","First line: write (x, y) → (x, 2 − y) for the effect of reflecting in y = 1."],"codes":["9.01d"]},{"q":"Two similar model statues are cast from the same bronze, so mass is proportional to volume. The smaller statue is 15 cm tall and has a mass of 2 kg. The larger, similar statue is 45 cm tall. Work out the mass of the larger statue. A calculator may be used.","a":"54 kg","sol":"The length scale factor is 45 ÷ 15 = 3. M1. Because mass is proportional to volume, the mass scale factor is the cube of the length scale factor: 3³ = 27. M1. Mass of the larger statue = 2 × 27. M1. A1 for 54 kg. Total 4 marks.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Mass behaves like volume here, not like length.","Find the length scale factor first, then decide what power of it you need for something that scales like volume.","First line: write 45 ÷ 15 for the length scale factor."],"codes":["9.04c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A point (4, 3) is enlarged by scale factor −2 about the origin. Write down the coordinates of its image.","a":"(−8, −6)"},{"q":"Two reflections are made in mirror lines that cross at 40°. What single transformation is equivalent to the two?","a":"A rotation of 80° about the point where the mirror lines cross."},{"q":"OP = p and OQ = q. M is the midpoint of PQ. Write OM in terms of p and q.","a":"OM = 1/2(p + q)"},{"q":"Two similar shapes have a volume scale factor of 27. What is their length scale factor?","a":"3 (the cube root of 27)"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Enlargement by a negative scale factor","back":"The image lies on the opposite side of the centre of enlargement, turned through 180°, with every length multiplied by the size of the scale factor. Object and image stay similar.","code":"9.04b","open":false},{"front":"Describing an enlargement fully","back":"State the word enlargement, the scale factor including its sign, and the centre of enlargement as coordinates. All three are needed, for one transformation only.","code":"9.04b","open":false},{"front":"Length and area scale factor for an enlargement","back":"Under an enlargement of scale factor k, lengths are multiplied by |k| and areas by k². A negative k still gives a positive area scale factor.","code":"9.04b","open":false},{"front":"Two reflections in parallel mirror lines","back":"The pair is equivalent to a translation perpendicular to the mirrors, of twice the distance between them, in the direction from the first mirror to the second.","code":"9.01d","open":false},{"front":"Two reflections in mirror lines that cross","back":"The pair is equivalent to a rotation about the point where the two mirror lines cross, through twice the angle between them.","code":"9.01d","open":false},{"front":"Two rotations about the same centre","back":"The pair is equivalent to a single rotation about that same centre, through the sum of the two angles.","code":"9.01d","open":false},{"front":"Invariance under combined transformations","back":"Reflections, rotations and translations preserve all lengths and angles, so any combination of them gives an image congruent to the original. An invariant point maps to itself.","code":"9.01d","open":false},{"front":"The vector AB from position vectors","back":"AB = OB − OA: the position vector of the finish minus the position vector of the start. With OA = a and OB = b, this gives AB = b − a.","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Triangle law for adding vectors","back":"Vectors add head to tail: AB + BC = AC. A chain of labelled steps from one point to another adds up to the vector joining them.","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Position vector of the midpoint of AB","back":"OM = 1/2(a + b), reached by OM = a + 1/2(b − a).","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"The point dividing AB in the ratio m:n","back":"The ratio cuts AB into m + n equal parts, so AP = (m/(m+n))AB and OP = a + (m/(m+n))(b − a).","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Test for parallel vectors","back":"Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the other, q = kp. Factorising an expression reveals the scalar k.","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Test for collinear points","back":"Two vectors joining the points must be scalar multiples of each other AND share a common point. Both parts are needed for the marks.","code":"9.03a","open":false},{"front":"Length, area and volume scale factors of similar shapes","back":"For a length scale factor k, the area scale factor is k² and the volume scale factor is k³. Take a square or cube root to work backwards.","code":"9.04c","open":false},{"front":"Give an example of a pair of similar 3-D solids","back":"two cubes of different size; two spheres of different radii; two cones with the same shape but different sizes","code":"9.04c","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Preamble: this room's four ideas (9.01d, 9.03a, 9.04b, 9.04c) are not in 6.02d, the specification's own list of formulae to know (which covers circle facts and, from Foundation tier up, Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios, plus, at Higher tier, also the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule and area = 1/2 ab sin C), and none of them appears on OCR's Foundation or Higher tier formulae sheet either (checked against appendix.md section 3) — every result below must be recalled or derived; check your own paper's insert on the day.","Recall: image of P under an enlargement of scale factor k about centre C = C + k × (vector CP); a negative k turns the image through 180° from the object — not printed on either sheet.","Recall: enlargement scale factors — lengths × |k|, areas × k² — not printed on either sheet.","Recall: two reflections in parallel mirror lines a distance d apart = a translation of 2d perpendicular to them; two reflections in crossing mirror lines = a rotation of twice the angle between them, about the crossing point; two rotations about one centre add their angles — none of this is printed on either sheet.","Recall: reflections, rotations, translations and every combination of them preserve all lengths and angles, so the image stays congruent to the object — not printed on either sheet.","Recall: AB = OB − OA = b − a, and vectors add head to tail, AB + BC = AC — not printed on either sheet.","Recall: the midpoint M of AB has OM = 1/2(a + b); a point dividing AB in the ratio m:n has OP = a + (m/(m+n))(b − a) — not printed on either sheet.","Recall: two vectors are parallel when one is a scalar multiple of the other; three points are collinear only when that test holds and the vectors share a common point — not printed on either sheet.","Recall: for similar shapes with length scale factor k, the area scale factor is k² and the volume scale factor is k³ — not printed on either sheet; use a square or cube root to work back to a length scale factor."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:07:09.813831Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.9,"notes":"All nine listed fixes are present and correct: the four replacement questions re-solve to C = (3, 4), MN = 1/2 PR (ratio 1:2), k = 15 and (9, 3) with a reflection in x = 5; example 5's first step now squares the ratio instead of restating the setup; the facts carry OCR's Higher tier numbers and the formulae preamble now matches appendix.md section 2. Nothing left is an error - only the twinned 'prove MN is parallel' pair at questions 7 and 12 is worth a cosmetic pass before publishing."}}},"mensuration":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"mensuration","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"OCR 10 Mensuration threads through every paper: length, area, volume, units and right-angled triangles turn up inside questions labelled algebra, ratio or probability just as often as inside a mensuration question of their own. OCR's Foundation formulae sheet prints the trapezium, the prism, the circle, Pythagoras' theorem and the three trigonometric ratios, so most of the marks in this room are lost not on recall but on choosing the correct formula, keeping every length in the same unit before calculating, and writing the correct unit — cm, cm² or cm³ — on the final answer. J560/02 is non-calculator, so the exact trigonometric values and answers left in terms of π must be second nature, while J560/01 and /03 expect a rounded decimal read correctly off the display.","idea":[{"h":"Standard units, and converting between them","t":"Length is measured in mm, cm, m and km; area in mm², cm², m² and km²; volume in mm³, cm³ and m³; capacity in ml and litres; mass in g, kg and tonnes; time in seconds, minutes, hours, days and years; money in £ and pence. Every measurement in a calculation must be in the same unit before you start: 1 cm = 10 mm, 1 m = 100 cm, 1 km = 1000 m, 1 kg = 1000 g, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 litre = 1000 ml = 1000 cm³. Foundation tier also expects you to convert standard units inside an algebraic expression, such as rewriting a length given as x metres as 100x centimetres. The unit belongs on the answer: writing an area as 40 rather than 40 cm² loses the accuracy mark.","code":"10.01a"},{"h":"Compound units: speed, density and pressure","t":"A compound unit combines two measurements by division: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume, and pressure = force ÷ area, together with everyday rates such as pay per hour or price per kilogram. The unit itself names the operation, so a speed in m/s must have the distance in metres and the time in seconds before dividing, and converting one part (for example km/h to m/s) means converting distance and time separately. Foundation tier also expects these formulae to be rearranged, and to be used inside an algebraic expression, such as writing a distance as st when speed s and time t are unknowns.","code":"10.01b"},{"h":"Map scales, bearings and scale drawings","t":"A scale of 1 : n means that 1 unit on the map or drawing stands for n of the same units in real life, so a map distance is multiplied by n and then converted into sensible units, using 100 000 cm = 1 km. A scale drawing is constructed and interpreted the same way, using a ruler and protractor to measure lengths to the nearest millimetre and angles to the nearest degree. A bearing is a direction measured clockwise from north, always written with three figures, so a direction of 70° is written 070°. Since north lines are parallel, the bearing of A from B and the bearing of B from A always differ by exactly 180°.","code":"10.01c"},{"h":"Perimeter of a rectilinear shape","t":"The perimeter of any rectilinear (straight-sided) shape is the total distance around its outside edge, found by adding every side length. In an L-shape or similar composite outline, some sides are often missing from the diagram and must be found first, using the fact that opposite sides of the overall rectangle add up to the same total, before every length is added together. Units matter here too: every length added into a perimeter must already be in the same unit.","code":"10.02a"},{"h":"Circumference of a circle and the arc length of a sector","t":"For a circle of radius r and diameter d, circumference = 2πr = πd; this is printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet. A sector is the slice of a circle between two radii, and the arc length of a sector is the fraction θ/360 of the whole circumference, where θ is the angle at the centre: arc length = θ/360 × 2πr. This formula for arc length is not printed on the sheet, so it must be recalled, and the fraction θ/360 is worth writing down as its own line of working.","code":"10.02b"},{"h":"Perimeter of composite shapes","t":"A composite shape's perimeter is built by identifying which formula each part of its outline needs — a straight edge, a whole circumference, or an arc — and adding only the edges that form the outside boundary. An edge that has become internal, where two shapes are joined, is never included. When a semicircular bite is removed from a straight edge, that straight length is replaced in the total by the length of the arc, not added to it.","code":"10.02c"},{"h":"Area of a triangle","t":"Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height. This formula is not printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet, so it must be recalled. The height used must always be the perpendicular height, measured at right angles to the base, never a sloping side — using the slant length instead of the perpendicular height is the most common error in this topic.","code":"10.03a"},{"h":"Area of a parallelogram","t":"Area of a parallelogram = base × perpendicular height; a rectangle is a special case of a parallelogram, so this same formula gives area of a rectangle = length × width. Neither version is printed on the formulae sheet. As with a triangle, the height must be the perpendicular distance between the two parallel sides, not a sloping edge.","code":"10.03b"},{"h":"Area of a trapezium","t":"Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the lengths of the two parallel sides and h is the perpendicular distance between them; this formula is printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet, so the marks are for substituting correctly rather than for recall. Adding the parallel sides and halving the total before multiplying by h keeps the working in a clear order that a mark scheme can follow.","code":"10.03c"},{"h":"Area of a circle and the area of a sector","t":"Area of a circle = πr², printed on the formulae sheet — halve the diameter first if that is what the question gives. The area of a sector is the fraction θ/360 of the whole circle's area: area of a sector = θ/360 × πr². This formula is not printed on the sheet and must be recalled; as with arc length, write the fraction θ/360 down as a separate line before multiplying.","code":"10.03d"},{"h":"Area of composite shapes","t":"A composite shape is split into simpler shapes — such as a rectangle and a semicircle, or a large shape with a smaller one removed from it — whose areas are each found separately and then added or subtracted. Every part must be identified before any calculation starts, and any length shared between two parts (such as a diameter that becomes the width of a rectangle) only needs to be read off once.","code":"10.03e"},{"h":"Volume and surface area of a cuboid, prism and cylinder","t":"Volume of a prism = area of cross section × length, printed on the formulae sheet; a cuboid is a prism with a rectangular cross section, giving volume = length × width × height, and a cylinder is a prism with a circular cross section, giving volume = πr²h. Surface area is the total area of every face: for a cuboid, add the areas of the three pairs of matching rectangular faces, 2(lw + lh + wh); for a cylinder, add the two circular ends, 2πr², to the curved surface, 2πrh. Neither surface-area formula is printed on the sheet or given in the exam — both must be built from the areas of the individual faces.","code":"10.04a"},{"h":"Volume and surface area of a cone, sphere and composite solids","t":"The volume and surface area formulae for a cone and a sphere are not printed on the formulae sheet, but the specification states they will always be given inside the exam question when they are needed, so they do not need to be memorised. A composite solid, such as a cone joined to a hemisphere, is handled by finding the volume (or surface area) of each part separately using the given formulae and then adding them; a hemisphere's volume is half of a full sphere's.","code":"10.04b"},{"h":"Volume and surface area of a pyramid","t":"The volume of a pyramid is a third of the area of its base times its perpendicular height; the specification states this formula will be given inside the exam question, so it does not need to be memorised, but the base area itself must be found using whichever area formula the base shape needs. The surface area of a pyramid is not a separate formula: it is the sum of the base area and the areas of the sloping triangular faces, each found using area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height.","code":"10.04c"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem","t":"In a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse c, a² + b² = c². This is printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet. Add the squares of the two shorter sides to find the hypotenuse; subtract the smaller square from the larger to find a shorter side, then take a square root either way. Label the hypotenuse first — it is always the side directly opposite the right angle.","code":"10.05a"},{"h":"Trigonometric ratios in right-angled triangles","t":"sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent, remembered as SOH CAH TOA; all three are printed on the formulae sheet. Label the hypotenuse first, then the side opposite the angle being used — whatever is left is the adjacent side. Use a trigonometric ratio whenever an angle is involved; use Pythagoras' theorem instead when only three sides are involved and no angle appears. To find an angle from two known sides, use the inverse function: sin⁻¹, cos⁻¹ or tan⁻¹.","code":"10.05b"},{"h":"Exact trigonometric values","t":"Because J560/02 is taken without a calculator, the values of sine, cosine and tangent at 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° and 90° must be known exactly: sin 0° = 0, sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, sin 90° = 1; cos 0° = 1, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, cos 90° = 0; tan 0° = 0, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3. None of these is printed on the formulae sheet. They come from a square cut along its diagonal (giving the 45° triangle with sides 1, 1, √2) and an equilateral triangle cut in half (giving the 30°/60° triangle with sides 1, √3, 2).","code":"10.05c"}],"examples":[{"title":"Area of a trapezium","setup":"A canal has a cross-section shaped like a trapezium. The width of the water's surface is 12 m, the width at the bed is 8 m, and the perpendicular depth between them is 3 m. Work out the area of the cross-section. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel widths and h is the perpendicular depth between them; this formula is printed on the formulae sheet.","Add the two parallel widths: a + b = 12 + 8 = 20.","Halve the sum: ½ × 20 = 10, so the area is 10 × 3 — the line the method mark is awarded for.","10 × 3 = 30, so the cross-section has an area of 30 m²."],"cues":["Which of the three given lengths plays the role of h in the formula?","What do the two parallel widths add up to?","What is left to multiply by once the sum has been halved?","Which unit belongs on an area measured in metres?"]},{"title":"Area of a composite shape","setup":"A window is made from a rectangle 18 cm tall and 10 cm wide, with a semicircle attached to the top 10 cm edge, so the semicircle has a diameter of 10 cm. Work out the total area of the window, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Split the shape into a rectangle and a semicircle; the semicircle's radius is half its diameter, which is 5 cm.","Rectangle: 18 × 10 = 180 cm².","Semicircle: ½ × π × 5² = 39.269… cm² — the line the method mark is awarded for.","Total area = 180 + 39.269… = 219.269…, which is 219.3 cm² correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which two familiar shapes is the window built from?","Which of the two shapes has the simpler area to find first?","How does the area of a semicircle compare with the area of a whole circle of the same radius?","What must happen to the two separate areas now both are known?"]},{"title":"Arc length of a sector","setup":"A garden sprinkler sprays water over a sector of a circular lawn. The radius reached is 9 m and the sprinkler sweeps through an angle of 80° at the centre. Work out the length of the curved edge of the watered sector, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The angle at the centre gives the fraction of the whole circle: 80/360, which cancels to 2/9.","The whole circumference is 2 × π × 9 = 18π m.","Arc length = 2/9 × 18π = 4π = 12.566… m — the line the method mark is awarded for.","Rounded to 1 decimal place, the curved edge is 12.6 m long."],"cues":["What fraction of a full turn is the sweep angle?","Which measurement of the whole circle does the curved edge come from?","How do the fraction and the whole circumference combine?","What does that value round to, to 1 decimal place?"]},{"title":"Volume and surface area of a cylinder","setup":"A cylindrical paint tin has radius 4 cm and height 15 cm. Work out (a) the volume of the tin and (b) its total surface area, both correct to 3 significant figures. A calculator may be used.","steps":["A cylinder is a prism with a circular cross section, so volume = πr²h; the area of the cross section is π × 4² = 16π cm².","Volume = 16π × 15 = 240π = 753.98… cm³, which is 754 cm³ correct to 3 significant figures — the line the method mark for the volume is awarded for.","The curved part of the surface is 2πrh = 2 × π × 4 × 15 = 120π cm².","The two circular ends together are 2 × πr² = 2 × π × 4² = 32π cm².","Total surface area = 120π + 32π = 152π = 477.52…, which is 478 cm² correct to 3 significant figures."],"cues":["What is the area of the tin's circular cross section?","Which two things multiply together to give the volume of any prism?","Which part of a cylinder's surface is curved, and what is its area?","What area do the two flat ends of the cylinder contribute?","How do the curved surface and the two ends combine into a total?"]},{"title":"Finding a length with a trigonometric ratio","setup":"A zip wire is fixed in a straight line from a high platform to a low post on the ground. The wire is 25 m long and makes an angle of 22° with the horizontal ground. Work out the vertical height between the two ends of the wire, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Label the sides: the wire is the hypotenuse, and the height is opposite the 22° angle.","Opposite and hypotenuse means the sine ratio: sin 22° = h/25 — the line the method mark is awarded for.","Rearranging, h = 25 × sin 22°.","25 × sin 22° = 9.365…, so the vertical height is 9.4 m correct to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which side of this triangle is the hypotenuse?","Which trigonometric ratio connects the side opposite the angle with the hypotenuse?","How is the equation rearranged to make h the subject?","What does that calculation round to, to 1 decimal place?"]},{"title":"Volume of a pyramid using a given formula","setup":"A sand sculpture at a beach competition is a square-based pyramid. The base has sides of 9 m and the perpendicular height is 6 m. Volume of a pyramid = ⅓ × area of base × height. Work out the volume of sand in the sculpture.","steps":["The base is a square, so its area = 9 × 9 = 81 m².","Volume = ⅓ × area of base × height = ⅓ × 81 × 6 — the line the method mark is awarded for.","⅓ × 81 = 27, and 27 × 6 = 162, so the volume of sand is 162 m³."],"cues":["What shape is the base, and what is its area?","Which two things does the given formula multiply the base area by?","What does that calculation come to?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A rectangle measures 2000 cm by 300 cm. Write both lengths in metres.","a":"20 m and 3 m"},{"q":"A sector has radius 10 cm and an angle of 90° at the centre. What fraction of the whole circle is the sector?","a":"1/4"},{"q":"Write down the formula for the volume of a prism.","a":"Volume = area of cross section × length"},{"q":"Which trigonometric ratio links the side opposite an angle with the side adjacent to it?","a":"tan θ = opposite/adjacent"}]},"room":{"facts":["Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height; area of a parallelogram (and a rectangle) = base × perpendicular height. Neither is printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet.","Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr², where θ is the angle at the centre. Neither is printed on the formulae sheet.","Surface area of a cuboid = 2(lw + lh + wh); surface area of a cylinder = 2πr² + 2πrh (the two circular ends plus the curved surface). Neither is printed on the sheet — build each from the areas of the faces.","Exact values: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2; cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2; tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3. Not printed on the formulae sheet.","Metric links: 1 litre = 1000 cm³, 1 km = 100 000 cm, 1 tonne = 1000 kg, 1 m² = 10 000 cm². Compound units: speed = distance ÷ time, density = mass ÷ volume. None of this is printed on the formulae sheet.","A three-figure bearing is measured clockwise from north; the bearing of A from B and the bearing of B from A differ by 180°.","OCR's Mathematics J560 is examined by three papers per tier: Foundation J560/01–03, Higher J560/04–06, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes long. Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator. Foundation is graded 5–1, Higher 9–4."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Convert 3.8 tonnes into kilograms.","a":"3800 kg","sol":"1 tonne = 1000 kg, so 3.8 × 1000 = 3800. B1 for 3800 kg.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["Tonnes and kilograms both measure mass, on the same metric ladder.","Use the conversion factor between tonnes and kilograms.","Write down how many kilograms are in one tonne, then multiply by 3.8."],"codes":["10.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) An L-shaped lawn is made from two rectangles. Going round the edge, the six sides measure 7 m, 4 m, 3 m, 2 m, 4 m and 2 m in order. Work out the perimeter of the lawn.","a":"22 m","sol":"Perimeter = sum of all six sides. M1 for 7 + 4 + 3 + 2 + 4 + 2. A1 for 22 m.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A rectilinear shape's perimeter is simply the distance all the way round its edge.","Add every one of the six given side lengths; none needs to be worked out first.","Write the six side lengths added together as one sum, then work out the total."],"codes":["10.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A triangle has a base of 14 cm and a perpendicular height of 5 cm. Work out its area.","a":"35 cm²","sol":"Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height. M1 for ½ × 14 × 5. A1 for 35 cm².","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A triangle is half of a rectangle with the same base and height.","The area formula for a triangle must be recalled; it is not on the formulae sheet.","Write area = ½ × base × perpendicular height and put in the two given lengths."],"codes":["10.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A circle has a radius of 4 cm. Work out its area, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"16π cm²","sol":"Area of a circle = πr², printed on the formulae sheet. M1 for π × 4². A1 for 16π cm².","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two circle formulae are printed for you; only one of them gives an area.","'In terms of π' means the π stays in the answer and nothing is rounded.","Write area = πr² and substitute the radius you are given."],"codes":["10.03d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A right-angled triangle has its two shorter sides 9 cm and 12 cm. Work out the length of the hypotenuse.","a":"15 cm","sol":"Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c². M1 for 9² + 12² (= 81 + 144 = 225). A1 for √225 = 15 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Three sides are involved and no angle, so this is a job for Pythagoras' theorem.","The hypotenuse is the longest side, so the two given squares are added.","Write a² + b² = c² and square each of the two shorter sides."],"codes":["10.05a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Write down the exact value of cos 30°.","a":"√3/2","sol":"cos 30° is one of the exact values to recall: from the half equilateral triangle with sides 1, √3, 2, cos 30° = √3/2. B1 for √3/2.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["This is one of the values that has to be recalled, since J560/02 has no calculator.","It comes from an equilateral triangle of side 2 cut in half.","Write down the three sides of that half triangle, then use cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse for the 30° angle."],"codes":["10.05c"]},{"q":"A metal block has a mass of 540 g and a volume of 60 cm³. Work out its density, in g/cm³. A calculator may be used.","a":"9 g/cm³","sol":"Density = mass ÷ volume. M1 for 540 ÷ 60. A1 for 9 g/cm³.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["Density is a compound unit built from two measurements you are already given.","Decide which of the two given quantities is divided by the other.","Write density = mass ÷ volume and substitute the two values."],"codes":["10.01b"]},{"q":"A map is drawn to a scale of 1 : 20 000. Two villages are 8.5 cm apart on the map. Work out the real distance between them, in km. A calculator may be used.","a":"1.7 km","sol":"1 cm on the map = 20 000 cm on the ground. M1 for 8.5 × 20 000 = 170 000 cm. M1 for converting using 100 000 cm = 1 km. A1 for 1.7 km.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The second number in the scale tells you what one map centimetre stands for.","Find the real distance in centimetres first, then convert it into kilometres.","Multiply the map distance by 20 000, then divide by the number of centimetres in a kilometre."],"codes":["10.01c"]},{"q":"A trapezium-shaped garden bed has an area of 24 m². Its parallel sides are 5 m and 7 m long. Work out the perpendicular distance between the parallel sides. A calculator may be used.","a":"4 m","sol":"Area = ½(a + b)h, printed on the formulae sheet, so 24 = ½(5 + 7)h. M1 for ½(5 + 7) = 6. M1 for 24 ÷ 6. A1 for 4 m.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["This formula is printed for you, but this time the area is already known and a length is missing.","Work out what ½(a + b) comes to first, using the two parallel sides you are given.","Write 24 = ½(5 + 7) × h and work out what h must be to make both sides equal."],"codes":["10.03c"]},{"q":"A cuboid water tank measures 40 cm by 25 cm by 30 cm. Work out how many litres of water it holds when full. A calculator may be used.","a":"30 litres","sol":"Volume = length × width × height. M1 for 40 × 25 × 30. A1 for 30 000 cm³. Since 1 litre = 1000 cm³, B1 for 30 litres.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A cuboid is a prism, so its volume is length × width × height.","Find the volume in cm³ first, then change it into a unit of capacity.","Multiply the three given lengths, then divide by 1000 to reach litres."],"codes":["10.04a","10.01a"]},{"q":"In a right-angled triangle, the hypotenuse is 13 cm and one of the other angles is 42°. Work out the length of the side adjacent to that angle, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"9.7 cm","sol":"Adjacent and hypotenuse means the cosine ratio: cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse. M1 for cos 42° = x/13. M1 for x = 13 × cos 42° = 9.6609… A1 for 9.7 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["An angle is involved as well as sides, so a trigonometric ratio is needed rather than Pythagoras' theorem.","Decide which side is the hypotenuse and which is adjacent to the given angle.","Write cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse with the angle you are given, then rearrange for the unknown side."],"codes":["10.05b"]},{"q":"A shape is made from a rectangle 16 cm by 9 cm with a semicircular bite of diameter 9 cm removed from one of the 9 cm ends. Work out the perimeter of the shape, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"55.1 cm","sol":"Straight sides: two lengths of 16 cm and the far 9 cm end. M1 for 16 + 16 + 9 = 41. The curved edge is a semicircle of diameter 9 cm: M1 for ½ × π × 9 = 4.5π = 14.137… A1 for 41 + 14.137… = 55.1 cm (1 dp).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["One straight edge has been replaced by a curved one, so it must not be counted twice.","Add the straight sides that remain, then work out the length of the curved edge separately.","The curved edge is a semicircle, so its length is half of a full circumference of diameter 9 cm."],"codes":["10.02b","10.02c"]},{"q":"A square tile has side 10 cm. A quarter circle of radius 10 cm is cut from one corner and removed. Work out the area remaining, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"21.5 cm²","sol":"Square area = 10 × 10 = 100 cm². M1. Quarter circle area = ¼ × π × 10² = 78.5398… cm². M1. A1 for 100 − 78.5398… = 21.5 cm² (1 dp).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The remaining shape is the square with a piece taken away, not added.","Find the area of the whole square and the area of the piece removed separately.","Subtract a quarter of the area of a circle of radius 10 cm from the area of the square."],"codes":["10.03b","10.03d","10.03e"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A solid is made from a hemisphere of radius 6 cm joined to a cone of radius 6 cm and perpendicular height 8 cm, base to base. Volume of a sphere = 4/3 πr³; volume of a cone = ⅓πr²h. Work out the total volume of the solid, giving your answer in terms of π.","a":"240π cm³","sol":"A hemisphere is half a sphere: M1 for ½ × 4/3 × π × 6³ = 144π cm³. Cone: M1 for ⅓ × π × 6² × 8 = 96π cm³. A1 for total 144π + 96π = 240π cm³.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The solid is made of two parts, so deal with each one separately before combining them.","Both formulae are given; remember a hemisphere is only half of a full sphere's volume.","Substitute the radius into each given formula, keeping π in the working throughout."],"codes":["10.04b"]},{"q":"A crystal ornament is a square-based pyramid with volume 300 cm³ and perpendicular height 9 cm. Volume of a pyramid = ⅓ × area of base × height. Work out the length of a side of the square base.","a":"10 cm","sol":"300 = ⅓ × area of base × 9, so area of base = 300 ÷ 3 = 100 cm². M1. Side of square base = √100. M1. A1 for 10 cm.","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["The formula is given, but this time the volume is already known and the base area is missing.","Rearrange to find the area of the square base before finding the length of its side.","Write 300 = ⅓ × area of base × 9 and work out what the area of the base must be."],"codes":["10.04c"]},{"q":"A ladder 6.2 m long leans against a vertical wall with its foot 2.1 m from the base of the wall. Work out the angle the ladder makes with the ground, correct to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"70.2°","sol":"cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse = 2.1/6.2 = 0.3387… M1. θ = cos⁻¹(0.3387…) = 70.2° (1 dp). A1.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["An angle and two of the triangle's sides are involved, so a trigonometric ratio is needed rather than Pythagoras' theorem.","Decide which given length is the hypotenuse and which is adjacent to the angle you want.","Write cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse using the two given lengths, then use the inverse cosine to find the angle."],"codes":["10.05b"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write 4200 g in kilograms.","a":"4.2 kg"},{"q":"A circle has a radius of 5 cm. Write down its circumference in terms of π.","a":"10π cm"},{"q":"Write down the exact value of tan 45°.","a":"1"},{"q":"A ship sails on a bearing of 200°. Write down the bearing it would sail on to return directly the way it came.","a":"020°"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Standard units and metric conversion factors","back":"Length mm–cm–m–km, area mm²–cm²–m²–km², volume mm³–cm³–m³, capacity ml/litres, mass g–kg–tonnes. 1 cm=10 mm, 1 m=100 cm, 1 km=1000 m, 1 kg=1000 g, 1 tonne=1000 kg, 1 litre=1000 cm³. Always write the unit on the answer.","code":"10.01a","open":false},{"front":"Give one compound unit and its formula","back":"speed = distance ÷ time; density = mass ÷ volume; pressure = force ÷ area","code":"10.01b","open":true},{"front":"Map scales and bearings","back":"Scale 1 : n means 1 drawn unit stands for n real units; multiply by n, then convert. A bearing is clockwise from north, always three figures; A-from-B and B-from-A bearings differ by 180°.","code":"10.01c","open":false},{"front":"Exact trigonometric values (0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°)","back":"sin: 0, 1/2, √2/2, √3/2, 1. cos: the same list reversed. tan (0° to 60°): 0, √3/3, 1, √3. None is on the formulae sheet — needed for the non-calculator paper.","code":"10.05c","open":false},{"front":"Circumference of a circle","back":"Circumference = 2πr = πd. Printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet. Use πd straight from the diameter; only double r first if you are given the radius instead.","code":"10.02b","open":false},{"front":"Arc length of a sector","back":"Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr, where θ is the angle at the centre. Not printed on the formulae sheet — must be recalled. Write the fraction θ/360 as its own line.","code":"10.02b","open":false},{"front":"Area of a triangle","back":"Area = ½ × base × perpendicular height. Not printed on the formulae sheet. The height must be at right angles to the base, never a sloping side.","code":"10.03a","open":false},{"front":"Area of a parallelogram","back":"Area = base × perpendicular height; a rectangle is the special case, area = length × width. Neither is printed on the formulae sheet.","code":"10.03b","open":false},{"front":"Area of a trapezium","back":"Area = ½(a + b)h, where a and b are the parallel sides and h their perpendicular separation. Printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet.","code":"10.03c","open":false},{"front":"Area of a circle and area of a sector","back":"Area of a circle = πr², printed on the formulae sheet. Area of a sector = θ/360 × πr², not printed — must be recalled, using the same fraction as arc length.","code":"10.03d","open":false},{"front":"Volume and surface area of a cuboid, prism and cylinder","back":"Volume = area of cross section × length (printed on the sheet); cylinder volume = πr²h. Surface area is the sum of every face's area — not printed on the sheet, build it yourself.","code":"10.04a","open":false},{"front":"Volume and surface area of a cone and a sphere","back":"Not printed on the formulae sheet — the specification guarantees these formulae are given inside the exam question whenever they are needed, so they need not be memorised.","code":"10.04b","open":false},{"front":"Volume of a pyramid","back":"Volume = ⅓ × area of base × height. Given inside the exam question, so it need not be memorised — but the base area itself must be worked out.","code":"10.04c","open":false},{"front":"Pythagoras' theorem","back":"a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. Printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet. Add to find the hypotenuse; subtract to find a shorter side.","code":"10.05a","open":false},{"front":"The trigonometric ratios (SOH CAH TOA)","back":"sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent. All three are printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet.","code":"10.05b","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Area of a trapezium = ½(a + b)h — printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet.","Volume of a prism = area of cross section × length — printed on the sheet; a cuboid and a cylinder are both prisms.","Circumference of a circle = 2πr = πd — printed on the sheet.","Area of a circle = πr² — printed on the sheet.","Pythagoras' theorem a² + b² = c² — printed on the sheet (6.02d also requires it to be known; the sheet governs, so it is given on the day).","The sine, cosine and tangent ratios in a right-angled triangle — printed on the sheet (6.02d also requires them to be known; the sheet governs, so they are given on the day).","Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height — not on the sheet: recall it.","Area of a parallelogram (and a rectangle) = base × perpendicular height — not on the sheet: recall it.","Arc length of a sector = θ/360 × 2πr — not on the sheet: recall it.","Area of a sector = θ/360 × πr² — not on the sheet: recall it.","Surface area of a cuboid, prism or cylinder — not on the sheet and not given in the exam: build it from the areas of the faces.","Curved surface area of a cone, surface area of a sphere, volume of a sphere and volume of a cone — not on the sheet: the specification says these are given inside the relevant exam question, so they need not be memorised.","Volume of a pyramid (a third of the base area times the perpendicular height) — not on the sheet: the specification says it is given inside the relevant exam question. The surface area of a pyramid is not given and is not a single formula — it is built from the base area and the triangular faces.","Exact values of sin θ and cos θ at 0°, 30°, 45°, 60°, 90°, and of tan θ at 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° — not on the sheet: recall them.","Metric conversion factors (length, area, volume, mass and so on) and the compound-unit formulae speed = distance ÷ time and density = mass ÷ volume — not on the sheet: recall them.","The sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle = ½ab sin C, and the quadratic formula appear only on the Higher tier formulae sheet; they are Higher tier content (6.02d) and are not needed for this Foundation room."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:52:04.331Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.87,"notes":"Every answer key, solution line, mark tally and worked example checks out on re-solution (16 questions, 6 examples, 4 exit-ticket and 4 check items, all verified numerically); the room stays inside the Foundation column — no sine or cosine rule, no 3D trigonometry, no bounds, and the cone, sphere and pyramid formulae are printed in the stems as the specification requires — and the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md exactly (trapezium, prism, circumference, area of a circle, Pythagoras and the three trigonometric ratios printed; triangle, parallelogram, arc, sector, surface areas, exact values and compound units to be recalled), so the only real repair before publishing is adding OCR's paper structure to room.facts."}}},"mensuration-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"mensuration-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"This room carries the five results OCR's specification adds at Higher tier to Triangle mensuration and Area calculations. Foundation work on triangles stops at a right angle: Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios only ever act inside a flat right-angled triangle, and the only area formula for a triangle is ½ × base × height. Here the triangle need not have a right angle at all — the sine rule, the cosine rule and Area = ½ab sin C solve any triangle from whichever three measurements are given — and Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios are carried into three-dimensional solids by hunting out a right-angled triangle hidden inside them. Examiners set these as multi-step AO3 problems: bearings, masts, fields and roofs on the calculator papers, and on Paper 5 sin 60° must become √3/2 so the answer can be left as an exact surd.","idea":[{"h":"Area = ½ab sin C","t":"The area of any triangle is half the product of two sides multiplied by the sine of the angle between them: Area = ½ab sin C. The angle C must be the included angle — the one sitting between the two sides a and b that are used; if the angle given is somewhere else in the triangle, find the included angle first. The formula also runs backwards: given the area and two sides, sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab) recovers the angle, and given the area, one side and the included angle, the other side follows by rearranging. On a non-calculator question the angle is usually 30°, 45°, 60° or 150°, so an exact value goes in and the answer is left as a surd such as 21√3/2. It is printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, so the marks are for choosing it, substituting correctly and rearranging it, not for recalling it.","code":"10.03a"},{"h":"Pythagoras' theorem in three-dimensional figures","t":"Nothing new is needed beyond a² + b² = c² — what is new is finding the right-angled triangle hidden inside a solid. The routine is always the same: name the triangle by its three vertices, check that it really does have a right angle, redraw it flat with the lengths you know marked on it, then apply the theorem. A line drawn straight up from a flat base is at right angles to every line in that base, which is what makes a right-angled triangle available in the first place. In a cuboid the longest diagonal needs the theorem twice: first across the base to find the base diagonal, then up the vertical edge, giving d = √(l² + w² + h²) for edges l, w and h. Keep the full unrounded value from one stage before it is used in the next, rather than a rounded figure.","code":"10.05a"},{"h":"Trigonometry of right-angled triangles in three-dimensional figures","t":"The same sin, cos and tan ratios used on a flat right-angled triangle work exactly the same way once the triangle is found inside a solid — name it, check the right angle, redraw it flat, then apply the ratio. The angle between a line and a plane is the angle between that line and its projection onto the plane, found by dropping a perpendicular from the far end of the line down to the plane and reading the angle in the right-angled triangle this makes. The angle between two planes works the same way, using a line in each plane that meets the shared edge at right angles. Where a face of the solid has no right angle in it at all, the sine rule or the cosine rule may be needed instead, on that flat triangular face, before the trigonometry of the solid can continue.","code":"10.05b"},{"h":"The sine rule","t":"Label a triangle so side a is opposite angle A, side b opposite angle B and side c opposite angle C. The sine rule, a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, is used whenever a side and the angle directly opposite it are known together with one further side or angle; turned upside down, as sin A/a = sin B/b = sin C/c, it is easier to use when the unknown itself is an angle. Each equation only ever uses two of the three fractions at once, so write down the pair that contains both the known values and the one wanted. It is printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet — the marks are earned for pairing each side correctly with the angle opposite it and for the substitution and rearrangement, not for recalling the rule itself.","code":"10.05d"},{"h":"The cosine rule","t":"The cosine rule, a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, is used in the two cases the sine rule cannot start from: two sides and the angle included between them, to find the third side; and all three sides, to find any angle, using the rearrangement cos A = (b² + c² − a²) ÷ (2bc). A negative value for cos A is not a mistake — it shows the angle is obtuse, and a calculator returns that obtuse angle directly from cos⁻¹. Whichever side or angle is unknown, always substitute the two known sides and the angle between them, or, for the rearranged form, all three sides, with the letter of the unknown matched correctly. It is printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet in the a² form only; the rearrangement for an angle must be made in the exam.","code":"10.05e"}],"examples":[{"title":"The sine rule for a missing side","setup":"In triangle ABC, angle ABC = 55°, angle BCA = 68° and AB = 15 cm. A calculator may be used. Work out the length of AC, to 1 decimal place.","steps":["Identify which side is opposite each known angle: AB is opposite the 68° angle at C, and AC is opposite the 55° angle at B.","Write the sine rule as AC/sin 55° = 15/sin 68° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","Rearrange to make AC the subject: AC = 15 × sin 55° ÷ sin 68°.","Evaluate: AC = 15 × 0.81915… ÷ 0.92718… = 13.252…","Round to 1 decimal place: AC = 13.3 cm."],"cues":["Which side lies opposite the angle at C, and which lies opposite the angle at B?","Which two ratios does the sine rule set equal to each other for this triangle?","What operation isolates AC on one side of the equation?","What do the two sine values, multiplied and divided as the formula requires, give?","To how many decimal places does the question ask for the answer?"]},{"title":"Diagonal of a cuboid and the angle it makes with the base","setup":"A cuboid ABCDEFGH stands on the horizontal base ABCD, with E above A, F above B, G above C and H above D. AB = 8 cm, BC = 6 cm and the vertical edge CG = 5 cm. Work out the length of the diagonal AG, to 1 decimal place, and the size of the angle this diagonal makes with the base, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Across the base first: triangle ABC has its right angle at B, so AC² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100 and AC = 10 cm.","CG is vertical while AC lies flat, so triangle ACG has its right angle at C, with AC = 10 cm and CG = 5 cm as its shorter sides.","AG² = 10² + 5² = 100 + 25 = 125, so AG = √125 = 11.2 cm to 1 decimal place — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","The diagonal meets the base along AC, so the angle wanted is GAC; in triangle ACG the side opposite GAC is CG = 5 cm and the side next to it is AC = 10 cm, giving tan GAC = 5/10.","GAC = tan⁻¹(0.5) = 26.6° to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which two edges form the right-angled triangle that contains AC?","Which triangle contains AG and has a right angle in it?","Which two lengths are the shorter sides of that triangle?","Which trigonometric ratio uses the opposite and the adjacent sides?","How is an angle recovered once its tangent is known?"]},{"title":"Cosine rule then sine rule in one triangle","setup":"In triangle ABC the side a = BC is 9 cm, the side b = AC is 7 cm and the angle C is 62°. Work out the length of the side c = AB, to 1 decimal place, and then the size of angle A, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The known angle sits between the two known sides, so the cosine rule applies in the form c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C.","Substituting gives c² = 9² + 7² − 2 × 9 × 7 × cos 62° = 130 − 126 cos 62° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","126 cos 62° = 59.1534…, so c² = 130 − 59.1534… = 70.8466… and c = 8.4 cm to 1 decimal place.","Angle A is opposite the 9 cm side and the 62° angle is opposite c, so the sine rule with the full unrounded value of c gives sin A/9 = sin 62°/8.41704…","sin A = 9 × sin 62° ÷ 8.41704… = 0.94409…, so A = 70.8° to 1 decimal place."],"cues":["Which two sides does the 62° angle sit between?","Which letters do 9, 7 and 62° replace in the formula?","What must be done to c² to reach c?","Which side is opposite angle A, and which is opposite the 62° angle?","Why should the stored value of c be used rather than 8.4?"]},{"title":"Area = ½ab sin C forwards and backwards","setup":"Triangle ABC has AB = 8 cm, AC = 11 cm and angle BAC = 40°. Triangle DEF has DE = 9 cm, DF = 12 cm and an area of 27 cm², and its angle EDF is acute. Work out the area of triangle ABC, to 1 decimal place, and the size of angle EDF. A calculator may be used.","steps":["In triangle ABC the given angle lies between the two given sides, so Area = ½ × 8 × 11 × sin 40° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","½ × 8 × 11 = 44, so the area is 44 sin 40° = 28.3 cm² to 1 decimal place.","Triangle DEF uses the same formula with the area known instead: ½ × 9 × 12 × sin EDF = 27.","½ × 9 × 12 = 54, so sin EDF = 27 ÷ 54 = 0.5.","EDF = sin⁻¹(0.5) = 30°."],"cues":["Does the 40° angle lie between AB and AC?","What do the halved side lengths multiply to?","Which quantity in the formula is the unknown this time?","What is the area divided by to leave the sine on its own?","Which acute angle has this value as its sine?"]},{"title":"Cosine rule with no calculator","setup":"(non-calculator) In triangle PQR, PQ = 5 cm, PR = 8 cm and angle QPR = 60°. Work out the exact length of QR. No calculator may be used.","steps":["The given angle is between the two given sides, so the cosine rule gives QR² = PQ² + PR² − 2 × PQ × PR × cos QPR.","Substituting gives QR² = 5² + 8² − 2 × 5 × 8 × cos 60° — this is the line the method mark is awarded for.","cos 60° = 1/2 exactly, so the last term is 80 × 1/2 = 40.","QR² = 25 + 64 − 40 = 49.","QR = √49 = 7 cm exactly."],"cues":["Which rule suits two sides and the angle between them?","Which letters do 5, 8 and 60° replace?","Which exact value replaces the cosine here?","What do the three terms come to altogether?","What undoes the squaring?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Before using Pythagoras' theorem or a trig ratio on a triangle drawn inside a solid, what must you check about that triangle?","a":"That it really does contain a right angle."},{"q":"In triangle ABC you know sides a and b and the angle C between them. Which rule finds c?","a":"The cosine rule, c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C"},{"q":"A triangle has sides 6 cm and 10 cm with an included angle of 30°. Work out its area.","a":"15 cm²"},{"q":"What right-angled triangle gives the angle between a line and a plane?","a":"The one formed by the line and its perpendicular projection onto the plane."},{"q":"In a cuboid, which theorem is used twice to find the longest diagonal?","a":"Pythagoras' theorem"}]},"room":{"facts":["The sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where side a is opposite angle A. Turned over as sin A/a = sin B/b = sin C/c when an angle is wanted.","The cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, with A the angle between sides b and c; rearranged for an angle it is cos A = (b² + c² − a²) ÷ (2bc).","Area of any triangle = ½ab sin C, where C is the angle included between the sides a and b.","The longest diagonal of a cuboid with edges l, w and h is √(l² + w² + h²), from Pythagoras' theorem used twice.","Exact values for non-calculator work: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 30° = 1/√3 = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","OCR's Higher tier papers are J560/04–06: three papers of 100 marks each, lasting 1 hour 30 minutes each; Paper 5 (J560/05) is non-calculator; Higher tier assesses grades 9–4."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle ABC has AB = 10 cm, AC = 7 cm and angle BAC = 30°. Work out the area of triangle ABC.","a":"17.5 cm²","sol":"The 30° angle is included between AB and AC, so Area = ½ab sin C = ½ × 10 × 7 × sin 30°. M1 for this substitution. sin 30° = 1/2 exactly, so Area = 35 × 1/2 = 17.5. A1 for 17.5 cm² with the unit.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two lengths you are given meet at the angle you are given.","There is one area formula that works from two sides and the angle between them.","Write the area formula with the two sides and the included angle in place, leaving the sine to be replaced by its exact value."],"codes":["10.03a"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, angle BAC = 42°, angle ABC = 73° and BC = 9 cm. Work out the length of AC, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"12.9 cm","sol":"BC is opposite angle A and AC is opposite angle B, so the sine rule gives AC/sin 73° = 9/sin 42°. M1 for a correct sine rule statement or its rearrangement AC = 9 sin 73° ÷ sin 42°. AC = 9 × 0.95630… ÷ 0.66913… = 12.8625… A1 for 12.9 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One side and the angle facing it are both known here.","Pair each side with the angle opposite it before writing anything down.","Write the sine rule as one fraction for the unknown side and one for the known side, each over the sine of its opposite angle."],"codes":["10.05d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A cuboid measures 3 cm by 4 cm by 12 cm. Work out the length of the longest diagonal of the cuboid.","a":"13 cm","sol":"Across the 3 cm by 4 cm base, the base diagonal d satisfies d² = 3² + 4² = 9 + 16 = 25. M1 for using Pythagoras' theorem on the base. The longest diagonal L then satisfies L² = 25 + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169, so L = √169 = 13. A1 for 13 cm.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The diagonal you want runs from one corner to the corner furthest away, through the inside of the solid.","Reach it in two stages, doing the flat base first and the vertical edge second.","Write Pythagoras' theorem for the right-angled triangle formed by the two edges of the base."],"codes":["10.05a"]},{"q":"In cuboid ABCDEFGH, standing on horizontal base ABCD with E above A, F above B, G above C and H above D, AB = 9 cm and the vertical edge BF = 12 cm. A calculator may be used. Work out the size of angle FAB, to 1 decimal place.","a":"53.1°","sol":"BF is vertical and AB lies in the horizontal base, so they meet at right angles at B, giving a right-angled triangle ABF with BF opposite angle FAB and AB adjacent to it: tan FAB = 12/9. M1 for this ratio. FAB = tan⁻¹(12/9) = 53.130…, so FAB = 53.1° to 1 decimal place. A1.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Two of the cuboid's edges meet at a right angle exactly where this triangle has its vertex B.","Once you have the right-angled triangle, one of the three trigonometric ratios connects the angle at A to the two edges you know.","Write that ratio with the vertical edge and the horizontal edge in their correct places before finding the angle."],"codes":["10.05b"]},{"q":"In triangle ABC, AB = 11 cm, BC = 8 cm and angle ABC = 47°. Work out the length of AC, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"8.1 cm","sol":"The known angle is between the two known sides, so the cosine rule gives AC² = 11² + 8² − 2 × 11 × 8 × cos 47°. M1 for this substitution. AC² = 185 − 176 × 0.68199… = 185 − 120.0317… = 64.9682… M1 (dep) for reaching AC². AC = √64.9682… = 8.0602… A1 for 8.1 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["No angle here is opposite a known side, so the sine rule cannot get started.","Two sides with the angle between them is exactly the case one of the two rules is built for.","Write the cosine rule with the unknown side squared on the left and the two known sides and their included angle on the right."],"codes":["10.05e"]},{"q":"A triangle has sides of length 5 cm, 8 cm and 11 cm. Work out the size of the largest angle, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"113.6°","sol":"The largest angle is opposite the longest side, so it is opposite the 11 cm side. Call it A, with b = 5 and c = 8: cos A = (5² + 8² − 11²)/(2 × 5 × 8). M1 for the rearranged cosine rule with 11 opposite A. cos A = (25 + 64 − 121)/80 = −32/80 = −0.4. A1 for −0.4. A = cos⁻¹(−0.4) = 113.578… A1 for 113.6°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["In any triangle the biggest angle always faces the longest side.","With all three sides known and no angle known, only one of the two rules can start.","Write the cosine rule rearranged so that the cosine of the angle is on its own on the left."],"codes":["10.05e"]},{"q":"Triangle XYZ has XY = 14 cm, XZ = 9 cm and an area of 45 cm². Angle YXZ is acute. Work out the size of angle YXZ, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"45.6°","sol":"Angle YXZ is included between XY and XZ, so ½ × 14 × 9 × sin YXZ = 45. M1 for this equation. ½ × 14 × 9 = 63, so sin YXZ = 45/63 = 0.71428… A1 for the sine. YXZ = sin⁻¹(0.71428…) = 45.584… A1 for 45.6°.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The area formula that uses two sides and the angle between them can be read in either direction.","Put what you know into that formula and treat the angle as the unknown in an equation.","Write the area formula with the two given sides in place and the area on the other side of the equals sign."],"codes":["10.03a"]},{"q":"A monument is a pyramid with a rectangular base 12 m by 16 m. Its apex is vertically above the centre of the base, and each of the four sloping edges is 15 m long. Work out the vertical height of the monument, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"11.2 m","sol":"A sloping edge, the vertical height and the line from the centre of the base to a base corner form a right-angled triangle, so the base diagonal comes first: diagonal² = 12² + 16² = 144 + 256 = 400, giving a diagonal of 20 m. M1 for Pythagoras' theorem across the rectangular base. The centre is the midpoint of that diagonal, so the distance from the centre to a corner is 10 m. A1. The height h then satisfies h² = 15² − 10² = 225 − 100 = 125. M1 for Pythagoras' theorem in the vertical triangle. h = √125 = 11.180… so the height is 11.2 m to 1 decimal place. A1.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["A sloping edge, the vertical line through the apex and a line drawn across the base meet in one right-angled triangle.","The centre of a rectangle is the midpoint of each of its diagonals, so the whole diagonal is worth having before it is halved.","Write Pythagoras' theorem for the diagonal of the rectangular base using its two edge lengths."],"codes":["10.05a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In triangle ABC, angle BAC = 45°, angle ABC = 60° and BC = 6 cm. Work out the exact length of AC, giving your answer in the form k√6 where k is an integer.","a":"3√6 cm","sol":"BC is opposite A and AC is opposite B, so AC/sin 60° = 6/sin 45°. M1 for a correct sine rule statement. Using sin 60° = √3/2 and sin 45° = √2/2 gives AC = 6 × (√3/2) ÷ (√2/2) = 6√3/√2. M1 for the exact values substituted. Rationalising, 6√3/√2 = 6√6/2 = 3√6. A1 for 3√6 cm.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A side and the angle opposite it are both known, which is what one of the two rules needs.","Both of these angles have exact sines, so no decimals should ever appear.","Write the sine rule as the unknown side over the sine of its opposite angle, equal to the known side over the sine of its opposite angle."],"codes":["10.05d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Triangle LMN has LM = 8 cm, LN = 9 cm and angle MLN = 45°. Work out the exact area of triangle LMN, giving your answer in surd form.","a":"18√2 cm²","sol":"The angle is included between LM and LN, so Area = ½ × 8 × 9 × sin 45°. M1 for this substitution. sin 45° = √2/2, so Area = 36 × √2/2. M1 for using the exact value. Area = 18√2 cm². A1.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two sides and the angle between them point at one particular area formula.","A surd answer is asked for, so the sine must be replaced by its exact value rather than a decimal.","Write the area formula with the two sides in place, leaving the sine of the included angle unevaluated."],"codes":["10.03a"]},{"q":"In cuboid ABCDEFGH, standing on horizontal base ABCD with E above A, F above B, G above C and H above D, AB = 12 cm, AD = 5 cm and the vertical edge AE = 4 cm. A calculator may be used. Work out the size of the angle between the diagonal AG and the face ABFE, to 1 decimal place.","a":"21.6°","sol":"The diagonal AG meets the face ABFE at A, and G lies a perpendicular distance AD = 5 cm from that face, so the angle wanted is angle GAF, where F is the foot of that perpendicular. In right-angled triangle ABF, right-angled at B, AF² = AB² + BF² = 12² + 4² = 144 + 16 = 160, since BF = AE = 4 cm. M1 for Pythagoras' theorem on AF. AF = √160 = 12.649… A1 for AF. In right-angled triangle AFG, right-angled at F, the side opposite angle GAF is FG = AD = 5 cm and the side adjacent to it is AF, so tan GAF = 5 ÷ 12.649…. M1 for this ratio. GAF = tan⁻¹(5 ÷ 12.649…) = 21.568…, so the angle is 21.6° to 1 decimal place. A1.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The diagonal AG leaves the face ABFE at A and reappears at G, which sits a fixed perpendicular distance from that face.","Find where a perpendicular from G meets the face first, using Pythagoras' theorem within the face itself, then build the right-angled triangle that contains the angle wanted.","Work out AF using the two edges of the face ABFE, ready to use as the side adjacent to the angle."],"codes":["10.05a","10.05b"]},{"q":"A triangular plot of land ABC has AB = 85 m, BC = 62 m and angle ABC = 108°. Turf costs £4.50 per square metre. Work out the cost of covering the whole plot with turf, to the nearest pound. A calculator may be used.","a":"£11 277","sol":"Angle ABC is included between AB and BC, so Area = ½ × 85 × 62 × sin 108°. M1. Area = 2635 × 0.95105… = 2506.03… m². A1 for the area (accept 2506 m²). Cost = 2506.03… × 4.50 = 11 277.15… M1 for multiplying the area by 4.50. To the nearest pound the cost is £11 277. A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The plot has no right angle, so no base-and-height pair is available.","Find the area from the two sides and the angle between them first, then deal with the money.","Write the area formula with the two given sides and the included angle in place."],"codes":["10.03a"]},{"q":"A vertical mast AB stands with its foot B on horizontal ground. P and Q are points on the ground, in a straight line with B, with Q between P and B. From P the angle of elevation of the top A is 32°, and from Q it is 51°. PQ = 40 m. Work out the height of the mast, to the nearest metre. A calculator may be used.","a":"51 m","sol":"In triangle APQ, angle AQP = 180° − 51° = 129°, so angle PAQ = 180° − 32° − 129° = 19°. B1 for 129° or 19°. Sine rule: AQ/sin 32° = 40/sin 19°. M1. AQ = 40 × 0.52991… ÷ 0.32556… = 65.107… A1 for AQ (accept 65.1 m). In right-angled triangle ABQ, AB = AQ × sin 51°. M1. AB = 65.107… × 0.77714… = 50.597… so the mast is 51 m tall to the nearest metre. A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["Neither of the two triangles on the ground line is right-angled on its own, but the one containing both sight lines can still be solved.","Use angles on a straight line to fill in the triangle formed by P, Q and the top of the mast, then find a slant distance before the height.","Write down the angle at Q inside triangle APQ using angles on a straight line."],"codes":["10.05d","10.05b"]},{"q":"A square-based pyramid has a base of edge 10 cm and four sloping edges each of length 13 cm. Work out the angle between a sloping edge and the base, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"57.0°","sol":"The sloping edge meets the base along half a base diagonal. Base diagonal = √(10² + 10²) = √200 = 14.142… M1 for Pythagoras' theorem on the base. Half of it is 7.0710… cm. A1. The sloping edge, the half-diagonal and the vertical height form a right-angled triangle, so cos θ = 7.0710…/13. M1. θ = cos⁻¹(0.54392…) = 57.048… so the angle is 57.0° to 1 decimal place. A1.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The apex sits directly above the point where the two base diagonals cross.","The sloping edge, its shadow on the base and the vertical height make a right-angled triangle; find the shadow first.","Write Pythagoras' theorem for the diagonal of the square base."],"codes":["10.05a","10.05b"]},{"q":"A ship sails from port P on a bearing of 040° for 18 km to a point Q. It then sails on a bearing of 155° for 25 km to a point R. Work out the distance PR, to 1 decimal place. A calculator may be used.","a":"23.8 km","sol":"The bearing of P from Q is 040° + 180° = 220°. B1. Angle PQR = 220° − 155° = 65°. B1. Two sides with the included angle, so PR² = 18² + 25² − 2 × 18 × 25 × cos 65°. M1. PR² = 949 − 900 × 0.42261… = 949 − 380.356… = 568.643… M1 (dep). PR = √568.643… = 23.846… so PR = 23.8 km. A1.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["A sketch with a north line drawn at Q turns the two bearings into one angle inside the triangle.","Find the angle at Q first, using the back bearing of the first leg, then treat PQR as an ordinary triangle.","Write down the bearing of P from Q by adding 180° to the bearing of the first leg."],"codes":["10.05e"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A cube has edge length 1 cm. Write down the exact length of its space diagonal.","a":"√3 cm"},{"q":"Write down the cosine rule for a² in triangle ABC.","a":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A"},{"q":"In cuboid ABCDEFGH, standing on horizontal base ABCD with E above A, F above B, G above C and H above D, AB = 6 cm, BC = 8 cm and the vertical edge CG = 10 cm. Work out the size of the angle between the diagonal AG and the base, to 1 decimal place.","a":"45.0°"},{"q":"In triangle ABC, a = 8 cm, angle A = 40° and angle B = 65°. Work out b, to 1 decimal place.","a":"11.3 cm"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle","back":"Area = ½ab sin C, where C is the angle between sides a and b. It works for any triangle, with no perpendicular height needed.","code":"10.03a","open":false},{"front":"Area = ½ab sin C rearranged for the angle","back":"sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab), then C = sin⁻¹ of that value. The two sides used must be the ones enclosing angle C.","code":"10.03a","open":false},{"front":"The included angle","back":"The angle lying between the two sides being used. Both the cosine rule and Area = ½ab sin C need it; if the given angle is elsewhere, find the included angle first.","code":"10.03a","open":false},{"front":"The longest diagonal of a cuboid","back":"d = √(l² + w² + h²), for edges l, w and h. It comes from Pythagoras' theorem used twice: first across the base, then up the vertical edge.","code":"10.05a","open":false},{"front":"Method for Pythagoras' theorem in a three-dimensional solid","back":"Name the triangle inside the solid by its vertices, check it really has a right angle, redraw it flat with the known lengths marked, then apply a² + b² = c².","code":"10.05a","open":false},{"front":"A line perpendicular to a plane","back":"It is at right angles to every line drawn in that plane, which is what makes a right-angled triangle available for Pythagoras' theorem inside a solid.","code":"10.05a","open":false},{"front":"The angle between a line and a plane","back":"The angle between the line and its projection on the plane. Drop a perpendicular from the far end of the line to the plane and use the right-angled triangle formed.","code":"10.05b","open":false},{"front":"Method for trigonometry in a three-dimensional figure","back":"Name the triangle inside the solid, check it has a right angle, redraw it flat with the known lengths marked, then apply sin, cos or tan.","code":"10.05b","open":false},{"front":"The angle between two planes","back":"Found using a line in each plane that meets their shared edge at right angles; the angle between those two lines is the angle between the planes.","code":"10.05b","open":false},{"front":"The sine rule","back":"a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where side a is opposite angle A. Use it when a side and the angle opposite it are both known.","code":"10.05d","open":false},{"front":"When to use the sine rule","back":"When a side and the angle directly opposite it are known, together with one further side or angle.","code":"10.05d","open":false},{"front":"The sine rule rearranged for an angle","back":"sin A/a = sin B/b = sin C/c. Easier to use than the rule's usual form when the unknown wanted is an angle.","code":"10.05d","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule","back":"a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A, where angle A lies between sides b and c. Use it for two sides and the included angle.","code":"10.05e","open":false},{"front":"The cosine rule rearranged for an angle","back":"cos A = (b² + c² − a²) ÷ (2bc). Use it when all three sides of the triangle are known.","code":"10.05e","open":false},{"front":"When to use the cosine rule","back":"For two sides and the included angle, or for all three sides. A negative cosine is not an error — it means the angle is obtuse.","code":"10.05e","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["The sine rule: a/sin A = b/sin B = c/sin C, where a, b and c are the sides opposite angles A, B and C — printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, so it is given on the day; the choice, the labelling and the substitution are what earn the marks.","The cosine rule: a² = b² + c² − 2bc cos A — printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet in this form only. The rearrangement for an angle, cos A = (b² + c² − a²) ÷ (2bc), is not printed and must be made in the exam.","Area of a triangle = ½ab sin C, with C the angle between sides a and b — printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet. The rearrangement for an angle, sin C = 2 × Area ÷ (ab), is not printed and must be made in the exam.","Pythagoras' theorem: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse — printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, and needed twice over for a diagonal inside a three-dimensional solid.","The right-angled trigonometric ratios sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse, tan θ = opposite/adjacent — printed on the Higher tier formulae sheet, and used inside a solid exactly as in a flat figure once the right-angled triangle is found.","The longest diagonal of a cuboid, d = √(l² + w² + h²) — not printed on the formulae sheet; derive it by applying Pythagoras' theorem across the base and then up the vertical edge, both of which the sheet does print.","The angle between a line and a plane, and the angle between two planes — geometric definitions, not formulae, and neither sheet states them; apply the printed trigonometric ratios to the right-angled triangle each definition creates.","Exact values for non-calculator work: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, cos 30° = √3/2, cos 45° = √2/2, cos 60° = 1/2, tan 30° = √3/3, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3 — on neither tier's formulae sheet: these must be recalled, carried forward as prior knowledge from the Foundation room."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:59:07Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"The mathematics is sound throughout - all 15 questions, 5 worked examples, 5 check questions and 4 exit items re-solved and correct, marks tally to m everywhere, four non-calculator questions are marked and give 17.5 cm², 13 cm, 3√6 cm and 21√3/2 cm² by hand, difficulties run 4/7/4 and the Formulae sheet extra matches appendix.md exactly (sine rule, cosine rule, ½ab sin C, Pythagoras and the three ratios printed on the Higher sheet; the cuboid diagonal, the rearrangements and the exact values correctly flagged as recall); fix the missing OCR paper facts and the duplicated exit question before publishing."}}},"probability":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"probability","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"OCR spreads probability across two content areas — Basic probability and experiments, and Combined events and probability diagrams — because it is where reliable marks sit next to marks lost every year: writing a probability as a ratio such as 3 : 4 instead of a fraction, adding when the question needs multiplying, or forgetting that a counter taken and not replaced changes the numbers on the next branch. This room builds the habits the mark scheme rewards: write every probability as a fraction, decimal or percentage between 0 and 1; set out every possibility once in a table, frequency tree, sample space, Venn diagram or tree diagram; multiply along a tree path and add between paths; and turn a probability into an expected number of outcomes. Papers 1 and 3 allow a calculator, but Paper 2 does not, so the fraction and decimal arithmetic has to be secure by hand.","idea":[{"h":"The probability scale from 0 to 1","t":"Every probability is a number on the scale from 0 to 1: 0 means impossible, 1 means certain, and 0.5 (an even chance) sits exactly halfway. Words such as unlikely, evens and likely describe positions on this same scale, and a question that gives a word ('there is an even chance of rain') expects a number back (0.5). Whatever method finds a probability — counting equally likely outcomes, or recording an experiment — the answer must be written as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage. A probability is never written as a ratio such as 3 : 5, and it is never given as a bare count such as '3 out of 8' with no fraction bar; both of those forms lose the accuracy mark even when the working is correct.","code":"11.01a"},{"h":"Recording outcomes: frequency tables and frequency trees","t":"An experiment is only as good as its record. A frequency table lists each outcome with the number of times it happened; a two-way table sorts the same results by two features at once, with a total row and a total column that must agree. A frequency tree carries frequencies, not probabilities: each pair of branches splits the number it came from, so the two numbers on a pair always add back to the number at the branch point, and the numbers at the ends of the tree add back to the grand total. Some questions give a tree frame that is already partly filled in; fill every remaining gap by adding or subtracting along the branches before answering anything else, and check that both a row and a column of numbers agree with the total given.","code":"11.01b"},{"h":"Relative frequency as an estimate, and larger samples","t":"There are two ways to get a probability. Theoretical probability comes from equally likely outcomes — count the favourable ones and divide by the total. Relative frequency (experimental probability) comes from trials actually carried out: relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ number of trials. Use relative frequency when the outcomes are not equally likely, such as a drawing pin or a spinner that might be biased. As the number of trials increases, an unbiased sample's relative frequency gets closer to the true theoretical probability, so an estimate from more trials is the better estimate, and comparing a relative frequency with the theoretical value is how a question asks whether something is biased.","code":"11.01c"},{"h":"Equally likely outcomes, fairness and expected number","t":"'Fair', 'unbiased' and 'at random' all mean the same thing: every outcome is equally likely, so each of the six faces of a fair dice has probability 1/6. For any experiment with equally likely outcomes, P(event) = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of outcomes, and this still works for a simple combined event such as the total when two dice are rolled, provided every pairing is counted once. Once a probability is known, the expected number of times it happens in n trials is expected number = probability × number of trials; a fair spinner with five equal sections, spun 200 times, is expected to land on a chosen section 1/5 × 200 = 40 times. An expected number need not be a whole number, and it is not a promise: say 'about 40 times', never that it must happen exactly.","code":"11.01d"},{"h":"Sample spaces: tables and grids for combined events","t":"A sample space (possibility space) lists every outcome of one experiment, or of two experiments combined, exactly once. For a single event it can be a simple list; for two combined events a grid suits it best — one experiment down the side, the other along the top, one cell for every pairing. Two fair six-sided dice give 6 × 6 = 36 outcomes in the grid, and writing the total (or the product, or the difference) in each cell turns any question about that quantity into counting cells: P(event) = number of matching cells ÷ total number of cells. The trap is counting outcomes rather than cells — a total of 7 can be made in six different ways, so the eleven possible totals from 2 to 12 are not equally likely even though the thirty-six cells are.","code":"11.02a"},{"h":"Systematic listing: enumeration strategies","t":"Reliable counting starts from a systematic list, in which every possibility appears exactly once and none is missed or repeated. A systematic strategy fixes an order and works through it methodically: to list every arrangement of the letters E, F and G, fix which letter comes first, then list the two orders of the remaining letters before moving to the next first letter, giving EFG, EGF, FEG, FGE, GEF, GFE. To list every two-digit number made from a set of digits, fix the tens digit and run through the possible units digits in order before moving to the next tens digit. A list written in a fixed order beats a random scribble every time, because a random list cannot be checked for gaps or repeats.","code":"11.02b"},{"h":"Venn diagrams and set notation","t":"A Venn diagram suits overlapping groups: two circles inside a rectangle, with the overlap holding the members of both groups, each other region filled once, and anything in neither group placed in the rectangle outside both circles. Work from the inside out — fill the overlap first, then subtract it from each group's total so nobody is counted twice, then subtract everything placed from the grand total to find the 'neither' region. Set notation describes a set of numbers or objects using braces: A = {even numbers} lists a type, D = {x : 1 < x < 3} describes every x satisfying that inequality, and E = {x : x is a factor of 280} describes every factor of 280; you are not required to know the symbols for intersection (∩), union (∪) or complement (′) at this tier.","code":"11.02c"},{"h":"Tree diagrams for successive events","t":"A tree diagram records the outcomes and probabilities of a sequence of events: the first stage is a set of branches spreading from a single point, one branch for each outcome, and the probabilities on branches from the same point always add up to 1. A second stage grows a fresh set of branches from the end of every first-stage branch, so a two-stage tree with two outcomes at each stage has four end points in total, one for every possible pairing. Some questions give a tree frame already partly drawn or partly labelled; complete whatever is missing using the rule that branches from one point sum to 1 before using the tree to answer anything else.","code":"11.02d"},{"h":"The addition law: not A, and A or B","t":"A set of outcomes is exhaustive if it covers everything that can happen, and mutually exclusive if no two of them can happen together. For such a set the probabilities add up to 1, and its most-used special case is P(not A) = 1 − P(A): if the probability a bus is on time is 0.82, the probability it is not on time is 1 − 0.82 = 0.18. When two events can overlap, the addition law is P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B): the subtraction removes the overlap that adding the two separate probabilities counted twice. When A and B are mutually exclusive, P(A and B) = 0, so the law simplifies to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). When a table of probabilities has one entry missing, add the ones given and subtract that total from 1.","code":"11.02e"},{"h":"The multiplication law: independent and dependent events","t":"Two events are independent when the first does not change the probability of the second — a coin does not remember, and a counter that is replaced leaves the bag exactly as it was; then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). Events are dependent when the first does change the second, which is what 'without replacement' means: take a red counter from 5 red and 3 blue and the second pick is from 4 red out of 7, so both the favourable count and the total can change. On a tree diagram, multiply the probabilities along one path to get the probability of that combination, and add the probabilities of the different paths that fit the description being asked for. Look carefully at whether an item is replaced before deciding which probability belongs on the second set of branches.","code":"11.02f"}],"examples":[{"title":"A frequency tree for permission slips","setup":"A school asks 90 pupils whether a parent has signed their trip permission slip. 50 of the 90 pupils are in Year 10, and 34 of them have a signed slip; the rest of the Year 10 pupils do not. Of the pupils not in Year 10, 31 have a signed slip. Draw a frequency tree for these results and use it to find the number of the 90 pupils who do not have a signed slip.","steps":["Year 10 branch splits into signed and not signed: 50 − 34 = 16 Year 10 pupils are not signed.","The other branch is the pupils not in Year 10: 90 − 50 = 40 pupils.","That branch splits too: 40 − 31 = 9 of them are not signed.","Add the two 'not signed' ends of the tree: 16 + 9 = 25 pupils altogether do not have a signed slip."],"cues":["How many of the 50 Year 10 pupils do not have a signed slip?","How many pupils in total are not in Year 10?","How many of those pupils do not have a signed slip?","How do the two 'not signed' branch ends combine into one total?"]},{"title":"A sample space grid for two dice","setup":"Two ordinary fair six-sided dice are rolled and the two scores are added together. Draw a sample space grid to show every possible total, and use it to work out the probability that the total is 9.","steps":["Draw a 6 by 6 grid, one row for each score on the first dice and one column for each score on the second: 6 × 6 = 36 cells in total.","Write the total in every cell, for example row 3 gives 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 and row 6 gives 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12.","Count how many cells show a total of 9: (3,6), (4,5), (5,4) and (6,3), which is 4 cells.","P(total = 9) = 4/36 = 1/9."],"cues":["How many cells does a grid for two six-sided dice need altogether?","What number goes inside each cell of the grid?","How many of the 36 cells show the total you want?","How is that count of cells turned into a probability in its simplest form?"]},{"title":"Listing arrangements systematically","setup":"The three letters E, F and G are written down in some order, once each, to make a three-letter arrangement. List every possible arrangement of the three letters systematically, and use your list to find the probability that an arrangement chosen at random starts with the letter E.","steps":["Fix E first and list the two orders of the remaining letters: EFG, EGF.","Fix F first and do the same: FEG, FGE.","Fix G first and do the same: GEF, GFE — six arrangements in total, each equally likely.","Two of the six arrangements start with E, so P(starts with E) = 2/6 = 1/3."],"cues":["If E is written first, in how many orders can the other two letters follow?","How many more arrangements does fixing F first give?","How many arrangements are there altogether once G has been fixed first too?","How many of those arrangements start with E, and what fraction is that of the list?"]},{"title":"A Venn diagram for two languages","setup":"In a class of 30 students, 18 study French, 12 study German, and 5 of the students study both French and German. Draw a Venn diagram to show this information, and work out the probability that a student chosen at random studies neither French nor German.","steps":["The overlap holds the students who study both languages, so write 5 in the overlap.","French only: 18 − 5 = 13 students; German only: 12 − 5 = 7 students.","Students in at least one circle, out of the 30 in the class: 13 + 5 + 7 = 25.","Students in neither circle: 30 − 25 = 5, so P(neither) = 5/30 = 1/6."],"cues":["Which region of the Venn diagram must be filled in first?","Once the overlap is known, how many students study only French, and only German?","How many students in total are inside at least one circle?","How many students are left for the region outside both circles, and what probability is that?"]},{"title":"A tree diagram with no replacement","setup":"A box holds 10 pens: 6 are blue and 4 are black. Aidan takes one pen at random and does not put it back, then takes a second pen at random from those left. Draw a tree diagram for the two picks and work out the probability that both pens are blue.","steps":["First pair of branches: P(blue) = 6/10 and P(black) = 4/10.","The first pen is not replaced, so after a blue pen there are 5 blue left out of 9, giving P(second blue) = 5/9.","Both blue is one path through the tree, so multiply the probabilities along it: 6/10 × 5/9.","6/10 × 5/9 = 30/90 = 1/3, so P(both blue) = 1/3."],"cues":["What is the probability that the first pen taken is blue?","How many pens are left for the second pick, and how many of those are blue?","What do you do with the two probabilities along one path of a tree?","What does that multiplication simplify to?"]},{"title":"Filling the gap with the addition law","setup":"A biased four-sided dice can land on 1, 2, 3 or 4 only. The probabilities are P(1) = 0.15, P(2) = 0.27 and P(3) = 0.34, and P(4) is missing. The dice is rolled 300 times. Work out P(4), and the expected number of times it lands on 4.","steps":["The four outcomes are exhaustive and mutually exclusive, so all four probabilities add up to 1, whatever the 300 rolls to come give.","Add the three that are given: 0.15 + 0.27 + 0.34 = 0.76.","The missing probability is what is left over: P(4) = 1 − 0.76 = 0.24.","Expected number = P(4) × number of rolls = 0.24 × 300 = 72."],"cues":["What must all four probabilities add up to, since they cover every outcome?","What do the three given probabilities come to when added together?","How is the missing probability found from that total?","Which calculation turns a probability into an expected number of rolls, and what does it give?"]}],"check":[{"q":"There is 'an even chance' that a coin lands heads. Write this as a probability.","a":"0.5 (or 1/2)"},{"q":"The probability that a delivery arrives on time is 0.72. Work out the probability that it does not arrive on time.","a":"0.28"},{"q":"A bag holds 9 counters, 4 of which are red. One counter is taken at random. Write down the probability that it is red.","a":"4/9"},{"q":"A fair spinner has 4 equal sections. It is spun 120 times. How many times would you expect it to land on a chosen section?","a":"30"},{"q":"A counter is taken from a bag and not put back before a second counter is taken. Does the first pick affect the probabilities for the second pick? Explain.","a":"Yes. Not replacing the counter changes how many counters are left in the bag, so the total (and possibly the favourable count) for the second pick is different."}]},"room":{"facts":["Every probability is a number from 0 to 1, written as a fraction, a decimal or a percentage — never as a ratio.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add up to 1, so P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","Theoretical probability = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes.","Relative frequency (experimental probability) = number of times the outcome happened ÷ total number of trials.","Expected number of outcomes = probability of the outcome × number of trials.","The addition law: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B); for mutually exclusive events this becomes P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B).","For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B); when an item is not replaced the second probability changes to match what is left.","On a tree diagram: multiply the probabilities along a path, and add the probabilities of the separate paths that fit the description.","As an unbiased sample gets larger, its relative frequency gets closer to the theoretical probability.","OCR GCSE (9-1) Mathematics J560 has three papers per tier: Foundation is J560/01, /02 and /03 (grades 5-1); Higher is J560/04, /05 and /06 (grades 9-4). Each paper is 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator, the rest allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) A weather forecaster says there is 'an even chance' of snow tomorrow. Write this as a probability.","a":"0.5","sol":"'Even chance' is the point exactly halfway along the 0 to 1 probability scale. P(snow) = 0.5 — B1 for 0.5 (or 1/2).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The probability scale runs from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain).","'Even chance' names one particular point on that scale.","Write the probability as a decimal or a fraction, not as a word."],"codes":["11.01a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The probability that a parcel is delivered on time is 0.82. Work out the probability that it is not delivered on time.","a":"0.18","sol":"On time and not on time are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, so their probabilities add up to 1. P(not on time) = 1 − 0.82 = 0.18 — B1 for 0.18.","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The parcel either arrives on time or it does not, and there is no third case.","The two probabilities have to account for everything that can happen.","Write P(not on time) = 1 − P(on time) before you put a number in."],"codes":["11.02e"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 5 red counters, 4 green counters and 3 yellow counters. One counter is taken at random. Write down the probability that it is green, as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"1/3","sol":"Total number of counters = 5 + 4 + 3 = 12 — M1 for 12 as the denominator. P(green) = 4/12 = 1/3 — A1 for 1/3.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["'At random' means every counter in the bag is equally likely to be picked.","A theoretical probability is a count of what you want over a count of everything possible.","Work out how many counters there are altogether before you write the fraction, then simplify."],"codes":["11.01d"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A fair spinner has 6 equal sections numbered 1 to 6. It is spun 150 times. Work out the expected number of times it lands on an odd number.","a":"75","sol":"Odd sections are 1, 3 and 5, so P(odd) = 3/6 = 1/2 — M1 for 1/2 × 150. Expected number = 1/2 × 150 = 75 — A1 for 75.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Decide first which of the six numbers on the spinner are odd.","A fair spinner means every section is equally likely.","Write expected number = P(odd) × number of spins before substituting."],"codes":["11.01d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Set A = {x : x is a factor of 20}. List the members of set A.","a":"1, 2, 4, 5, 10, 20","sol":"A factor of 20 divides 20 exactly: 1, 2, 4, 5, 10, 20 — M1 for at least 4 correct factors, A1 for all six factors and no extra numbers.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Set notation describes the members that belong to the set.","A factor of 20 is a whole number that divides 20 exactly, with nothing left over.","Check each whole number from 1 up to 20 in turn, listing the ones that divide it exactly."],"codes":["11.02c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A driving instructor records the results of 60 driving tests. 36 of the 60 candidates take an automatic test, and 27 of them pass. Of the 24 candidates who take a manual test, 15 pass. Complete a frequency tree for these results and use it to work out the relative frequency that a candidate chosen at random passed their test, as a decimal.","a":"0.7","sol":"The automatic branch splits into pass and fail: 36 − 27 = 9 do not pass. The manual branch has 24 candidates, splitting into 15 who pass and 24 − 15 = 9 who do not. Total who passed = 27 + 15 = 42 — M1 for 27 + 15. Relative frequency of passing = 42/60 — M1 for 42/60. = 0.7 — A1 for 0.7.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["First complete the missing frequencies on each branch of the tree using subtraction.","Add the number of passes from both branches to get the total number of passes.","Write relative frequency = number of passes ÷ total number of candidates before dividing."],"codes":["11.01b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Two fair six-sided dice are rolled and the two numbers are multiplied together. Work out the probability that the product is greater than 20.","a":"1/6","sol":"The sample space grid has 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes — M1 for 36. Products greater than 20 are 4 × 6 and 6 × 4 (24), 5 × 5 (25), 5 × 6 and 6 × 5 (30) and 6 × 6 (36): 6 outcomes — M1 for 6 favourable outcomes. P(product > 20) = 6/36 = 1/6 — A1 for 1/6.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Every pair of scores is equally likely, so this is a counting job.","A grid with one dice down the side and the other along the top holds each pair once.","Work out how many cells the grid has, then list the pairs whose product is big enough."],"codes":["11.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 5 red counters and 3 blue counters. A counter is taken at random, its colour recorded, and it is put back. A second counter is then taken at random. Work out the probability that both counters are red.","a":"25/64","sol":"The counter is replaced, so the bag is unchanged and the two picks are independent: P(red) = 5/8 each time — M1 for 5/8 used for both picks. P(both red) = 5/8 × 5/8 — M1 for multiplying the two probabilities. = 25/64 — A1 for 25/64.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first counter goes back before the second is taken, so think about what the bag looks like for the second pick.","Two things happening one after the other is a path through a tree diagram.","Write down the probability of red on each pick, then combine them."],"codes":["11.02f"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A drawer contains 8 keys: 5 silver and 3 gold. Two keys are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Work out the probability that both keys are gold.","a":"3/28","sol":"First pick: P(gold) = 3/8. The key is not put back, so 2 gold keys are left out of 7 keys: P(second gold) = 2/7 — M1 for 2/7 as the second probability. P(both gold) = 3/8 × 2/7 — M1 for multiplying along the path. = 6/56 = 3/28 — A1 for 3/28.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The first key taken is not put back, so the drawer is different for the second pick.","Count what is left in the drawer after a gold key has been taken.","Write the probability of a gold key on each pick, then combine them along the path."],"codes":["11.02f"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. The digits 2, 6 and 9 are each used once to write a three-digit number. List every three-digit number that can be made, and use your list to work out the probability that the number is even.","a":"2/3","sol":"Fixing the hundreds digit in turn: 269, 296, 629, 692, 926, 962 — six arrangements in total — M1 for all six listed once with no repeats. A number is even when its last digit is 2 or 6: 296, 692, 926, 962 are even, and 269, 629 are odd — M1 for identifying the even ones. P(even) = 4/6 = 2/3 — A1 for 2/3.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Fix which digit goes in the hundreds place first, then list the arrangements of the other two.","A systematic list has every arrangement appearing exactly once, in a fixed order.","A number is even only if its last digit is even; check the units digit of each number in your list."],"codes":["11.02b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A four-sided spinner has sections A, B, C and D. Priya spins it 400 times and it lands on A 98 times. Work out the relative frequency of A as a decimal, and state whether the results give evidence that the spinner is biased.","a":"0.245; close to the theoretical 0.25 for a fair spinner, so no evidence of bias.","sol":"Relative frequency of A = 98/400 — M1 for 98/400. = 0.245 — A1 for 0.245. For a fair four-sided spinner the theoretical probability is 1/4 = 0.25, and 0.245 is very close to 0.25 over as many as 400 spins, so there is no evidence that the spinner is biased — B1 for the comparison with 0.25 and the conclusion.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two probabilities can be compared here: one from the spins and one from the four equal sections.","Work out the proportion of the spins that gave A, then work out what a fair spinner would give.","Write relative frequency = frequency of A ÷ number of spins before dividing."],"codes":["11.01c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a group of 40 students, 22 play football, 17 play tennis and 6 play neither sport. One student is chosen at random. Work out the probability that this student plays both sports.","a":"1/8","sol":"Students who play at least one sport = 40 − 6 = 34 — M1 for 34. By the addition law, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B), so in frequencies 22 + 17 = 39 counts the students who play both sports twice — M1 for 22 + 17 = 39. Number who play both = 39 − 34 = 5 — M1 for 5. P(both) = 5/40 = 1/8 — A1 for 1/8 (or 0.125).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["A Venn diagram with two overlapping circles holds every student exactly once.","The students who play neither sport are outside both circles, which tells you how many are inside at least one.","Add the two sports totals and compare that with the number who play at least one sport."],"codes":["11.02c","11.02a","11.02e"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bag contains 3 red counters and 4 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Work out the probability that at least one of them is red.","a":"5/7","sol":"'At least one red' is the opposite of 'no reds at all', which means both counters are blue. P(blue then blue) = 4/7 × 3/6 — M1 for 4/7 × 3/6 (the second probability reduced to 3 blue out of 6). = 12/42 = 2/7 — A1 for 2/7. P(at least one red) = 1 − P(both blue) — M1 for subtracting from 1. = 1 − 2/7 = 5/7 — A1 for 5/7.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Listing every way of getting at least one red takes three paths; there is a much shorter route.","Ask what has to happen for the event not to occur, and find the probability of that instead.","Work out the probability that both counters are blue, remembering the first is not replaced."],"codes":["11.02f","11.02e"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A biased spinner can land on red, blue or green only. P(red) = 0.35 and P(blue) = 0.45. The spinner is spun twice. Work out the probability that it lands on green exactly once.","a":"0.32","sol":"P(green) = 1 − (0.35 + 0.45) = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2, so P(not green) = 0.8 — M1 for P(green) = 0.2. Exactly one green happens two ways: green then not green, or not green then green. Each path gives 0.2 × 0.8 = 0.16 — M1 for 0.2 × 0.8. Adding the two paths: 2 × 0.16 — M1 for doubling (or adding the two equal paths). = 0.32 — A1 for 0.32.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The three colours are the only possibilities, so one probability can be found from the other two.","Draw a tree with green and not green on each pair of branches.","There is more than one order in which exactly one green can happen — find both paths."],"codes":["11.02e","11.02f","11.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A box contains red, blue and green counters only. A counter is taken at random. P(red) = 0.2 and P(blue) = 0.35. There are 18 green counters in the box. Work out how many counters there are altogether.","a":"40","sol":"P(red) + P(blue) = 0.2 + 0.35 = 0.55 — M1 for 0.55. The three colours are exhaustive, so P(green) = 1 − 0.55 = 0.45 — M1 for 0.45. The 18 green counters are 0.45 of the box, so total = 18 ÷ 0.45 — M1 for 18 ÷ 0.45. = 1800 ÷ 45 = 40 — A1 for 40.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Only three colours are in the box, so the green probability can be found from the other two.","The green counters are a known fraction of the whole box; you know the fraction and the frequency.","Find P(green) first, then write total = number of green ÷ P(green)."],"codes":["11.02e","11.01d"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Nadia rolls a dice 50 times and gets 12 sixes. Omar rolls the same dice 500 times and gets 96 sixes. Using the better of the two sets of results, estimate how many sixes would be expected in 2000 rolls of this dice.","a":"384","sol":"Omar's results should be used: a larger unbiased sample gives a relative frequency closer to the true probability — B1 for choosing Omar's 500 rolls with that reason. Estimated P(six) = 96/500 = 0.192 — M1 for 96/500. Expected number = 0.192 × 2000 — M1 for multiplying the estimated probability by 2000. = 384 — A1 for 384.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The dice may be biased, so the probability of a six has to be estimated from the trials.","Two estimates are on offer; decide which set of trials you should trust more, and why.","Write estimated P(six) = number of sixes ÷ number of rolls for the set you have chosen."],"codes":["11.01c","11.01d"]}],"exit":[{"q":"In a class of 25 students, 15 study Spanish, 8 study Art, and 3 study both. How many students study neither Spanish nor Art?","a":"5"},{"q":"A fair coin is flipped twice. Work out the probability that it lands on heads both times.","a":"1/4"},{"q":"A tree diagram gives P(first counter red) = 0.4. If the first counter is red, P(second counter red) = 0.3. Work out P(both counters red).","a":"0.12"},{"q":"A dice is rolled 25 times and gives 6 sixes. The same dice is rolled 500 times and gives 81 sixes. Which set of results gives the better estimate of the probability of a six, and why?","a":"The 500 rolls, because a larger sample's relative frequency is closer to the true probability."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The probability scale","back":"Every probability is a number from 0 to 1: 0 is impossible, 1 is certain, 0.5 is an even chance. Write probabilities as fractions, decimals or percentages, never as ratios.","code":"11.01a","open":false},{"front":"Frequency tree","back":"A tree whose branches carry frequencies rather than probabilities. Each pair of branches splits the number it came from, so the pair adds back to it, and the end numbers add back to the total.","code":"11.01b","open":false},{"front":"Relative frequency (experimental probability)","back":"Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ total number of trials. It estimates a probability when the outcomes are not equally likely.","code":"11.01c","open":false},{"front":"The effect of increasing the number of trials","back":"The relative frequency of an unbiased sample gets closer to the theoretical probability as the sample gets larger, so more trials give a better estimate.","code":"11.01c","open":false},{"front":"Theoretical probability of an event with equally likely outcomes","back":"P(event) = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of possible outcomes, given in its simplest form.","code":"11.01d","open":false},{"front":"Expected number of outcomes in n trials","back":"Expected number = probability of the outcome × number of trials. It is what should happen on average, so the actual count usually differs a little.","code":"11.01d","open":false},{"front":"Sample space (possibility space)","back":"A list, table or grid showing every outcome of one or more experiments exactly once, so that each entry is one equally likely outcome.","code":"11.02a","open":false},{"front":"Systematic listing strategy","back":"A method for listing every possible outcome exactly once, in a fixed order (for example, fixing the first item and listing every order of the rest before moving on), so nothing is missed or repeated.","code":"11.02b","open":false},{"front":"Set notation for a set of numbers or objects","back":"Braces list or describe a set, e.g. A = {even numbers}, or D = {x : 1 < x < 3} meaning every x between 1 and 3. Symbols for intersection, union and complement are not required.","code":"11.02c","open":false},{"front":"Venn diagram","back":"Overlapping circles for two groups: the overlap holds the members of both, and the box outside the circles holds the members of neither. Each member is entered once.","code":"11.02c","open":false},{"front":"Reading a tree diagram","back":"Each stage shows one branch per outcome spreading from a point; the probabilities on branches from the same point add up to 1. A later stage grows fresh branches from the end of each earlier branch.","code":"11.02d","open":false},{"front":"The addition law for two events","back":"P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Subtracting removes the overlap, which adding the two probabilities has counted twice; for mutually exclusive events P(A and B) = 0.","code":"11.02e","open":false},{"front":"The probability that an event does not happen","back":"P(not A) = 1 − P(A).","code":"11.02e","open":false},{"front":"Independent events","back":"Events where the first does not change the probability of the second, as when a counter is replaced. Then P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B).","code":"11.02f","open":false},{"front":"Dependent events (taken without replacement)","back":"The first result changes what is left for the second pick, so the total (and often the favourable count) changes. Multiply the adjusted probabilities along the tree path.","code":"11.02f","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). GIVEN on the day at Foundation tier. OCR's Foundation tier formulae sheet for exams in 2026 (J560/01–03), read on 19 September 2026 at https://www.ocr.org.uk/Images/742416-foundation-tier-formulae-sheet.pdf, prints this as 'the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another'. The specification's own 2b/6.02d does not list any probability formula among those Foundation learners must know from memory — where that is so, 2b says any formula still needed 'will be given in the assessment', and this is the one the published sheet actually provides. Know what each symbol means anyway: the subtraction removes the overlap that adding P(A) and P(B) counts twice.","For mutually exclusive events, P(A and B) = 0, so the addition law reduces to P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B). RECALL it: the sheet prints only the general rule above, not this special case, so apply it yourself whenever the two events cannot both happen.","P(not A) = 1 − P(A). RECALL it. Not on either tier's sheet.","The probabilities of an exhaustive set of mutually exclusive outcomes add up to 1. RECALL it. Not on either tier's sheet; it is the fact behind every missing entry in a table of probabilities.","Theoretical probability = number of favourable outcomes ÷ total number of equally likely outcomes. RECALL it. Not on either tier's sheet.","Relative frequency = number of times the outcome happened ÷ total number of trials. RECALL it. Not on either tier's sheet.","Expected number of outcomes = probability of the outcome × number of trials. RECALL it. Not on either tier's sheet.","For independent events, P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). RECALL it. Not on either tier's sheet: the Higher tier sheet prints only the more general multiplication rule using a conditional probability, which is beyond Foundation tier.","Provenance: since 2022 Ofqual has required a formulae sheet in every GCSE Mathematics paper, and OCR issues one per tier per series. That sheet is outside this specification document (which under 2b promises no formula sheet and lists only 6.02d's formulae as guaranteed) and is reissued each series, so read the sheet at the front of your own paper before relying on anything above being printed."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:57:13Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-solved all 16 questions and all 6 worked examples from scratch in node: every answer key is correct, every sol tally adds exactly to its m, difficulty splits 5/6/5, marks run 1-4 with no d=1 above 2 marks, ten questions are marked (non-calculator) with hand-workable numbers and the other six say a calculator may be used, all 15 cards are term-fronted with backs inside 40 words, and all ten sub-refs 11.01a-11.02f are now covered. All six faults from the previous verdict are genuinely fixed and correct: questions[8] is now 3/8 x 2/7 = 3/28 (no longer example 4's 6/10 x 5/9), questions[5] is a new 60-test frequency tree giving 42/60 = 0.7 for 11.01b, facts[9] carries OCR's paper numbers, questions[10].a now states the no-bias conclusion its B1 pays for, the exit ticket no longer repeats lesson.check, and example 0's 28 became 31 so its answer (25, re-solved) is nowhere in the setup. Nothing Higher-only has crept in — \"conditional\" appears once, in the Formulae sheet, correctly labelled as the Higher sheet's rule and beyond Foundation — and the Formulae sheet matches appendix.md exactly: the addition rule marked GIVEN as the one probability result the 2026 Foundation sheet prints, everything else marked RECALL. Publishable; the three points left are wording, not mathematics."}}},"probability-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"probability-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"OCR's Higher tier reserves strand 11.02 and content reference P9 for probability skills that Foundation does not reach: recognising which diagram suits an unfamiliar problem, counting outcomes with the product rule instead of listing them, building your own tree diagram, two-way table or Venn diagram when none is given, and working with conditional probability from first principles. These skills are examined together because a stretch question rarely tells you which picture to draw or hands you a template to fill in — you choose the representation, construct it from raw totals or percentages, and then divide inside the correct restricted group. The marks reward the method as much as the final fraction, so this room teaches you to justify the diagram you pick, multiply systematically rather than list exhaustively, and always restrict before you divide.","idea":[{"h":"Choosing the most appropriate diagram for an unstructured problem","t":"Higher tier questions often give you no diagram at all: no table, no tree, no grid. Part of the mark is recognising which representation makes the problem tractable. A sample space grid or systematic list suits a small, single- or two-stage set of equally likely outcomes where every combination matters. A two-way table suits two categories measured on the same group of items or people. A tree diagram suits events that happen one after another, especially when a probability changes at the second stage (dependent events) or several paths need adding. A Venn diagram suits overlapping categories described by 'and', 'or' and 'not'. When listing every outcome would take too long — three coins and a four-sided spinner is 32 rows — look for a shortcut, such as multiplying the probabilities for independent stages, instead of drawing the whole grid.","code":"11.02a"},{"h":"The product rule: counting outcomes without listing them","t":"When a situation has several independent stages — a PIN code, a meal from a menu, an outfit from a rail of clothes — the total number of possible outcomes is the product of the number of choices at each stage: multiply, do not list. A 4-digit PIN chosen from the digits 0 to 9 with no digit repeated has 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 5040 possible codes; listing them would be impossible in an exam. If every outcome is equally likely, the probability of one particular outcome is 1 divided by that product. The product rule replaces systematic listing once the number of outcomes is too large to write out, but check first that the stages really are independent and that ordered choices, not unordered groups, are being counted.","code":"11.02b"},{"h":"Constructing your own tree diagram, two-way table or Venn diagram","t":"Higher tier problems may ask you to build the picture yourself from a sentence of raw totals or percentages, with no frame printed on the page — and this specification does not require the set symbols for 'and', 'or' and 'not' (intersection, union, complement), so every diagram is labelled and read in words instead. For a Venn diagram, write the overlap in first from the fact that states both, then subtract from each set's total to find the 'only' regions, then subtract everything from the grand total for 'neither'. For a two-way table, put the two categories on the rows and columns, fill in every total you are given, and use subtraction to find the missing cells so each row and column adds up. For a tree diagram with no replacement, remember the second set of branches uses one fewer of whichever item was taken and one fewer overall. Once the diagram is built, conditional probabilities are read off exactly as in idea 11.02f: restrict to the named region, row, column or branch, then divide.","code":"11.02c"},{"h":"Conditional probability from first principles: P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B)","t":"P(A given B) is the probability of A once you know B has already happened, so every case in which B did not happen is thrown away first: P(A given B) = (number of cases with both A and B) ÷ (number of cases with B). Written in probabilities, the same statement is P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B), which the exam expects you to derive or apply informally rather than just quote — for example, in a random cut of a pack of 52 cards, P(diamond given red) is worked out directly from the 26 red cards, not from all 52. Rearranging gives P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B). Two warnings: P(A given B) and P(B given A) are usually different numbers, because the same overlap is divided by two different totals; and A and B are independent exactly when P(A given B) = P(A) — if knowing B changes nothing, the events do not affect each other, and this is the formal test the specification names.","code":"11.02f"}],"examples":[{"title":"Diamonds from a red card","setup":"A card is drawn at random from a standard pack of 52 playing cards. There are 26 red cards (13 hearts and 13 diamonds) and 26 black cards. Given that the card drawn is red, work out the probability that it is a diamond. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["The condition 'given that the card is red' restricts the choice to the 26 red cards, not all 52.","Count the diamonds inside that restricted group of 26: there are 13, since every diamond is red.","P(diamond given red) = 13/26.","Simplify: 13/26 = 1/2."],"cues":["Which cards are still being considered once you are told the card is red?","Which of the diamonds are inside that restricted group?","Which two numbers go on the top and bottom of the fraction?","Can the fraction be simplified?"]},{"title":"Building a two-set Venn diagram from raw totals","setup":"At an office of 60 employees, 34 employees use the instant-messaging app and 27 employees use the video-call app; 15 employees use both apps. No diagram is given. Construct a Venn diagram for the 60 employees, then work out the probability that an employee chosen at random from the employees who use the messaging app also uses the video-call app. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["Start in the middle: 15 goes in the overlap of the two circles, since that is the only total describing both apps at once.","Work outwards by subtraction: messaging only = 34 − 15 = 19, video-call only = 27 − 15 = 12, and neither = 60 − 19 − 15 − 12 = 14.","Conditioning on 'uses the messaging app' leaves only the messaging circle, which holds 19 + 15 = 34 employees.","P(video-call given messaging) = 15/34."],"cues":["Which single number can be written into the diagram before any subtraction?","Which three totals do you still need, and how do you get each from what is given?","Once you know the employee uses the messaging app, which region of the diagram is still being considered?","Which two numbers form the fraction?"]},{"title":"Choosing a diagram for a three-colour bag","setup":"A bag contains 3 red, 4 blue and 5 green counters, 12 counters altogether. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Decide which diagram best shows this situation, and use it to work out the probability that the two counters are different colours. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["A sample space grid would need 12 × 11 = 132 rows, so a tree diagram grouped by colour is the more appropriate choice here.","P(same colour) is the sum of three paths: red-red = 3/12 × 2/11, blue-blue = 4/12 × 3/11, green-green = 5/12 × 4/11.","Adding the three paths over a common denominator of 132: (6 + 12 + 20)/132 = 38/132 = 19/66.","'Different colours' is the complement of 'same colour', so P(different) = 1 − 19/66 = 47/66."],"cues":["Roughly how many ordered pairs would a full sample space have to list?","How many same-colour paths does the tree have, and what is each one's probability?","How do you combine the three same-colour paths into a single probability?","How does the probability of 'different colours' relate to the probability of 'same colours'?"]},{"title":"The product rule for a PIN code","setup":"A 4-digit PIN code is formed by choosing 4 different digits from 0 to 9, with no digit used twice, and the order of the digits matters. Use the product rule to work out how many different PIN codes are possible.","steps":["The first digit can be any of the 10 digits, so there are 10 choices for it.","The second digit cannot repeat the first, so there are only 9 digits left to choose from.","The third digit has 8 remaining choices, and the fourth digit has 7 remaining choices.","Multiply the choices at each stage: 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 5040 different PIN codes."],"cues":["How many digits are available for the very first position?","Once the first digit is fixed, how many digits remain for the second position?","What happens to the number of remaining choices at each further position?","How do you combine the number of choices at the four positions into a single total?"]},{"title":"Constructing a tree diagram without replacement","setup":"A bag contains 5 orange beads and 3 purple beads, 8 beads altogether. Two beads are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. No tree diagram is given. Construct one, and use it to work out the probability that both beads are purple. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","steps":["First set of branches: P(orange) = 5/8 and P(purple) = 3/8.","After a purple bead is taken, 2 purple and 5 orange beads remain out of 7, so the second purple branch shows P(purple given purple) = 2/7.","Multiply along the purple-purple path: 3/8 × 2/7 = 6/56.","Simplify: 6/56 = 3/28."],"cues":["What are the probabilities on the very first set of branches?","How many beads of each colour are left once a purple bead has been taken, and how many beads in total?","How do you combine the two branch probabilities along a single path?","Can the resulting fraction be simplified?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which total becomes the denominator when you work out P(A given B) from a two-way table?","a":"The row or column total of B — the number of cases in which the condition already holds — not the grand total."},{"q":"50 people were surveyed. 30 own a bike, and 18 of those 30 also own a scooter. Work out P(scooter given bike).","a":"18/30 = 3/5"},{"q":"A restaurant offers 4 soups and 5 breads. Use the product rule to find how many soup-and-bread combinations there are.","a":"4 × 5 = 20"},{"q":"A bag has 5 red and 3 blue discs. One disc is taken and not replaced, and it is red. What is P(the next disc taken is blue)?","a":"3/7"},{"q":"P(A given B) = P(A). What does this tell you about A and B?","a":"They are independent: knowing B has happened does not change the probability of A."}]},"room":{"facts":["P(A given B) = (number of cases with both A and B) ÷ (number of cases with B): the condition restricts which cases are counted, so it sets the denominator.","P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B), and rearranged, P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B).","The product rule for counting: when a situation has several independent stages, the total number of outcomes is the product of the number of choices at each stage.","Choosing a diagram: a sample space or systematic list for a small set of equally likely combined outcomes; a two-way table for two categories on the same group; a tree diagram for sequential (possibly dependent) events; a Venn diagram for overlapping categories described in words.","This specification does not require the set symbols for 'and', 'or' and 'not' (intersection, union, complement): every diagram is built and read using those words.","Filling a Venn diagram of frequencies: write the overlap in first, subtract it from each set total to get the 'only' regions, then subtract all three from the grand total to get 'neither'.","Without replacement, the second set of branches on a tree diagram uses one fewer of whichever item was taken and one fewer overall, so the second probability depends on the first.","A and B are independent when P(A given B) = P(A), equivalently when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B); otherwise they are dependent.","P(A given B) and P(B given A) are usually different, because the same overlap is divided by two different totals.","OCR's Higher tier is assessed by three papers, J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 5 (J560/05) is non-calculator; Higher tier is graded 9 to 4, with an allowed grade 3."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) In a class of 24 students, 15 students study Spanish, and 9 of those 15 students also study French. A student is chosen at random from the students who study Spanish. Work out the probability that this student also studies French. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/5","sol":"Condition restricts to the 15 Spanish students (denominator); the numerator is the 9 who also study French: 9/15 (M1) = 3/5 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The words 'chosen from the students who study Spanish' tell you which group you are now working inside.","A conditional probability only counts the cases in which the condition already holds, so the denominator is not the whole class.","Write the number who study both languages over the number of Spanish students, then simplify."],"codes":["11.02f"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) For two events A and B, P(B) = 0.6 and P(A and B) = 0.15. Work out P(A given B).","a":"0.25","sol":"P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B) = 0.15 ÷ 0.6 (M1) = 0.25 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The formulae sheet prints a rule linking P(A and B), P(A given B) and P(B).","Make P(A given B) the subject by dividing both sides of that rule by the probability of the condition.","Divide the probability of both events by the probability of the event you're told has happened."],"codes":["11.02f"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A café offers 3 types of sandwich and 4 types of drink. A meal deal is one sandwich and one drink. Use the product rule to work out how many different meal deals are possible.","a":"12","sol":"Product rule: number of sandwich choices × number of drink choices = 3 × 4 (M1) = 12 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Each meal deal pairs one choice from the first list with one choice from the second.","The product rule multiplies the number of choices at each stage instead of listing every meal deal.","Multiply the number of sandwich types by the number of drink types."],"codes":["11.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A spinner has 4 equal sections numbered 1 to 4 and is spun once, then a fair coin is flipped once. Decide which diagram lets you list every outcome most clearly, then use it to find P(spinner shows an even number and coin shows heads).","a":"1/4","sol":"A sample space diagram lists all 4 × 2 = 8 equally likely outcomes clearly (M1); 2 of the 8 outcomes are an even spinner number with heads, so P = 2/8 = 1/4 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about which single diagram could show every spinner-and-coin outcome in one grid.","List or count how many of the equally likely outcomes satisfy both conditions at once.","Put the number of outcomes that fit over the total number of outcomes."],"codes":["11.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A sports club has 90 members. 54 of the members play tennis and the rest do not. Of the members who play tennis, 20 also play badminton. Altogether 35 of the 90 members play badminton. Construct a two-way table for these members, then work out the probability that a randomly chosen member who plays badminton also plays tennis. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"4/7","sol":"Not-tennis = 90 − 54 = 36; badminton-and-not-tennis = 35 − 20 = 15, completing the table (M1). Conditioning on 'plays badminton' leaves the badminton row, total 35 (M1). P(tennis given badminton) = 20/35 = 4/7 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The two conditions ('plays tennis', 'plays badminton') sit on different rows and columns of the same table.","Use the totals you are given to complete every missing cell before dividing.","The condition 'plays badminton' tells you which row or column total becomes your denominator."],"codes":["11.02c","11.02f"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. In a survey of 50 people, 28 people like tea, 19 people like coffee and 9 people like both. Construct a Venn diagram for these results, then work out P(likes coffee given likes tea). Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"9/28","sol":"Overlap of 9 goes in the middle of the Venn diagram, from the people who like both (M1). Tea-only = 28 − 9 = 19, so the tea circle (tea-only + both) = 19 + 9 = 28, which is the denominator once you condition on 'likes tea' (M1). P(coffee given tea) = 9/28 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["No diagram is drawn for you, so decide which one fits two overlapping categories.","Fill the overlap of the Venn diagram first, since that is the only figure describing both groups at once.","Divide the overlap by the total of the circle named by the condition."],"codes":["11.02c"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A restaurant offers 5 starters, 6 mains and 3 desserts. A three-course meal is one starter, one main and one dessert. Use the product rule to work out the total number of different three-course meals, then work out the probability that a meal chosen at random is a customer's favourite combination.","a":"1/90","sol":"Product rule: total meals = 5 × 6 × 3 = 90 (M1). Every meal is equally likely, so the favourite combination is 1 of the 90 (M1). P = 1/90 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Each course is chosen independently of the others, so think about how the total number of meals is built up.","Multiply the number of choices at each of the three stages rather than listing every meal.","Once you know the total number of equally likely meals, the favourite one is just one of them."],"codes":["11.02b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. Three coins are flipped and a four-sided spinner numbered 1 to 4 is spun. Choose an appropriate method to work out the probability of getting exactly two heads and the spinner landing on an odd number. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"3/16","sol":"Listing all 8 equally likely coin outcomes (HHH, HHT, HTH, THH, HTT, THT, TTH, TTT) shows exactly 3 give two heads, so P(two heads) = 3/8 (M1). The spinner gives an odd number on 2 of its 4 sides, so P(odd) = 1/2 (M1). The coin flips and the spin are independent, so multiply: 3/8 × 1/2 = 3/16 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Two separate random processes are happening here, and 'exactly two heads' and 'an odd spinner number' both need considering.","Work out each probability separately before deciding whether to multiply or add them.","Combine the two probabilities for the coins and the spinner using the rule for independent events."],"codes":["11.02a"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A bag contains 8 yellow counters and n purple counters. Two counters are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. The probability that both counters are yellow is 4/15. Work out the value of n.","a":"n = 7","sol":"P(both yellow) = 8/(8+n) × 7/(7+n) (M1). Setting this equal to 4/15 and cross-multiplying: 15 × 56 = 4(8+n)(7+n), so 840 = 4(n² + 15n + 56) (M1). Dividing by 4 and rearranging: n² + 15n − 154 = 0 (M1). Factorising, (n − 7)(n + 22) = 0, and n cannot be negative, so n = 7 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two probabilities on the tree diagram both involve the unknown n, so write them as algebraic fractions.","Set the product of the two branch probabilities equal to the given value and clear the fractions by cross-multiplying.","Rearranging gives a quadratic in n; solve it and reject any negative root."],"codes":["11.02f"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A drawer contains 8 black socks and 6 grey socks. Two socks are taken at random, one after the other, without replacement. Given that the two socks are the same colour, work out the probability that both socks are black. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"28/43","sol":"Both black: 8/14 × 7/13 = 56/182 (M1). Both grey: 6/14 × 5/13 = 30/182 (M1). P(same colour) = 56/182 + 30/182 = 86/182 (M1). Conditioning on 'same colour', P(black given same colour) = 56/86 = 28/43 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["You are told the outcome already belongs to one of two possible paths, and asked how likely the first of them is.","Work out the probability of each same-colour path before you use the conditional probability formula.","Divide the probability of the path you want by the total probability of 'same colour'."],"codes":["11.02c","11.02f"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A computer password consists of 3 characters, each chosen from the letters A to J (10 letters), and a letter may be used more than once. Using the product rule, work out how many different passwords are possible. A password is chosen at random by someone who does not know the correct one; work out the probability that they guess it correctly on their first attempt.","a":"1000; 1/1000","sol":"Product rule: 10 × 10 × 10 = 1000 possible passwords (M1, A1). Each password is equally likely, so P(correct on first attempt) = 1/1000 (B1).","m":3,"d":3,"hints":["There is no sensible way to list every three-character password by hand.","Multiply the number of choices at each of the three positions to count the passwords.","Once you know the total number of equally likely passwords, a single correct guess is one of them."],"codes":["11.02a","11.02b"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A college has 480 students. 5/12 of the students take Music. Of the students who take Music, 30% also take Drama. Of the students who do not take Music, 15% take Drama. Construct a two-way table of expected frequencies for the 480 students, then work out the probability that a student chosen at random from the students who take Drama also takes Music. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"10/17","sol":"Students who take Music = 5/12 × 480 = 200, so 280 do not (M1). Drama among Music students = 30% × 200 = 60; Drama among the rest = 15% × 280 = 42 (M1). Total taking Drama = 60 + 42 = 102 (M1). P(Music given Drama) = 60/102 = 10/17 (A1).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two percentages apply to two different groups of students, so treat them separately.","Turn every fraction and percentage into a number of students, then build the two-way table.","Divide the Music-and-Drama cell by the total number of students who take Drama."],"codes":["11.02c","11.02f"]},{"q":"A calculator may be used. A shop lets a customer build an outfit from 4 jackets, 5 pairs of trousers and 2 pairs of shoes. Use the product rule to work out how many different outfits there are, then work out the probability that an outfit chosen at random includes one particular jacket. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"1/4","sol":"Total outfits = 4 × 5 × 2 = 40 (M1). Outfits with the particular jacket fixed = 1 × 5 × 2 = 10 (M1). P = 10/40 = 1/4 (A1).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The particular jacket is fixed, but the trousers and shoes can still be any of the choices.","Work out the total number of outfits and the number that include the fixed jacket separately.","Divide the number of outfits with that jacket by the total number of outfits."],"codes":["11.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) In a group of 20 people, 11 people like puzzles, 13 people like games, and 6 people like both. Construct a Venn diagram for these 20 people, then work out the probability that a person chosen at random from the 20 likes neither puzzles nor games. Give your answer as a fraction in its simplest form.","a":"1/10","sol":"Overlap of 6 goes in the middle, since 6 people like both puzzles and games; only puzzles = 11 − 6 = 5 and only games = 13 − 6 = 7, so neither = 20 − 6 − 5 − 7 = 2 (M1). P(neither) = 2/20 = 1/10 (A1).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Decide what the overlap of the two circles represents before drawing anything else.","Use the fact you're given about 'both' to fill the middle of the Venn diagram first.","Subtract the overlap from each set's total to find the 'only' regions, then subtract everything from the grand total for 'neither'."],"codes":["11.02c"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A tree diagram for two counters taken from a bag without replacement shows: first counter P(red) = 0.5; second counter, given the first was red, P(red) = 0.3 and P(blue) = 0.7. Read from the tree: what is P(second counter is blue given the first was red)?","a":"0.7"},{"q":"A vending machine order is one of 4 snack types, one of 3 sizes and one of 2 payment methods. Use the product rule to work out how many different orders are possible.","a":"24"},{"q":"In a survey of 40 people, 22 like walking, 15 like cycling, and 8 like both. A person who likes cycling is chosen at random. Work out the probability that they also like walking.","a":"8/15"},{"q":"For events A and B, P(A) = 0.3, P(B) = 0.5 and P(A and B) = 0.15. Are A and B independent?","a":"Yes - P(A) × P(B) = 0.3 × 0.5 = 0.15 = P(A and B)."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Conditional probability","back":"The probability of an event worked out on the assumption that another event has already happened, written P(A given B). Only the cases in which B holds are counted.","code":"11.02f","open":false},{"front":"P(A given B) in terms of probabilities","back":"P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B). It is the multiplication rule P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B) rearranged, so the divisor is always the probability of the condition.","code":"11.02f","open":false},{"front":"The multiplication rule for P(A and B)","back":"P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B). It is printed on the OCR Higher tier formulae sheet, so at Higher tier it is given on the day.","code":"11.02f","open":false},{"front":"Test for independent events","back":"A and B are independent when P(A given B) = P(A), equivalently when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). If the condition changes the probability, the events are dependent.","code":"11.02f","open":false},{"front":"P(A given B) counted from frequencies","back":"P(A given B) = number of cases with both A and B ÷ number of cases with B. The condition sets the denominator, which is never the grand total unless B is certain.","code":"11.02f","open":false},{"front":"The product rule for counting","back":"When a situation has several independent stages, the total number of outcomes is the product of the number of choices at each stage: multiply rather than list them all.","code":"11.02b","open":false},{"front":"Probability of one particular outcome among equally likely ones","back":"If a situation has N equally likely outcomes, found using the product rule, the probability of one named outcome is 1 ÷ N.","code":"11.02b","open":false},{"front":"Choosing between a sample space, a two-way table, a tree diagram and a Venn diagram","back":"Sample space: a small set of equally likely combined outcomes. Two-way table: two categories on one group. Tree diagram: events in sequence, possibly dependent. Venn diagram: overlapping categories described in words.","code":"11.02a","open":false},{"front":"When a full sample space grid is impractical","back":"When the number of possible outcomes is very large (for example, several dice or several stages), listing every outcome is impractical; multiply probabilities or use the product rule instead.","code":"11.02a","open":false},{"front":"Constructing a Venn diagram from raw totals","back":"Write the overlap in first from the fact that states both, subtract it from each set's total for the 'only' regions, then subtract everything from the grand total for 'neither'.","code":"11.02c","open":false},{"front":"Constructing a two-way table from raw totals","back":"Put the two categories on the rows and columns, enter every total given, then use subtraction so that every row and column adds up correctly to find the missing cells.","code":"11.02c","open":false},{"front":"Constructing a tree diagram without replacement","back":"The first set of branches uses the original numbers; the second set uses one fewer of whichever item was taken and one fewer in total, so it is built, not copied from the first.","code":"11.02c","open":false},{"front":"Set notation on this specification","back":"The symbols for intersection, union and complement (and, or, not) are not required; Venn diagrams here are built and described in words instead.","code":"11.02c","open":false},{"front":"Name one context where the product rule is used rather than listing outcomes","back":"A PIN code or password; a menu combination of starter, main and dessert; an outfit built from separate items of clothing.","code":"11.02b","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["P(A and B) = P(A given B) × P(B). GIVEN on the day at Higher tier. Specification 6.02d does not list this among the formulae learners must know, but OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet for exams from 2026 prints the multiplication rule for the probability of one outcome and another using a conditional probability. Where 6.02d and the sheet disagree, the sheet governs what is in front of you. Know what it means anyway: the probability of both is the probability of the condition times the probability of the other event once the condition has happened.","P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). GIVEN on the day at Higher tier (prior knowledge from P8). OCR's Higher tier sheet also prints the addition rule for the probability of one outcome or another; the subtraction removes the overlap that adding P(A) and P(B) counts twice, which is useful when reading a Venn diagram.","P(A given B) = P(A and B) ÷ P(B). RECALL or derive it. Only the product form is printed on the sheet, so the rearrangement is yours to make; it is the form nearly every conditional-probability question needs.","P(A given B) = number of cases with both A and B ÷ number of cases with B. RECALL it. This counting form is in neither 6.02d nor on either tier's sheet, and it is what you use on a two-way table, a tree of frequencies or a Venn diagram.","The product rule for counting: total number of outcomes = product of the number of choices at each independent stage. RECALL it. Not in 6.02d and on neither sheet; it replaces listing when the number of outcomes is too large to write out.","Independence: A and B are independent when P(A given B) = P(A), equivalently when P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B). RECALL it. Neither line appears on the sheet, which prints the general multiplication rule only.","Without replacement, the second branch of a tree uses one fewer item of the chosen kind and one fewer item in total, so P(second given first) has a denominator one smaller than the first branch. RECALL it. Not in 6.02d and on neither sheet.","P(not A) = 1 − P(A), and inside a condition P(not A given B) = 1 − P(A given B). RECALL both. Neither is in 6.02d's lists nor on either tier's sheet."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:03:28Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.88,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit: every one of the nine listed fixes has landed and is correct - facts[9] now carries J560/04-06, 100 marks, 1 hour 30 minutes, Paper 5 non-calculator and grades 9-4; questions[10] is a clean two-value ask with sol M1+A1+B1 = m = 3; questions[13] (neither) re-solves to 2/20 = 1/10 with M1+A1 = 2; example 2 cue 0 no longer leaks 132 and example 0 step 1 now counts rather than restates. All 14 answers, 5 worked examples, 5 check and 4 new exit items re-solved independently in node and every one agrees; mark tallies all add to m, difficulty splits 5/5/4, six questions marked (non-calculator) with hand-workable numbers, card backs 21-34 words, and the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md (the Higher sheet's addition rule and conditional multiplication rule GIVEN, the rearrangement, counting form, product rule, independence and complements RECALL). Publishable; what is left is the duplicated Venn 'neither' between exit[2] and questions[13] and the impossible spinner tree in exit[0]."}}},"statistics":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"statistics","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Statistics is a whole strand of OCR 12: sampling, choosing the right chart, and analysing data with averages, spread, misrepresentation and scatter diagrams. It is ordinary arithmetic wrapped in real data, so it is some of the most reliable marks on all three Foundation papers — Paper 2 tests it without a calculator, so the numbers must be handled by hand, while Papers 1 and 3 allow a calculator for the same ideas. The examiner's favourite traps are comparing two data sets with only an average and no measure of spread, reading a line of best fit far outside the data it was drawn from, and being fooled by a chart whose axis does not start at zero. Learn to write the full comparison sentence, to name extrapolation, and to check where an axis starts, and this strand becomes routine marks.","idea":[{"h":"Populations and samples","t":"A population is every member of the group you are interested in; a sample is the part of it you actually measure. Because measuring everybody is usually impossible, you infer: work out the fraction of the sample with a property and multiply it by the size of the population to estimate the population figure. That answer is an estimate, never a certainty. Simple random sampling means every member of the population has an equal chance of being chosen, usually by numbering the population and picking numbers at random. Two things decide how much weight an estimate carries — how big the sample is, because a small sample swings wildly on a few unusual answers, and how it was chosen, because a sample that is not random is biased and will not represent the population however large it is.","code":"12.01a"},{"h":"Choosing and building a chart for the data","t":"A frequency table counts how many times each outcome happens, and a table can be designed with rows or columns that classify the data in the way a question needs. For categorical data (colours, sports, destinations) use a bar chart with equal-width bars and gaps between them, a pictogram with a key saying what one symbol is worth, or a pie chart, where each item's angle is (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°. A composite or multiple bar chart shows several categories side by side or stacked for comparison. For ungrouped discrete numerical data (number of pets, number of goals) use a vertical line chart: a thin line at each value with height equal to its frequency. For time series data — a value recorded at regular times — use a table and a line graph with time along the horizontal axis, points joined in time order so trends, including seasonal variation, can be seen.","code":"12.02a"},{"h":"Summary statistics: averages, spread and comparing populations","t":"The mean is the total of the values divided by how many there are; from a frequency table it is Σfx ÷ Σf, and from a grouped table you use each class midpoint for x, which is why the answer is only an estimate of the mean. The median is the middle value once the data are in order — the ((n + 1)/2)th value, or the mean of the two middle values when n is even. The mode is the most common value, and for grouped data the modal class is the class with the largest frequency. The range, largest value − smallest value, measures spread. To describe a population, choose the average that suits the question and say what it shows in context; to compare two populations, use 'like for like' summary values — the same average and the same measure of spread for each — and say which is larger on average and which is more consistent. Each type of average has advantages and disadvantages: the mean uses every value but is pulled by extreme ones, the median ignores extremes but ignores most of the data's actual sizes, and the mode can fail to exist or fail to be unique.","code":"12.03a"},{"h":"Misrepresenting data","t":"A chart can be technically accurate and still give a false impression. The commonest trick is a vertical axis that does not start at zero: only the part of each bar above the break is shown, so a small real difference between two bars can be stretched to look enormous, or a large difference squashed to look small. Other ways a chart misleads: unequal or missing scale labels, so the reader cannot tell what a gridline is worth; a scale that changes part-way up an axis; 3-D or picture effects that distort area or volume; or a pictogram whose symbols are different sizes rather than different counts. Spotting misrepresentation means checking the axis starts at zero (or noticing the break, usually shown as a zig-zag), checking the scale is even, and working out the true values and the true difference between them for yourself.","code":"12.03b"},{"h":"Scatter graphs, correlation and the line of best fit","t":"A scatter graph plots two variables measured on the same items, one on each axis, as unjoined points, and this is bivariate data. If the points rise from bottom left to top right there is positive correlation; if they fall from top left to bottom right, negative correlation; if there is no pattern, no correlation. The closer the points lie to a straight line the stronger the correlation, so describe it with both words: 'strong negative', 'weak positive', and always interpret it in the context of the two variables named, never just as an abstract pattern. A line of best fit is a single straight line drawn by eye through the middle of the points, following the trend with roughly as many points above as below, and it is used to predict: read up from the known value to the line and across. Predicting inside the range of the data is interpolation and is fairly safe; predicting outside it is extrapolation and is unreliable, because nothing says the trend continues there. However strong the correlation, it does not indicate causation — a link is not proof that one variable causes the other.","code":"12.03c"},{"h":"Outliers","t":"An outlier is a value that does not fit the pattern of the rest of the data — far from the other values in a list, or a point sitting well away from the trend on a scatter graph. Outliers can arise from real but unusual cases or from errors in how the data were recorded, so once you spot one it is worth suggesting why it might have happened. An outlier inflates the range, because it is often the largest or smallest value, and it pulls the mean towards itself while barely moving the median, so the median often describes a data set with an outlier more fairly than the mean does. On a scatter graph, an outlier is a point that lies a long way from the line of best fit that the rest of the points follow; it is usually left out when the line of best fit is drawn, since including it would distort the trend for every other point.","code":"12.03d"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a frequency table into pie chart angles","setup":"A class of students each chose one favourite fruit. 14 chose apple, 10 chose banana, 9 chose orange and 7 chose pear. These results are to be shown on a pie chart. Work out the angle of each of the four sectors.","steps":["Add the frequencies: 14 + 10 + 9 + 7 = 40 students altogether","A full turn is 360°, so one student is worth 360 ÷ 40 = 9°","Apple: 14 × 9 = 126°","Banana: 10 × 9 = 90°","Orange: 9 × 9 = 81°","Pear: 7 × 9 = 63°","The four sectors are 126°, 90°, 81° and 63°, which check to 126 + 90 + 81 + 63 = 360° as they should"],"cues":["How many students are there altogether?","How many degrees are in a full turn, and how many students share them?","Which fruit was chosen most often, so which sector is the largest?","Which fruit was the next most popular?","Which fruit comes third?","Which frequency has not been used yet?","What should the four angles add up to, and do they?"]},{"title":"Mean, median, mode, range and an outlier","setup":"In seven football matches a team scored 2, 3, 3, 5, 6, 8 and 15 goals. Work out the mean, the median, the mode and the range of the number of goals, say which score could be an outlier, and work out the mean again with that score left out. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Add the scores: 2 + 3 + 3 + 5 + 6 + 8 + 15 = 42, then divide by 7 to get a mean of 6 goals","The list is already in order and there are seven values, so the median is the 4th one: 5 goals","3 appears twice and every other score once, so the mode is 3 goals","Range = 15 − 2 = 13 goals","15 sits far above the other six scores, so it is a possible outlier; leaving it out, the mean is (42 − 15) ÷ 6 = 27 ÷ 6 = 4.5 goals"],"cues":["What do you divide the total by to reach the mean?","Which position in the list is the middle one?","Which score appears more than once?","Which two values does the range use?","Which score sits a long way from the rest, and what happens to the total when it goes?"]},{"title":"Reading a scatter graph and its line of best fit","setup":"A scatter graph plots the number of hours each of 10 students revised, from 1 hour to 9 hours, against their mark out of 60 in a test. The points rise steadily from bottom left to top right and lie close to a straight line. A line of best fit is drawn and passes through the points (2, 18) and (8, 48). Describe the correlation, use the line to estimate the mark of a student who revised for 5 hours, explain why the line should not be used for a student who revised for 20 hours, and state what the graph cannot prove.","steps":["Rising from left to right with the points clustered tightly around the line: the correlation is strong and positive","Gradient of the line = (48 − 18) ÷ (8 − 2) = 30 ÷ 6 = 5 marks for each extra hour","5 hours is 3 hours further on than 2 hours, so the estimated mark is 18 + 3 × 5 = 33 marks","20 hours lies outside the 1 to 9 hours the data cover, so reading the line there is extrapolation and the trend may not continue","Correlation does not indicate causation, so the graph cannot show that revising is what caused the higher marks"],"cues":["Which way do the points go, and how tightly do they sit around the line?","Which two points on the line give you its steepness?","How far along is 5 hours from the first known point, and what does each hour add?","What range of hours does the data actually cover?","Does a link between two variables show that one makes the other happen?"]},{"title":"Using a sample to estimate a figure for a whole population","setup":"A school has 600 students. A random sample of 40 students is asked how they travel to school, and 14 of them walk. Estimate how many of the 600 students walk to school, and give two reasons why that estimate may not be accurate. A calculator may be used.","steps":["The fraction of the sample who walk is 14 out of 40, which is 14 ÷ 40 = 0.35","Assuming the sample behaves like the whole school, the estimate is 0.35 × 600 = 210 students","A sample of 40 is small next to 600, so a few unusual answers would change the estimate a lot","If the 40 students were not really chosen at random — all from one year group, say — the sample is biased and does not represent the school"],"cues":["What fraction of the sample walks, written as a decimal?","What do you multiply that fraction by to reach the whole school?","How large is the sample next to the whole school?","What could make the chosen students unlike the rest of the school?"]},{"title":"An estimate of the mean from a grouped frequency table","setup":"The table shows the times, t minutes, that 40 people waited at a bus stop. 0 < t ≤ 10: frequency 6. 10 < t ≤ 20: frequency 15. 20 < t ≤ 30: frequency 12. 30 < t ≤ 40: frequency 7. Write down the modal class, find the class that contains the median, and work out an estimate of the mean waiting time. A calculator may be used.","steps":["Fifteen is the biggest of the four frequencies, so the modal class is 10 < t ≤ 20","Running totals are 6, 21, 33 and 40; with 40 values the median is between the 20th and 21st, and both of those lie in 10 < t ≤ 20","Midpoints of the four classes are 5, 15, 25 and 35 minutes","Σfx = 5 × 6 + 15 × 15 + 25 × 12 + 35 × 7 = 30 + 225 + 300 + 245 = 800","Estimate of the mean = 800 ÷ 40 = 20 minutes"],"cues":["Which class holds the most people?","Which value or values sit in the middle when there are 40 of them?","What single time can stand for everybody in a class?","How do you combine each midpoint with its frequency?","What do you divide that total by?"]},{"title":"Spotting a misleading bar chart","setup":"A newspaper bar chart compares the average house price in Town A and Town B last year. The vertical axis starts at £150 000 instead of £0, with gridlines every £10 000. Town A's bar reaches £190 000 and Town B's bar reaches £160 000, and in the printed chart Town A's bar looks four times as tall as Town B's. Explain what is misleading about the chart and find the true percentage by which Town A's average price exceeds Town B's.","steps":["The axis starts at £150 000, not £0, so only the part of each bar above £150 000 is actually drawn","Above the break, Town A's bar shows 190 000 − 150 000 = £40 000 and Town B's shows 160 000 − 150 000 = £10 000","£40 000 is four times £10 000, which is why Town A's bar looks four times as tall as Town B's","The true prices are £190 000 and £160 000, a difference of 190 000 − 160 000 = £30 000","As a percentage of Town B's price: 30 000 ÷ 160 000 × 100 = 18.75%, so Town A's price is about 19% higher — nowhere near four times as much"],"cues":["Where does the vertical axis actually start, and what does that hide?","How tall does each bar look above that starting value?","What is the ratio between those two visible heights?","What are the two towns' actual prices, and what is the real difference between them?","What percentage of Town B's price is that difference?"]}],"check":[{"q":"In a survey of 20 people, 6 chose cycling. What angle would cycling get on a pie chart of the results?","a":"108° (360 ÷ 20 = 18°, then 6 × 18 = 108°)"},{"q":"Write down the median and the range of 8, 3, 10, 5, 19.","a":"Median 8, range 16"},{"q":"A scatter graph of the outdoor temperature against the number of hot drinks sold has points falling from top left to bottom right, close to a line. Describe the correlation.","a":"Strong negative correlation"},{"q":"A sample of 50 eggs from a large delivery contains 4 cracked eggs. The delivery holds 1500 eggs. Estimate how many are cracked, and give one reason why the estimate may be unreliable.","a":"120 cracked eggs (4 ÷ 50 × 1500); the sample of 50 is small compared with 1500, or it may not have been taken from all parts of the delivery, so it may not be representative"}]},"room":{"facts":["Pie chart: the angle for an item = (its frequency ÷ the total frequency) × 360°, so one unit of frequency is worth 360° ÷ total frequency, and all the angles must add to 360°.","Mean = total of the values ÷ number of values. From a frequency table, mean = Σfx ÷ Σf; for grouped data, use each class midpoint as x, so the result is only an estimate of the mean.","Median: put the values in order; for n values it is the ((n + 1)/2)th one, and when n is even it is the mean of the two middle values. Mode = the most common value; modal class = the class with the largest frequency.","Range = largest value − smallest value. An outlier is a value far from the rest: on a list it inflates the range and pulls the mean towards it (the median often describes the data better); on a scatter graph it is a point far from the line of best fit.","Estimating a population figure from a sample: estimate = (number in the sample with the property ÷ sample size) × population size. It is an estimate, and its reliability depends on the sample size and on whether the sample was chosen at random and is representative.","A comparison of two data sets must name an average and a measure of spread, both in context — for example 'A has the higher median and the smaller range, so A is larger on average and more consistent'.","A chart misrepresents data when its vertical axis does not start at zero, its scale is uneven or unlabelled, or its symbols vary in size: always check the axis and work out the true values before comparing bars by eye.","Correlation does not indicate causation, and a line of best fit should only be read inside the range of the data (interpolation); reading it outside (extrapolation) is unreliable.","OCR's GCSE (9–1) Mathematics J560 is assessed by three papers per tier, each 100 marks and 1 hour 30 minutes long: J560/01–03 for Foundation (grades 5–1) and J560/04–06 for Higher (grades 9–4). Paper 2 (Foundation) and Paper 5 (Higher) are non-calculator; the other four papers allow a calculator."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The daily maximum temperatures in a town for six days were 7 °C, 11 °C, 4 °C, 9 °C, 13 °C and 6 °C. Work out the range of these temperatures.","a":"9 °C","sol":"The largest temperature is 13 °C and the smallest is 4 °C. Range = 13 − 4 = 9 °C. B1 for 9 °C with its unit (total 1 mark).","m":1,"d":1,"hints":["The range is a measure of how spread out the data are, not an average.","Only two of the six readings are used: the biggest and the smallest.","The first line is (largest temperature) − (smallest temperature) — pick those two out of the list first."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) Eight students scored 12, 15, 9, 20, 15, 7, 11 and 18 marks in a spelling test. Work out the median mark.","a":"13.5 marks","sol":"In order: 7, 9, 11, 12, 15, 15, 18, 20. There are 8 values, so the median is the mean of the 4th and 5th: (12 + 15) ÷ 2 = 27 ÷ 2 = 13.5. M1 for the values written in order (or for identifying 12 and 15 as the middle pair), A1 for 13.5 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The median is the middle value, so the order the marks are listed in is not the order you need.","With an even number of values there is no single middle one, so two values share the job.","The first line is to rewrite the eight marks from smallest to largest — then look at the two in the centre."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) 30 people were asked to name their favourite hot drink and 12 of them said tea. The results are shown on a pie chart. Work out the angle of the sector for tea.","a":"144°","sol":"The 30 people share a full turn of 360°, so one person is worth 360 ÷ 30 = 12°. Tea: 12 × 12 = 144°. M1 for 360 ÷ 30 = 12° (oe, such as 12/30 × 360), A1 for 144° (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["A pie chart shares out one full turn between everybody asked.","Find what one person is worth in degrees before you deal with the tea drinkers.","The first line is 360 ÷ (the number of people asked) — that gives the degrees for one person."],"codes":["12.02a"]},{"q":"A scatter graph plots the number of hours of sunshine and the number of ice lollies sold at a park kiosk, for 12 days. The points rise from bottom left to top right and lie close to a straight line. A student makes three statements about the graph: (1) There is strong positive correlation between hours of sunshine and lollies sold. (2) More sunshine causes more lollies to be sold. (3) A day with more sunshine tends to have higher lolly sales. Which one of the three statements is wrong, and why? A calculator may be used.","a":"Statement (2) is wrong — correlation does not indicate causation, so the scatter graph can only show a link between sunshine and sales, not prove that the sunshine causes the sales","sol":"Statements (1) and (3) correctly describe what the scatter graph shows: the points rise together, so there is strong positive correlation, and a day with more sunshine tends to have higher sales. B1 for identifying statement (2) as the wrong one. Statement (2) wrongly claims causation: however strong the correlation, it does not prove that one variable causes the other, only that they are linked. B1 for a reason naming that correlation does not indicate causation (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Correlation describes a pattern between two variables; it does not by itself explain why that pattern exists.","Two of the three statements simply describe the pattern in the scatter graph; the other one claims something stronger that a scatter graph alone can never show.","The first line is to check each statement against what a scatter graph can actually tell you — a link, or a cause?"],"codes":["12.03c"]},{"q":"A factory makes 5000 light bulbs each day. A random sample of 200 of one day's bulbs is tested and 6 of them are faulty. Estimate the number of faulty bulbs made that day. A calculator may be used.","a":"150 bulbs","sol":"Fraction of the sample that is faulty = 6/200 = 0.03. Estimate for the day = 0.03 × 5000 = 150 bulbs. M1 for 6/200 × 5000 (oe, such as 5000 ÷ 200 = 25 then 6 × 25), A1 for 150 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Assume the whole day's production is faulty in the same proportion as the sample.","Work out the fraction of the sample that failed, then apply it to the whole day.","The first line is (number faulty in the sample) ÷ (size of the sample) — scale that up afterwards."],"codes":["12.01a"]},{"q":"A shop has recorded its takings at the end of each of the 12 months of last year. It wants a diagram that shows how the takings changed through the year. Write down the most suitable statistical diagram and give a reason for your choice. A calculator may be used.","a":"A line graph for the time series, because the takings are values recorded in time order and joining the points shows the trend through the year","sol":"The data are a time series — one value at each of 12 regular points in time — so the appropriate diagram is a table and line graph, with the months on the horizontal axis and the points joined in time order. B1 for line graph (time series graph), B1 for a reason referring to values over time or to showing the trend (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["Think about what kind of data has been collected: categories, separate counts, or measurements taken over time.","The diagram has to make a change through the year visible, not just the size of each month.","The first line is to decide what kind of data twelve monthly totals are; the choice of diagram follows from that."],"codes":["12.02a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A frequency table shows the number of pets owned by each of 25 students. 0 pets: 4 students. 1 pet: 5 students. 2 pets: 6 students. 3 pets: 7 students. 4 pets: 3 students. Work out the mean number of pets per student.","a":"2 pets","sol":"Σfx = 0 × 4 + 1 × 5 + 2 × 6 + 3 × 7 + 4 × 3 = 0 + 5 + 12 + 21 + 12 = 50 pets in total. Σf = 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 + 3 = 25 students. Mean = 50 ÷ 25 = 2 pets. M1 for at least three correct products fx, M1 (dep) for dividing their total by 25, A1 for 2 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The mean is still the total number of pets divided by the number of students; the table just saves you writing 25 numbers out.","Multiply each number of pets by how many students own that many, then add those products.","The first line is 0 × 4 + 1 × 5 + … — build the total number of pets before dividing."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"The grouped frequency table shows the masses, m kg, of 50 parcels. 0 < m ≤ 2: 9 parcels. 2 < m ≤ 4: 16 parcels. 4 < m ≤ 6: 15 parcels. 6 < m ≤ 8: 10 parcels. Work out an estimate of the mean mass of a parcel. A calculator may be used.","a":"4.04 kg","sol":"Midpoints: 1, 3, 5 and 7 kg. Σfx = 1 × 9 + 3 × 16 + 5 × 15 + 7 × 10 = 9 + 48 + 75 + 70 = 202. Mean = 202 ÷ 50 = 4.04 kg. M1 for using the midpoints 1, 3, 5, 7, M1 for Σfx ÷ 50, A1 for 4.04 kg (total 3 marks). It is an estimate because the individual masses within each class are not known.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The table does not tell you any individual mass, so each class needs one value to stand for all of it.","Take the value halfway across each class, multiply by that class's frequency, and add the results.","The first line is to write down the midpoint of each class — halfway between the two ends — before any multiplying."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"A scatter graph shows the mass, in kg, of each of 12 puppies against its age in weeks, for puppies aged from 4 to 20 weeks. A line of best fit is drawn and passes through the points (6, 3.5) and (18, 9.5). Use the line to estimate the mass of a puppy aged 12 weeks. A calculator may be used.","a":"6.5 kg","sol":"Gradient of the line = (9.5 − 3.5) ÷ (18 − 6) = 6 ÷ 12 = 0.5 kg per week. From (6, 3.5), 12 weeks is 6 weeks further on: 3.5 + 6 × 0.5 = 3.5 + 3 = 6.5 kg. M1 for a correct gradient calculation, M1 for adding the gradient × 6 to 3.5 (oe, such as taking the mean of 3.5 and 9.5 since 12 is halfway between 6 and 18), A1 for 6.5 kg (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["Everything you need is on the line of best fit itself, not on the individual points.","Work out how much mass the line adds for each extra week, then move along from a point you know.","The first line is (change in mass) ÷ (change in age) between the two given points — that is the mass gained per week."],"codes":["12.03c"]},{"q":"Twenty tomato plants were given fertiliser A and twenty were given fertiliser B. For A the median height was 14 cm and the range was 6 cm. For B the median height was 11 cm and the range was 15 cm. Compare the heights of the two sets of plants. A calculator may be used.","a":"The A plants are taller on average (median 14 cm against 11 cm) and their heights are more consistent (range 6 cm against 15 cm)","sol":"Average: A's median of 14 cm is greater than B's median of 11 cm, so on average the plants given A are 3 cm taller. Spread: A's range of 6 cm is smaller than B's range of 15 cm, so A's heights are more consistent, while B's vary much more. B1 for comparing the medians in context, B1 for comparing the ranges in context (total 2 marks); a comparison that names only one of the two earns only the one matching mark.","m":2,"d":2,"hints":["A full comparison always has two halves, and one of them is not about the typical height.","Say which fertiliser gives the taller plants on average, then say whose heights are bunched together and whose are spread out.","The first line is 'A has the higher median …' — then write the matching sentence about the ranges."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pictogram compares the number of cars sold by two dealerships last month, where each whole car symbol represents 10 cars sold. Dealership X used 4 whole symbols and Dealership Y used 3 whole symbols, but X's symbols are drawn twice as tall and twice as wide as Y's. Work out the actual number of cars sold by each dealership and the true difference between them, and explain why drawing X's symbols twice the size in each direction makes the pictogram misleading.","a":"Dealership X sold 40 cars and Dealership Y sold 30 cars, a true difference of only 10 cars (about a third more); but because X's symbols are twice as tall and twice as wide, each one covers about four times the area of a Y symbol, so X's picture looks far more than a third bigger than Y's, exaggerating the true difference","sol":"Each symbol is worth 10 cars, so X: 4 × 10 = 40 cars and Y: 3 × 10 = 30 cars. M1 for using the symbol value to find both 40 and 30. True difference = 40 − 30 = 10 cars. A1 for 10 (oe, one third more). Doubling a symbol's height and width multiplies its area by 2 × 2 = 4, so each of X's symbols looks about four times the size of one of Y's, making X's picture look far more than a third bigger even though the true difference is only 10 cars. B1 for identifying the area scaling from the doubled dimensions as the cause of the distortion (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A pictogram's symbols are meant to show quantity by how many are drawn, not by how big each one is drawn.","Work out how many cars each dealership actually sold using the symbol's value, then compare that with how the drawn pictures compare in size.","The first line is (number of whole symbols) × (cars each symbol represents), worked out for each dealership separately."],"codes":["12.03b"]},{"q":"A town has 30 000 residents. In a survey, a random sample of 50 residents was asked whether they shop in the town centre at least once a week, and 18 said yes. Work out the percentage of the sample who said yes, and use it to estimate the number of residents of the town who shop in the town centre at least once a week. A calculator may be used.","a":"36%; an estimated 10 800 residents","sol":"Percentage of the sample: 18 ÷ 50 = 0.36 = 36%. M1 for 18 ÷ 50 (oe), A1 for 36%. Estimate for the town: 0.36 × 30 000 = 10 800 residents. M1 for their percentage as a decimal × 30 000 (ft), A1 for 10 800 (total 4 marks). The figure is only an estimate: 50 is a small sample from 30 000, and the residents asked may not represent the whole town.","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The sample is standing in for the whole town, so whatever proportion holds in the sample is assumed to hold in the town.","Turn 18 out of 50 into a percentage first, then apply that percentage to the population.","The first line is (number who said yes) ÷ (size of the sample) — write that as a percentage before you use it to estimate for the whole town."],"codes":["12.01a","12.03a"]},{"q":"A scientist records the temperature, in °C, of a cooling cup of tea at various times after it was poured, for 12 readings taken between 2 minutes and 16 minutes after pouring. The points show strong negative correlation and lie close to a straight line. A line of best fit is drawn and passes through (2, 92) and (16, 64). Use the line to estimate the tea's temperature 10 minutes after pouring, and explain why the line should not be used to estimate the tea's temperature 60 minutes after pouring. A calculator may be used.","a":"76 °C; 60 minutes lies far outside the 2 to 16 minute range of the data, so using the line there is extrapolation and unreliable — the tea cannot keep cooling at a constant rate forever","sol":"Gradient = (64 − 92) ÷ (16 − 2) = (−28) ÷ 14 = −2 °C per minute. M1 for a correct gradient. From (2, 92), 10 minutes is 8 minutes further on: 92 + 8 × (−2) = 92 − 16 = 76 °C. M1 for 92 + their gradient × 8, A1 for 76 °C. B1 for saying that 60 minutes lies outside the range of the data, so the prediction is an extrapolation and unreliable (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["The two points given are on the line of best fit, so the line behaves like a straight-line graph between them.","Find how much the temperature falls for each extra minute, then step along from one of the given points; afterwards compare 60 minutes with the times that were actually recorded.","The first line is (change in temperature) ÷ (change in time) between the two given points."],"codes":["12.03c"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The masses, in kg, of nine dogs at a rescue centre are 12, 14, 15, 15, 16, 18, 19, 20 and 51. Work out the mean mass and the median mass, and state which of the two better describes a typical dog at the centre, giving a reason.","a":"Mean 20 kg, median 16 kg; the median, because 51 kg is an outlier that pulls the mean up","sol":"Total = 12 + 14 + 15 + 15 + 16 + 18 + 19 + 20 + 51 = 180 kg, so the mean = 180 ÷ 9 = 20 kg. M1 for their total ÷ 9, A1 for 20 kg. The nine masses are already in order, so the median is the 5th value, 16 kg. B1 for 16 kg. The median describes a typical dog better because 51 kg is an outlier, far above the other eight masses, and it drags the mean above all but one of the dogs. B1 for choosing the median with a reason naming the outlier (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work the two averages out first, then look at how the list is shaped before you choose between them.","Once you have both values, compare each with the masses of the individual dogs — does either sit above nearly all of them?","The first line is to add the nine masses; then count in from each end of the ordered list to find the middle one."],"codes":["12.03a","12.03d"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A pie chart shows how 180 employees travel to work. The sector for 'car' has an angle of 144° and the sector for 'bus' has an angle of 90°. Work out how many more employees travel by car than by bus, and work out the percentage of the employees who travel by bus.","a":"27 more by car; 25% travel by bus","sol":"The 180 employees share 360°, so one employee is worth 360 ÷ 180 = 2°. M1 for 2° per employee (oe). Car: 144 ÷ 2 = 72 employees. Bus: 90 ÷ 2 = 45 employees. M1 for both 72 and 45 (ft their degrees per employee). Difference = 72 − 45 = 27 employees. A1 for 27. Bus as a percentage = 90/360 × 100 = 25%. B1 for 25% (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Each sector's angle is a share of the full turn, and the full turn is shared between all 180 employees.","Convert both angles into numbers of employees before subtracting; the percentage can come straight from the angle instead.","The first line is 360 ÷ 180 — that is the number of degrees one employee is worth."],"codes":["12.02a"]},{"q":"A scatter graph plots the number of staff and the weekly takings, in £, for 10 branches of a shop chain, with staff numbers from 2 to 10. Nine of the points show strong positive correlation, and a line of best fit passes through (2, 3000) and (6, 7000). The tenth branch has 4 staff but weekly takings of £11 000, well above the line. (a) Use the line of best fit to find the takings the trend predicts for a branch with 4 staff. (b) Explain why the point for this branch is an outlier, and suggest one reason its takings do not fit the pattern. A calculator may be used.","a":"£5000 predicted by the trend; the branch is an outlier because its actual takings of £11 000 are about £6000 above the line — e.g. it may be in an unusually busy location, have taken a large one-off order, or the figure may have been recorded incorrectly","sol":"Gradient = (7000 − 3000) ÷ (6 − 2) = 4000 ÷ 4 = 1000 (extra takings per extra member of staff). M1. From (2, 3000), 4 staff is 2 more: 3000 + 2 × 1000 = £5000. M1 (dep), A1 for £5000. The actual point (4, 11 000) is 11 000 − 5000 = £6000 above the trend line, far from the general pattern, so it is an outlier. B1. A sensible context reason earns the final mark — e.g. an unusually busy location, a one-off large order, or a recording error. B1 (total 5 marks).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["An outlier sits a long way from the trend the rest of the points follow — the trend itself comes from the line of best fit.","Use the two given points on the line to find how much the takings rise for each extra member of staff, then step forward from a point you know.","The first line is (change in takings) ÷ (change in staff) between the two given points — that gives the rise in takings per member of staff."],"codes":["12.03c","12.03d"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Write down the range of 4, 8, 1, 12 and 6.","a":"11"},{"q":"Find the mode of 3, 5, 5, 5, 8 and 9.","a":"5"},{"q":"Which value in 3, 4, 4, 5, 26 is an outlier?","a":"26"},{"q":"Is reading a line of best fit for a value outside the range of the data called interpolation or extrapolation?","a":"Extrapolation"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Population (in statistics)","back":"Every member of the group being investigated — all the students in a school, all the parcels in a depot, all the residents of a town.","code":"12.01a","open":false},{"front":"Sample","back":"The part of the population that is actually measured or asked. It is used to infer properties of the population, which gives an estimate rather than a certainty.","code":"12.01a","open":false},{"front":"Bias in a sample","back":"A sample is biased when it is not chosen at random and so is not representative of the population. A larger sample does not remove bias; only a fair, random method of choosing does.","code":"12.01a","open":false},{"front":"Pie chart: the angle for an item","back":"Angle = (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°. Equivalently, one unit of frequency is worth 360° ÷ total frequency. All the angles must add up to 360°.","code":"12.02a","open":false},{"front":"Choosing a statistical diagram","back":"Categorical data: bar chart, pictogram or pie chart. Ungrouped discrete numerical data: vertical line chart. Values recorded over time: table and line graph. Two variables on the same items: scatter graph.","code":"12.02a","open":false},{"front":"Time series line graph","back":"Time goes on the horizontal axis and the points are joined in time order, so the trend over the period, including any seasonal variation, can be seen. Used for values recorded at regular times, such as monthly takings.","code":"12.02a","open":false},{"front":"Mean from a frequency table","back":"Ungrouped: mean = Σfx ÷ Σf, using each value x and its frequency f. Grouped: use each class midpoint as x; Σfx ÷ Σf is then only an estimate, since individual values are not known.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Median","back":"The middle value once the data are in order: the ((n + 1)/2)th of n values, or the mean of the two middle values when n is even.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Mode and modal class","back":"The mode is the most common value; there can be more than one, or none. For grouped data the modal class is the class with the largest frequency.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Range","back":"Range = largest value − smallest value, a measure of spread: the smaller the range, the more consistent the data.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Comparing two distributions","back":"Use 'like for like' summary values: name an average and a measure of spread, both in context — which set is larger on average, and which is more consistent. An average on its own does not score full marks.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Misrepresenting data","back":"A chart can mislead without being wrong: a vertical axis not starting at zero exaggerates or shrinks differences between bars, and an uneven or unlabelled scale, or unequal pictogram symbols, also distort the true picture.","code":"12.03b","open":false},{"front":"Types of correlation","back":"Positive: one rises as the other rises. Negative: one rises as the other falls. None: no pattern. Strong if the points lie close to a line, weak if scattered. Correlation never proves causation.","code":"12.03c","open":false},{"front":"Line of best fit: interpolation and extrapolation","back":"A line of best fit is drawn by eye through the trend of the points, with roughly as many above as below. Reading it inside the data's range is interpolation and fairly safe; reading it outside is extrapolation and unreliable.","code":"12.03c","open":false},{"front":"Outlier","back":"A value that does not fit the pattern of the rest — far from the other values in a list, or a point far from the line of best fit. It inflates the range and pulls the mean towards it.","code":"12.03d","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Pie chart angle for an item: angle = (frequency ÷ total frequency) × 360°, so one unit of frequency is worth 360° ÷ total frequency. Recall: not in 6.02d and not printed on OCR's Foundation formulae sheet.","Mean of a list of values: mean = (sum of the values) ÷ (number of values); rearranged, total = mean × number of values. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Foundation sheet.","Mean from a frequency table, ungrouped or grouped: mean = Σfx ÷ Σf, where x is each value (or class midpoint for grouped data) and f its frequency. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Foundation sheet.","Position of the median: for n values in order the median is the ((n + 1)/2)th value; for even n take the mean of the two middle values. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Foundation sheet.","Range = largest value − smallest value. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Foundation sheet.","Estimating a population figure from a sample: estimate = (number in the sample with the property ÷ sample size) × population size. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Foundation sheet.","Gradient of a line of best fit between two points on it = (change in the y value) ÷ (change in the x value); a prediction is then (y at a known point) + gradient × (change in x). Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Foundation sheet."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"Where these formulae come from","items":["Provenance: 6.02d, the specification's own list of formulae learners must know, contains no statistics result — for this content area it only requires circle, Pythagoras and trigonometry formulae — and OCR's Foundation tier formulae sheet (J560/01–03, current series) prints the trapezium, prism, circumference, circle area, Pythagoras, the three right-angled trigonometric ratios, compound interest and the probability addition rule. None of these cover statistics, so every result in this room must be recalled from memory on all three Foundation papers."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T18:54:15.775Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.84,"notes":"Every answer key, mark tally and worked example is correct and the room stays inside the Foundation statements — no cumulative frequency, box plots, quartiles or unequal-width histograms — but the facts list needs OCR’s paper numbers added and the exit ticket, two examples and one bank question need re-contexting away from their near-duplicates."}}},"statistics-higher":{"id":"OCR-J560","topic":"statistics-higher","family":"quantitative","lesson":{"why":"Statistics and probability carry a third of the marks on Higher tier, and this room holds the two diagrams that only Higher tier students meet: histograms with unequal class widths, and cumulative frequency graphs used to read off quartiles and to build box plots. The marks are almost all method marks you can secure reliably: frequency density is one division, the frequency shown by a bar is one multiplication, and the median and quartiles are three readings taken off a curve, with the inter-quartile range just one subtraction afterwards. The examiner's favourite traps are plotting frequency instead of frequency density when the classes have different widths, plotting a cumulative frequency point at the middle of a class instead of at its upper boundary, and comparing two box plots using only the medians with no measure of spread. Learn that the area of a bar is the frequency, that n/4, n/2 and 3n/4 are the positions to read across at, and the two-sentence comparison of a median and an inter-quartile range, and this room becomes some of the most dependable marks on Papers 4, 5 and 6.","idea":[{"h":"Histograms with unequal class widths, and cumulative frequency graphs","t":"A histogram displays grouped continuous data as bars that touch, with no gaps between them. When every class has the same width you could plot frequency, but as soon as the widths differ that is misleading, because a class twice as wide would look twice as important for no real reason. So the vertical axis is frequency density, where frequency density = frequency ÷ class width, and the frequency a bar stands for is then the area of the bar, not its height: frequency = frequency density × class width. Forwards, you are given a grouped table with unequal classes and divide each frequency by its class width to get the bar heights; backwards, you are given the bars and multiply height by width to recover each frequency. A third version asks for part of a class, and there you assume the values are spread evenly through it and take that fraction of the frequency. A cumulative frequency graph is a different diagram for a different job: it shows a position in the data rather than its shape. Cumulative frequency is the running total of the frequencies, and each point is plotted at the upper class boundary — not the midpoint, which is the commonest lost mark — then joined with a smooth curve starting at zero at the lower boundary of the first class.","code":"12.02b"},{"h":"Quartiles, the interquartile range, and box plots","t":"The quartiles cut an ordered set of data into four equal parts. For grouped data read from a cumulative frequency graph of n values, read across at n/2 for the median, at n/4 for the lower quartile and at 3n/4 for the upper quartile, then down to the horizontal axis; every answer taken this way is an estimate, because the graph stands in for the original list. For a short ordered list, find the median first, then the lower quartile is the middle of the values below it and the upper quartile is the middle of the values above it. Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile, which measures the spread of the middle half of the data and, because it throws away the top and bottom quarters, is not distorted by one unusually large or small value the way the range is. A box plot displays five values on one scale — minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum — with a box running from quartile to quartile, a line inside it at the median, and a whisker reaching out to each extreme. Box plots exist to be compared, and a comparison scores only when it gives both an average and a measure of spread, each written in context: one sentence on the medians, one on the inter-quartile ranges.","code":"12.03a"}],"examples":[{"title":"Frequency densities for a histogram with unequal classes","setup":"The waiting times, t minutes, of 50 customers at a coffee shop are grouped as follows. 0 < t ≤ 5: 15 customers. 5 < t ≤ 15: 15 customers. 15 < t ≤ 25: 10 customers. 25 < t ≤ 45: 10 customers. Work out the frequency density for each class and say what the bars of the histogram look like.","steps":["Subtract the two ends of each class to get widths of 5, 10, 10 and 20 minutes","Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width, so 15 ÷ 5 = 3 and 15 ÷ 10 = 1.5 customers per minute","For the last two classes, 10 ÷ 10 = 1 and 10 ÷ 20 = 0.5 customers per minute","So the bars have heights 3, 1.5, 1 and 0.5 customers per minute and are drawn edge to edge with no gaps, the narrowest class giving the tallest bar"],"cues":["How wide is each of the four classes, in minutes?","What must each frequency be divided by before it can be plotted?","Which of the remaining two classes will give the smaller frequency density?","Which class gives the tallest bar, and which gives the shortest?"]},{"title":"Reading frequencies back out of a histogram","setup":"A histogram shows the ages, a years, of the 80 members of a chess club. The bar for 0 < a ≤ 10 has frequency density 1.6 members per year, the bar for 10 < a ≤ 20 has frequency density 4, the bar for 20 < a ≤ 50 has frequency density 0.6 and the bar for 50 < a ≤ 70 has frequency density 0.3. Estimate how many members are older than 30.","steps":["Frequency = frequency density × class width, so the first two bars stand for 1.6 × 10 = 16 members and 4 × 10 = 40 members","The other two bars stand for 0.6 × 30 = 18 members and 0.3 × 20 = 6 members, and 16 + 40 + 18 + 6 = 80 as a check","Ages over 30 lie in the top part of the class 20 < a ≤ 50, which is (50 − 30) ÷ 30 = 2/3 of that class, so take 18 × 2/3 = 12 members","Add the whole of the last bar: 12 + 6 = 18 members are older than 30"],"cues":["What do you multiply a bar's height by to find the number of members it represents?","What should the four frequencies add up to?","What fraction of the 20 to 50 class lies above 30?","Which other bar lies entirely above 30?"]},{"title":"A cumulative frequency graph, its median and its quartiles","setup":"The masses, m grams, of 60 tomatoes picked from a greenhouse are grouped as follows. 40 < m ≤ 55: 8 tomatoes. 55 < m ≤ 65: 16 tomatoes. 65 < m ≤ 75: 20 tomatoes. 75 < m ≤ 85: 12 tomatoes. 85 < m ≤ 100: 4 tomatoes. Work out the cumulative frequencies, say where the points are plotted, and use the graph to estimate the median, the quartiles and the inter-quartile range.","steps":["Keep a running total: the cumulative frequencies are 8, 24, 44, 56 and 60 tomatoes","Plot each total at the upper end of its class, giving (55, 8), (65, 24), (75, 44), (85, 56) and (100, 60), and join them with a smooth curve from (40, 0)","There are 60 values, so read across at 30 and down to the axis: the median is about 68 g","Read across at 15 and at 45 for the quartiles: the lower quartile is about 59.4 g and the upper quartile about 75.8 g","Inter-quartile range = 75.8 − 59.4 = 16.4 g — readings from a hand-drawn curve vary, so a small margin either side is accepted — and the middle half of the tomatoes' masses lie within that spread"],"cues":["What do you add to each frequency to get the next running total?","At which end of each class does its cumulative frequency point belong?","Which cumulative frequency do you read across from for the median?","Which two cumulative frequencies give the quartiles?","Which two of the three readings does the inter-quartile range use?"]},{"title":"Turning graph readings into a box plot","setup":"A cumulative frequency graph has been drawn for the diameters of 100 sunflower heads grown for a competition. The smallest diameter is 8 cm and the largest is 34 cm. Reading off the curve gives a diameter of 16 cm at a cumulative frequency of 25, 21 cm at 50 and 27 cm at 75. Work out the five values needed for a box plot, describe the box plot, and find the inter-quartile range.","steps":["With 100 values, the median is read at 50, the lower quartile at 25 and the upper quartile at 75","So the median diameter is 21 cm, the lower quartile is 16 cm and the upper quartile is 27 cm","The five values in order are 8 cm, 16 cm, 21 cm, 27 cm and 34 cm: the box runs from the lower to the upper quartile with a line at the median, and a whisker reaches out to the smallest sunflower and to the largest","Inter-quartile range = 27 − 16 = 11 cm, which is the width of the box"],"cues":["What are n/4, n/2 and 3n/4 when there are 100 sunflower heads?","Which of the three readings is the median?","Which two of the five values are the ends of the whiskers?","Which measurement of the box gives the inter-quartile range?"]},{"title":"Comparing two box plots in context","setup":"Two box plots show how long, in hours, a single charge lasts on two models of wireless headphones. Model X: minimum 6, lower quartile 12, median 16, upper quartile 20, maximum 28. Model Y: minimum 10, lower quartile 15, median 17, upper quartile 19, maximum 24. Compare the two models using an average and a measure of spread, and say which model to buy if a consistent battery life matters.","steps":["Inter-quartile range for model X = 20 − 12 = 8 hours","Inter-quartile range for model Y = 19 − 15 = 4 hours","Comparing the averages, model Y's median of 17 hours is 1 hour longer than model X's, so a Y charge typically lasts a little longer","Comparing the spreads, model Y's inter-quartile range of 4 hours is smaller than model X's 8 hours, so Y's battery life is more consistent — buy model Y"],"cues":["Which two of model X's five values give its inter-quartile range?","What is the same calculation for model Y?","Which model has the higher median, and by how much?","Which model has the narrower box, and what does that say about consistency?"]}],"check":[{"q":"A class of width 16 contains 24 values. Work out the frequency density.","a":"1.5"},{"q":"On a cumulative frequency graph drawn for 300 values, at what cumulative frequency do you read across to estimate the lower quartile?","a":"75"},{"q":"A box plot has a lower quartile of 22 and an upper quartile of 39. Work out the inter-quartile range.","a":"17"},{"q":"Why is the median often a fairer average than the mean for data with an outlier value?","a":"It uses only the position of the middle value, so it is not pulled up or down by one very large or small value."},{"q":"A cumulative frequency point for the class 50 < x ≤ 65 is plotted at which value of x?","a":"65, the upper end of the class"}]},"room":{"facts":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. On a histogram it is the frequency density, never the frequency, that goes on the vertical axis when the classes have different widths.","Frequency = frequency density × class width, so the frequency is given by the area of the bar; the bars touch because the data are continuous.","To estimate the frequency in part of a class, assume the values are spread evenly through it and take that fraction of the class: half the class width holds about half the frequency.","Cumulative frequency is the running total of the frequencies, and each point is plotted at the upper end (the upper class boundary) of its class, then joined with a smooth curve.","From a cumulative frequency graph of n values: read across at n/2 for the median, at n/4 for the lower quartile and at 3n/4 for the upper quartile, then down to the horizontal axis.","Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. It measures the spread of the middle half of the data and is not distorted by an outlier.","A box plot shows five values: minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum. The box spans the quartiles with the median inside it and the whiskers reach the extremes.","Comparing two distributions needs an average and a measure of spread, both in context: compare the medians in one sentence and the inter-quartile ranges in the next.","OCR Mathematics has three papers per tier: Higher tier is assessed by J560/04, J560/05 and J560/06, each worth 100 marks and lasting 1 hour 30 minutes; Paper 5 (J560/05) is non-calculator, and Higher tier reports grades 9 to 4."],"questions":[{"q":"(non-calculator) The class 10 < x ≤ 30 contains 50 values. Work out the frequency density.","a":"2.5","sol":"Class width = 30 − 10 = 20. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width = 50 ÷ 20 = 2.5. M1 for 50 ÷ 20, A1 for 2.5 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The vertical axis of a histogram is not frequency when the classes have different widths.","Find the distance between the two ends of the class before you divide by anything.","Write frequency ÷ class width, then put the numbers in."],"codes":["12.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A bar of a histogram has class width 12 and frequency density 3.5. How many values does it represent?","a":"42","sol":"Frequency = frequency density × class width = 3.5 × 12. Without a calculator, 3.5 × 12 = 3 × 12 + 0.5 × 12 = 36 + 6 = 42. M1 for 3.5 × 12, A1 for 42 (total 2 marks).","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["On a histogram the frequency is shown by the area of the bar, not by how tall it is.","The rule for frequency density can be turned round to give the frequency.","Write frequency = frequency density × class width before multiplying."],"codes":["12.02b"]},{"q":"A cumulative frequency graph is drawn for 240 items. State the cumulative frequency you read across at to estimate (a) the median, (b) the lower quartile, (c) the upper quartile. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) 120 (b) 60 (c) 180","sol":"With n = 240, read at n/2 = 240 ÷ 2 = 120 for the median, at n/4 = 240 ÷ 4 = 60 for the lower quartile, and at 3n/4 = 3 × 240 ÷ 4 = 180 for the upper quartile. B1 for 120, B1 for both 60 and 180 (total 2 marks). Each reading is then taken down to the horizontal axis to give a value.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The quartiles cut the data into four equal parts, and the median cuts it into two.","Each reading is a fraction of the total number of items, not of the largest value.","Write the three positions as fractions of n before replacing n with 240."],"codes":["12.02b","12.03a"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) A box plot shows a minimum of 5, a lower quartile of 18, a median of 27, an upper quartile of 45 and a maximum of 60. Work out the inter-quartile range and the range.","a":"IQR 27, range 55","sol":"Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile = 45 − 18 = 27. Range = maximum − minimum = 60 − 5 = 55. B1 for 27, B1 for 55 (total 2 marks). The inter-quartile range is the width of the box and the range is whisker tip to whisker tip.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One of these two measures uses the ends of the box, and the other uses the ends of the whiskers.","Both are found by subtracting one of the five values from another.","Write inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile, and range = maximum − minimum."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"The masses, m kg, of 90 parcels at a depot are grouped as 0 < m ≤ 5: 9 parcels; 5 < m ≤ 15: 36 parcels; 15 < m ≤ 25: 30 parcels; 25 < m ≤ 50: 15 parcels. Work out the frequency density for each class so that a histogram can be drawn. A calculator may be used.","a":"1.8, 3.6, 3 and 0.6 parcels per kg","sol":"Class widths are 5, 10, 10 and 25 kg. Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width: 9 ÷ 5 = 1.8; 36 ÷ 10 = 3.6; 30 ÷ 10 = 3; 15 ÷ 25 = 0.6, all in parcels per kg. M1 for frequency ÷ class width used for at least one class, A1 for any two correct, A1 for all four correct (total 3 marks). The tallest bar is the 5 < m ≤ 15 bar even though its class is not the narrowest.","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The classes are not all the same width, so the frequencies cannot be plotted as they stand.","Work out how wide each class is first, then treat every class the same way.","Write frequency ÷ class width as a new column heading before filling it in."],"codes":["12.02b"]},{"q":"A histogram shows the rainfall, r mm, recorded on each of 100 days in a town. The bar for 0 < r ≤ 5 has frequency density 4, for 5 < r ≤ 13 has frequency density 5, for 13 < r ≤ 23 has frequency density 3, and for 23 < r ≤ 43 has frequency density 0.5. Estimate how many of the 100 days had rainfall between 8 mm and 20 mm. A calculator may be used.","a":"46 days","sol":"Frequency = frequency density × class width: 4 × 5 = 20, 5 × 8 = 40, 3 × 10 = 30 and 0.5 × 20 = 10. The range 8 to 20 mm cuts across two classes. From 8 to 13 mm is the top part of 5 < r ≤ 13, which is (13 − 8) ÷ 8 = 5/8 of that class, so 40 × 5/8 = 25 days. From 13 to 20 mm is the bottom part of 13 < r ≤ 23, which is (20 − 13) ÷ 10 = 7/10 of that class, so 30 × 7/10 = 21 days. Total = 25 + 21 = 46 days. M1 for a frequency found as frequency density × class width, M1 for taking the correct fraction of each of the two split classes, A1 for 46 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["8 mm and 20 mm each fall inside a different class, so part of two bars counts, not the whole of either.","Turn the bar heights into numbers of days first, then decide what share of each split class to keep.","Write frequency = frequency density × class width for the two bars that 8 mm and 20 mm fall inside."],"codes":["12.02b"]},{"q":"(non-calculator) The ages, in years, of 15 people at a family reunion are 4, 7, 9, 10, 12, 14, 15, 17, 19, 21, 23, 25, 27, 29, 33. Work out the median, the lower quartile, the upper quartile and the inter-quartile range.","a":"Median 17, LQ 10, UQ 25, IQR 15","sol":"The list is in order and n = 15, so the median is the 8th value = 17 years. The 7 values below it are 4, 7, 9, 10, 12, 14, 15, whose middle (4th) value is the lower quartile, 10 years. The 7 values above it are 19, 21, 23, 25, 27, 29, 33, whose middle (4th) value is the upper quartile, 25 years. Inter-quartile range = 25 − 10 = 15 years. B1 for the median 17, B1 for both quartiles 10 and 25, B1 for 15 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["The quartiles split the data into four equal parts, so find the middle value first.","Once the median is found, each half of the list has its own middle value.","Count 15 values and find the position of the middle one before you count along either half."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"The prices, in £, of 10 second-hand bikes at a shop are 40, 55, 60, 68, 75, 82, 90, 95, 110, 130. Write down the five values needed to draw a box plot of these prices. A calculator may be used.","a":"£40, £60, £78.50, £95 and £130","sol":"The list is in order and n = 10. Median = mean of the 5th and 6th values = (75 + 82) ÷ 2 = £78.50. Lower quartile = middle of the first five values 40, 55, 60, 68, 75, which is the 3rd value, £60. Upper quartile = middle of the last five values 82, 90, 95, 110, 130, which is the 3rd value, £95. Minimum = £40 and maximum = £130. B1 for the median £78.50, B1 for both quartiles £60 and £95, B1 for the minimum and maximum £40 and £130 (total 3 marks).","m":3,"d":2,"hints":["A box plot needs the two ends of the whiskers and the three values that make the box.","With an even number of values, a middle value is the mean of the two either side of the gap.","Find the middle of the whole list first, then treat each half the same way."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"The distances, d km, cycled by 70 riders in a sportive are grouped as 20 < d ≤ 40: 8 riders; 40 < d ≤ 55: 16 riders; 55 < d ≤ 65: 24 riders; 65 < d ≤ 80: 14 riders; 80 < d ≤ 100: 8 riders. Work out the cumulative frequencies and use a cumulative frequency graph to estimate the median distance and the inter-quartile range. A calculator may be used.","a":"Median about 59.6 km (accept 59 to 60), IQR about 20.9 km (accept 20 to 22)","sol":"Cumulative frequencies: 8, 24, 48, 62, 70, plotted at 40, 55, 65, 80 and 100 km. n = 70, so read across at 35 for the median: it falls in the 55 < d ≤ 65 class, giving 55 + (35 − 24)/24 × 10 ≈ 59.6 km. Read across at 17.5 for the lower quartile: it falls in the 40 < d ≤ 55 class, giving 40 + (17.5 − 8)/16 × 15 ≈ 48.9 km. Read across at 52.5 for the upper quartile: it falls in the 65 < d ≤ 80 class, giving 65 + (52.5 − 48)/14 × 15 ≈ 69.8 km. Inter-quartile range = 69.8 − 48.9 = 20.9 km. M1 for the correct cumulative frequencies, M1 for identifying the classes holding the 35th, 17.5th and 52.5th values, A1 for the median about 59.6 km, M1 for the readings at 17.5 and 52.5, A1 for the inter-quartile range about 20.9 km (total 5 marks).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The graph is built from running totals, not from the separate class frequencies.","Three readings across from the cumulative frequency axis give everything you need, once the class each falls in is found.","Write the running totals of the frequencies, and plot each one at the top end of its class."],"codes":["12.02b","12.03a"]},{"q":"A histogram shows the heights, h cm, of 150 plants in a nursery. The bar for 0 < h ≤ 20 has frequency density 1.8, the bar for 20 < h ≤ 35 has frequency density 4, the bar for 35 < h ≤ 60 has frequency density 1.6, and the bar for 60 < h ≤ 100 has frequency density 0.35. Estimate the median height. A calculator may be used.","a":"29.75 cm","sol":"Frequency = frequency density × class width: 1.8 × 20 = 36, 4 × 15 = 60, 1.6 × 25 = 40 and 0.35 × 40 = 14, and 36 + 60 + 40 + 14 = 150 as a check. Cumulative frequencies are 36, 96, 136, 150, so the 75th value (150 ÷ 2) lies in the class 20 < h ≤ 35, which is the 37th to the 96th plant. Assuming an even spread through that class, median = 20 + (75 − 36)/60 × 15 = 20 + 9.75 = 29.75 cm. M1 for frequency = frequency density × class width, A1 for all four frequencies 36, 60, 40 and 14, M1 for identifying the class holding the 75th value, M1 for the interpolation (75 − 36)/60 × 15, A1 for 29.75 cm (total 5 marks).","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["The histogram hides the frequencies, so recover them before thinking about the middle plant.","Running totals show which class the middle plant falls in.","Write frequency = frequency density × class width for every bar before any totalling."],"codes":["12.02b","12.03a"]},{"q":"Two box plots show the mock exam scores, out of 40, of two Year 11 classes. Class M: minimum 10, lower quartile 22, median 27, upper quartile 33, maximum 39. Class N: minimum 4, lower quartile 24, median 26, upper quartile 30, maximum 37. (a) Work out the inter-quartile range for each class. (b) State which class has the more consistent scores. (c) Explain why the range would be a less reliable way to compare the two classes here. A calculator may be used.","a":"(a) Class M: IQR 11; Class N: IQR 6. (b) Class N. (c) Class N's minimum of 4 is an unusually low score that inflates its range to 33, even though its inter-quartile range of 6 shows the middle scores are closely bunched; the range is distorted by that one low score, but the inter-quartile range is not.","sol":"Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. Class M: 33 − 22 = 11. Class N: 30 − 24 = 6. B1 for 11, B1 for 6. Class N has the smaller inter-quartile range, so its scores are more consistent: B1. Range: Class M = 39 − 10 = 29, Class N = 37 − 4 = 33 — Class N's low minimum of 4 is an outlier that makes its range larger even though its middle scores are tightly bunched, so the range would wrongly suggest Class N is the less consistent class; the inter-quartile range ignores that one extreme value: B1 (total 4 marks).","m":4,"d":3,"hints":["Work out both inter-quartile ranges before deciding which class is more consistent.","The class with the smaller inter-quartile range has scores bunched closer to the middle.","Compare each class's range with its inter-quartile range, and look for a value far from the rest of that class's data."],"codes":["12.03a"]},{"q":"The amounts, £x, won by 60 competitors in a raffle are grouped as 0 < x ≤ 5: 20 competitors; 5 < x ≤ 10: 18 competitors; 10 < x ≤ 20: 12 competitors; 20 < x ≤ 40: 7 competitors; 40 < x ≤ 100: 3 competitors. Work out an estimate of the mean amount won, estimate the median from a cumulative frequency graph, and explain which of the two is the larger and why. A calculator may be used.","a":"Mean £13.08, median about £7.78 (accept £7 to £8.50); the mean is larger because a few competitors won much more than the rest and the mean counts every value","sol":"Midpoints: 2.5, 7.5, 15, 30 and 70. M1 for using the midpoints. Σfx = 2.5 × 20 + 7.5 × 18 + 15 × 12 + 30 × 7 + 70 × 3 = 50 + 135 + 180 + 210 + 210 = 785. M1 for Σfx ÷ 60. Mean = 785 ÷ 60 = £13.08 (2 d.p.). A1. Cumulative frequencies: 20, 38, 50, 57, 60; with n = 60 the median is read at 30, which falls in the class 5 < x ≤ 10, giving 5 + (30 − 20)/18 × 5 ≈ £7.78. M1 for the reading at a cumulative frequency of 30, A1 for the median. B1 for saying that the mean is the larger because the small number of large prizes in the 40 < x ≤ 100 class pull the mean up, while the median only depends on the position of the middle value (total 6 marks).","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two different averages are wanted, and each comes from a different way of using the table.","For the mean, each class needs a single value to stand for it; for the median, the running totals are what matter.","Write down the midpoint of each class, and separately the running total of the frequencies, before calculating either average."],"codes":["12.02b","12.03a"]}],"exit":[{"q":"A histogram has one bar for a class of width 5 with frequency density 8, and another bar for a class of width 20 with frequency density 3. Which bar represents more values, and by how many?","a":"The second bar, by 20 (60 values against 40)"},{"q":"On a histogram a bar of width 6 has a frequency density of 4.5. How many values does it represent?","a":"27"},{"q":"Box plot A has a median of 18 minutes and box plot B has a median of 25 minutes, for two samples of the same size. By how many minutes is B's median greater than A's?","a":"7 minutes"},{"q":"A cumulative frequency table for 80 delivery times, t minutes, gives running totals of 12 for 0 < t ≤ 10, 30 for 10 < t ≤ 20, 55 for 20 < t ≤ 30, 72 for 30 < t ≤ 45 and 80 for 45 < t ≤ 60. Which class contains the median?","a":"20 < t ≤ 30"}]},"cards":[{"front":"Frequency density","back":"Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. It is what goes on the vertical axis of a histogram, and its units are 'per cm', 'per minute' and so on.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"What a bar of a histogram represents","back":"The area of the bar is the frequency: frequency = frequency density × class width. The height alone only gives the frequency when every class has width 1.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"Class width of a continuous class","back":"The distance between the two class boundaries: the class 20 < t ≤ 35 has width 15. Bars are drawn from boundary to boundary and touch, because the data are continuous.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"Estimating the frequency in part of a class","back":"Assume the values are spread evenly through the class and take the same fraction of its frequency: the top half of a class holds about half of it. It is an estimate, not exact.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"Cumulative frequency","back":"The running total of the frequencies: each class's frequency added to all those before it. The final cumulative frequency is the total number of items.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"Plotting a cumulative frequency graph","back":"Plot each cumulative frequency against the upper end of its class, not the midpoint, starting at zero at the lower boundary of the first class, and join the points with a smooth curve.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"Reading how many are more than a value from a cumulative frequency graph","back":"Go up from the value to the curve and across: that gives how many are less than or equal to it. Subtract from the total to get how many are more than it.","code":"12.02b","open":false},{"front":"Median from a cumulative frequency graph of n values","back":"Read across at a cumulative frequency of n/2 and down to the horizontal axis. The answer is an estimate, because the graph is used in place of the original values.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Lower and upper quartiles from a cumulative frequency graph of n values","back":"Read across at n/4 for the lower quartile and at 3n/4 for the upper quartile, then down to the horizontal axis. They cut the data into four equal parts.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Quartiles of a short ordered list","back":"Find the median first. The lower quartile is the middle of the values below it and the upper quartile is the middle of the values above it; with an even number, average the two middle values.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Inter-quartile range","back":"Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. It is the spread of the middle half of the data and, unlike the range, is not distorted by an outlier.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"The five values a box plot shows","back":"Minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum. The box runs from quartile to quartile with a line at the median; the whiskers reach the minimum and the maximum.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Comparing two distributions from box plots","back":"Give an average and a measure of spread, both in context: compare the medians in one sentence, then the inter-quartile ranges. The smaller inter-quartile range means the more consistent data.","code":"12.03a","open":false},{"front":"Give one reason for choosing a cumulative frequency graph rather than a histogram","back":"It gives the median and the quartiles; it shows how many values are less than (or more than) a given value; it shows a position in the data, not the shape of the distribution.","code":"12.02b","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"formulae","title":"Formulae sheet","items":["Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. Recall: not listed in the specification's 6.02d and not printed on OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet.","Frequency from a histogram: frequency = frequency density × class width, the area of the bar. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","Estimating a frequency inside part of a class: frequency ≈ (width of the part wanted ÷ class width) × frequency of the class, assuming the values are spread evenly. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","Cumulative frequency = the running total of the frequencies, plotted against the upper end of each class. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","Positions read off a cumulative frequency graph of n grouped values: median at n/2, lower quartile at n/4, upper quartile at 3n/4. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","Median of a short ordered list of n values: the ((n + 1)/2)th value, taking the mean of the two middle values when n is even. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","Inter-quartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","Range = largest value − smallest value, the whisker-tip to whisker-tip length of a box plot. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","The five values of a box plot: minimum, lower quartile, median, upper quartile, maximum. Recall: not in 6.02d, not on the Higher sheet.","What OCR's Higher tier formulae sheet prints (2026 series): area of a trapezium, volume of a prism, circumference of a circle, area of a circle, the quadratic formula, Pythagoras' theorem, the sine, cosine and tangent ratios, the sine rule, the cosine rule, the area of a triangle from two sides and the included angle, compound interest, the addition rule for probability and the multiplication rule using a conditional probability — not one of them belongs to this room, so on the day the sheet gives you nothing here.","What 6.02d lists as formulae learners must know: the quadratic formula, the sine rule, the cosine rule and Area of a triangle = ½ab sin C (Higher tier additionally), plus the circle formulae, Pythagoras' theorem and the trigonometric ratios carried up from earlier columns — no statistics result is among them, so everything above must be written from memory, including on Papers 4 and 6, where the calculator does the arithmetic but not the choosing.","Not a formula but marked like one: when the classes have different widths it is the area of the bar, not its height, that represents the frequency.","Not a formula but marked like one: a cumulative frequency point is plotted at the upper class boundary, never at the midpoint of the class.","Not a formula but marked like one: any value read from a cumulative frequency graph, or found by interpolation, is an estimate, because it assumes the values are spread evenly within each class.","Not a formula but marked like one: a comparison of two distributions must give an average and a measure of spread, both in context."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-19T19:08:02Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-sonnet-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.92,"notes":"Re-judged against the current kit. All 12 questions, 5 worked examples, 5 check questions and 4 exit items re-solved from scratch with node and every answer key is right: the rewritten question 5 (rainfall histogram) gives frequencies 20, 40, 30 and 10 totalling the stated 100 days, with 5/8 of 40 = 25 days plus 7/10 of 30 = 21 days = 46 days; the rewritten question 10 gives IQRs 11 and 6 with ranges 29 and 33, so part (c) is exactly right that Class N's minimum of 4 inflates its range while its middle half stays tight; the new exit items check out at 60 against 40 (more by 20), 27, a 7-minute median gap, and the class 20 < t <= 30 holding the 40th delivery. Every sol tally adds to its m, the four non-calculator questions remain hand-workable, difficulty is 4/4/4 with no d = 1 question over 2 marks, there are 14 cards with one open and every back inside its word limit, and the Formulae sheet extra still matches appendix.md item for item, correctly concluding that the Higher sheet prints nothing this room uses. All three faults the previous verdict raised are properly fixed: room.facts now carries J560/04-06, 100 marks, 1 hour 30 minutes, Paper 5 non-calculator and grades 9-4; the exit ticket re-asks nothing from lesson.check; and neither rewritten question retraces a worked example. kit-tools check reports OK. Publishable."}}}} };
